1. 　The doorbell chimes through the quiet shop.\nI pause from my studies and glance up.
2. 　This spot, which has practically become my reserved seat,\nlets me look out at the shop unbothered,\nthanks to the potted plants that serve as a partition.
3. "Hmm? So this is the place."
4. "Yes, right this way."
5. "Welcome~! Please wait just a moment while I show you to a seat--"
6. "Ah, that won't be necessary."
7. "Uh......"
8. 　The woman escorted in by the lawyer completely ignores the part-timer\nand strides briskly toward the back of the shop.
9. 　The lawyer carefully sets down the heavy-looking bundle in her arms on a nearby table,\nthen follows after the woman.
10. 　The part-timer, wearing a suspicious expression, quietly slips into the kitchen.\nGoing to tell Toritani, no doubt.
11. "This is it?"
12. "Yes."
13. "Hmm......"
14. 　The two of them come to a stop in front of the Magpie painting.\nThe woman confirms with the lawyer, then eyes the painting with brazen scrutiny.
15. "Just as I thought. You can fool anyone else you want, but you can't fool my eye. Honestly... that woman, she was never anything but a skilled liar. What a bother, wasn't she?"
16. "......Yes."
17. "So? Where is that woman's daughter?"
18. 　With an arrogant face, she sweeps her eyes across the shop.\nThere are no other customers besides me.
19. "Ma'am, I'm terribly sorry, but you're startling the other customers.\nCould I ask you to keep your voice down?"
20. "Ha, putting on airs. There aren't any other customers.\nIt's dead in here at this hour--aren't you close to shutting down for good?"
21. 　Technically, I'm here... well, from her position this seat is probably hard to see.\nI pull back my chair and step out from behind the plants.
22. 　The woman glances at me but resolutely decides to ignore me completely.\nShe doesn't seem to have any intention of retracting what she just said.
23. 　She looks Toritani up and down and snorts derisively.
24. "......What tacky clothes. Well, they suit the daughter of the Toritani house well enough."
25. "Do you have some business here, Reika-san? If so, I'll hear you out in the back, or upstairs."
26. 　Reika... just as I thought. So this is the woman at the root of it all.
27. "Huh? Why on earth would I go trudging up the stairs into that filthy room of yours?\nMakes no sense."
28. 　Toritani whispers a few brief instructions to the part-timer,\nwho's watching the situation from just behind her.
29. 　The part-timer scurries out of the shop and hangs the CLOSED sign on the door.\nThen, apparently, she took her break.
30. "Well, then...... I've come all the way here for you, so\nyou know what you're supposed to do, don't you?"
31. 　Toritani lets out a soft breath, then looks Reika straight in the eye.\nNot as a Chimera employee, but as Toritani Makoto, she confronts Reika.
32. "Oh? I have no idea, and I don't want to know."
33. "My......"
34. 　Reika's eyes blink in apparent surprise.
35. "You...... I knew already, but you really are stupid, aren't you? Ha...... it can't be helped.\nSuetake, please explain it politely so that even this idiot can understand."
36. "Honma Reika-sama, present here, is requesting the return\nof the magpie vase in the Toritani family's possession--the Snowscape Magpie Vase."
37. "Return......? That vase is part of the Toritani collection.\nI think there's been some kind of misunderstanding."
38. "My, the nerve. Like parent, like child, isn't it...... ah, come to think of it,\nShizuru was just the same."
39. 　Shizuru......? Why is the name of the master of Chimera, Toritani's cousin,\ncoming up here......?
40. 　No. There's no "why" about it, is there. I see......\nthe seeds of trouble the master planted in the past ran quite deep, then.
41. "I'll enlighten you, since you know nothing. That magpie vase......\nit was supposed to have been given to me. Shizuru and I agreed on it,\nbut she got greedy and hid it away."
42. "Hid it...... don't tell me, that's why she took such a risk...... over the vase?"
43. "Also, we've long since seen through your little scheme to collude with the police\nagainst me. So we've prepared countermeasures on our end."
44. 　Ignoring Toritani's question as if it had never been asked in the first place,\nReika jerks her chin lightly at the lawyer.
45. 　The lawyer unties the knot of the wrapping cloth she'd carried in\nand carefully lifts out the item inside a wooden box.
46. "This is......"
47. 　It was a vase. The design on the vase was\nan exact match for the Magpie painting hanging on Chimera's wall.
48. "You see, I only want the real thing.\nAn artwork ought to be held by someone with the eye to recognize genuine quality, wouldn't you agree?"
49. "The true value of that magpie vase can only be brought out\nwhen it's owned by me--someone who knows real art."
50. "So, listen carefully. Swap this vase with the one at the Toritani house.\nIf you do that, I'll consent to forget about Kei's painting."
51. "Understand? I'm saying I'll forgive Onda Kirino for injuring my child.\nBe grateful for my mercy."
52. "It was your child who badly injured Kirino-san's daughter!"
53. "Huh? What are you talking about? She had it coming. And yet you turn around and\nblame us......? A thief crying wolf, as they say."
54. "Those words hardly become someone who planted a bug in Kirino-san's clothes to eavesdrop on us...... you're the worst."
55. "Oh, that's right. That woman broke that device, didn't she. I've billed her for it. Tell her to pay up properly."
56. "Billed her...... Reika-san, do you understand what you're saying?\nWhat you've done is a crime!"
57. "What was that? The audacity, ignoring your own actions to say something like that.\nJust as expected from the daughter of the thieving woman who seduced my brother."
58. "Your Toritani clan has been nothing but trouble for me since the old days,\nand honestly, I've had enough of it. Taking Kei and Kei's paintings, which belong to the Nakamura family...... do the Toritani have thieves' blood running through their veins?"
59. "Belong to...... Kei is a person. He doesn't belong to anyone."
60. "Ah...... poor brother. He should never have married a woman like that."
61. 　Reika lets out an exaggerated sigh and stands up.\nThere's no meeting of minds in this conversation at all.
62. "Well then, we're leaving. I'm a busy woman."
63. "Yes, Reika-sama."
64. "Well then, Toritani-sama. We will contact you again another day regarding the details,\nand at that time, we would appreciate your cooperation."
65. 　The lawyer casts a fleeting, meaningful glance at Toritani.
66. 　But it was only for an instant, and she immediately returned to her businesslike expression.
67. "Now then, if you'll excuse us."
68. 　The lawyer bows politely, then follows after Reika.
69. "Say, Suetake. Can't I have that magpie painting too?\nI mean, why not? I'm only asserting my legitimate rights."
70. "Yes, it's just as you say, Reika-sama."
71. "I'm going out of my way to pay a fortune to employ a slow, incompetent creature like you,\nso get it done quickly. Don't be a nuisance to me."
72. "......Understood."
73. 　Reika and the lawyer leave Chimera.
74. "Sorry...... I dragged you into something strange."
75. "Ah, no...... I don't mind at all, but......\nwhat that woman was saying--it's completely absurd, isn't it?"
76. "Yes. Self-righteous, self-centered, and utterly convinced of her own delusions......\nshe bends the facts however it suits her."
77. "She projects her own actions onto other people,\nthen denounces them without a shred of doubt that it's the truth."
78. "You can't sum it up with just 'selfish'......\nit's not that she was never like that before, but......"
79. "I felt it ever since we met again at Sunday school--her age has only made her original\npersonality more entrenched. Honestly, she's become unmanageable."
80. "Yeah. Still, to think that this Reika person had a hand even in the burglary\nyou were talking about back at the Toritani house......"
81. "Reika didn't personally break into the house, mind you......\nbut apparently the burglar said the woman ordered him to do it."
82. "Wait, the headmaster caught the burglar?"
83. "Who knows? She does hire bodyguards or something, so\nmaybe one of them caught him?"
84. "I heard it was settled through discussion, and she never contacted the police--\nI'd been thinking that was odd......"
85. "She never contacted the police? Then this business about the police being in her pocket\nthat Reika was going on about is pure fantasy?"
86. "Precisely. But...... it's possible that's when that lawyer\nand the headmaster first met."
87. "That lawyer seemed to have it rough too......"
88. "Yes......"
89. "You were talking in front of that Magpie painting the other day, weren't you.\nAbout deceiving someone with a work made based on that painting."
90. "Ah...... I did say that. You've figured it out already?"
91. "Yeah. You told me she'd griped that she'd tricked too many people\nand the bill had come due."
92. "That's right. This is the continuation of that bill. But surely even the master\nnever imagined the fallout from that debt would land on us."
93. "Meaning that Reika woman's grudge runs that deep, and her delusions run that strong."
94. "Exactly. Over the long years, Reika-san has grown into a monster\nbeyond anything the master ever anticipated...... something like that, I suppose."
95. "Sorry, but I'm closing up for today.\nI'll call the part-timer, then go change."
96. "Are you going to the headmaster's? Then I'm coming too."
97. "You don't need to come along...... is what I'd like to say, but after hearing all of this,\nyou're not the type who'd listen if I told you to go home, are you."
98. "That's right, and besides, I'm already in this thing up to my neck. So\ngive up and take me along. I might just be of some use, you know?"
99. "I know that. Honestly...... this is why Kusanagi is such a handful."
100. "Am I? A handful?"
101. "Who knows?"
102. 　By the time we managed to catch the busy headmaster, it was already night.
103. 　The headmaster, having listened silently to the whole story, looks back and forth between me and Toritani.
104. "So then, what do the two of you intend to do?"
105. "Are you saying to just hand her the magpie vase?"
106. "No. Someone like her won't be satisfied until she has everything."
107. "She intends to get her hands on the magpie vase and on Kei's painting."
108. 　At the very least, that Reika woman doesn't doubt it for a second.\nShe must think that, as always, everything will go exactly her way.
109. "That's right. And getting her hands on that will only bring shame down on her."
110. "There's apparently an antique appraisal event coming up. Connoisseurs will be gathering with their most prized pieces.\nI imagine that's where she plans to take it."
111. "I see."
112. "...... If necessary, she may demand the magpie vase.\nBut I'll deal with things in my own way."
113. "Headmaster Toritani, I'm not asking you to do anything about it.\nBut keeping each other informed is important."
114. "Indeed. Sharing information is extremely important."
115. "Yes. So I just wanted to let you know that Reika came to me\nand told me to swap the magpie vase for a fake."
116. "Or is that already known to you as well?\nYou seem to be plotting various things with that lawyer, after all."
117. "......Attorney Suetake is Honma Reika's personal counsel."
118. "I see. She seems fairly worn out by Reika's selfishness, though."
119. 　No, it's not just being worn out by selfishness anymore.\nToritani knows that, but is deliberately using mild words.
120. 　That lawyer is utterly exhausted by Reika's outrageous demands.\nThe headmaster is not one to fail to notice that.
121. "In any case, I'll settle things in my own way. So I'll leave the settlement to you."
122. "You don't need to tell me. But rest assured. It will all be resolved in a few more days.\nOtherwise, I wouldn't be keeping the Onda mother and daughter cooped up like this."
123. "......Can you win?"
124. "You see, victory and defeat are, for the most part, decided before the battle begins."
125. "You could have just gone home, you know. You must be tired."
126. 　Toritani gives me an exasperated look for tagging along all the way back to Chimera.\nBut there's a note of concern in her voice.
127. "You're about to start thinking, aren't you. About how to handle Reika."
128. "Mm...... well, yes. If their settlement is coming up soon,\nI'd like to wrap this up quickly on our end too."
129. "Then, why don't you hear me out."
130. "Yours, Kusanagi? Don't tell me you've already come up with something in this short time?"
131. "Sure have. When it comes to wicked schemes, leave it to me."
132. "Wicked schemes...... hmm."
133. "Ah, no, it's not that villainous a scheme, all right?"
134. "I know that. ......Fine, let me hear it."
135. "I see......"
136. 　Hearing my idea, Toritani folds her arms with a serious expression.\nYou can see from her face that she's giving it careful consideration.
137. "Well, it does touch on your taboos, at least somewhat, so\nif you don't want to do it, you don't have to--"
138. "I'll do it."
139. "That's an instant answer."
140. "Yes."
141. "It's true that, given my way of life and my management principles as Shirasu Toko,\nI refused to let that Four Stars vase out into the world."
142. "I'm not saying those were empty words. But this is a matter of priorities."
143. "I want to settle things before the headmaster and Reika reach their settlement.\nIn that case, something that can be carried out immediately is best."

=== lines=188 stop_reason=end_turn in=6628 out=4683 cache_read=0 cache_create=3750 last_entry_continued=0 ===
1. "It's hooot..."
2. "Shut up. Get moving, chop chop!"
3. "Whew... but it really is hot..."
4. "Rina, are you okay?"
5. "Well, Yumi's kind enough to hold a parasol over me, so I'm fine."
6. "Temperature-wise, that other job was way hotter, though..."
7. "I guess that's what they call runner's high, or maybe a trance state..."
8. "Yes, that was fun."
9. "Come on, less chitchat and more cleaning!"
10. "You've got to scrub this out by evening. Chop, chop, chop, chop!"
11. "Yeah, yeah..."
12. "It's your fault the training camp had to be held at school!"
13. "Our fault...? You guys are the ones who stuck your noses in."
14. "But the ones sneaking around behind the scenes were Kusanagi-senpai and Akashi-senpai, weren't they?"
15. "Wrong! I wasn't the ringleader of that plan, and I didn't help mastermind it either! I only took part in the actual work!"
16. "Is that so? But outwardly, you're the ringleader, aren't you, senpai?"
17. "That's exactly why it's a pain for me too! Honestly, it's infuriating!"
18. 　The end of August.
19. 　We're in the middle of our training camp.
20. 　We were supposed to be surrounded by greenery, with the sea breeze in our hair...\n
21. and yet, for some reason, we're cleaning a chlorine-reeking pool.
22. 　That training camp we couldn't go on... we're holding it at Yumihari Academy instead.\n
23. Of all places, why did it have to be at school?\n
24. It couldn't be more disappointing...
25. "Hmm, right now, weren't you thinking something like, 'Of all places, why do we have to do this at school? It couldn't be more disappointing~'?"
26. "Uh, n-no, of course not!"
27. 　She hit the bullseye.\n
28. Was it that obvious on my face?
29. "What is that! If that's true, I'll kill you!"
30. "N-no, that's not..."
31. "That's right. You mustn't ever think such a thing.\n
32. This training camp I was so looking forward to, even bought a swimsuit for.\n
33. Kusanagi-senpai and Akashi-senpai ruined the whole thing..."
34. "Ahaha..."
35. 　Sure enough, once talk turns to the cancelled camp, she gets murderous.\n
36. Which, well, is only natural, I suppose...
37. "Oh, come on, it's fine. We still get to have a camp like this, right?"
38. "How is it fine...?\n
39. Swimming at the beach and cleaning a pool are worlds apart, you know?"
40. "I was kind of looking forward to sketching out in nature.\n
41. But this is fun too.\n
42. It's the first time we've all cleaned a pool together."
43. "Oh, yeah, shasei... enjoying shasei out in nature... oh yeah."
44. "You there! Don't be rubbing the tip of that mop like that!"
45. "Whew..."
46. 　From the pool, you can see the wall of the church.
47. 　Which stands to reason.\n
48. It all began when I was napping by the pool and spotted Akashi.
49. 　Naturally, the church is visible from here.
50. "Well, how should I put it... it's really something, isn't it?\n
51. The church from this angle has its own quiet charm."
52. "Yeah. When we finished that piece, I was prepared for anything..."
53. "Well, it was the kind of thing you had to be prepared for..."
54. "But in the end, it all worked out."
55. "It pisses me off when you say it so blithely."
56. "Why?"
57. 　A lot has happened since the day we finished that mural on the church wall.
58. 　Truly, more than you'd ever imagine happening in an ordinary summer break.
59. 　To speak only of results:\n
60. the painting was allowed to remain,\n
61. and I -- and the art club -- were allowed to survive.
62. 　With one exception, at least...
63. 　The last day of July.
64. 　Or rather, the early morning of the first day of August.
65. 　That work was completed.
66. 　Like cherry trees that had bloomed wildly out of season in high summer, it appeared abruptly upon the church wall.\n
67. Kusanagi Ken'ichirou's "The Footprints of the Sakura."
68. 　Using small, childlike footprints in place of paint, it depicts cherry trees in full bloom,\n
69. and further, the petals scatter down onto a stained-glass surface fashioned to resemble water.
70. 　The stained glass, seen from inside the church, is beautiful, of course.\n
71. Drawing in an abundance of outside light, the blue-toned glass dyes the polished white marble in exquisite hues.
72. 　But it doesn't end there.\n
73. The stained glass, seen from outside, is not the radiant blue of the interior but a dim, beautiful, translucent water's surface.
74. 　That effect only comes together once the "sakura" is painted upon the wall.\n
75. Cherry blossoms adrift in the sky as the mural, water's surface as the stained glass -- by combining these two elements, the somber dimness is brought into relief.
76. 　As for completed cherry works by Kusanagi Ken'ichirou -- excluding studies and the like -- "The Reclining Sakura" was the only one.
77. 　That was the common understanding in the art world.\n
78. Precisely for that reason, "The Footprints of the Sakura," which he had completed as a design during his lifetime, would draw a response not just from the art world but from all quarters.
79. 　Just like the shock I felt when I first saw this design myself... no, greater than that.
80. 　What I saw was the design.
81. 　But what everyone else saw was the "finished product" that appeared out of nowhere on a midsummer's night.
82. 　Anyone who had anything to do with Kusanagi Ken'ichirou would have considered the "sakura" motif to be something special to him, and as if to prove it, up to that point he had never released a "sakura" work other than "The Reclining Sakura."
83. 　That is precisely why I thought this plan had a chance.\n
84. Completing this work was by no means a losing battle.
85. 　In that sense, one could say this outcome was, in a way, already factored in.
86. 　No, that's probably going too far...
87. 　If it goes well, so much the better.\n
88. If it turns out that way, we come out ahead -- that was more or less how I thought about it.\n
89. Maybe.
90. 　Either way, depending on how well it turned out, our fate could have been entirely different.
91. 　If it had turned out in a way that not only ruined Kusanagi Ken'ichirou's work but the building itself, expulsion wouldn't have been the end of it.
92. 　It was a dangerous bridge to cross.\n
93. But if we could complete this work to perfection, we had every prospect of winning.\n
94. The possibility that a crime deserving punishment could be turned into an honor deserving praise.
95. 　That day.
96. 　Almost the moment we finished, the security guard found us.\n
97. Probably because we made such a racket when we completed it.
98. 　If we had crept quietly away after finishing, we might not have gotten caught.
99. 　But once we'd finished that piece, sneaking off was unthinkable.\n
100. When it was done, we made a huge commotion.
101. 　Naturally, the guard came running.
102. 　We had trespassed after hours and, on top of that, painted an enormous "graffiti" on the church wall.
103. 　It was a massive uproar.\n
104. At first it seemed like the police were about to be called in.
105. 　Despite the early hour, the teachers were summoned and came in droves.\n
106. It was as though a scandal unprecedented in the school's history had broken out.
107. 　Ai was among them, of course.
108. 　While the other teachers panicked, shouted, and made a fuss,\n
109. Ai simply stood looking at the cherry blossom painting Kusanagi Ken'ichirou had left behind.
110. 　On and on, amid all that clamor, calm as though she were a visitor in a museum, she gazed at that painting.
111. 　After taking her time to appreciate it,\n
112. Ai looked over at me and smiled... and then,
113. 　...I got hit.
114. 　A little later, the Akashi sisters arrived.
115. 　They were crying.
116. 　They'd seen the scene from the hospital and rushed over.\n
117. Which figures.
118. 　That hospital has the best seat in the house.\n
119. They must have been startled beyond words.
120. 　A cherry blossom painting that hadn't been there yesterday had suddenly appeared.
121. "Well? Was he happy?"
122. "H-happy, you say?\n
123. Y-you idiot."
124. "Nn."
125. "Huh?"
126. 　Kosachi, who had seemed to be the one who slapped Akashi's cheek, looked startled.
127. 　Because the one who had cut in front of Kosachi and struck Akashi's cheek was Komaki.
128. "...Komaki."
129. "Do you really think this kind of thing is allowed... Onii-chan?"
130. "I couldn't care less whether it's allowed or not...\n
131. What I'm interested in is whether the priest was happy... and--"
132. "Whether the two of you were happy."
133. "...Nn.\n
134. Yes, the priest was happy...\n
135. And... we were happy too..."
136. "I see. Did it manage, even a little, to evoke the thousand-year cherry that you saw?"
137. "Yes, of course."
138. 　Komaki, still crying, smiled brightly.
139. "The thousand-year cherry..."
140. 　The phantom cherry that Akashi Komaki said she saw one summer's night.
141. 　A cherry said to bloom beautifully in any season.
142. 　Whether this piece takes that cherry as its motif, I don't know...
143. 　But there is certainly something in it that draws people in.
144. 　Probably, that isn't the power of Kusanagi Ken'ichirou alone.
145. 　True, it's a work that could only exist because of his genius vision and artistic sense.
146. 　But it wasn't Kusanagi Ken'ichirou who saw it through to completion.
147. 　What draws people to this cherry blossom painting is Akashi's feelings.\n
148. Feelings for Komaki, for Kosachi, and for the priest.
149. 　And it's only because of the passion of these guys, who set out to complete such a work, that it exists at all...
150. 　There's a saying, "to make the Buddha but forget the soul" -- and even if this design was Kusanagi Ken'ichirou's, if the ones who completed it hadn't poured their souls into it, it never would have become the kind of work that draws people in like this.
151. 　The passion of everyone here.\n
152. And their skill -- together, they gave form to Kusanagi Ken'ichirou's final work.
153. 　That is precisely why this work is...
154. "It's wonderful... truly..."
155. "Now then, who was the ringleader!\n
156. You lot are the art club, aren't you! Who's the club president! Toritani?"
157. "Ugh, quit your incessant chirping!\n
158. Won't you even let a man bask in the emotion for a moment? A work of this caliber has just been born!\n
159. Truly, teachers are the most tasteless bunch there is!"
160. "It was me! The responsibility for all of this lies with me, Akashi Wataru.\n
161. I was the one who deceived these kids!\n
162. Well then, take me away!"
163. "Are you an idiot? You think it ends with that! All of you, come with me!"
164. "O-ow..."
165. "They've got nothing to do with it! Don't manhandle them!"
166. "These children won't resist. Please refrain from handling them roughly."
167. "Natsume-sensei..."
168. "I am the advisor of the art club. This is my responsibility as well.\n
169. I will accept the punishment..."
170. "H-hold on a moment. Ai-sensei, you're the advisor in name only, aren't you...? Everyone knows the real advisor is Wakata-sensei...\n
171. Anyway, why isn't Wakata-sensei here?"
172. "That man is no good in the morning.\n
173. I doubt he'd answer the phone on a day off like this."
174. "That good-for-nothing..."
175. 　Only Akashi being taken away...\n
176. Things weren't going to work out so conveniently.
177. 　Everyone who had been there was called into a separate classroom and grilled with questions, like an interrogation.
178. 　That interrogation seemed like it would drag on a long while, but it ended quickly.
179. 　Triggered by a single remark of mine.
180. "What were you thinking!"
181. "What was I thinking? I don't think you'd understand even if I told you."
182. "'You'? What do you take a teacher for, punk?"
183. "Who knows. At least I've figured out that you're clueless enough to feel nothing looking at that painting..."
184. "You little--! Talking to a teacher like that! D-don't think you'll get off cheap!"
185. "Cheap? Are you an idiot? Does that look cheap to you?\n
186. Its market value is in the millions of yen. No -- as of now, it could easily fetch tens of millions."
187. "Wh-what are you talking about?"
188. "You'll be showing this to the highest-ranking person in the school, right?\n

=== lines=192 stop_reason=end_turn in=8291 out=6529 cache_read=3750 cache_create=0 last_entry_continued=0 ===
1. [Naoya] "Phew...... Good grief."
2. 　The girls gathering at the school gate after class seem to grow more menacing by the day.
3. 　Fame has its ups and downs...... though come to think of it,\nI don't feel like I've been enjoying any of the ups.
4. 　Once I'd confirmed I'd shaken them off completely, I headed for Chimera.
5. 　If I got tailed and ended up causing trouble for the Natsume household, that would be bad......\nWell, causing trouble for Chimera wouldn't be great either.
6. 　Chimera had been a haunt of mine even back when I was living alone,\nbut lately, it seemed to have become my regular refuge.
7. [Man in a Suit] "Well then, I'll be back. Ah, here's my card."
8. [Makoto] "No. No matter how many times you come, I have no intention of selling it."
9. [Man in a Suit] "Now, now, you might change your mind, right?\nWell then, sorry for the intrusion."
10. 　A middle-aged man emerged from Chimera and forced his business card\ninto the hand of Toritani, dressed in her maid outfit, before leaving.
11. 　Toritani looked as though she'd bitten into something bitter,\nbut the man in the suit showed no remorse at all.
12. 　......Some kind of solicitation?\nNo, Toritani said something about "selling."
13. [Makoto] "Oh my."
14. 　Toritani noticed me. I gave her a light wave.
15. 　It would be a hassle if that group of girls found me,\nso for now, I decided to go inside Chimera.
16. [Naoya] "What was that about? That old man."
17. [Makoto] "Ah, he's some antique dealer. He saw the photo in the catalogue and said he absolutely wants the remaining vases."
18. [Naoya] "Remaining vases? Ah, come to think of it, you did say you made four."
19. [Makoto] "Right. I used Mucha's Four Stars as the motif, so four vases."
20. 　Even someone with no interest in art would look at his works\nand think, ah, that one.
21. 　Alphonse Mucha, hailed as "the darling of the Belle Epoque."\nHis body of work is deeply tied to the Art Nouveau style.
22. 　"The Four Stars," with the "Evening Star," the "Moon," the "Morning Star," and the "Polar Star" as its motifs, was the last series of decorative panels Mucha produced.
23. [Naoya] "Mucha, huh......"
24. [Makoto] "What?"
25. [Naoya] "Lalique, Galle, and Daum too, wasn't it? You said you were interested, but do you really like Art Nouveau that much?"
26. [Makoto] "Hmm, not particularly......"
27. [Makoto] "I'm interested for sure, but as for what I actually like, I prefer heavier, more voluminous things, chunkier pieces......"
28. [Makoto] "Works from that period are relatively easy to imitate. Actually they aren't, but they trick you into thinking\nyou could imitate them, you know......"
29. [Makoto] "On the upper half of the vase, I put a faint female figure and floral openwork pattern using a hotarude-like technique. It was partly to disguise the fact that I'd made the lower half double-layered."
30. [Makoto] "Well, it's only 'like' hotarude, nowhere near the real thing......\nBy the way, proper hotarude is done in porcelain, but I tried using stoneware clay."
31. [Naoya] "Hold on a second. What's hotarude?"
32. [Makoto] "Ah. I got the impression you knew everything when it came to art,\nso I thought I could skip that kind of explanation......"
33. [Naoya] "I know roughly, but I'd like to hear the details this time."
34. [Makoto] "I see, then let me start over. Hotarude is a technique where you take a piece with openwork carving, bisque-fire it, then fill the openwork holes with a transparent glaze and fire it again."
35. [Makoto] "The openwork parts become translucent and glow when light passes through them,\nand because it looks like the light of fireflies, it came to be called hotarude, or 'firefly hand.'"
36. [Makoto] "There are various theories about the origin of the technique,\nbut Ming dynasty ceramics are probably the most famous examples."
37. [Makoto] "Incidentally, quite exquisite pieces were also made in Japan during the Meiji era, and they were exhibited at the 1893 Chicago World's Fair to great acclaim."
38. [Makoto] "So, for that translucent openwork pattern, I used Mucha's Four Stars."
39. [Makoto] "I wanted to reproduce them as faithfully as possible,\nbut my technique wasn't good enough, so I made some adjustments."
40. [Makoto] "The one featured in the catalogue with a photo was based on the 'Moon,'\nso the openwork depicts the female silhouette and the flowers used in the decorative frame."
41. [Naoya] "A faint female silhouette rising up, plus that wind-flute mechanism......\nNo wonder it became a talking point."
42. [Makoto] "Ugh...... I did think it might be fun if it surprised people a little.\nBut it's a completely different story if that ends up driving the price up."
43. [Naoya] "That reminds me, about those vases -- I get it's frustrating that they were bought under false pretenses, but if you hate it that much, why not buy them back?"
44. [Makoto] "Well...... I'd love to buy them back, but......\nWith all that history attached, the price has gotten a bit out of reach."
45. [Naoya] "That much?"
46. [Makoto] "Want to see?"
47. 　Toritani opened the laptop she kept behind the counter\nand showed me an antique-related website.
48. [Makoto] "See, quite the price, isn't it?"
49. [Naoya] "Yeah......"
50. 　Even as a reference price, it was genuinely high. A truly startling figure.
51. [Naoya] "Pieces with a story always have an allure to them......"
52. 　But a price this high couldn't be explained by the story alone.
53. 　Toritani insists she was only imitating, that it's a failed work......
54. 　But the affinity between the chosen motif and the hotarude technique, the sheer level of finish --\nthe appeal of the vase itself couldn't be denied either.
55. [Part-timer] "Well then, Makoto-san, I'll be heading off."
56. [Makoto] "Yes, good work. Thanks for staying so late as always."
57. [Part-timer] "Not at all~ See you, see you."
58. 　Just past ten at night,\nthe part-timer on the evening shift clocked out.
59. [Naoya] "A little early today, huh?"
60. [Makoto] "She said she's going to karaoke with friends."
61. [Naoya] "At this hour?"
62. [Makoto] "Because it's this hour, isn't it?"
63. [Naoya] "Is that how it works......"
64. 　Depending on the day of the week, there'd be customers at night too,\nbut today Chimera was quiet.
65. 　......Or so I was thinking when a customer came in.
66. 　One of the regulars.
67. [Makoto] "Welcome, please take any seat you like."
68. [Regular A] "Y-Yes."
69. 　The woman smiled at Toritani and took a seat.\nI observed the customer as discreetly as I could.
70. 　......She really does look alike.
71. [Naoya] "......Toritani."
72. 　I approached Toritani as she brewed the coffee\nand spoke to her in a low voice across the counter.
73. [Makoto] "What?"
74. [Naoya] "Do you know that woman's name?"
75. [Makoto] "Her name? No, I don't."
76. [Naoya] "I see."
77. [Naoya] "Hey, Toritani, could you give me some candy? I'll pay for it."
78. [Makoto] "That's fine, but...... you...... you aren't sticking your nose into something weird again, are you?"
79. [Naoya] "Nothing weird, and nothing to feel guilty about."
80. [Makoto] "Liar. You've got a frown between your brows."
81. [Naoya] "N-No I don't."
82. 　As we talked, I glanced over at the woman.
83. 　She had a hand pressed lightly to her chest and had been staring down the whole time.\nOccasionally she'd lift her head...... and her expression was pained.
84. [Makoto] "Haah...... Honestly, candy's fine.\nWait a moment, I'll deliver the coffee first."
85. 　Toritani set the finished coffee on a tray and carried it over to the woman.
86. [Makoto] "Sorry to keep you waiting."
87. [Regular A] "A-Ah, thank you......"
88. 　The woman smiled at Toritani, but the smile was stiff.
89. 　Toritani must have noticed something was off with her too,\nbecause she gave her a concerned look.
90. 　......Looking at that woman, there's a face she reminds me of.
91. 　Their expressions are so different that at first glance they don't resemble each other at all......\nAnd yet, they really do.
92. 　It was......
93. [Makoto] "......Please take your time."
94. 　With those kind words, Toritani turned to leave.
95. [Regular A] "U-Um, Toritani-san."
96. [Makoto] "......Yes?"
97. 　Clatter.
98. [Regular A] "Ngh!!"
99. [Makoto] "Ah."
100. 　The woman had half-risen, extending her hand,\nand her hand struck the coffee cup, knocking it over.
101. [Regular A] "Ah, aah...... I'm sorry......!"
102. [Makoto] "It's all right, did any of the coffee splash on you?\nKusanagi, sorry, could you grab that cloth over there --"
103. [Regular A] "I-I'm sorry...... I'm sorry...... ugh......"
104. 　The woman staggered away from the chair\nand sank weakly to the floor, covering her face.
105. [Makoto] "......Ma'am."
106. [Regular A] "I'm sorry...... I always...... really......"
107. 　Toritani quietly approached the woman as her shoulders trembled,\nand gently touched her shoulder so as not to startle her.
108. [Makoto] "It's all right...... Let's fix you another drink at the counter."
109. [Regular A] "But I...... a person like me, Toritani-san......\nI don't deserve to have you be kind to me......"
110. [Makoto] "Ma'am......"
111. 　I picked up the cloth and walked over to the two of them.\nI wiped the coffee off the table, then checked to see if any had spilled on the floor.
112. [Naoya] "Look, working till this hour is one thing,\nbut isn't there a daughter at home waiting for you?"
113. [Regular A] "T-That......"
114. [Regular A] "A-Ah...... that's right, we did talk a little, didn't we......\nI do have a daughter, but......"
115. [Naoya] "Haven't you been taking home candy a few times recently?"
116. [Regular A] "Wh-Why do you know that......?"
117. [Naoya] "A daughter with a leg injury, who needs rehabilitation."
118. [Regular A] "Wh-What...... huh, how......"
119. 　Toritani frowned and folded her arms.\nHer expression said, see, you are sticking your nose into something weird.
120. [Naoya] "Just as I thought...... I had a feeling."
121. 　Flustered, the woman fell into a mild panic at my remark.
122. [Naoya] "You're Onda Nei's mother. And also,"
123. [Naoya] "you're Kei's mother."
124. 　The woman visibly shook.\nBeside her, Toritani rose to her feet with a stunned expression.
125. [Makoto] "H-How......"
126. 　Toritani looked back and forth between the woman and me, still shocked, and continued.
127. [Makoto] "Why do you, Kusanagi, know that this person is Kei's mother?"
128. 　The woman stared at Toritani in shock.\nBut I wasn't surprised.
129. [Regular A] "H-How...... did you know......"
130. [Naoya] "......So you noticed too."
131. [Makoto] "Yes...... well, of course."
132. [Regular A] "U-Um, uh......"
133. 　Completely bewildered, the woman looked back and forth between us.\nClearly she couldn't grasp the situation at all.
134. 　Toritani nodded and let out a long sigh.
135. [Makoto] "For now, let's talk over at the counter.\n......This will probably take a while, so I'll close the shop for today."
136. 　After closing up, Toritani came back to the counter.\nIn the meantime, I brewed the coffee.
137. [Naoya] "Sorry, I helped myself to your equipment."
138. [Makoto] "I don't mind...... it smells good."
139. 　The woman customer...... or should I say Nei's mother. Or Kei's mother?
140. 　With a completely deflated expression, she huddled her already thin body\nsmall in the counter seat.
141. [Makoto] "First, may I ask your name?"
142. [Naoya] "Wait, you don't know?"
143. [Makoto] "I don't know."
144. [Naoya] "Well, I figured you realized she was Kei's mother\nbecause you'd met her long ago or something."
145. [Makoto] "No. The first time I met her was before...... when I was handing out flyers. So I wanted to know too."
146. [Kirino] "......Kirino,"
147. [Kirino] "My name is Onda Kirino......"
148. 　She gave her name in a mumbling voice, almost fading away.
149. 　Ever since, she'd kept a hand pressed lightly to her thin chest,\nwith a look on her face like she was at the end of her rope, about to burst into tears.
150. [Makoto] "Onda-san, please tell us -- start wherever you can."
151. 　Head bowed, Kirino-san began to speak, haltingly.
152. 　First, she reaffirmed that she was Nei's mother,\nand also Kei's mother.
153. 　When she was still in her teens, she was taken advantage of by Nakamura Shouichi, the principal's ex-husband and current head of the Nakamura family, and gave birth to Kei.
154. 　The reason Toritani had never met Kirino-san was that after Kei's birth,\nshe'd been paid off and forced to leave the city of Yumihari.
155. [Makoto] "So that's why we'd never met even once."
156. [Kirino] "Yes. I even signed a document forbidding me from approaching the Nakamura family. Parting with Kei was truly painful. But looking back now...... it was for the best."
157. [Kirino] "The company I worked at as a clerk was under the Nakamura family's umbrella."
158. [Kirino] "If I refused, I would lose my job...... more than that, I wouldn't be able to survive......\nI was so terrified that I just accepted whatever they said, and gave birth to Kei......"
159. [Kirino] "Of course I loved Kei. But I had no idea what to do from there on,\nand I was at a total loss, holding that newborn child......"
160. [Kirino] "It was that lady...... Nakamura's wife who was truly kind to me at that time."
161. 　Nakamura's wife...... she means the principal.
162. [Kirino] "The document was strict, and although the money they gave me\nwas called a settlement, she also arranged housing and a job for me......\nI could never thank her enough."
163. [Naoya] "So why, then, did you come back to Yumihari now?"
164. [Kirino] "......I'm sorry."
165. 　Kirino-san bowed her head and clutched her skirt tightly with both hands.
166. [Kirino] "Over ten years passed...... things happened at the place where I'd settled, and I had to leave there too...... and then I found myself missing my hometown."
167. [Kirino] "Of course, I never wanted to cause trouble for Kei or Madam.\nSo I made absolutely sure not to go near them."
168. [Kirino] "I gave birth to Nei...... it wasn't an easy life by any means,\nbut I thought the two of us would live our best, mother and daughter."
169. [Kirino] "And yet......"
170. 　With a grief-stricken look, Kirino-san's lips trembled.
171. [Naoya] "Nei was injured, wasn't she."
172. 　Kirino-san nodded silently.
173. 　Nei's injury was caused by a fight between children.\n"Fight" might sound better, but it was really bullying and assault.
174. 　As a result, Nei sustained an injury serious enough to require rehabilitation.\nFor a mother-daughter household with no other family, it was a heavy blow both emotionally and financially.
175. 　Still, if she went through rehabilitation, there was a chance she could walk again.\nKirino-san began working even harder than before.
176. 　But that wasn't the end of it.
177. 　The parents of the child who injured Nei began to threaten Kirino-san.
178. [Makoto] "Wait, threaten...... why......?"
179. [Kirino] "Their child was also hurt just a little...... only a scratch, really, but a wound nonetheless.\nAnd they claimed it was our fault......"
180. 　Beyond mere reverse blame, the parents of the child who'd hurt Nei\nmade utterly unreasonable demands of Kirino-san.
181. 　She was intimidated and asked for a large sum of money -- money she of course couldn't pay.
182. [Kirino] "Even knowing it was empty threats, being surrounded and shouted at by a group of men,\nI was just terrified...... with no family, no one to help me......\nWhen I said I'd go to the police, they said things would only get worse......"
183. 　Kirino-san covered her face.
184. [Kirino] "I really didn't know what to do......"
185. [Makoto] "Kirino-san......"
186. 　At Toritani's voice, Kirino-san lifted her head and gave a weak smile.
187. [Kirino] "That was when I met you, Toritani-san. I was so tired...... so truly tired I couldn't even think of dying, just staring blankly at the town...... it was then."
188. [Makoto] "Yes. I had a really bad feeling and I couldn't just leave you alone."
189. [Kirino] "Since then, this shop and a single cup of coffee have been my only comfort. I can never thank you enough...... you're the second savior of my life."
190. [Kirino] "And yet...... I...... I can't do it. Deceiving you like this......"
191. [Makoto] "Deceiving? Me?"
192. [Kirino] "Yes...... it was a little while ago......"

