1. If it be a void, then the void itself is thus,
2. and to some degree common to us all,
3. for as all in me are everyone,
4. so am I everything in each of them.
5. --Preface to "Spring and Asura"
6. 　I simply watch the cherry blossoms falling.
7. 　In that scene there was neither sorrow nor pain.
8. 　Was that, perhaps, why I did not weep?
9. 　The pale crimson dancing on the wind was indeed beautiful, but to me it felt no more than that.
10. 　A coffin borne carefully by human hands through a cloying scent of cherry blossoms--no, cherry blossoms have no such potent fragrance.
11. 　My father's corpse, cradled within the beauty of the cherry blossoms,\n
12. was simply, purely, ugly.
13. 　It could not even serve to set off the beauty of the cherry blossoms.\n
14. My father's corpse, packed into a wooden box.
15. 　What sort of world did he see, gazing out from that wooden box?
16. 　The end of his world.
17. 　The vanishing point of the world he believed in.
18. 　The cherry-blossom grove.
19. 　The wind blows.
20. 　The blossoms dance.
21. 　Staining the spring sky pale crimson.
22. 　The sight is beautiful.
23. 　My vision blurred by pale crimson petals raining down over the funeral hall, I saw off the coffin of my father, my only living kin.
24. 　With the close of the small ceremony attended only by those involved, a period was, provisionally, placed at the end of his life.
25. 　Standing now in the cherry-blossom grove, no tears fall from my eyes.
26. 　And I find no trace, either, of the feeling called sorrow.
27. 　Nowhere in this scene can I find sorrow.
28. 　What about the people around me?\n
29. Are they, in fact, saddened?\n
30. Do their hearts ache?
31. 　But... gazing at that corpse beneath the falling blossoms, there is something I cannot help but recall, whether I want to or not.
32. 　A single crack races through my heart for an instant.\n
33. The vision of the cherry tree my father painted...
34. 　My father left countless works to this world, and yet\n
35. that man painted only one cherry tree.
36. 　And yet, that one painting was called his masterpiece.
37. 　Not any of the other endless subjects, but that single work was what made him an immovable artist.
38. 　--The Reclining Sakura.
39. 　True to its bizarre title, the painting depicted\n
40. a cherry tree twisted and crawling flat upon the ground.
41. 　It called to mind, absolutely, the image of the dead.
42. 　A cherry tree upon its deathbed--\n
43. scattering white blossoms across a dim darkness.
44. 　Of course, there is no such bizarre cherry tree here.\n
45. There is only the utterly familiar scenery of spring.
46. 　And yet, the cherry-blossom grove that framed that man's death seemed,\n
47. as though cursed by "The Reclining Sakura" itself, to be scattering forth an eerie pale sakura.
48. 　Aside from "The Reclining Sakura," that man never released another painting of a cherry tree.
49. 　Even so, the general image of him was one of cherry blossoms, and he himself did indeed love the cherry.
50. 　The critics say:
51. 　A beautiful world at a standstill. The instant of death.
52. 　The ancient cherry, immense, shedding its pale blossoms, is like a corpse giving off the stench of rot, and like a transparent, eternal death,
53. 　an image of death itself, at once beautiful and ugly.
54. 　I've come across writing of that sort more than once in magazines.
55. 　If the great and clever people called critics say so, then it's probably true.
56. 　I don't really understand it,\n
57. and I don't much want to.
58. 　But there are things even I can understand.
59. 　The scene here, in this place, right now.
60. 　In his corpse, borne beneath this grove where cherry-pink flutters and falls, there is no such complex aspect.
61. 　There is only the most obvious of contrasts.
62. 　The beautiful cherry blossoms and an ugly corpse.\n
63. A powerful fragrance in full bloom, and a corpse pickled in preservatives to kill the stench of decay.
64. 　Beneath the cherry-blossom grove.\n
65. Now, I recall that painting.\n
66. And I think about it, just a little.
67. 　About the scene from here, and about that man's painting.\n
68. But not because they are the same.\n
69. It is because they are decisively different that the one calls up the other.
70. 　That was the conclusion I reached.
71. 　The death of a man who had painted extraordinary cherries closed its curtain with an utterly ordinary funeral.
72. 　Perhaps thinking so was nothing more than a childish delusion--that this would enact the most humiliating revenge upon Kusanagi Ken'ichirou...
73. 　In truth, I don't really know about that either.
74. 　I don't really know, but--
75. 　Either way, this was the day the last person in the world with whom I shared blood vanished from it.
76. 　That much alone was certain.
77. "It's over, Naoya..."
78. "Yeah. It's over..."
79. "That must have been hard on you..."
80. "No, not really..."
81. "This is the second family funeral for me. Nothing all that hard about it..."
82. "Naoya..."
83. 　A face like a young girl's peered into mine with a sorrowful expression.
84. 　Moist eyes... translucent skin.
85. 　Any man who didn't know better, gazed at by such a face wearing such an expression, might well find himself unable to keep his composure.
86. 　But once one learns the true sex of the owner of that beautiful face, and if one is another male, I think one is filled with a rather different emotion.
87. 　Naturally, this creature is nothing so quaint as a young girl.
88. 　His name is Natsume Kei. Male.
89. "Kei, don't look at me with that face..."
90. "Naoya... what's wrong?"
91. "It's a little... making me sick..."
92. "What is it? Are you feeling ill, Naoya?"
93. "Yeah... maybe I am..."
94. 　I look up at the sky... and gaze at the falling cherry blossoms.
95. "That was a lie, actually..."
96. "It was?"
97. "I couldn't bring myself to tell you the truth, Kei..."
98. "T-the truth? What truth, Naoya?"
99. "No matter how you slice it, I couldn't say to your face that your face is gross..."
100. "Wh-what did you say!"
101. "'When a face that gross peers into mine, of course it's going to make me sick'--I could never say a thing like that..."
102. "Waaah! What did you say! My face isn't gross at all! That's just cruel, out of nowhere!"
103. "Wh-why do you know what I was thinking?!"
104. "Ah! Damn it! I was trying to keep it in the monologue so I wouldn't hurt your feelings, Kei! Aah, that must be it! Kei was so incredibly disgusting that the voice in my heart leaked out into the open air (in monotone)."
105. "What is that even supposed to mean! Calling me disgusting is going too far! Take it back! Take it back! Take it back or, waaaah, I'll kill youuu!"
106. "Ahahahahaha, don't cling on to me! You disgusting creature, I'll kill you first!"
107. "What did you say! You're the one who's going to be killed! Waaah! Every word out of your mouth pisses me off! I'm not clinging! I'm punching you! Take this!"
108. "Wh-whoa... w-wait..."
109. "Ouch, ouch... you're a dangerous one..."
110. "It's because Naoya makes fun of me... calling people gross, gross, over and over!"
111. "But... I mean..."
112. 　My eyes meet his.
113. "Ahahahahahaha!"
114. "Why are you laughing!"
115. "Ahahahahahahahahahaha!"
116. "Waaah! What's that! I can't take this anymore!"
117. 　Damn it.\n
118. Of all people, I let my emotions slip out and burst into laughter.
119. "Ahahahahaha! W-wait! There's a reason for this! Uhahahaha!"
120. "What sort of reason! Laughing right in someone's face!"
121. "R-right, that. They say laughter and fear are two sides of the same coin, don't they?"
122. "Wh-what do you mean? What is that?"
123. "N-no, listen, you came at me, and I got scared, see..."
124. "I came at you? Scared?"
125. "Yeah, I was so terrified I ended up laughing...\n
126. Because you were so terrifying."
127. "R-really? W-was I really that scary? That terrifying? R-really? Sorry about that."
128. 　For s-o-m-e r-e-a-s-o-n Natsume Kei is laughing happily.
129. 　The fact of having "terrified" someone seemed to fill him with satisfaction.
130. "Right? When a chivalrous man like me really turns on the menace, of course it's going to scare people."
131. "A chivalrous man..."
132. 　Natsume Kei, going so far as to deploy tautology in order to emphasize his own "manhood."
133. 　I felt a small pang of pity for him.
134. 　The creature called Natsume Kei looks like nothing but a girl.
135. 　But the man himself refuses utterly to accept that fact.
136. 　Kei is a man who cannot see reality...
137. 　Watching him like this...\n
138. it was so sad, that I...
139. "Ahahahahahahahahaha!"
140. "Don't laugh like thaaat... Naoya, I'm not that scary. I'm nice to you, aren't I? Always."
141. "Ahahahahahahahahahahahahaha!"
142. "It's true, I do look pretty tough, and I really am tough..."
143. "Uhahahahahahahahahahahaha uhahahahahaha!"
144. "...Naoya."
145. "Wahahahahahahahahahahahahahahahahahahahahaha!"
146. "Hey... you're lying, aren't you?"
147. "On what grounds?"
148. 　In all things, going too far is as bad as not going far enough.\n
149. Mustn't overdo it.
150. 　I return to my usual, calm self.
151. "As if I would lie... pfft."
152. "What's that snort for! I knew it, Naoya, you're making fun of me!"
153. Something about his eyes made me snort.
154. "Waaah! I'll kill you! I'll kill you! I mean it this time, I'll kill you! Deeper than the sea! Higher than the mountains! Vaster than the sky, I'll kill you!"
155. "Ahahahaha! It tickles! It tickles! Ahahahahaha!"
156. "Tickles, my ass! I'm punching you!"
157. "Ahahahahahaha! Stop! Please, stop!"
158. "Nyaa!"
159. "Whew, whew, I thought I was going to die laughing..."
160. "Naoya... that hurt. My head's throbbing..."
161. "Yeah... well. I did hit you hard."
162. "Why did you hit me. That hurt..."
163. "Hey, you attacked me first."
164. "M-my attack was made of love! S-so it doesn't hurt!"
165. "Ahahahahaha, that's because you're weak. You girly bastard."
166. "Waaah! That's cruel! Calling me girly is going too far! You jerk! Don't make fun of me just because I don't have hair on my shins!"
167. "Th-that's not what I was..."
168. "Waah!"
169. "Haa, haa, haa, phew..."
170. "Haa, haa, haa..."
171. 　A faint chill still lingered on the wind. An afternoon in April.
172. 　Beneath the falling cherry blossoms, Kei and I lay stretched out on the ground.
173. 　The sky was visible.
