128. "This should do it..."
129. 　It was something my old man had given me when I was a kid.
130. 　A pretty large, sturdy easel -- not something a kid could really use, but even so, as a child I used it, stretching to reach.
131. 　Along with that mysterious cast...
132. "Ugh... just remembering it pisses me off..."
133. 　Almost every memory involving Ken'ichirou is one that pisses me off.
134. 　Even so...
135. "Looking at it now, it's not really all that big..."
136. 　When I actually try using it, it's just the right size.
137. "Ah..."
138. 　When I pulled the easel out and slung it over my shoulder, the other easels that had been stacked up all collapsed at once.\n
139. Sunlight streaming in through the window lit up the dust swirling in the air.
140. "Ahaha... damn it... I really should have waited and done this on a day when the art club members were around..."
141. "That said..."
142. 　After a few days, I'll forget just about any favor I've been asked to do.
143. 　When I'm asked to do something, I can't rest easy until I get it out of the way...
144. "Spilled water doesn't return to the tray..."
145. 　The easels that have fallen won't go back the way they were...
146. 　"Like hell they won't!"
147. 　There's no one here to shoot back a retort like that, so I make my swift escape.
148. 　Each time the wind brushes against my skin,
149. 　the rows of cherry trees stir quietly, scattering fragments of white peach.

=== lines=42 stop_reason=end_turn in=3085 out=898 cache_read=3222 cache_create=0 last_entry_continued=0 ===
1. "It really is heavy..."
2. 　This easel, being as sturdy as it is, is unbelievably heavy.
3. "Whew..."
4. 　My feet came to a stop of their own accord.
5. 　As though the fluttering petals were blocking my path.
6. 　In that moment--.
7. 　A sudden gust that felt like it was passing right through my body swept down Yume-no-Ukisaka.
8. 　Born in this town, I've climbed this slope of Yume-no-Uki hundreds -- no, thousands of times.
9. 　But it felt different from any of those times.
10. 　The melody of the cherry blossoms.
11. 　As if resonating with something, every cherry tree was stirring at once--
12. 　A strange, overlapping resonance of sound.
13. 　As if to avert my eyes from the swirling cherry blossoms, I covered my face with one arm.
14. 　And then--.
15. 　The wind stops.
16. 　The rustling of the grove vanishes, and for an instant, silence takes hold of the world.
17. 　But I realize at once that it's an illusion.
18. 　The dignified rustling of the trees, the distant clamor of the town -- everything is erased,
19. 　as if only the colors dancing through my field of vision were being drawn out in sharp relief.
20. 　A silence that exists within sound.
21. 　As if beckoned, I lift my face.
22. 　My concrete-paved field of vision opens wide.\n
23. Slowly, slowly.
24. 　A girl...?
25. 　Who... is that?
26. 　Have I met her somewhere before...?
27. 　Skin like white porcelain, scarcely bearing any pigment. Eyes and nose so finely shaped they were clear even from a distance.
28. 　And -- lips pressed tightly closed, as if to stifle every emotion.
29. 　Her downcast expression lends her a somehow cold impression...
30. 　The girl's gaze extends straight toward me.
31. 　The moment I saw those eyes.
32. 　I nearly spoke her name aloud.
33. 　But--
34. 　The sound of the wind.
35. 　The balance of sound breaks, and my vision is dyed white with cherry blossoms.
36. 　I lose sight of everything in the rising wind.
37. 　And then--.
38. "She's gone..."
39. 　As if.
40. 　Into a dream the cherry blossoms were dreaming--
41. 　she had been spirited away--.
42. 　Once again, the hill of Yumihari regains its stillness.

=== lines=148 stop_reason=end_turn in=5087 out=3428 cache_read=3222 cache_create=0 last_entry_continued=0 ===
1. "Phew, that's a bit tidier now..."
2. "I thought this was a fairly spacious apartment, but...\n
3. once even a little of my old man's stuff comes in, it gets pretty cramped..."
4. I set the easel I brought back yesterday down in the living room.
5. Of course, that alone doesn't make the room feel smaller.
6. Beyond that, for whatever reason, Kusanagi Ken'ichirou-related objects keep gathering in this room.
7. Even though I've been stripped of my inheritance rights, more and more junk keeps getting sent over without my permission.
8. The people involved probably don't know I refused the inheritance, so they just keep shipping things to this apartment.
9. What a nuisance.
10. If they're sending things by mistake, at least let it be cash!
11. "I wish it were cash..."
12. I hug the mysterious little package that just arrived and mutter to myself.
13. I hadn't opened it yet, so the contents were unknown, but it was definitely not cash.
14. It was labeled "Refrigerate," after all...
15. "Refrigerate, huh..."
16. When I opened it, for some reason, out came pork prepped for ginger stir-fry.
17. "So Kusanagi Ken'ichirou's estate includes stuff like this too..."
18. For now, I toss it into the fridge.
19. Anything other than cash has no value.
20. No, of course groceries have their own value, but...\n
21. it's a different story when they've become part of Kusanagi Ken'ichirou's estate.
22. After a heavy sigh, I gaze at the easel I hauled off from the art room yesterday.
23. If I sold something like this, it'd probably fetch a decent price...
24. It has to be worth something, after all...
25. As I mused on such things, I suddenly remembered what happened yesterday.
26. When I took this easel and headed down the school hill.
27. I saw a strange sight.
28. The cherry-blossom grove stirred.
29. The moment I thought that, a girl appeared, and then vanished along with the wind...
30. As if she had been spirited away by the cherry blossoms...
31. "It was a hallucination or a ghost or a UFO, probably."
32. Because it's too much trouble, I'll settle it that way.
33. Apparently the human brain burns calories at the highest rate of any animal.
34. The brain is said to make up about two percent of a person's total body weight.
35. And yet its calorie consumption is ten times that -- close to twenty percent!
36. In other words, "thinking" abnormally burns calories.
37. The act of "thinking" means getting hungry.
38. And so, even a little brain work quickly leaves me starving.
39. I'm hungry.
40. "All right then..."
41. "...things are pretty much tidied up."
42. I grab my wallet and head out the front door.
43. I've worked hard today.
44. So a little indulgence should be allowed.
45. I may be broke, but every now and then a bit of extravagance is permitted.
46. "Today, I'll splurge on a beef bowl."
47. "Now then... whose beef bowl should I have..."
48. Even after mulling it over, my feet end up carrying me to the same place after all.
49. I put my hand on the door of the coffee shop.\n
50. Beside it stands a signboard with characters brushed in India ink.
51. Cafe Chimera...
52. It's an odd place, but the food is good.\n
53. And the prices are dirt cheap.\n
54. On top of that, the menu is extensive.
55. The interior is tastefully decorated,\n
56. and attached to it is a space where the master sells\n
57. coffee and vessels he's collected from all over the world.
58. Because the master personally sources the coffee beans himself,\n
59. he's said to be out on the road almost all the time.
60. "Welcome, welco-- oh, it's just you. Kusanagi."
61. "Toritani, huh..."
62. "You again? Honestly, if it's you coming in, I want back that 'Welcome' with the bow I just did."
63. "I'm a customer. Even if we're classmates, of course a staff member should bow. \n
64. And at least finish saying 'Welcome' before demanding it back."
65. "'Customer,' huh..."
66. Her arrogance was so complete it was hard to believe she was the same person who had beamed that customer-service smile the moment I walked in.\n
67. This woman's name is Toritani Makoto.
68. She's wearing something maid-uniform-ish, but of course she isn't a professional maid.\n
69. She's my classmate at Yumihari Academy.\n
70. She works part-time at Cafe Chimera.
71. "Well, if you're actually going to pay, I'll happily play the charming waitress. But I'm not saintly enough to wait on someone who's come looking for a handout."
72. "H-Handout...? T-Today I didn't come here to beg for bread crusts.\n
73. Today I came as a proper customer."
74. "Oh, really? I thought you'd come for bread crusts again."
75. "I haven't come for those lately, have I?"
76. There was that period when I was really scraping by and this place did throw me some charity... but it wasn't that often... I think...
77. "Bread crusts are actual merchandise at most bakeries, you know.\n
78. It's you, Kusanagi, who's weird for assuming they come free."
79. "Ugh... well, I can't deny that..."
80. Perhaps I had been leaning on the fact that this was Toritani's workplace a bit too much.
81. "Well, but today you're a customer, right?\n
82. In that case, I'll give you special service. Want me to smile for you? A top-grade smile."
83. "No thanks. I don't need your heartless customer-service smile."
84. "Oh? My customer-service smile does have heart in it, though..."
85. "The moment you call it a 'customer-service smile,' the heart's already gone..."
86. "It depends on who I'm smiling at, you know...\n
87. Never mind. Hey, hey, so, what kind of service would you like to try?"
88. "Whatever service you offer is bound to be trivial. Just hand over the menu."
89. "Oh? I was thinking of hiking up my skirt in greeting for you."
90. In greeting?\n
91. Hiking up her skirt?
92. She said?
93. "Oh, did your expression just change?"
94. "No... nothing in particular... but what do you mean, 'hike up'?"
95. "You know, the self-lift thing? It's a maid-outfit classic, isn't it?"
96. "S-Since when was this the kind of shop that offered that kind of service?!"
97. "W-What? I didn't expect you to lean in like that."
98. "Ah, no, I meant nothing by it. I just thought the owner here was forcing that kind of service on his part-timers..."
99. "He isn't. Chimera is just a normal coffee shop, and the manager is a kind man.\n
100. But, well, I do cause you trouble in various ways, so I thought today I might offer you some service."
101. "Ohh... i-is that so..."
102. It's true that Toritani has caused me nothing but trouble... but I didn't think she was self-aware about it...
103. "So? What do you say?"
104. "Wh-what do you mean?"
105. "The service."
106. "Huh? S-Service, as in that skirt-lifting thing?"
107. "Yes, the skirt-hiking service."
108. "'Skirt-hiking service'..."
109. That means lifting the skirt and showing panties, right?\n
110. Even I couldn't let a classmate perform such a shameless service on--
111. "If you don't want it, I won't do it, though?"
112. "N-No, do it! I mean, plizz do it!"
113. I completely tripped over my tongue.
114. No, I mean, what's up with that?
115. If she's offering service, there's no harm in accepting, right?\n
116. It's just a service, right?
117. "...Y-You're always acting like you're half-dead from exhaustion, but now you've got so much energy.\n
118. If only you had half this vigor in your everyday life."
119. "I do not always act half-dead from exhaustion!"
120. "How nice, though. That you'd react so strongly to my service.\n
121. Well, there don't seem to be any other customers, so let's call it a once-in-a-lifetime service..."
122. "Y-Yeah..."
123. Toritani grasps the hem of her skirt.\n
124. If the hem rises any higher, the thighs hidden beneath the skirt will be bared, and further, further below, the forbidden delta zone will--
125. A soft flutter.
126. Lightly.
127. Toritani lifts the hem of her skirt just a little, and greets me with a smile.
128. "Welcome, Kusanagi Naoya-sama."
129. "Nnh..."
130. Unwittingly,\n
131. I did find it a little cute.
132. It was completely different from the offhand way she'd been treating me a moment ago.\n
133. And also subtly different from her initial smile.
134. But that's not it.
135. That's not it, is it!
136. "How was it?"
137. "'How was it,' she asks..."
138. "Oh? That was a once-in-a-lifetime service for me, you know? Didn't it strike your fancy?"
139. "No, well, if I had to say, it was good, but...\n
140. 'Hiking up'... isn't that generally called a curtsy?"
141. "Is that so? It's called a curtsy?"
142. "Well, a curtsy is a European greeting, right? It's been around for a while..."
143. "Then there's no problem, is there? What? Was it different from what you imagined, Kusanagi?"
144. "N-No... not particularly..."
145. In dictionary terms, "hiking up" doesn't necessarily mean lifting cloth until the underwear shows.\n
146. The phrase "rolling up one's sleeves" pretty much tells you all you need to know.
147. And on top of that, Toritani did say "in greeting."\n
148. The usage and the action were both perfectly correct.

