=== lines=148 stop_reason=end_turn in=6781 out=3514 cache_read=3222 cache_create=0 last_entry_continued=0 ===
1. "Nice room."
2. An utterly ordinary six-mat tatami room.
3. It was unadorned, but it had an air of cleanliness about it.
4. I had been told the room they'd given me hadn't been used in ages, but you wouldn't have guessed it -- it was that well kept.
5. It must have been a room that mattered to someone...
6. Had the upkeep been neglected, had it just been left alone, it would have been far more worn.
7. It had been maintained meticulously... as though someone could move in at any time.
8. When I threw open the window, a slightly chilly breeze drifted in.
9. But against a room warmed by the April sun, the coolness the wind carried in felt just right.
10. "It's warm."
11. "And to think, until just recently it was the dead of winter..."
12. Early April. The temperature swings in this season always catch me off guard.
13. The cold of just a few weeks ago felt like a lie against this softened sunlight.
14. I could see the mountain cherries through the window.
15. Unusually, it seemed this year the Somei-Yoshino and the mountain cherries had bloomed at the same time...
16. "It did warm up all of a sudden."
17. The contrast between the white Somei-Yoshino and the faintly vermilion-tinted mountain cherries was really quite something...
18. "Now then, this isn't the time to be admiring the view. First things first -- I have to open up these bags in front of me and get sorted."
19. I started sorting through the books in the cardboard box Kei had left half-opened.
20. "That idiot... all he did was mess up the books inside the box..."
21. "How's the unpacking going?"
22. "I just started."
23. "I'll help after all."
24. "No need. I'm almost done."
25. "Huh? Done?"
26. "Yeah, that's the end of it..."
27. "This is really all you brought?"
28. "It's only until construction on my apartment's finished."
29. What had been packed into a few cardboard boxes was hung in the closet or stood up on the bookshelf, and it was all put away in no time.
30. "All that's left is to decide where to store the drawing board leaning against the wall and my art supplies."
31. "You left all your furniture behind? Nothing else being delivered later?"
32. "I'm only borrowing this place for a few weeks or months, so this is plenty."
33. "You really did bring next to nothing... huh?\n
34. There's a photo album in here."
35. "A photo album?"
36. "Yeah. This is an album, right?"
37. Kei pulled a single volume from the corner of the bookshelf and flipped it open on the spot.
38. Naturally, the owner's permission was not part of this entirely reflexive sequence of actions.
39. The file with the documents from the recent inheritance... the paperwork I'd been left to handle when my mother died, plus various other things...
40. Ever since I'd moved into that apartment, important-looking things had piled up on a certain bookshelf.
41. I remembered dumping everything on that shelf into cardboard boxes without really checking what was there.
42. So I'd completely forgotten the album was in there.
43. "So there was a photo album in there. If I'd known, I wouldn't have brought something so unnecessary..."
44. "Don't call it unnecessary. Memories are important."
45. "Important memories, huh..."
46. "Huh, there's hardly any photos in it."
47. Flipping idly through the pages, Kei let the words slip out. The album itself was fairly thick, but only about a tenth of its pages were actually in use.
48. "Huh, it just looks like some kid-era photos slapped in at random. And the layout's all full of gaps..."
49. "That's what a photo album basically is, isn't it?"
50. "No, that's not what I mean. How do I put it? To put it bluntly -- boring?"
51. "Why is it my job to entertain you through a photo album?"
52. "You know, if you edited it around a theme, like growing up, it'd really pull the reader in."
53. "Why do I have to lay my past bare with an eye on the reader?"
54. "I mean, for my entertainment."
55. "Your face is entertainment enough."
56. "How many times -- my face isn't funny!"
57. "There's no way my photo album's going to be entertaining. Anyway, there aren't many photos because it was entrusted to me."
58. "Entrusted? But it's full of your pictures."
59. "Well, there's a lot behind it."
60. "A lot, huh."
61. "Look, look at this face."
62. "Yeah. Adults were always scolding me because I had such a nasty look in my eyes."
63. "Scolding you? Really? I always kind of pictured you picking fights with adults, you know..."
64. "It really wasn't like that..."
65. "Haha, but back when we were little, I secretly thought you were pretty cool for never flinching around adults."
66. "Like I said, it wasn't anything like that."
67. As he said this, Kei kept comparing the young me in the album with how I looked now.
68. "Oh, brings me back. Isn't this from around when we first met?"
69. "Back then you were a kid making waves at the Open Exhibition."
70. "Huh? Who's this? The girl in this photo."
71. In the right corner of the two-page spread Kei held out was a single faded photograph.
72. In it, a girl stood alone.
73. In a forest of cherry blossoms... she was smiling into the camera.
74. "What's with you... suddenly going quiet... what's the matter?\n
75. Something about this girl?"
76. "What is it? Who is this girl?"
77. "Who knows."
78. "You're just pretending not to know."
79. "What makes you think that?"
80. "Because the moment you saw the picture, you froze. Your expression froze completely. That was absolutely not the face of someone who doesn't know who it is. It was more like, 'Oh, this photo...'"
81. "Sigh... you've got a pointless knack for observation."
82. "Yeah, I remember."
83. "So who is she? Your pictures too, but this girl -- she's from when she was pretty young."
84. "Yeah, it's from when she was a kid. She used to live in my neighborhood."
85. "In the same apartment building?"
86. "No, not that. Nearby. Right next to the building."
87. "Nearby? Wasn't the lot right next to your building an empty plot?"
88. "Yeah, it may well be by now..."
89. "This girl's family name is Misakura. Her name is Rin."
90. "Huh. So where is she now? She's not at Yumihari Academy, is she?"
91. "She moved away. She's not in this town anymore."
92. "I see. That's kind of a shame."
93. "A shame? What is?"
94. "I mean, this girl is unbelievably cute."
95. "Kei, are you a lolicon?"
96. "Why does that make me a lolicon?!"
97. "Because you looked at this photo and said she was unbelievably cute."
98. "No! I mean she must be a real beauty by now!"
99. "Cute, huh."
100. I had never really thought about it, but...
101. It was true that around the time of this photo, I may have been faintly fond of Rin.
102. Though it was only "fond" in the way a kid means it.
103. A lot of time had passed since then. How the feelings between us had changed...
104. I no longer really knew.
105. "About when did she move?"
106. "It's been six, seven years now, I guess. It was a while back."
107. "Hmm. Haven't seen her at all since?"
108. "Nope. Not once."
109. "Not at all? No letters or anything?"
110. "Letters, huh..."
111. "Hm? What's up?"
112. "Ah, how do I put it. Rin sent them, but I never once sent a reply... I was just thinking about that."
113. "Why not?!"
114. "Well, that's the kind of personality I have, I guess..."
115. "What kind of personality is that? Not answering letters from a childhood friend -- you're a monster, you bastard!"
116. "Hmm. Yeah, that might be right, but I couldn't quite bring myself to send one."
117. "Hmm, I really want to meet this girl somehow."
118. "What, are you seriously that taken with Rin? Love at first sight, is it?"
119. "N-no, I don't mean it like that. I mean, she'd know all these stories about your childhood that people like me don't, right?"
120. "There are no stories like that."
121. "You know, ridiculous, hilarious stories about kid-Naoya!"
122. "If you want ridiculous and hilarious, you're still cranking those out daily."
123. "I am not!"
124. "Well, either way, she's probably already forgotten about me too."
125. "That can't be true. Memories from when you're little tend to stick around."
126. "Memories from when you're little stick around, huh..."
127. "Yeah, that's right."
128. "I suppose. There's no telling how they stick, though. Better be careful."
129. "What's with that phrasing? Did you do something bad to this girl?"
130. "Maybe I did."
131. "Ugh... you did something bad to her, then stopped answering her letters when you didn't need her anymore -- how much of a monster are you..."
132. Muttering that under his breath, Kei let his shoulders drop and turned the album's page further.
133. "Huh?"
134. "What? That's the end of the photos. There shouldn't be anything past that."
135. "No, right, but... it's not that. Why is there a cherry blossom petal pressed into every page after this girl's photo?"
136. "Cherry blossom petal?"
137. When I leaned in, I saw petals pressed into several pages beyond Rin's photograph.
138. "This is..."
139. Until this very moment, I hadn't noticed...
140. That this many cherry blossom petals had been tucked in here...
141. "Was someone trying to make pressed flowers?"
142. "No, that's not it..."
143. "Then why are the petals in there?"
144. "I don't know. Like I said, the album was entrusted to me. I don't know why the original owner pressed the petals in it."
145. "But... probably because when she opened the last page, she was under a cherry tree..."
146. "What's that mean? What are you talking about?"
147. "Never mind, it's my own business.\n
148. More importantly, give it back already. It's someone else's property to begin with -- it's not something you should just be looking through."

