100. "Why do you think that?"
101. "Because Wilde and you are too different."
102. "Is that so? I don't know much about Wilde, and I don't know his ideas in detail either. So even if you tell me that, it doesn't really click."
103. "Even so, there are some interesting coincidences."
104. "Interesting coincidences?"
105. "The Happy Prince goes like this."
106. "On the tallest column in the town, the statue of the Happy Prince stood."
107. "His whole body was covered in gilding of pure gold, two shining sapphires were his eyes, and a great ruby gleamed on the hilt of his sword."
108. "This beautiful prince was also the great pride of the townspeople."
109. "One night, a swallow who was on his way to migrate to warmer lands went to sleep at the prince's pedestal, when great drops of water fell from directly above."
110. "The startled swallow looked up, and the eyes of the Happy Prince were overflowing with tears."
111. "The Happy Prince, standing in the highest place, could see everything. So the prince was terribly sad about all the misfortune of every person happening in this town."
112. "The prince made a request of the little swallow. He asked him to give his jewels to the unfortunate people."
113. "The town had already grown cold, and the swallow needed to head south as soon as possible, but because the Happy Prince looked so terribly sad, the little swallow took pity on him."
114. "As he was asked, the swallow delivered the ruby set into the hilt of the sword to a poor mother whose small child was ill."
115. "Next the prince asked him to give one of the sapphires to a starving student playwright, and the other sapphire to a little match-selling girl."
116. "The swallow wept, saying he couldn't take away both of the prince's eyes, but moved by the prince's strong will, he reluctantly carried them to each of them."
117. "The Happy Prince said to the little swallow: You must go south now."
118. "But the swallow said: The Happy Prince no longer has eyes to see with. So I have decided to stay by your side forever."
119. "In place of the prince who could no longer see, the swallow flew about the town and told the prince what the town was like."
120. "For the sake of the many unfortunate people still remaining, the prince asked him to peel the gold leaf from his own body and share it among them."
121. "The swallow peeled the splendid gold leaf from the Happy Prince, sheet by sheet."
122. "Each time, the gold leaf passed into the hands of the poor, and little by little the streets of the town filled with smiles and laughter."
123. "But the Happy Prince, having given away everything, was at last reduced to a shabby, gray figure."
124. "As the Happy Prince became shabbier, the swallow too grew weaker and weaker from the cold."
125. "Even so, the swallow would not leave the prince's side. The swallow had come to love the prince completely."
126. "One evening, the swallow, sensing his death was near, gathered his last strength and flew up to the prince's shoulder."
127. "And the swallow said this to the prince."
128. "Farewell, dear prince. Let me kiss your hand one last time."
129. "Little swallow, I am glad. So you have finally decided to leave for the south? You have stayed here too long. But since I have come to love you, kiss me on the lips, not the hand."
130. "I am going to the house of Death. Death is the brother of Sleep."
131. "And when the swallow had kissed the Happy Prince on the lips, he fell down at the prince's feet, dead."
132. "In that instant, a strange snapping sound, as of something breaking, echoed within the statue."
133. "In truth, in that moment, his heart of lead had cracked cleanly in two. It was a bitterly, bitterly cold night."
134. "The next morning, the town's dignitaries found this shabby prince."
135. "What a shabby statue. We don't need something like this in our town."
136. "The town's dignitaries had the statue of the Happy Prince pulled down from the column and melted in the furnace."
137. "But somehow, only the prince's heart would not melt.\nThe foreman of the foundry, finding it eerie, threw it onto the heap of dust."
138. "In the heap of dust where the heart was thrown, the body of the swallow was lying too."
139. "The two had been cast away as if nestled together."
140. "God spoke to one of the angels."
141. "Bring me the two most precious things in this town."
142. "So the angel carried the leaden heart and the body of the swallow to God."
143. "God said."
144. "What you have chosen is right."
145. "In the land of eternity, these two lives will live in happiness."
146. "The end."
147. "Where in that is there anything I have in common?"
148. "I wonder where."
149. 　Rin smiled brightly.

