69. [Naoya] "Even so, that's not proof he filled a sex doll with helium and flew just to look at the church from above. It's still only a possibility."
70. [Makoto] "I'm not saying this without evidence.\n
71. [Makoto] Ordinarily it would be unthinkable. But Akashi-senpai is the type who would do it.\n
72. [Makoto] Here, look at this..."
73. [Makoto] "These are photos and sketches Akashi-senpai took, along with his notes. He's done detailed structural analysis too. That's why I'm saying he did it to study the building's structure from above."
74. [Naoya] "Yeah, this really is..."
75. [NARRATION] They were notes of a kind impossible to produce without observing from above in flight.\n
76. [NARRATION] Just as Toritani said, decisive proof -- there's no explanation but that he flew through the sky to learn the church's structure.
77. [Naoya] "Still, why do you have photos that Akashi took?"
78. [Makoto] "Everything Akashi-senpai had was confiscated by the school. Of course it was. After causing a commotion like that, all of this was bound to be seized."
79. [Naoya] "So then why do you have it..."
80. [Makoto] "That's a matter of various things.\n
81. [Makoto] There's a saying, isn't there -- 'set a snake to catch a snake.'"
82. [NARRATION] Right -- probably from Wakata, then.
83. [NARRATION] Same as when Kusanagi Ken'ichirou's rough sketches turned up. The pipeline between Wakata and Toritani seems just slightly different from the usual student-and-teacher relationship.
84. [Naoya] "I see -- so you'd investigated that far.\n
