17. "Yeah, never mind that.\n
18. The roof gets scorching in summer -- you'll dry up."
19. "Wow, what nice weather...\n
20. Far from drying up, isn't this a lovely breeze... it feels good."
21. "In the early hours of the morning, there's actually quite a bit of shade..."
22. We climbed up onto the roof of the old wing of Yumihari General Hospital.
23. It was my first time setting foot up here, and there stood an enormous triangular wall for sunshade.
24. Fitted into it was a huge pane of glass, giving it the look of a church sanctuary of sorts.
25. "Which means, if light were to pour through..."
26. "What's the matter?"
27. "Ah, no. I was just thinking Yumihari Academy is clearly visible from this window."
28. "You're right. From here you can see Yumihari Academy quite well."
29. Yumihari Academy stood on the wall side, so you had to view it through the great pane of glass.
30. Komaki walked along the edge of the roof.
31. "When the season's in full bloom, you can see the cherry blossoms of Yumihari Hill in a single sweeping view..."
32. "I bet. Or rather, you might as well say the old wing of this hospital is built almost right inside Yumihari Hill."
33. The cherry blossoms visible from here must be a marvelous sight.
34. "Though in midsummer, you can't see any cherry blossoms on Yumihari Hill..."
35. "If cherry blossoms bloomed in midsummer, that would be an abnormality..."
36. "The October cherry, the winter cherry, the four-seasons cherry, the kobuku cherry... there are cherries that bloom even without going off-season."
37. "I've never heard of any cherries on Yumihari Hill that bloom outside of spring."
38. "You're right. October cherries, winter cherries, four-seasons cherries, kobuku cherries... I've never heard of such varieties being planted there. Nor of them growing wild..."
39. "Then a cherry blossoming in midsummer would be a total anomaly, wouldn't it..."
40. "Yes... there's no such thing as a cherry blossoming in midsummer, is there..."
41. "Even if I said I'd seen the thousand-year cherry, you wouldn't believe me, Kusanagi-san..."
42. "Have you seen it?"
43. "Yes... I've seen the summer cherry."
44. "I see. So you've seen the thousand-year cherry..."
45. "You don't believe me, do you... normally, no one would..."
46. "I wonder..."
47. "Sometimes you see things even when you don't want to believe them..."
48. "Have you never watched the sun rise in the east?"
49. "Of course I have. What of it?"
50. "The sun climbs the sky and sinks into the western sky...\n
51. it circles this earth."
52. "We see such a sight all the time,\n
53. and yet, seeing it, no one imagines the sun is revolving around the earth, do they?\n
54. We know the earth is the one revolving around the sun."
55. "I don't get it. What are you saying?"
56. "What you see isn't necessarily the way it is. Nor is what your eyes catch necessarily the fact of the matter."
57. "So there's no reason to deny the fact of what you saw. Whether it's the thousand-year cherry of Yumihari legend, I don't know, but you probably did see some strange cherry blossom."
58. "Hehe... you say the exact opposite of what the Father says."
59. "What did the Father say?"
60. "Because you have seen me, have you believed? Blessed are those who have not seen and yet have believed."
61. "I don't quite follow, but is that from the Bible?"
62. "You caught on quickly. Yes -- 'The Gospel According to John, Chapter 20, Verse 29.' A passage on the resurrection of Christ."
63. "'Caught on' isn't really the word -- if the Father was going to quote anything, it'd have to be the Bible...\n
64. Sorry, though. I've no grounding in scripture, so I have no idea what it means."
65. "It's from the story of a man called Thomas, who insisted he wouldn't believe in Christ's resurrection until he saw the marks left by the crucifixion."
66. "I see. So it's the difference between one who 'will not believe' until he sees with his own eyes, until he verifies the truth for himself, and one who believes in Jesus's resurrection out of faith..."
67. "You caught on quickly."
68. "When you saw the thousand-year cherry... did the Father tell you that story?"
69. "It was because no one would believe me.\n
70. Worse than that, everyone called Komaki a liar."
71. "Ahaha, well, I kept insisting I'd seen what I saw.\n
72. If everyone was saying otherwise, I could just have said I hadn't seen it..."
73. "But Komaki said she saw it.\n
74. She said she saw cherry blossoms blooming on a summer night..."
75. "What's this? Were you bullied for it?"
76. "I wasn't bullied, but..."
77. "She was called a liar..."
78. "And what the Father said to us then was..."
79. "To believe in a miracle is not to see with your eyes, not to hear with your ears, not to have been there, not to have proof -- it is simply to believe."
80. "Believing everything just makes you a fool, though..."
81. "What's that supposed to mean?"
82. "No, Kosachi-chan. He's right."
83. "To believe is not to see with your eyes, not to hear with your ears, not to have been there, not to verify... it is simply to believe."
84. "But that doesn't mean believing in just anything.\n
85. It doesn't mean believing carelessly, blindly, in order to stop thinking.\n
86. Believing in order to stop thinking is sloth."
87. "So faith is like... believing in something that lies beyond words, beyond even what can be spoken of?"
88. "Roughly, yes."
89. "But the Father didn't believe your words in the same way he believes in God, did he? In that case, 'The Gospel According to John, Chapter 20, Verse 29' is a bit off the mark."
90. "Well, I don't know about the difficult things.\n
91. It's true that believing in God's miracles and believing what a child like me was saying are different. Even I understand that much, that they're plainly different."
92. "But...\n
93. But even so, the Father gave us those words."
94. "The difficult talk is beyond me.\n
95. All I can say is that when a person believes in something, in the end it's not 'deduction' or 'induction' or 'verification' or 'falsification.'"
96. "People don't know how to 'doubt' from the moment they're born. Those who don't know doubt can't possibly know how to 'believe' either...\n
97. It's only by learning what it means to 'believe' that a person first learns to 'doubt.'"
98. "To believe -- it's always something without any proof at all, isn't it."
99. "Ahaha... what you're saying, Kusanagi-san, is far more difficult."
100. "You're right...\n
101. I tried to tie it up neatly and made a total mess of it..."
102. "Believing in God and believing what you say are separated by a vast gulf, but...\n
103. still, they're probably the same.\n
104. Different, and yet the same thing..."
105. "I don't get any of it..."
106. "It's okay. I don't understand what Kusanagi-san's saying either -- it's too hard."
107. "Same here."
108. "What are you saying it for if you don't even understand it!?\n
109. Don't just rattle off things you don't understand yourself!\n
110. I ended up thinking about it really seriously despite myself!"
111. "Isn't that fine?\n
112. Thinking is a good thing.\n
113. Think, and think, and the more you doubt, the more you should come to understand what it is to 'believe.'"
114. "Wh-what's that... those oh-so-meaningful words of yours -- you're just spouting them off the cuff, aren't you?"
115. "Who knows..."
116. "Well then, if we waste too much more time, morning visiting hours will be over, won't they?"
117. "You're the one who kept us here!"
118. "You're right. I'm the one who kept you. \n
119. So I'll take responsibility and say it."
120. "Get. To. The. Fa. Ther. Al. Rea. Dy."
121. "Who do you think you are! You!"
122. "Come on, Kusanagi-san's right. We have to hurry."
123. "K-Komaki, not you too --"
124. "Kusanagi-san, thank you very much.\n
125. And... I'm counting on you."
126. "There's nothing for you to count on me for..."
127. "I'm just going to do what I believe in."
128. "H-hold on, what does that mean? Why are you the only one who's satisfied, Komaki?"
129. "Come on, come on, let's go. Kosachi-chan."
130. Because people know how to doubt, they can believe...
131. It's precisely because people believe, because they want to believe, that they come to doubt...
132. "So that's how it is..."

