55. [NARRATION] A lot has happened since the day we completed the mural on the chapel wall.
56. [NARRATION] Truly, more than you'd ever expect in a normal summer vacation.
57. [NARRATION] To speak only of the outcome:\n
58. [NARRATION] the painting was allowed to remain,\n
59. [NARRATION] and I -- and the art club -- were allowed to continue.
60. [NARRATION] With one exception, however...
61. [NARRATION] The last day of July.
62. [NARRATION] Or rather, the morning of the first day of August.
63. [NARRATION] That work was completed.
64. [NARRATION] Like a cherry tree suddenly blooming out of season in high summer, it appeared on the chapel wall without warning.\n
65. [NARRATION] Kusanagi Ken'ichirou's "The Footprints of the Sakura."
66. [NARRATION] Using small footprints reminiscent of a child's in place of paint, it depicts a cherry tree in full bloom,\n
67. [NARRATION] and further, its petals scatter down onto a stained glass window fashioned to resemble a water's surface.
68. [NARRATION] The stained glass, seen from inside the chapel, is of course beautiful.\n
69. [NARRATION] With abundant light drawn in from outside, the blue-toned glass dyes the polished white marble in exquisite hues.
70. [NARRATION] But it doesn't end there.\n
71. [NARRATION] The stained glass seen from outside is not the radiant blue seen from within, but a dim, beautiful, transparent water's surface.
72. [NARRATION] That effect is only realized by painting the "cherry blossoms" on the wall.\n
73. [NARRATION] The cherry blossoms swirling in the sky as mural, the water's surface as stained glass -- the combination of the two materials is what brings that dim beauty to the fore.
74. [NARRATION] Among Kusanagi Ken'ichirou's completed works featuring cherry blossoms, setting aside preliminary studies, "The Reclining Sakura" was the only one.
75. [NARRATION] Or so common knowledge in the art world had it.\n
76. [NARRATION] Which is precisely why "The Footprints of the Sakura," which he had left finished as a design during his lifetime, is bound to elicit reactions not just from the art world but from every quarter.
77. [NARRATION] The same shock I myself felt when I first saw the design... no, even greater than that.
78. [NARRATION] What I saw was a design.
79. [NARRATION] But what everyone else saw was a "completed work" that appeared without warning on a midsummer night.
80. [NARRATION] Anyone who had anything to do with Kusanagi Ken'ichirou believed that the motif of "cherry blossoms" held some special meaning for him, and as if to prove it, until then Kusanagi Ken'ichirou had not released any work featuring "cherry blossoms" other than "The Reclining Sakura."
81. [NARRATION] That is precisely why I thought this plan had a chance.\n
82. [NARRATION] Completing this work was by no means a losing battle.
83. [NARRATION] In that sense, this outcome was, in a way, already factored in.
84. [NARRATION] No, that would be overstating it...
85. [NARRATION] It would be nice if it turned out that way.\n
86. [NARRATION] If it did, we'd count ourselves lucky -- that was more or less how I was thinking.\n
87. [NARRATION] Maybe.
88. [NARRATION] Either way, depending on the quality of the finished work, our fates could have turned out completely different.
89. [NARRATION] If we had ruined not just Kusanagi Ken'ichirou's work but the building itself, expulsion wouldn't have been the end of it.
90. [NARRATION] It was a dangerous bridge to cross.\n
91. [NARRATION] But if we completed this work as a perfect piece, we had a real chance of winning.\n
92. [NARRATION] The possibility that a punishable crime would flip into an honor deserving of praise.
93. [NARRATION] That day.
94. [NARRATION] Almost the moment it was finished, we were spotted by the security guard.\n
95. [NARRATION] Probably because we made such a huge fuss when we finished.
96. [NARRATION] If we had quietly slipped away after finishing, we might not have been caught.
97. [NARRATION] But having completed that piece, sneaking off quietly was unthinkable.\n
98. [NARRATION] We celebrated with a huge racket the moment it was done.
99. [NARRATION] Naturally, the guard came running.
100. [NARRATION] We had trespassed at night without permission, and on top of that scrawled a massive piece of graffiti on the chapel wall.
101. [NARRATION] It was a serious commotion.\n
102. [NARRATION] At first there was even talk of calling the police.
103. [NARRATION] Despite the early hour, teachers gathered in response to the call.\n
104. [NARRATION] The uproar was on the scale of the greatest scandal since the school's founding.
105. [NARRATION] Ai, of course, was among them.
106. [NARRATION] While the other teachers panicked, shouted, and raged,\n
107. [NARRATION] Ai simply stood looking at the cherry blossom painting Kusanagi Ken'ichirou had left behind.
108. [NARRATION] For a long, long time, amid the clamor, calm as a visitor at a museum, she gazed at that painting.
109. [NARRATION] After taking her time to appreciate it,\n
