1. The cherry, said to bloom beautifully in any season.
2. Whether this piece takes that cherry as its motif or not, I couldn't say...
3. But there is, without doubt, something in it that captivates people.
4. And that is probably not the power of Kusanagi Ken'ichirou alone.
5. Sure, it's a work made possible only by his genius conception and artistic sense.
6. But the one who saw it through to completion wasn't Kusanagi Ken'ichirou.
7. What draws people to this cherry blossom painting is Akashi's feelings.\n
8. His feelings for Komaki, for Kosachi, and for the priest.
9. And it exists because of the passion of these people, who were determined to complete such a work...
10. There's a saying, "to make the Buddha and forget the soul." Even if the design was Kusanagi Ken'ichirou's, if the people who completed it had no soul in them, it never would have become a work capable of drawing people in like this.
11. The passion of everyone gathered here.\n
12. And their skill -- that is what gave form to Kusanagi Ken'ichirou's final work.
13. Which is exactly why this piece...,
14. "It's magnificent... truly..."
15. "So, who's the ringleader?!\n
16. You're all art club members, right?! Who's the club president?! Toritani?!"
17. "Ahh, stop your chirping and squawking!\n
