109. "Hey, drop that manner of speaking in front of the headmaster."
110. "Responsibility, hmm... So does that mean you're the one in overall charge?"
111. "No, that's not right.\n
112. I mean, no, that's not the case..."
113. "Hmm, you're... let me see..."
114. "Yes, Kusanagi Naoya.\nThe son of the painter Kusanagi Ken'ichirou, who completed the design, the drafts, and even the interior of this work."
115. "Ah, I remember. I saw you at Kusanagi Ken'ichirou's funeral."
116. I didn't remember, though...
117. "More to the point, you just said something.\n
118. 'That's not the case' -- what did you mean by that?"
119. "Did you look at them? Those documents."
120. I pointed at the papers placed on the headmaster's desk.
121. "Yes, I looked.\n
122. These appear to be blueprints drawn by Kusanagi Ken'ichirou himself."
123. There is a Kusanagi Ken'ichirou signature on them, to be sure.\n
124. Still, this headmaster accepts the authenticity of these blueprints far too readily.
125. And in front of a student who caused such an enormous incident, she wasn't the least bit shaken.\n
126. I'd expected her, like that other teacher, to start by shrieking at me...
127. Even a headmaster isn't the owner of this school.
128. The headmaster herself could be held responsible...,\n
129. so where does that composure come from?\n
130. "Those are all the documents necessary for a full renovation of that chapel.\n
131. The interior renovation was already completed over ten years ago, but..."
132. "Yes, that's right. I remember.\n
133. That time, too, it was carried out without any authorization, just like today..."
134. "Though of course, unlike today, it couldn't be finished in a single day -- it was more like something done in secret..."
135. "I see, so no permission that time either.\n
136. That's why the school fired the teacher in charge.\n
137. Made him take responsibility."
138. "H-hey, Kusanagi! I don't know what you're talking about, but!\n
139. Don't say anything reckless!\n
140. You're in front of the headmaster, you know! You!"
141. "Reckless? No, this is simply fact.\n
142. No matter who I'm in front of, the facts don't change."

=== lines=141 stop_reason=end_turn in=6360 out=4415 cache_read=3222 cache_create=0 last_entry_continued=0 ===
1. [Teacher A] "H-how would I know if it's true or not.\n
2. [Teacher A] And besides, how can you declare something from that long ago as fact!"
3. [Headmaster] "It is fact.\n
4. [Headmaster] The one who dismissed Kusanagi Ken'ichirou was me. I did it myself."
5. This headmaster?
6. Her answer surprised me a little.
7. Kusanagi Ken'ichirou teaching here was something from quite a long time ago...
8. She looks about the same age as Ken'ichirou... no, even younger, but did she have that kind of authority back then?
9. [Headmaster] "What's the matter? I merely affirmed what you said -- why are you so dumbfounded?"
10. [Naoya] "For starters, Kusanagi Ken'ichirou was dismissed by the school.\n
11. [Naoya] That's precisely why the fact that Kusanagi Ken'ichirou was the one who renovated that chapel was concealed.\n
12. [Naoya] Because it would have been inconvenient."
13. [Naoya] "That said, back then it couldn't be helped. In that era, Kusanagi Ken'ichirou was just an unknown teacher, nothing more."
14. [Naoya] "But it's one thing back then -- now it's different.\n
15. [Naoya] To be a 'work by Kusanagi Ken'ichirou' is nothing short of an honor."
16. [Naoya] "Even in death -- no, precisely because he's dead now, he's become famous enough to spark a minor art boom in this country."
17. [Naoya] "Naturally. Postwar, artists who achieved worldwide success can be counted on one hand.\n
18. [Naoya] Moreover, in this era, no matter the field, anyone who's succeeded on the world stage is a superstar."
19. [Naoya] "And yet, the fact that that chapel is a work by Kusanagi Ken'ichirou was concealed."
20. [Naoya] "Why is that?"
21. [Headmaster] "Why do you think?"
22. [Naoya] "The school punished Kusanagi Ken'ichirou.\n
23. [Naoya] And in doing so, left a world-class painter's work unfinished."
24. [Naoya] "For an ordinary school it would be one thing, but for this school, which has produced so many artists, it's dishonor of the highest order. Not something that can be made public."
25. [Naoya] "Of course you'd want to hide it. It's an incident that exposes the total absence of your eye for beauty."
26. [Teacher A] "Kusanagiii!"
27. [Headmaster] "Could you have Tamura-sensei step outside... Vice Principal."
28. [Headmaster] "Could you have everyone besides Kusanagi-kun and Akashi-kun step outside."
29. [Teacher A] "Y-you're leaving just these two?"
30. [Vice Principal] "Yes, yes, Tamura-sensei, let's go..."
31. The vice principal who had been silent all along and the teacher who had done nothing but shout stepped out.
32. Silence fell over the headmaster's office, which had been so noisy until now.\n
33. If anything, the air felt slightly heavier than before...
34. [Headmaster] "Now then, back to the discussion, Kusanagi-kun."
35. [Headmaster] "Do you have any questions about the fact that I fired Kusanagi Ken'ichirou-shi?"
36. [Naoya] "No, that itself isn't the issue.\n
37. [Naoya] The point is that because that work was left unfinished, it couldn't be made public.\n
38. [Naoya] That's all I wanted to confirm."
39. [Headmaster] "Just as you say, Kusanagi-kun. Precisely because we dismissed him and left the work unfinished, we couldn't announce it to the world."
40. [Headmaster] "As you said, if this were an ordinary school there might have been no issue, but well, Yumihari's traditions wouldn't allow it, I suppose."
41. [Naoya] "And that's why Kusanagi Ken'ichirou's work was buried in darkness, left unfinished."
42. [Headmaster] "Mostly correct, but there are places where you're off."
43. [Headmaster] "Kusanagi Ken'ichirou-shi's departure is on the books as a voluntary resignation, not a dismissal."
44. [Naoya] "Voluntary resignation?"
45. [Headmaster] "We received his letter of resignation, too...\n
46. [Headmaster] That said, as you say, in substance the one who fired him was me.\n
47. [Headmaster] I'm the one who left that work unfinished. That's fact."
48. [Akashi] "Kusanagi Ken'ichirou... resigned voluntarily..."
49. [Headmaster] "Do you understand what this means?"
50. [Akashi] "I see... in other words..."
51. [Headmaster] "The student over there seems to have grasped it."
52. [Akashi] "The truth is one thing, but on paper Kusanagi Ken'ichirou's departure is filed as a 'voluntary resignation.' Which means it doesn't come out as the school forcibly firing him and thus 'leaving Kusanagi Ken'ichirou's work unfinished.'"
