15. "Nakamura's one of the ten most common surnames in Japan."
16. "Now that you mention it, yeah."
17. "More importantly, why don't you take a peek inside the shrine?"
18. Yumi and I peered inside the shrine.
19. I'd lived in Yumihari for ages, but this was the first time I'd looked at Hakuki Shrine so closely.
20. "A big picture scroll's been dedicated here.\n
21. What is it? There's a ship painted on it..."
22. "That's probably a treasure ship."
23. "The treasure ship, as in the one the Seven Lucky Gods ride on?"
24. "Look closely -- no one's riding this treasure ship.\n
25. And try reading the characters written on the scroll."
26. "Ch-characters? Umm... The calligraphy's so fancy I can barely read it..."
27. "'Nakakiyono / toononefurino / minamesame / naminorifuneno / otonoyokikana.'"
28. "Now try reading it backwards."
29. "Backwards? Um..."
30. "Huh? What's this?"
31. "Right. It's a palindrome."
32. "A palindrome, meaning it reads the same forwards and backwards?"
33. "That's right. Well-crafted, isn't it? What's astonishing is that the author is unknown."
34. "Huh... I wonder what it means..."
35. "Well, no one knows for sure. The poem itself is mysterious -- there are all kinds of interpretations."
36. "The one thing that can be said for certain is that this palindrome poem sings of 'dreams.'"
37. "Dreams, huh... Now that you mention it, this ship has 'Baku' written on its sail."
38. "Right. And apparently there's a legend that if you put this picture of the baku treasure ship together with the palindrome poem under your pillow on the second day of the new year, you'll have a good first dream of the year."
39. "You're really well-informed, Yumi."
40. "W-well, sort of."
41. For some reason, Yumi looked away.
42. "Anyway, all of this tells us that Hakuki Shrine is a shrine that enshrines the god of dreams."
43. "The slope up here is called Yume-no-Ukisaka, too, isn't it?"
44. "Like I said, this whole hill was once a single shrine, so names having to do with dreams remain scattered all around. Remember the name of this city."
45. "Huh? The city? Yumihari City?"
46. "Yumihari City takes its name directly from this hill of Yumihari."
47. "And going further back, the place name 'Yumihari' was apparently originally 'Yumebari.'\n
48. The common theory is that Yumebari got corrupted into Yumihari."
49. "Yumebari?"
50. "Yeah, Yumebari. The 'bari' is the same character used when you say 'to fill with water.'"
51. "H-huh, I see...\n
52. But this shrine enshrines the baku, right? Then why is the formal name Hakuki Shrine? It seems like it could just as well be called Baku Shrine, the way everyone refers to it."
53. "Hakuki is a Chinese god. His name isn't very well known in Japan, but he's a divine beast that devours dreams."
54. "He didn't get passed down as Hakuki, but in Japan he got mixed up with another mythical beast called the baku. Originally in China, the baku ate copper, iron, and snakes."
55. "This place probably originally enshrined Hakuki, but afterward it shifted to the baku, which was more familiar to Japan."
56. "In fact, baku worship was popular from the Muromachi period, and by the Edo period it had spread to the common people. Nowadays it's completely forgotten, though."
57. "But the whole New Year's first-dream tradition is said to be a remnant of baku worship."
58. "I see... It's almost eerie how closely it matches the dream I had..."
59. "True. But it's also entirely possible you already knew about it, Rina."
60. "The folklore of Yumihari gets turned into picture books pretty often, so maybe you read one at some point."
61. "I don't remember doing that."
62. "Then what could it be?"
63. "Maybe it's your past life, Rina."
64. "Ugh... I really don't want to become one of those mystical girls who starts saying stuff like 'past lives.'"
65. "Really? I could learn to like that side of you too."
66. "Yes, yes, sure you could..."
67. "But, you know, we did see that cherry tree..."
68. "Ah, that... Kusanagi-senpai said we were just seeing things, but..."
69. "All three of you seeing the same thing wrong at once?"
70. "Yeah, that part I don't really get either..."
71. "Maybe the legend of the thousand-year cherry is actually true."
72. "If it is, that's kind of scary, isn't it?"
73. "Scary? Why? It grants your wishes, doesn't it?"
74. "Ah, no. Resonating with people's feelings and blooming -- put like that, it sounds like a beautiful legend, but after that dream I had it feels sort of scary..."
75. "I think the feelings the thousand-year cherry blooms with -- what it spits out -- are the sort of thing senpai was talking about."
76. "The sort of thing Kusanagi was talking about... you mean curses?"
