1. [Akashi] "Huh? Y-yes, that's right. I-isn't that what I've been saying? I'm the world-famous Wataru Akashi!"
2. [Nakamura Shouichi] "Just who is this man? Do you know him?"
3. [Friedman] "He's already explained himself, hasn't he? Then there's no need for further explanation. It's exactly as he said......"
4. [NARRATION] Just what is this foreigner......
5. [NARRATION] He barges in out of nowhere, and takes the initiative in the whole room.
6. [Nakamura Shouichi] "Wataru Akashi. Are you really with the Moore Foundation?"
7. [Akashi] "Wellll, whether I'm a man of the Moore Foundation, or what should I say, more of a middleman perhaps~?"
8. [Friedman] "In other words, a buyer, would that be right?"
9. [Nakamura Shouichi] "A buyer......"
10. [Akashi] "That's it exactly. I make my living as a buyer, you see."
11. [NARRATION] What is this man Friedman thinking?
12. [NARRATION] My eyes meet Friedman's.
13. [NARRATION] But this man's expression doesn't shift in the slightest.
14. [NARRATION] Good will...... doesn't quite seem to be it either.
15. [Nakamura Shouichi] "If the senior curator is genuine...... then this man is genuine too?"
16. [Akashi] "That's what I've been saying from the very beginning~"
17. [NARRATION] No, the fact is, Friedman never explicitly said Akashi was a buyer.
18. [NARRATION] Just now he only annotated the word "middleman" with, "In other words, a buyer, would that be right?"
19. [NARRATION] Old man Nakamura simply misunderstood it on his own......
20. [NARRATION] Just as my father said, this foreigner is a considerable operator.
21. [Akashi] "As you see, I have merely brought a splendid proposition to Nakamura-sama, nothing more~"
22. [Akashi] "Please, if you would just sign this affidavit, then we can discuss a deal worth hundreds of millions."
23. [Friedman] "Pay me no mind. I only came here as the trustee of Kusanagi Ken'ichirou's estate, to see Kusanagi Naoya."
24. [Friedman] "However, in a deal this large, drawing up an affidavit beforehand isn't so strange, wouldn't you say?"
25. [Akashi] "Precisely so. It's merely a confidentiality affidavit. If you can't read that much English, shall I read it aloud for you?"
26. [Nakamura Shouichi] "I don't need that! Hand it over!"
27. [NARRATION] At last, old man Nakamura signs Akashi's affidavit.
28. [Nakamura Shouichi] "Well then? What is this profitable proposition?"
29. [Akashi] "Yes. Earlier, while chatting, I happened to hear about Kusanagi-shi's debts, and I thought -- how about settling that sum with paintings?"
30. [Nakamura Shouichi] "With paintings? Do you mean Kusanagi Ken'ichirou's paintings?"
31. [Akashi] "Yes, exactly so."
32. [Nakamura Shouichi] "Ridiculous. Hundreds of millions? Most of Kusanagi Ken'ichirou's paintings are worth tens of millions of yen at best. Only that one, 'The Reclining Sakura,' is said to have value in the hundreds of millions."
33. [Akashi] "Well informed of you."
34. [Nakamura Shouichi] "Then you can't possibly move that kind of money with Kusanagi Ken'ichirou's paintings."
35. [Akashi] "That's not quite so."
36. [Nakamura Shouichi] "What? Does he have works by other famous artists in his collection?"
37. [Akashi] "He might, but even so, they'd be about on par with Kusanagi Ken'ichirou's market value."
38. [Akashi] "For instance. For instance, have you ever thought of it this way?\nWhy did Kusanagi Ken'ichirou produce only a single masterpiece of the sakura, and never exhibit another after that?"
39. [Nakamura Shouichi] "I heard the theme of that piece was a requiem for Mizuna."
40. [Akashi] "Mizuna?"
41. [Nakamura Shouichi] "Ah, no. I only read in some magazine that the painting was completed in the wake of his wife's death."
42. [Akashi] "Indeed. That painting is surely a requiem."
43. [Nakamura Shouichi] "Stop beating around the bush! What are you getting at?"
44. [Akashi] "Attached to 'The Reclining Sakura' is a verse."
45. [Nakamura Shouichi] "A verse?"
46. [Akashi] "You don't know it?"
47. [Nakamura Shouichi] "How does it go?"
48. [Akashi] "H-how does it go? U-uhm......"
49. [Friedman] "Across the phantom spring-hued sky in every direction,\nlamps of blind causal exchange\nshine bright, bright, bright."
50. [Friedman] "Tourmaline of Watermelon,\nthe crossroads of sound and language,\nin a world overflowing more and more with color."
51. [Friedman] "Before the landscape I do not exist,\nbefore me the landscape does not exist."
52. [Friedman] "In a soft, fluffy forest of sakura the world rang out,\nwith a beautiful tone the world rang out."
53. [Friedman] "It is said to be an extremely philosophical verse, and this philosophical quality itself is said to have been born from his relationship with his late wife, Madame Mizuna."
54. [Friedman] "Kusanagi Ken'ichirou himself has, in interviews on several occasions, quoted the wordplay of neuroscientists to expound his own views on mind, body, and the world."
55. [Friedman] "That said, much of it is considered enigmatic."
56. [Akashi] "That's it! Exactly that!"
57. [Akashi] "The most enigmatic part of all is said to be the seven sakura!"
58. [NARRATION] No, there's nothing especially enigmatic about that.\nThe seven sakura are based on the seven steps to truth that originated in ancient Babylonia and were later inherited by the Christian world.
59. [NARRATION] Not particularly familiar in Japan, but a fairly well-known metaphor in the West.
60. [NARRATION] This number seven was assigned, in Zoroastrianism, to the gods of good and evil respectively, and its influence carries on into the Christian sphere. The famous phrase "the seven deadly sins" also has its origin here.
61. [NARRATION] It was this groundwork that lay behind my father's verse of the seven sakura.
62. [NARRATION] The seven sakura are both the stairway to the truth of the world and the seven deadly sins.
63. [NARRATION] This interpretation is one I heard from my father himself.
64. [Nakamura Shouichi] "What about the enigmatic part! Get to the point! I hate this dawdling!"
65. [NARRATION] Old man Nakamura, hot-tempered by nature, begins to grow irritated.
66. [NARRATION] Now that he's signed the affidavit, he must want to know the truth of the matter as soon as possible.
67. [Akashi] "But if you set forth a certain hypothesis, it ceases to be an enigma at all."
68. [Nakamura Shouichi] "A hypothesis?"
69. [Akashi] "The seven sakura truly exist."
70. [NARRATION] Friedman lets out a breath.\nDid he laugh?
71. [Nakamura Shouichi] "What is it, Friedman-san? Is it just as I thought -- nonsense?"
72. [Friedman] "No, no, I was just surprised to hear such a thing brought up in a place like this. Kukuku......"
73. [Friedman] "Well, if it truly were a set of seven, the value would be considerable indeed."
74. [Nakamura Shouichi] "Eh? A set of seven?"
75. [Friedman] "That's what he's saying. That the seven sakura truly exist -- doesn't that mean as works of art? That a series of seven sakura exists in Kusanagi Ken'ichirou's oeuvre."
76. [Akashi] "Precisely so."
77. [Nakamura Shouichi] "T-that is to say, apart from that 'Reclining Sakura,' there exist six more works."
78. [Akashi] "Yes. This is information that was scheduled to be released after Kusanagi Ken'ichirou-shi's death."
79. [Akashi] "Yes, it's certain. Ken'ichirou-shi intends to make it public posthumously."
80. [Nakamura Shouichi] "W-why after his death?"
81. [Akashi] "Well, I can't say for certain. But coming here today, I have become convinced."
82. [Nakamura Shouichi] "Of w-what?"
83. [Akashi] "Ken'ichirou-shi has debts with Nakamura-sama. If he dies, these are wiped clean."
84. [Nakamura Shouichi] "I-indeed...... it's not a loan with any guarantor."
85. [Akashi] "And once they're wiped clean, wouldn't he intend to leave his fortune to his son, Kusanagi Naoya?"
86. [Nakamura Shouichi] "What?! That bastard! What a cowardly stunt!"
87. [Akashi] "Yes, it's unforgivable. As Ken'ichirou-shi's friend, I too must insist that what one borrows should be returned!"
88. [Nakamura Shouichi] "Th-then you, you must explain this to him!"
89. [Akashi] "No. Faced with his own death, he has become extremely stubborn. Well, perhaps it is atonement for not having given his children proper time with him. Perhaps he means to leave that behind in the form of money."
90. [NARRATION] Incredible.\nEvery bit of it is pure fabrication.
91. [NARRATION] And yet, the logic all holds together.
92. [Akashi] "Shall I mediate between the two of you?"
93. [Nakamura Shouichi] "Eh? What was that?"
94. [Akashi] "If it's me, I can guarantee delivery of six of these seven sakura into Nakamura-sama's hands."
95. [Friedman] "That's a story I can't quite let pass."
96. [Nakamura Shouichi] "W-what is it, you!"
97. [Friedman] "As for the seven sakura -- if such things do exist, it would be the work of the Moore Foundation, which has been entrusted with the administration of Kusanagi Ken'ichirou-shi's estate."
98. [Akashi] "Now, now, the trustee's work only begins after Ken'ichirou-shi's passing, does it not?"
99. [Friedman] "But the Moore Foundation has fully backed Ken'ichirou-shi. His works cannot be bought and sold arbitrarily. This is an absolute condition."
100. [Akashi] "Well, that is troubling. The Moore Foundation, of all opponents."
101. [Nakamura Shouichi] "W-world-famous Wataru Akashi-kun, is there nothing to be done?"
102. [Akashi] "Something can be done. After all, I am the world-famous Wataru Akashi~"
103. [NARRATION] Hey, old man Nakamura, are you seriously buying Akashi's act?
104. [NARRATION] No -- Friedman's shakedown is working perfectly.
105. [Nakamura Shouichi] "If you can manage something, then--!"
106. [Akashi] "Very well then. In that case, would you be so kind as to sign this affidavit as well~?"
107. [NARRATION] This time it was an affidavit of considerable thickness, bound with book-binding tape.
108. [NARRATION] Even a proper contract wouldn't run to that many pages.\nWhat could possibly be written in something that thick?
109. [Nakamura Shouichi] "What? What is this?"
110. [Akashi] "It is an affidavit of exclusive handling regarding this matter."
111. [Nakamura Shouichi] "Not a contract?"
112. [Akashi] "Ahh, in the trade of paintings, exclusive dealings conducted in secret are chiefly set down in an affidavit. The finer contractual terms are all set out within as well~"
113. [NARRATION] Of course, I've never heard of such a thing, and there's no basis for it in fact.
114. [NARRATION] That document has terms laid out in as fine detail as any contract. Just what on earth is written in it?
115. [Friedman] "For now, Wataru Akashi. If there are six sakura remaining, I want you to submit an application to the Moore Foundation immediately."
116. [Akashi] "No, please wait just a little longer~"
117. [Friedman] "No. If that's the attitude you take, then I have plans of my own."
118. [Akashi] "Nakamura-sama, decide quickly. As things stand, the Moore Foundation will obstruct me, and I will be unable to act on your behalf. The affidavit, quickly."
119. [Friedman] "No, Mister Nakamura. You should not exchange affidavits with a man like that."
120. [Nakamura Shouichi] "What are you saying! You've just done a complete about-face from what you said before! Moore Foundation or not, in the end you're just money-grubbers."
121. [Nakamura Shouichi] "Damn it, pen and seal."
122. [Black-suit A] "A-are you sure? I've never heard of such an arrangement."
123. [Nakamura Shouichi] "You can tell from that Moore Foundation's attitude. Hurry!"
124. [NARRATION] Taking the pen and seal handed to him by the black suit, old man Nakamura signs the affidavit once more.
125. [NARRATION] The first time he had at least glanced over it, but this time he shows no sign of checking anything at all.
126. [NARRATION] Well, no one would want to read a long English document like that.\nBut this time it's that thick affidavit.
127. [NARRATION] Without a doubt, its contents must exceed even a contract.
128. [NARRATION] Signing without reading it is nothing short of fatal.
129. [Friedman] "Tch. So you've signed that affidavit after all. As for me, it's a great disappointment."
130. [Nakamura Shouichi] "The Moore Foundation is no doubt in cahoots with Kusanagi Ken'ichirou anyway! What's this about disappointment!"
131. [Friedman] "I see...... Put that way, I have no room to argue."
132. [Nakamura Shouichi] "Anyway, about that painting."
133. [Akashi] "A moment, please, a moment -- if you'll give me just a moment, I will most certainly persuade Ken'ichirou-shi."
134. [Nakamura Shouichi] "I'm counting on you. World-famous Akashi-kun!"
135. [Friedman] "I haven't given up yet. If such works do exist, Moore will not let it rest."
136. [Akashi] "Ha ha haan. You'd best not underestimate the world-famous Wataru Akashi, Mister Friedman."
137. [Friedman] "That's my line."
138. [Nakamura Shouichi] "F-for now, I'm counting on you. Don't let Moore get the jump on us!!"

=== lines=146 stop_reason=end_turn in=8145 out=5440 cache_read=3222 cache_create=0 last_entry_continued=0 ===
1. [Akashi] "Yes! Leave it to me!"
2. [NARRATION] I'd been watching the whole thing play out, but it was about time to steer the conversation back to its original topic.
3. [NARRATION] I raised my voice and announced it to all of them.
4. [Naoya] "For what it's worth, every one of you is trespassing. I'm calling the cops."
5. [Friedman] "I am with the Moore Foundation, you know?"
6. [Naoya] "Even so! This is Japan. For starters, could you stop coming in with your shoes on?"
7. [Friedman] "Oh, my apologies."
8. [NARRATION] Still, though......\nThe conversation had taken a truly outrageous turn.
9. [NARRATION] Was it coincidence......or something else?
10. [Naoya] "So, that's what happened."
11. [Ken'ichirou] "Pfahahahaha!"
12. [Naoya] "Hey, hey, is it really okay for a sick man to be laughing his head off like that?"
13. [Ken'ichirou] "No, but it's just too funny."
14. [Naoya] "But still......"
15. [Ken'ichirou] "Hahaha, well now, that's rich. I'd have loved to see it. Kukuku."
16. [Ken'ichirou] "Ow!"
17. [Naoya] "See, what did I tell you?"
18. [NARRATION] I thought he'd recover right away, but for the next several minutes my old man kept clutching at his chest.
19. [Naoya] "What, does it hurt that much? Are you all right?"
20. [NARRATION] For all he was saying, the pain looked considerable.
21. [NARRATION] He might put on a lively front, but the doctors had told him he didn't have much time left.
22. [NARRATION] Even talking like this must be taking a fair toll on him.
23. [Naoya] "Want me to call the nurse?"
24. [Ken'ichirou] "No problem......"
25. [Naoya] "It doesn't look that way to me. No point playing the tough guy when you're in a hospital, right?"
26. [Ken'ichirou] "Playing tough, huh...... Pain is proof that you're alive, you know."
27. [Naoya] "What kind of gag line is that supposed to be?"
28. [Ken'ichirou] "No, no, it's actually pretty important. Somewhere in that long life of yours, the meaning will suddenly come to you."
29. [Naoya] "In life? Not when I'm sick?"
30. [Ken'ichirou] "No -- in life."
31. [Ken'ichirou] "Suffering matters."
32. [Ken'ichirou] "If you can't feel anything,"
33. [Ken'ichirou] "then you can't tell whether you're alive or dead."
34. [Ken'ichirou] "It hurts because you're trying to keep living."
35. [Ken'ichirou] "It hurts because your body wants to live."
36. [Ken'ichirou] "Because you're resisting."
37. [Ken'ichirou] "You're resisting extinction,\nand that's why it hurts."
38. [Ken'ichirou] "Accept the pain."
39. [Ken'ichirou] "That's what it means to live."
40. [Naoya] "Even so, you're still better off with painkillers, right?"
41. [Ken'ichirou] "Ah, sure, painkillers are better to have.\nAnd painkillers to dull the pain of life aren't so bad either......"
42. [Naoya] "Then I'm calling the doctor."
43. [Ken'ichirou] "Don't bother. I got some painkillers just this morning, so they probably won't give me more."
44. [Ken'ichirou] "So I've got to grit my teeth a little......"
45. [Naoya] "But even if you got some this morning, if you're in pain, they should hand out at least painkillers, right?"
46. [Ken'ichirou] "Ahh, about that. The painkillers are a little too good, you see. You get all kinds of fun hallucinations, it's a real hoot. I kept begging for more, and they put me on a quota."
47. [Naoya] "What's funny is your behavior!"
48. [Naoya] "'Accept the pain,' my ass...... Talking all cool like that. Turns out you were just getting high on morphine."
49. [Ken'ichirou] "If there's any upside to being this sick, it's about the only one -- getting to shoot up morphine."
50. [Naoya] "What was that whole speech from just now, then?"
51. [Ken'ichirou] "It's my own truth."
52. [Ken'ichirou] "It's an important thing."
53. [Naoya] "I see. I don't really get it right now, but I'll keep it in mind for later."
54. [Naoya] "It's not like you've ever talked to me about stuff like that much before."
55. [Ken'ichirou] "Is that so...... ow......"
56. [NARRATION] Ken'ichirou kept a hand pressed to his chest, but after some ten-odd minutes he managed to settle back to normal.
57. [Ken'ichirou] "Still, Friedman's timing was way too good."
58. [Naoya] "Yeah, I never thought he'd show up at the house right at that moment."
59. [Ken'ichirou] "Well, Friedman was probably keeping tabs on your place, right?"
60. [Naoya] "Huh? Does he really have that much free time?"
61. [Ken'ichirou] "No, no, he probably hired some part-timer. That guy'll slip cash to a homeless man on the corner and use him as a lookout."
62. [Naoya] "Then what about what happened this time?"
63. [Ken'ichirou] "My guess is Friedman's had a hand in it from the very start."
64. [Naoya] "And Akashi?"
65. [Ken'ichirou] "I don't know anyone called Akashi. But given he had an affidavit in English on him and one of my business cards, it's safe to say he'd been in contact with Friedman."
66. [Ken'ichirou] "And the fake nationality and the disguise -- those bother me too."
67. [Naoya] "No, he sometimes shows up in weird outfits. His usual getup of a school uniform with a coat thrown over it is ridiculous enough as it is. That's less a disguise and more his idea of what a hero should look like."
68. [Ken'ichirou] "Hmm, is that so. Well, if you say so, maybe that's all it is. Even so, I still smell Friedman's cunning at work."
69. [Naoya] "So how does an ordinary student like Akashi end up hand in hand with a Moore Foundation curator like Friedman?"
70. [Ken'ichirou] "How should I know. Go ask them yourself."
71. [Ken'ichirou] "Still, Nakamura Shouichi's an idiot as ever. Who would fall for that, normally?"
72. [Naoya] "How to put it -- that old man struck me as the type who caves easy to authority."
73. [Ken'ichirou] "True, I do know Nakamura Shouichi, and he was a philistine among philistines. But he does seem more senile than before. Still, I suppose that shows how badly cornered he must be."
74. [Naoya] "Word is Nakamura Pharmaceuticals is doing poorly."
75. [Ken'ichirou] "I don't know the details, but with a guy like that as chairman, no business is going to run smoothly."
76. [Ken'ichirou] "That aside -- for all the laugh I got out of it......\nWhat do you plan to do about the lie that Akashi guy told?"
77. [Naoya] "Ah, well, no such seven-part work exists, after all."
78. [Ken'ichirou] "Of course it doesn't exist. Who could churn out seven of those?"
79. [Naoya] "But the Nakamuras have started believing Akashi's lie.\nThey probably believe from the bottom of their hearts that he's a buyer."
80. [Naoya] "So, first, my first request is--"
81. [Ken'ichirou] "Refused."
82. [Naoya] "I haven't said anything yet."
83. [Ken'ichirou] "Sure you have -- you're going to ask me to knock together some seven-part work, right? There's no way. I've got half a year left to live."
84. [Naoya] "So what. The god of manga, you know what he did? He was drawing manga right up to the moment he died."
85. [Naoya] "When he lost consciousness and came to again, his first words were, 'Bring me my manuscript and pen.'"
86. [Ken'ichirou] "That's that, and this is this."
87. [Ken'ichirou] "Besides, the god of manga was a genuine genius. I'm basically a fraud. No genius about me."
88. [Naoya] "Even so, haven't you kept fooling the world with that fraudster's talent of yours?"
89. [Ken'ichirou] "Well, sure. That's because I'm a genius."
90. [Naoya] "Which is it, seriously."
91. [Ken'ichirou] "If you're the son of a fraud...... then how about solving it in a fraud's son's way?"
92. [Naoya] "Man, you're being awfully cavalier about this."
93. [Ken'ichirou] "Naoya, do you know what's said to be the oldest profession in the world?"
94. [Naoya] "Prostitution."
95. [Ken'ichirou] "Then what's the second?"
96. [Naoya] "Forgery."
97. [Ken'ichirou] "You've done your homework."
98. [Naoya] "Yeah, the renowned film director Orson Welles left the words 'as old as the tree in Eden.' In fact, plenty of Phoenician and Sumerian forgers were churning out fakes of ancient Greek works."
99. [Ken'ichirou] "That's not all. The masters have dirtied their hands with forgery too. Take Michelangelo, whom you idolize -- there's a famous story about him, isn't there?"
100. [Naoya] "No, I don't know it."
101. [Ken'ichirou] "In the Renaissance, the young Michelangelo, to catch the eye of his patron Lorenzo de' Medici, forged a Roman-era sculpture and buried it in the Medici gardens."
102. [Ken'ichirou] "And more -- he copied drawings by many of the past masters. His copies were apparently forgeries elaborate enough to be nearly indistinguishable from the originals: he used smoke to stain and soil them and so on."
103. [Naoya] "With skill like Michelangelo's, he could have caught his patron's eye with originals, right? Why go and make forgeries?"
104. [Ken'ichirou] "Michelangelo was a genius forger. There's a story, for instance, that he'd borrow a genuine work to copy and return the forgery in its place, keeping the original for himself."
105. [Naoya] "There are similar stories in the East, about calligraphy. Either way, what you're trying to say is that in art, forgery and the genuine article have a very intimate relationship, right?"
106. [Naoya] "So then? Old man, will you tell me the pigment formulation for 'The Reclining Sakura'?"
107. [Ken'ichirou] "Oh? What's this, what's this? You mean you've made up your mind to fabricate the Sakura seven-part work, and pretty easily at that?"
108. [Naoya] "That's impossible. The right hand you raised is no longer mine to use."
109. [Ken'ichirou] "Hahaha, the right hand I raised, huh. Nicely put."
110. [Naoya] "Yeah, I still hold a grudge over it, you know. That painter's training brace."
111. [Ken'ichirou] "Come on. If you want a special move, you can't skip the secret training, right?"
112. [Naoya] "Even so! What kind of parent slaps a training brace on the arm of a child barely old enough to walk!"
113. [Ken'ichirou] "That sort of thing, the earlier the better, they say. Motor nerves, muscle strength -- recent research says childhood environment matters more than genetics."
114. [Naoya] "That kind of research flips to the exact opposite theory within a few years."
115. [Naoya] "What was that all about, anyway? A parody of Star of the G-ants?"
116. [Ken'ichirou] "Well, there is that too."
117. [Naoya] "So there is!"
118. [Ken'ichirou] "You know Schumann, right?"
119. [Naoya] "Yeah, that composer you like."
120. [Ken'ichirou] "He was also a pianist, and it's said he ruined his hand from overdoing it with a training device that used weights to lift up the fingers."
121. [Naoya] "Hey, hey, doesn't that mean the training device ruined his hand?"
122. [Ken'ichirou] "Ah, but that popular version is apparently wrong."
123. [Naoya] "Then Schumann's got nothing to do with it."
124. [Ken'ichirou] "No, no -- the fact that such training-brace-like devices existed in that era is real, actual examples still survive, and there's a good chance Schumann used one."
125. [Ken'ichirou] "There's a certain romance to a historical artist wearing something like a training brace, don't you think? So you're going to be that kind of artist too."
126. [Naoya] "That's not what people call romance."
127. [Ken'ichirou] "But thanks to that brace, you can paint like me now, can't you?"
128. [Naoya] "Ah, that's true enough. But whatever the twist of fate, just like your beloved Schumann, I ended up unable to use my dominant arm."
129. [Ken'ichirou] "Look at it the other way -- maybe it only ended up that mild because of my training brace."
130. [Naoya] "I won't deny it. Back then, my right hand alone could win at arm-wrestling against grown men with ease."
131. [Naoya] "So if you'd strapped it to my left hand too, maybe by now I'd be able to paint with my left."
132. [Ken'ichirou] "No, that trick only works because a kid's doing it. Once you're an adult with a fully grown body, just do normal strength training."
133. [Naoya] "I do strength training. The menu you left me is more athlete than painter."
134. [Ken'ichirou] "Ah, that menu I left you -- you're properly working through it?"
135. [Naoya] "Well, the parts you told me to, yeah."
136. [Ken'ichirou] "And so? Does it look like you can paint with that left hand you've trained up?"
137. [Naoya] "Well, at the level of maybe passing the entrance exam to Geidai......"
138. [Ken'ichirou] "Don't lump passing something like Geidai in with my painting. I'm a world-class painter!"
139. [Naoya] "Which is exactly why I can't paint them. Your paintings, sure, but I can't even hold a candle to Kei's."
140. [Ken'ichirou] "And yet you're going to ask me about the pigment formulations I use."
141. [Ken'ichirou] "Fufufufu, you say fabricating it is impossible, but you're already starting the preparations.\nGood grief, I've got a fine son."
142. [Naoya] "That part of me takes after you."
143. [Ken'ichirou] "Well, fine. The pigment chart is properly on file. Ask Friedman and he'll produce it. 'The Reclining Sakura' won the Platine Epaulard only because Friedman of the Moore Foundation was backing it."
144. [Ken'ichirou] "So from the time I was conceiving that work, Friedman's been organizing my materials and so on."
145. [Naoya] "Materials. That's news to me. That changes a lot."
146. [Ken'ichirou] "Oho. You're serious about this.\nThe masterwork of a world-class artist -- forging it as a seven-part series."

