65. [Ken'ichirou] "More importantly, Naoya, what do you think criticism means in art?"
66. [Naoya] "It's what critics and reviewers do, isn't it?"
67. [Ken'ichirou] "Ha. Someone who says that is third-rate. No good at all."
68. [Naoya] "Then what is it!"
69. [Ken'ichirou] "A closed world is not art.\nAnd going further, a closed world must not be perfect."
70. [Naoya] "A closed world? Must not be perfect?"
71. [Ken'ichirou] "That's right. That's what it means for a sound to ring out in a soundless forest of cherry blossoms."
72. [Naoya] "Those are your words, aren't they? The verse that accompanies 'The Reclining Sakura.'"
73. [Naoya] "That verse is too obscure, and even more, I don't understand at all what you're saying now.\nDo you have any intention of explaining to me?"
74. [Ken'ichirou] "Yeah, I do. Want me to give a simple commentary, then?"
75. [Naoya] "Do it."
76. [Ken'ichirou] "Art is completed by being seen."
77. [Ken'ichirou] "A work is a corpse.\nBut a corpse that doesn't rot. Something like a clean specimen."
78. [Ken'ichirou] "A corpse is a corpse. No matter how high its degree of completion, it's a corpse.\nSo if the world is closed, it remains a corpse forever."
79. [Ken'ichirou] "Geniuses, for all their gifts, tend to live inside a closed world.\nAfter all, that world is perfect."
80. [Ken'ichirou] "The girl called Misakura Rin is precisely a genius.\nIn the sense, too, that her works are closed."
81. [Ken'ichirou] "In that sense, one could say art is not genius-like but human."
82. [Ken'ichirou] "Because art is not a closed world."
83. [Naoya] "So Misakura Rin is that much of a genius......"
84. [Ken'ichirou] "I also had her listen to the theories of art left behind by the great figures. Aristotle and the like."
85. [Naoya] "Speaking of Aristotle -- you mean that famous line?"
86. [Ken'ichirou] "Art imitates nature."
87. [Ken'ichirou] "But it didn't seem to suit Rin. The exact opposite thought seemed to sit better with her. She was fond of things like 'The Critic as Artist' and 'The Decay of Lying.'"
88. [Naoya] "What's that? I don't know. Books like that."
89. [Ken'ichirou] "They're not books you need.\nThe idea that everything in the world is nothing but an imitation of art -- it's incompatible with your artistic sensibility."
90. [Ken'ichirou] "It's called aestheticism. Well, it probably suited a genius like Rin."
91. [Ken'ichirou] "But aestheticism, too, places importance on the intervention of another's eye -- so it was convenient for teaching Rin, who lived inside the closed world of a genius."
92. [Ken'ichirou] "I was the one who introduced Kuzu and Rin."
93. [Ken'ichirou] "I thought those two would work out well together..."
94. [Ken'ichirou] "I thought that what Rin needed first of all was an audience.\nAnd not just any audience -- one that wouldn't be easily moved."
95. [Naoya] "Why is it bad to be moved?"
96. [Ken'ichirou] "For Rin, other people's eyes are of no use whatsoever."
97. [Ken'ichirou] "She probably knew it innately. That every human being has a lesser sense of beauty than her own, a lesser imagination. Naturally, at the ultimate reaches of beauty she attains, an audience holds no value at all."
98. [Ken'ichirou] "Even I was captivated in an instant by that girl's paintings.\nHer paintings move you -- that's a given. Other people's eyes are no measure at all."
99. [Ken'ichirou] "Look, that girl -- when she was two years old, she supposedly drew a picture that startled her parents so badly that ever since, she never drew in front of them again."
100. [Naoya] "Why? Her father's a painter and a dealer, isn't he?\nWouldn't he be delighted if his daughter was that much of a genius?"
101. [Ken'ichirou] "That's just it. She knew, unconsciously. That her parents were lesser than her.\nAnd she didn't want to admit it either."
102. [Ken'ichirou] "That's why she never drew in front of her parents."
103. [Naoya] "I see. Hearing that, I understand a little of what you felt about Rin......"
104. [Ken'ichirou] "Right? She was a girl who was, when it came to painting -- or rather, when it came to beauty -- cursed from birth."
105. [Naoya] "Then why did you introduce a girl like that to Kuzu?"
106. [Ken'ichirou] "Back then. Kuzu had no such thing as emotions."
107. [Naoya] "No emotions?"
108. [Ken'ichirou] "Yeah. Since she hadn't fully awakened as Hakuki, the Nakamura family didn't know, but that was probably a state peculiar to a girl with Hakuki's disposition."
109. [Ken'ichirou] "I wanted to give a girl like Kuzu emotions -- so that she could live as an ordinary girl, ordinarily."
110. [Ken'ichirou] "Unfortunately, not Ai, and not even I, a painter, could give Kuzu emotions."
111. [Ken'ichirou] "But I thought that Rin might.\nWith Rin, it even seemed possible to bring a heart into being out of nothing......"
112. [Naoya] "That's more sheer praise than sounds like you......"
113. [Ken'ichirou] "Jealous?"
114. [Naoya] "No. I can't paint anymore. There's no way for me to be jealous through painting."
115. [Ken'ichirou] "Hmph...... I see."
116. [Ken'ichirou] "To place a heart in a girl who has none."
117. [Ken'ichirou] "Even for the genius Rin, it wasn't an easy thing."
118. [Ken'ichirou] "A Kuzu who showed no reaction even to her paintings."
119. [Ken'ichirou] "For the first time, Rin had met such a person."
120. [Ken'ichirou] "So Rin went on painting in front of the girl who had no heart."
121. [Ken'ichirou] "Every day, every day, she painted."
122. [Ken'ichirou] "Everything in this world,\nand everything that isn't in this world."
123. [Naoya] "Everything in this world, let alone everything not in this world -- you can't paint that......"
124. [Ken'ichirou] "No, Rin's paintings had the power to make others believe it."
125. [Ken'ichirou] "You could call her a monster of beauty. A girl cursed by beauty."
126. [Ken'ichirou] "Because she was such a girl, she was able to place a heart in Kuzu."
127. [Ken'ichirou] "The one who changed Kuzu from Hakuki into a human was Misakura Rin."
128. [Ken'ichirou] "And the one who gave Rin an audience was Kuzu."
129. [Ken'ichirou] "That was when Rin came to know the joy of showing her work to others."
130. [Ken'ichirou] "From then on, just a little, her paintings ceased to be a closed world.\nThough even so, she kept painting things that seemed cursed by beauty."
131. [Naoya] "I can't imagine Rin as that kind of person."
132. [Ken'ichirou] "When she wasn't painting, Rin was really a gentle girl.\nJust a little awkward."
133. [Naoya] "That much I know too."
134. [Ken'ichirou] "She's kind at the core. That girl -- there's no way to put it except that she was cursed."
135. [Naoya] "Why would Rin have something like a curse?"
136. [Ken'ichirou] "That, I can't say."
137. [Ken'ichirou] "To put it this way. Suppose a god existed, and if that god were perfect,\nthere'd be no reason for the god to make this world, would there?"
138. [Ken'ichirou] "That's right, being perfect. That alone is complete. So nothing more is needed. There's no reason for god to make a world."
139. [Ken'ichirou] "It's about that unfathomable."
140. [Naoya] "It's your analogy that's too difficult and unfathomable to me."
141. [Ken'ichirou] "Really? Intuitively, I find it strange that a perfect god, who, being perfect, ought to suffice on his own, would nevertheless make a world."
142. [Ken'ichirou] "And I feel that this sensation resembles the mystery of Rin."
143. [Naoya] "I don't get it at all."
144. [Ken'ichirou] "She says she has a god within her. If that's so, there's no meaning in producing works outward."
145. [Ken'ichirou] "In any case, if she feels a god within herself, and thinks that god is more valuable than anything else, then that is a strong god."
146. [Naoya] "A strong god?"
147. [Ken'ichirou] "Yeah. I once told Rin that her god was a strong god.\nShe was laughing......"
148. [Naoya] "A strong god......"

=== lines=147 stop_reason=end_turn in=7334 out=3718 cache_read=3222 cache_create=0 last_entry_continued=0 ===
1. "You get it?"
2. "Ah, right, Naoya, you're an idiot, aren't you."
3. "That's because you're talking like you've given up on explaining. You're the idiot."
4. "Well, my lack of explanatory skill is, admittedly, a problem in its own right......"
5. "Anyway, I get that Rin was a genius.\nAnd that a girl like her needed Kuzu. So? What came after?"
6. "After that, huh...... After that, there was that great fire."
7. "The great fire......"
8. "And with that, everything was reset.\nReset? No, maybe that's not quite it either."
9. "Rin lost a lot in that fire."
10. "Yeah, that's right. You lost a lot in it too, but you and Rin aren't the only ones who lost things. That whole affair is tangled up in complicated ways."
11. "Rin's father and you tried to hide the truth of that incident.\nBelieving that would be for Rin's sake."
12. "But it wasn't only the two of you who thought that way.\nAbout that accident, a lot of people are trying to hide the truth."
13. "Just as you chose to do."
14. "It's a fact that I tried to hide it.\nBut the way you put it -- you mean there's a truth beyond mine as well?"
15. "Yeah, exactly."
16. "You only know one side of that incident. There's a different aspect to it."
17. "A different aspect......"
18. "The thousand-year cherry, and Rin's power of materialization -- you could only call it a superhuman ability.\nIf, hypothetically, the two of them ever met, what would happen......"
19. "You know it well enough yourself, don't you.\nMiraculous wishes come true."
20. "That may sound wonderful, but in truth it's nothing but bending the laws of the world."
21. "Laws twisted out of shape are always offset by something else."
22. "All the more so if that were to bury a miracle that had already happened under a further miracle......"
23. "The distortion would wreck every human relationship in reach......"
24. "A miracle covered by a further miracle......\nCould that possibly be......"
25. "Well, the moral you can take from it is that cleaning up after a 'miracle' with another 'miracle' isn't a very wise thing to do."
26. Ken'ichirou gave a small nod, and then--
27. "But that's as far as this goes."
28. "I won't talk about what comes beyond that."
29. "Why won't you talk......"
30. "If it's necessary, when the time comes, the ones involved will speak."
31. "Not to invoke 'to hide it is the flower,' but things kept hidden shouldn't be dragged out recklessly."
32. "It's been concealed. If there's no need for it, then it should all be forgotten. Even you can understand that much, can't you?"
33. "Not convinced?"
34. "No, I get most of it."
35. "Doesn't look like it, though."
36. "Well, if I'm told Rin was that much of a genius......, of course I can't stay calm about it."
37. "Ah, that. But even if you do meet Rin again, there's nothing to fear. She's probably just the ordinary, gentle girl you knew."
38. "Not a girl cursed by beauty."
39. "That talent is gone now."
40. Somehow, I felt everything beginning to connect inside me.
41. "Alright, I get it for now."
42. "I don't think I'll be seeing Rin again, though."
43. "Don't say that. Rin seemed to like you an awful lot, you know."
44. "Really? In front of a transcendent artist like Rin, I was just a mediocre painter, wasn't I."
45. "Even a transcendent artist is still a girl. She falls in love too."
46. "You're a real ladykiller, huh."
47. "Ladykiller...... Even if it were like you think, it'd only be Rin."
48. "Idiot. There's also the girl who was given a heart by Rin."
49. "Rin used to talk about you a lot while she painted......"
50. "An artist who gave a heart to a heartless girl.\nIf that's so, what feelings do you suppose that born heart would be shaped by......"
51. "Well, I've been with that girl for a long time too, so I think I understand it well enough......"
52. "She's more than you deserve, really......"
53. "So what are you even talking about?"
54. "Ah, never mind."
55. "What is it?"
56. "Rin gave Kuzu a heart. That influence probably shows up in all sorts of places. That's what I think."
57. "I said, what kind of influence? I have no idea what you're on about."
58. "When you have sex with Kuzu, make sure to use protection."
59. "Wh-- why Kuzu?! And why would I do that in the first place?! Are you stupid?!"
60. "Don't shout in a hospital like that. Ow, my ears."
61. "You take after me -- you're pretty popular. Well, that's because I'm this good-looking."
62. "I heard from Kuzu. That you said you really, really hate that I look like you."
63. "Well, one handsome guy is enough. I think everyone should just fall for me alone."
64. "Then you should've just remarried after Mom died."
65. "Idiot. Kusanagi blood runs true. Just like that old Natsume granny, we can only love one person."
66. "Yeah, right. I still think you were making a mess of women in New York."
67. "Are you dumb? As if I'd be like you. You've got that hopeless virgin thing going -- you're the one who's guaranteed to run around screwing women once you finally get a taste."
68. "No way in hell. Unlike you, I'm the genuine article -- a one-woman man."
69. "Yeah, right. You're a virgin so you don't know it yet, but I've got a track record of staying loyal all the way through."
70. "That's exactly what I can't take on faith."
71. "On what basis are you even saying that? An idiot like you, just go home!"
72. "I'm going home whether you tell me to or not! Idiot."
73. "Shut up. Idiot."
74. "The one who calls people idiots is the idiot."
75. "You said it first. Idiot."
76. I stood up from my seat.
77. And headed for the door.
78. "But -- I'm grateful. Thanks to you, Mom was happy."
79. "That's wrong. That Mizuna was happy right up to the end was thanks to you. I'm grateful."
80. "Then I won't be coming by for a while. Don't you go dying before things are done."
81. "Like I'd die. I'll die after I've watched you finish the job properly."
82. "One last thing."
83. "When you take custody of Kuzu, be sure to get your hands on the sacred implement the Nakamura family keeps -- the Hakuki kagura-suzu."
84. "Hakuki kagura-suzu? What's that?"
85. "As long as it's in their hands, Kuzu's safety can't be guaranteed."
86. "Even with a written pledge?"
87. "You keep throwing nothing but hard problems at me......"
88. "Well, of course. It's because I trust you."
89. "Gee, thanks for that......"
90. I burst out of the door.
91. I finished dinner as usual, drew a bath, and was reading a book when Kuzu came into the living room, fresh from her bath.
92. "You're done with the bath?"
93. "Guess it's my turn to get in, then."
94. "Were you able to talk properly with Ken'ichirou?"
95. "I'm sorry. For hiding things about Rin all this time......"
96. "Huh? Wh-- what are you talking about? Hiding, whatever -- there wasn't ever a chance to bring it up, was there?"
97. "Yeah, that's true, but...... I was still trying to hide it, and there are things I haven't told you yet."
98. "Then we're even. I've never told you about Rin either."
99. "That really is a case of never having the chance."
100. "Six of one, half a dozen of the other."
101. "Naoya, you really haven't been in contact with Rin since then?"
102. "Is it because you don't want to make Rin remember that incident?"
103. "Yeah. She's got amnesia from the shock of it."
104. "Do people really lose their memories from shock......?"
105. "No, it happens, doesn't it? It's a subject that gets used in manga and dramas all the time."
106. "Mm, maybe...... but."
107. "Come on, that's enough. Just don't worry about it -- Rin is a thing of the past."
108. "A thing of the past...... That way of putting it is too lonely."
109. "Even so. In reality, I broke off contact so she wouldn't remember the past."
110. "I know that...... but that phrasing is lonely."
111. "I love Rin."
112. "So......"
113. "My heart is like a copy of Rin's, after all......"
114. "Your heart belongs to you......"
115. "That's not what I meant. I -- what I feel about Naoya--"
116. "About me?"
117. Kuzu's face turned bright red.
118. "No, it's nothing. Good night."
119. "Yeah, good night......"
120. Kuzu left the living room as if fleeing.
121. "Even if I say good night, Kuzu doesn't actually sleep, though......"
122. "Well then."
123. I pulled out my smartphone.
124. Two emails, identical in wording, sent to two people.
125. There's no time.
126. Starting tomorrow, I'll have to get to work.
127. "Oh my, well, welcome."
128. "Is the second floor open?"
129. "Second floor? It's open, but?"
130. "Then I'll take it. And don't let anyone else up to the second floor."
131. "Huh? What are you talking about? That'd kill business."
132. "In that case, I want the whole place to ourselves. I'll pay for that too."
133. "Friedman."
134. "Who? Is this person?"
135. "Ah, an acquaintance of my old man."
136. "Also, if possible, I'd like the young lady to step out as well."
137. "Eh? Me too?"
138. "It's an important negotiation concerning Kusanagi Ken'ichirou. Ordinarily I'd want to use a rented conference room......"
139. "But we happened to be unable to book one."
140. "Fine, whatever. I'll let the master know.\nBut we can't have literally no one here, so I'll be waiting in the back, just in case."
141. "Yeah, that's not a problem."
142. "Honestly, Kusanagi, you're as selfish as ever. Normally I couldn't accept a demand like that -- telling even the staff to leave."
143. "Yeah, sorry."
144. "Well, just the fact that your eyes aren't dead -- that alone makes me willing to do something for you."
145. "It's been a while since I've seen you with such good eyes. Fine, then. This time of day doesn't get many customers anyway.\nSo? An hour ought to do?"
146. "That's enough."
147. "Then, payment up front~"

=== lines=149 stop_reason=end_turn in=8212 out=5118 cache_read=3222 cache_create=0 last_entry_continued=0 ===
1. "Ah, but give me a receipt."
2. Toritani lightly tidied up the shop, then stepped out to give us space.
3. Say what you will, the guy really does put up with my unreasonable demands.
4. "Has Toritani gone?"
5. "You still avoid Toritani as much as ever, huh."
6. "She pries into things and gets on my nerves. Besides, just being seen with Kusanagi would be enough to arouse suspicion."
7. "That's because you're always doing suspicious things."
8. "Save the chitchat for when you're alone together. Let's get straight to the point."
9. "More importantly -- why are we holding this strategy meeting here? Wouldn't a rented conference room really have been better?"
10. "Ah, that. A lot of the real estate around this area has Nakamura's fingers in it. If you're not careful, you can end up in serious trouble."
11. "In fact, that trouble at the moving company the other day was exactly the sort of thing that can happen."
12. "There's no problem here?"
13. "Right. The Nakamura family's reach doesn't extend to this place."
14. "For now, I'll trust Naoya. So, to the main topic..."
15. "That's right! The main topic! Kusanagi! Can you really produce a forgery of Kusanagi Ken'ichirou's work?"
16. "'Who knows?' isn't good enough! My life is on the line here!"
17. "What are you talking about? You're the one who got egged on by Friedman."
18. "Originally, all I asked was for him to keep watch on Kusanagi Naoya."
19. "Really now. Didn't you set this up knowing exactly what would happen?"
20. "Think what you like...... but that sort of action was not something I proposed."
21. This whole Nakamura affair. Well, to state the conclusion first, the inside story was pretty much what it looked like on the surface.
22. Friedman knew about the connection between my old man and the Nakamura family.\nSo after returning to Japan, thinking something would happen between the Kusanagi and Nakamura families, he apparently asked Akashi -- an acquaintance of mine -- to keep watch.
23. How Friedman and Akashi came to know each other, I have no idea.\nOr rather, it doesn't matter.
24. If anyone would be prowling around my house all the time, it would probably be Akashi or Kei.
25. Kei is one of the Natsumes. That being the case, it inevitably had to be Akashi he asked.
26. Akashi should have limited himself to surveillance, but for some reason he tried to get involved in this whole affair.\nDid Friedman perhaps tell him there'd be a huge amount of money in it?
27. I don't know the truth, but either way, the result was this: Akashi would play the role of deceiving the Nakamura family, I would play the role of completing the forgeries, and Friedman would handle everything else.
28. "That commotion when Nakamura Shouichi came to our apartment -- that was all a script written by you, wasn't it, Friedman?"
29. "I handed Akashi Ken'ichirou's business card and the pledge document, but I never told him to burst in through the window."
30. "Well, no kidding. Nobody writes a script that stupid.\nBut everything else -- wouldn't you say it was pretty much all a script of your own writing?"
31. "If that's what you think, then that's how it must be."
32. Always with that irritating way of putting things, this man.
33. "So? Why did you pull a stunt like this? Was it out of obligation, helping Ken'ichirou, your longtime business partner?"
34. "Ah, that's right. He looked after me, after all. This is repayment for all he's done."
35. "I've heard from my old man that you only move for money. And that when the chips are down, you'll turn on anyone -- an outrageous bastard, that's what I heard."
36. "In that case, Ken'ichirou was mistaken.\nI am a thoroughly sincere man."
37. What a shameless line to deliver with a straight face.
38. After dragging Akashi into something this dangerous, there's no way that's true.
39. "So? Since you've stuck your neck into this, does that mean there's actually money in it?"
40. "If you deliver work that satisfies me, yes."
41. "I'm a businessman, you see. It's my policy never to do anything that won't turn a profit.\nWhich is why I have high hopes for Kusanagi Naoya."
42. "High hopes, huh. Even supposing I complete the forgeries, wouldn't they be exposed almost immediately?"
43. "Well, likely so. Even if Nakamura Shouichi can be fooled, the Moore Foundation can't be...... whether that's a matter of months or years, I don't know."
44. "Then isn't it pointless?"
45. "Thomas Hoving, the former director of the Metropolitan Museum of Art, said this. Of the works brought to him during his sixteen-year tenure, sixty percent were fakes."
46. "And on top of that, the New York Times has stated that forty percent of the major works sold on the art market are forgeries."
47. "Forty percent? That many are forgeries?"
48. "There are far more forgeries in circulation among artworks than the general public imagines. For example, Jean-Baptiste-Camille Corot is said to have around 2,500 authentic works, yet the collections in the United States alone are said to contain 7,800."
49. "Theodore Rousseau, the 19th-century French painter, put it this way."
50. "We should recognize that we can only speak of poor forgeries -- the ones that get exposed. The well-made forgeries still hang on the walls."
51. "In other words, the art world is sustained by forgeries......\nThat's a terrifying thought."
52. "Forgery in art is like a twin younger brother. Only the elder gets the attention, but really, forgeries can be said to be a part of art history itself."
53. "Even so, is it really all right for someone from the Moore Foundation to be involved with forgeries?"
54. "Heh. Do you think an art dealer has some grand sense of ethics or something?"
55. "No. If art dealers had any grand sense of ethics, forty percent of the major works in circulation wouldn't be forgeries, would they."
56. "Exactly. Art, in every respect, is built on the complicity of dealers and artists. That goes for authentic works and forgeries alike."
57. "Besides, forgeries aren't something you can just make and be done with. By attaching a suitable narrative to them, you can sell them off as more convincingly genuine."
58. "Yeah, I got to see that skill on display in the whole Nakamura Shouichi business. I never imagined someone from the Moore Foundation would put on a farce like that."
59. "A farce? I told no lies whatsoever in that room. I only spoke the truth."
60. "That's right. It was all arranged to look like whatever Akashi came up with."
61. "What's that? Me? I'm the only one at fault?"
62. "Didn't you notice? Aside from calling you 'the world-renowned Watar Akkashi,' Friedman said nothing but perfectly proper things."
63. "Friedman even went so far as to warn Nakamura Shouichi -- 'Do not sign a shady pledge document from someone like him!'"
64. "Th-then, if we're caught, I'm the only one going to solitary?"
65. "No, that's not how it works. Either way, if everything comes to light, every person in this room will be arrested for fraud. The justice system isn't that lenient."
66. "That would be bad."
67. "That's why we have to think through various ways to keep it from coming to that."
68. "No, you don't need to concern yourselves with that side of things.\nI'll handle everything."
69. "Is that so? As expected of Mr. Friedman."
70. "Leave the underhanded work to you, then?"
71. "Correct. It is our job to have the artist produce a fine work. That's all."
72. "Really now. Aren't you actually holding some means of pinning the forgery on us and slipping away yourself?"
73. "As I've said repeatedly, if it develops into a criminal case, we'd all be dragged in like a chain of sweet potatoes."
74. "Then what means do you have in mind?"
75. "I'm not an art dealer for show. I have a pretty good grasp of what kinds of paintings Nakamura Shouichi has acquired."
76. "It's not exactly uncommon for paintings to be used in money laundering."
77. "Trace the flow of paintings and you arrive at the flow of money. And that money is by no means all clean. In fact, the black end of it likely outweighs the white."
78. "Wait -- Friedman. You've got hold of that guy's dirty tail, haven't you?"
79. "I always keep risk management in mind."
80. "I see. In other words, by the time the six works of the seven-part series are deemed forgeries, it already means the end of him?"
81. "That's right. Those six works will only be labeled forgeries after he's arrested for his misdealings and driven to a situation where he has to sell them off."
82. "That said, Nakamura Shouichi will only get a few years in prison. There's the possibility of revenge after his release."
83. "Which brings us to the problem of you. Akashi is using a false name and a disguise. And further, the arrangement is that Nakamura will never realize, to the very end, that I was involved. However......"
84. "I'm the only one who'll be suspected. I'm the one who benefits from this sale.\nWell, I'll be on my guard against revenge a few years from now."
85. "By then, Nakamura Shouichi will be old. I doubt he'd come after you once he's already lost everything......"
86. "I hope that's the case."
87. "About when will the forgeries be exposed?"
88. "Who knows. Could be months, could be years. It's even possible they'll never be exposed."
89. "The line between forgery and authentic work is vague. Can a forgery hanging in a museum as an authentic work truly be called a forgery?"
90. "So, they might never be exposed?"
91. "Entirely possible."
92. "In that case, my safety would be assured too, which is a relief......"
93. "More importantly, what's the deadline for completing the forgeries?"
94. "Finish them in three weeks."
95. "Three weeks? Six of those massive works?"
96. "No, that's a reasonable line. Dragging it out too long puts us at a disadvantage. The risk of exposure grows."
97. "There's no telling how long Ken'ichirou has left. Keep to this deadline."
98. "Understood. I'll manage in three weeks."
99. At my reply, Friedman gave a wry smile.
100. "Heh, that was a quick answer."
101. "What's so funny?"
102. "It's a task that seems all but impossible. And here you accept it so easily -- I find that amusing."
103. "Ah, maybe so. But I don't mind this sort of adversity.\nIf I can somehow overcome the crisis, I'll spare no effort."
104. "If anything, what I hate most is leaving things to fate and doing nothing."
105. "I see. I'd heard you'd given up painting, but you truly are Kusanagi Ken'ichirou's son. The two of you are alike."
106. "That's the thing that pisses us both off the most to hear."
107. "So, what do we do from here?"
108. "I'll contact Nakamura. I'll persuade him again and again -- 'You should not engage in that kind of transaction with a suspicious party,' 'Let us handle it with you.'"
109. "Will he go along with that?"
110. "He won't."
111. "Then why?"
112. "Being told that will make him try to conclude the actual contract all the sooner."
113. "True enough!"
114. "And? How does Friedman himself intend to profit from this?"
115. "Simple. As long as the forgeries are valued at more than Ken'ichirou's debt, that's all that matters."
116. "I see. Give the forgeries a value greater than the debt, and profit off the surplus."
117. "How do you set the market price?"
118. "That's why I keep contacting him. I'll drill into his head just how valuable Kusanagi Ken'ichirou's seven-part series would be, if it were genuine."
119. "The more earnestly I explain to him, the less he'll trust me -- and the more he'll come to believe the fabled seven-part series carries considerable value."
120. "I see. The more you explain, the more the individual is doubted. However, precisely because you're so earnest, the thing itself must have value -- that's the impression you're arranging to create."
121. "I'll cite a figure exceeding my target amount. Akashi will present roughly the same figure at the negotiation. That way, the other side will believe that's the correct market price."
122. "Sure enough, if the same price emerges from two directions, you'd take it as the fair market rate."
123. "That said, being tight with money, Nakamura will try to haggle. Akashi should negotiate for a bit and then relent. If he manages to knock the price down, Nakamura will think he got a bargain."
124. "I see. So you build the discount into the price from the start."
125. "That way, we can more easily control the price. Here's the rough estimate for the price and expenses."
126. So saying, Friedman laid out a chart lined with detailed figures.
127. "Is this the itemized breakdown of every amount? You've planned it out to this level of detail......"
128. "Still, that's quite the sum......"
129. "Yeah, but what bothers me is the amount Nakamura is short by to buy the forgeries -- he must want cash so badly his hands are practically reaching out of his throat. How do you plan to squeeze the rest out of him?"
130. "That's already solved. So long as Akashi moves exactly as I tell him, there won't be a problem."
131. "As long as the amount comes out to enough to secure Kuzu, I have no complaints.\nI'm content to leave the surplus entirely to Friedman......"
132. "That's fine by me as well."
133. "Are you really okay with that? Trusting him completely?"
134. "What are you saying, Naoya.\nIf you don't trust me in the first place, how do you propose to put this negotiation together?"
135. "No, it's not all of it, but isn't the method of collecting the surplus especially unclear?"
136. "The guy has several paintings among his assets.\nAn amusingly large share are fakes, but some are the real thing."
137. "You mean to collect via paintings?"
138. "Well, something like that."
139. "Collecting in paintings, huh......"
140. "You object?"
141. "I have no problem, but if we collect in paintings, converting them back into cash on our end will require the appropriate channels."
142. "Ah, those channels I'll provide."
143. "'There's no way to arrange it except through Friedman' -- wouldn't that be the more accurate way to put it?"
144. "Perhaps so. And if it is, what will you do?"
145. "No, I have no problem, but...... Akashi?"
146. "No problem."
147. "Then it's settled. There are no other options.\nOr do you, on your end, have some clever alternative in mind?"
148. "No, nothing like that......"
149. "Well, that's how it is. There were no options from the very start."

