119. But starting tomorrow, I won't have time for such leisurely nonsense.
120. Not all of it, of course, but the art-supply shopkeeper managed to get me a fair portion of the pigments.
121. Carrying them, I head for the Natsume estate.
122. Naturally, the location is that atelier.
123. [Akashi] "Huff, huff, this is heavy. What's in this box?"
124. [Friedman] "Naoya's order. Even I don't know why I was made to procure such things."
125. [Naoya] "Because I need them. More importantly, get inside quickly. If you wander around the Natsume courtyard too long, you'll be spotted by one of the Natsume family."
126. I open the heavy storehouse door.
127. Then I turn on the light.
128. [Friedman] "So this is...... Kusanagi Ken'ichirou's atelier......"
129. [Naoya] "Yeah, that's right. Quite different from the one in New York, isn't it?"
130. [Friedman] "Yeah, but it's not unusual for unsold artists to use garages. The place itself doesn't surprise me."
131. [Naoya] "Really? Then what surprised you?"
132. [Friedman] "Because I understood what you're planning."
133. [Naoya] "Seriously? You figured it out just from stepping in here?"
134. [Friedman] "Yeah -- though when you asked me to bring four dozen of those adjustable-strength muscle-training springs, even I couldn't figure that one out."
135. [Akashi] "What're you two on about? I've no idea what's going on here."
136. [Naoya] "Well, normally you wouldn't. Friedman's the odd one for figuring it out the moment he stepped into this storehouse."
137. [Friedman] "No, I've thought a few times myself about whether Ken'ichirou's brushwork could somehow be reproduced."
138. [Naoya] "Even so, you're sharp to catch on."
139. [Friedman] "Conversely, Naoya -- how did you come up with this technique?"
140. [Naoya] "Since I was a kid, I've had these muscle-training springs on my hands. I'm intimately familiar with them."
141. [Naoya] "When I was little, I tried suspending myself from the springs on a whim, and it went unexpectedly well. It was just play, really."
142. [Friedman] "I see. So then, this method isn't something you came up with overnight?"
143. [Naoya] "No, fine-tuning is definitely still needed. This time it's not for play.\nAnd whether such a shabby method can really reproduce Kusanagi Ken'ichirou's brushwork is highly doubtful."
144. [Friedman] "But you'll do it anyway, right?"
145. [Naoya] "Yeah. So, mind giving me a hand? Both of you."

