1. [Akashi] "Help you?"
2. [Naoya] "Yeah, building the stage apparatus for the forgery."
3. Five-inch nails are driven into the four sides of the storehouse.
4. It's something that could only be done thanks to the storehouse's massive pillars.
5. Springs are hooked onto those five-inch nails on all four sides.
6. The training springs are each small on their own, but they can be linked together.
7. Extending them from the four sides toward the center, they're finally fastened to a ring-shaped piece at the middle.
8. What's important here is that the strength of each spring can be individually adjusted.\nBy loosening one of the springs on one side and tightening the opposite, the center point where the springs cross can be shifted.
9. [Naoya] "It's an apparatus to reproduce Ken'ichirou's brushwork. Still not complete, though."
10. [Friedman] "Ken'ichirou's dominant arm was extremely strong.\nNo, it's said his brushwork only comes into being because of that arm strength."
11. [Naoya] "Kusanagi Ken'ichirou's brushwork is said to be very soft.\nUnthinkably long brushes, manipulated with only the fingertips -- that's what produces that unique brush-feel."
12. [Akashi] "Powerful, yet soft......"
13. [Naoya] "You paint, Akashi, so you should get it.\nManipulating an absurdly long brush with just the fingers -- that means holding the brush with the arm perfectly still in midair."
14. [Naoya] "A brush supported by the arm's extraordinary muscle strength,\nfrom which is born a distinctive delicate, soft line, expressed solely through the fingers."
15. [Naoya] "'The Reclining Sakura' in particular pursues that softness to its very limit.\nThe reason he could give form to that impression of fleeting death lies in Kusanagi Ken'ichirou's rare arm strength."
16. [Akashi] "S-so that's it, that was the secret of his brushwork. That's why you're reinforcing with springs to complete the painting."
17. [Naoya] "That's how it works."
18. [Akashi] "I see, that's really instructive. So if it's got elasticity, you can reproduce that brush!"
19. [Naoya] "Instructive? What -- are you planning to reproduce Kusanagi Ken'ichirou's brushwork too, Akashi?"
20. [Akashi] "N-no, it's not like that."
