43. [Friedman] "One after another -- Kusanagi Naoya, you really do come up with all kinds of ideas. In a sense, you're a rare kind of artist."
44. [Naoya] "You're overestimating me. I just think about a lot of things. Jack of all trades, master of none."
45. [Friedman] "Jack of all trades, huh. Well, I'll keep it in mind."
46. The first day was consumed entirely by adjusting the springs stretched across the four sides.
47. This work has to be done as carefully as -- or more carefully than -- the painting itself.\nIf the brushwork isn't precise, the entire series could go to waste.
48. The work finished at dawn.\nAfter that, I took a nap.
49. I wake to pain throughout my body.
50. The storehouse floor is hard, as expected.
51. [Naoya] "Hm? A blanket?"
52. Though I'd simply lain down where I was for my nap, there was a blanket over me, and warmth being provided by a hibachi.
