110. I welcome them into the atelier.
111. [Akashi] "That's one huge canvas......"
112. [Naoya] "Yeah, it was a real pain. I'm grateful to the old man at the art-supply shop."
113. [Friedman] "So, were you able to give the piece its form?"
114. [Naoya] "That's exactly what I called you here to see."
115. [Friedman] "A series depicting corpses...... Now, how did it turn out."
116. [Akashi] "Corpses? A series of corpses?"
117. [Naoya] "That's right.\nThe Nine Aspects of Death -- depicting a person dying and rotting away."
118. [Naoya] "The Nine Aspects of Death: a Buddhist painting that divides into nine stages the decay of a corpse abandoned in the open."
119. [Naoya] "As the name suggests, the Nine Aspects depict nine transitions of death."
120. [Naoya] "In the first, shortly after death, the corpse is depicted beautifully."
121. [Naoya] "That first death, in this series, becomes 'The Reclining Sakura.'"
122. [Naoya] "The death depicted in the second is called chousou.\nHere the corpse is depicted swollen and grotesque."
123. [Naoya] "It probably expresses the way the body bloats internally from the gases of decay."
124. [Naoya] "The next form of death is called esou.\nDecay progresses further, and the skin festers, splits, and reveals the flesh beneath."
125. [Naoya] "The next form is called kechizusou.\nThe corpse's decay advances further -- flesh tears, fat festers, and blood and bodily fluids soak the earth."
126. [Naoya] "Next is nouransou -- the entire corpse dissolves."
127. [Naoya] "Next is shouousou.\nThe corpse changes color, becoming a dark blue-black."
128. [Naoya] "Tansou.\nMaggots swarm, flies swarm, insects clustering to death swarm."
129. [Naoya] "Sansou.\nThe corpse begins to scatter."
130. [Naoya] "Kotsusou.\nSkeletonization."
131. [Naoya] "And finally shousou.\nOnly ashes remain."
132. [Akashi] "W-what an eerie set of paintings......\nWhy would something like that exist?"
133. [Naoya] "Who knows. Maybe to express the impermanence of the world......\nAt any rate, this Nine Aspects depicts a beautiful woman. The concept is that something beautiful gradually becomes something horrifying to look upon."
134. [Naoya] "And there, 'The Reclining Sakura' -- said to express death, a beautiful death -- fits this subject perfectly."
135. [Naoya] "The nine phases I just described are only representative; the format varies. Sometimes it isn't even nine pieces."
