=== lines=146 stop_reason=end_turn in=6822 out=4668 cache_read=3222 cache_create=0 last_entry_continued=0 ===
1. But it felt oddly long.
2. [Ken'ichirou] "Right, then......"
3. The words that broke the silence came in a strangely deflating tone.
4. Suddenly, Kusanagi Ken'ichirou pulled off the brush I'd used for the forgery -- the one fitted into a spring -- and gripped it.
5. That impossibly long brush.
6. [Ken'ichirou] "Vermilion!"
7. He shouted for vermilion.
8. Grasping what he meant, I dissolved the pigment.
9. I held it out before Kusanagi Ken'ichirou.
10. [Ken'ichirou] "I'll inscribe the titles myself. Let these be my epitaph."
11. He took up the abnormally long brush.
12. The master Kusanagi Ken'ichirou's signature style.
13. Yet there was not the slightest wavering in midair.
14. His arm was immovable, as though the moment itself had frozen.
15. No need for any spring.\nThis was the true brush of Kusanagi Ken'ichirou.
16. From his fingertip the titles were struck.
17. 'The Sakura of Esou'
18. 'The Sakura of Kechizusou'
19. 'The Sakura of Nouransou'
20. 'The Sakura of Tansou'
21. 'The Sakura of Sansou'
22. 'The Sakura of Shousou'
23. The calligraphy of Kusanagi Ken'ichirou was set down.
24. [Ken'ichirou] "I'll carve my name into these."
25. [Naoya] "Yeah, do that, and this hexaptych will be perfect as a forgery."
26. [Ken'ichirou] "Perfect as a forgery?\n......No, that's not it."
27. [Naoya] "What? Is there some flaw?"
28. [Ken'ichirou] "Yeah, as a forgery it's far too flawed."
29. [Naoya] "That's bad, then. I'll fix it."
30. [Ken'ichirou] "Idiot. As if you could fix it.\nThis belongs to me now......"
31. [Naoya] "To you?"
32. [Ken'ichirou] "A fitting end for a swindler without peer......"
33. [Ken'ichirou] "This is the epitaph you offered me.\nAnd that's why I carve my name here."
34. [Ken'ichirou] "This is not my work."
35. [Ken'ichirou] "It's the work Kusanagi Naoya painted for my death."
36. [Ken'ichirou] "My grave will be heaped with flowers, no doubt. But all that is show.\nMy true grave lies here, beside this painting."
37. [Ken'ichirou] "......Tonight, just a moment ago, I had a dream that I had died.\nStrangely enough, it was the moment I had lived happily."
38. [Ken'ichirou] "Naoya. Thank you."
39. [Ken'ichirou] "This painting suits me. This is exactly the sort of work that could be offered to my death......"
40. [Ken'ichirou] "It was a good life......"
41. [Ken'ichirou] "Because things like this happen......"
42. [Ken'ichirou] "Painting is a wonderful thing......"
43. [Ken'ichirou] "Most people put words to their feelings when they speak."
44. [Ken'ichirou] "But speech, in the end, is a rather empty thing."
45. [Ken'ichirou] "That's why the wise, most of them, keep silent."
46. [Ken'ichirou] "Idle chatter clouds the body."
47. [Ken'ichirou] "And from a clouded body only smoke-like words are born......"
48. [Ken'ichirou] "But art has no need of idle chatter."
49. [Ken'ichirou] "Only the honed meaning rises to the surface."
50. [Ken'ichirou] "That's how this work will be treated......"
51. [Ken'ichirou] "But that's fine."
52. [Ken'ichirou] "This is, after all, an epitaph for me......"
53. [Ken'ichirou] "I'll be dead soon."
54. [Ken'ichirou] "It felt long, and yet very short, and yet still long after all...... that's the sensation."
55. [Ken'ichirou] "When you come to a stop like this, at the final place, all sorts of things become clear."
56. [Ken'ichirou] "Thank you."
57. [Ken'ichirou] "Naoya, that's all I can say......"
58. My throat closed up.
59. I could no longer force words out.
60. [Ken'ichirou] "I kept painting all this time...... and I'm glad I did."
61. [Ken'ichirou] "Yeah, I'm glad......"
62. Kusanagi Ken'ichirou wrote the titles on the six paintings, then took the artist's seal from his breast and pressed it down.
63. Onto 'The Six Aspects of the Sakura' the name of Kusanagi Ken'ichirou was carved.
64. The day itself.
65. Calling it a negotiation felt anticlimactic.
66. Friedman had already arranged every step in advance, no doubt.
67. Besides, that lot had no talent for looking at paintings to begin with.
68. Showing them to Nakamura Shouichi was nothing next to being exposed to Friedman's judging eye.
69. Much less to the tension of setting them before Kusanagi Ken'ichirou.
70. The Nakamura family had brought along several professional art dealers of their own, but not one of them raised an objection.
71. Well, the strength of the paintings played a part, but what mattered most was surely that the inscriptions were in Kusanagi Ken'ichirou's own hand, and the artist's seal was authentic.
72. The transaction concluded without fuss.
73. Then, at the end, the matter of Kuzu's custody.
74. Through this sale, the hexaptych was appraised at the equivalent of 600 million yen, and Kusanagi Ken'ichirou held 900 million yen in cash. Together, 1.5 billion yen.
75. With this the debt was cleared in full, and Kuzu was to be released.
76. [Friedman] "What's wrong? Naoya? For someone who pulled it off, you look pretty downcast."
77. [Naoya] "Do I look it?"
78. With that, Friedman lit a cigarette.
79. [Friedman] "A guilty conscience, at this hour?"
80. [Friedman] "Especially in the art world. Being sold a fake, or acquiring a genuine work -- that's part of the buyer's ability. Even that is part of what makes it art."
81. [Naoya] "No, that's not it."
82. [Naoya] "I don't imagine Nakamura Shouichi will take it well when he finds out it's a fake, but as you say, this is a sale of art. He'll have prepared himself for that much."
83. [Friedman] "Either way, most of what he holds is forgeries. His eye is barely worth the name......"
84. [Naoya] "Even though he brought along all those specialists?"
85. [Friedman] "That's just for this occasion. Six works from the Seven Aspects landing at once -- the story was, frankly, too good, and I don't think he trusted his own judgment alone."
86. [Friedman] "More to the point, are you sure about that?"
87. [Friedman] "About sending those out into the world as forgeries of Kusanagi Ken'ichirou."
88. [Naoya] "Of course. That's what I painted them for."
89. [Friedman] "For that? For what?"
90. [Naoya] "It's obvious, isn't it. To save Kuzu."
91. [Friedman] "Kuzu, huh...... Ken'ichirou too kept working in New York all that time to save her......"
92. [Naoya] "Yeah, I suppose so. All of that -- every bit of it -- I've inherited."
93. [Friedman] "Inherited...... I see. Let's put it that way."
94. [Naoya] "What is it? That's an awfully roundabout way to put it."
95. [Friedman] "No, it's accurate. Along with hundreds of millions in assets, you inherited the debts.\nAnd you've finished paying them."
96. [Naoya] "Not yet, right?"
97. [Friedman] "Ah, that's true. As administrator, I'll go and get the formal signature."
98. [Naoya] "Right. When is that?"
99. [Friedman] "Ordinarily it would be after Kusanagi Ken'ichirou's death, but it's his wish. He wants the inheritance papers drawn up as soon as possible."
100. [Naoya] "Well, naturally. Until my renunciation of the inheritance is settled, Kuzu's safety can't be guaranteed."
101. [Friedman] "Kusanagi Ken'ichirou's 900 million yen in cash. Plus the hexaptych appraised at 600 million. Together they wipe out the 1.5 billion in debt between Ken'ichirou and the Nakamura family."
102. [Friedman] "That's what I settled with Nakamura Shouichi. Well, on the hexaptych there was a small surplus."
103. [Naoya] "A surplus, huh...... that's welcome news."
104. [Friedman] "I'll have several documents prepared. Read them over beforehand.\nIn a few days you'll be asked to sign the deed of gift."
105. [Naoya] "Several? Not just one?"
106. [Friedman] "Ah, right. Akashi's share is also for you alone to decide."
107. [Naoya] "I see. Then have Akashi sit in as well."
108. [Friedman] "Out of the question. Nominally this is Kusanagi Ken'ichirou's inheritance, so all of it comes down to your decision."
109. [Friedman] "Well, either way, not a single yen is going to be left to you lot......"
110. [Naoya] "Friedman. Did you just say something?"
111. [Friedman] "No, nothing. But did you actually read them?"
112. [Naoya] "Read what?"
113. [Friedman] "The written pledge with the Nakamura family. The one Akashi exchanged with them."
114. [Naoya] "No, of course not -- Akashi handed it directly to Nakamura Shouichi, so there was no chance for me to see it."
115. [Friedman] "Then you had best check one copy of that pledge. I have a copy on hand."
116. [Friedman] "It states exactly how large a share the Moore Foundation is to receive."
117. [Naoya] "H-hold on! Why is the Moore Foundation getting a share?!"
118. [Friedman] "I've said the same thing from the very start. It was the Moore Foundation that backed Kusanagi Ken'ichirou. I'd never approve of any sale that leaves nothing at all for the Foundation."
119. [Naoya] "Wh...... but how did you arrange for money to flow to the Moore Foundation?"
120. [Friedman] "The genuine paintings we took from Nakamura are to be sold off through me. I took the liberty of adjusting the middleman's margin on those sales a touch."
121. So that was it. The surplus we were to receive on the hexaptych -- the reason it was being made up not in cash but in paintings......
122. [Friedman] "But relax. If it's money you want, don't save the girl, and just accept the inheritance. Then 1.5 billion yen tumbles into your lap."
123. [Naoya] "You...... you bastard...... pulling a stunt as low as that......"
124. [Friedman] "Low? When you sign a contract, you have to read the text of it. That's the most basic of basics in a sale. All the more so with a written pledge."
125. [Naoya] "Th-that's why I'm saying, I didn't even have a chance to lay eyes on it."
126. [Friedman] "That excuse won't fly. A pledge is a pledge."
127. [Friedman] "At the signing of the deed of gift I'll confirm about three times, I suppose.\nWhether you renounce the inheritance or not."
128. [Friedman] "Three confirmations should be enough for you to make your decision......"
129. [Naoya] "Only three?"
130. [Friedman] "But you still have time to think it over. There's a while yet until the contract day."
131. [Naoya] "Friedman......"
132. [Friedman] "Hm? Look at the time. I'd best be off to my next job."
133. [Naoya] "H-hey! Friedman, you bastard!"
134. [Friedman] "Remember this. I don't do anything that doesn't make money.\nYou'd best be more careful from now on......"
135. And with that, Friedman was gone.
136. [???] "Well, we got taken for a ride, huh."
137. [Naoya] "Wait, you--"
138. [Akashi] "Not a single yen coming in, huh. And after we pulled off a deal worth hundreds of millions."
139. [Naoya] "You're okay with that?"
140. [Akashi] "But nothing to be done, right?\nOr shall we scrap the renunciation and split it with me?"
141. [Naoya] "No no, hold on. Even supposing I did inherit, why would I have to split it with you?"
142. [Akashi] "Come on, aren't we brothers-in-arms by now, or as good as brothers? You can call me elder brother from today on, you know?"
143. [Naoya] "What kind of forfeit game is that......"
144. [Akashi] "Forfeit nothing. To have a dependable elder brother like me is the height of good fortune."
145. [Naoya] "A dependable guy wouldn't sign a written pledge without reading through it."
146. [Akashi] "Gk...... but it was in English."

