31. "Telling me to come live with Kei at the Natsume estate, going to such lengths for my sake......\nno, that in itself makes me happy. Thank you, but..."
32. "This isn't a time to be meddling with someone like me, is what I mean."
33. "Really, you and your family......\nyou're all so meddlesome, so eager to look after people, and so very kind..."
34. Toritani repeated the same thing she had murmured in front of 'Olympia.'
35. "But that's not a good thing."
36. "Why not?"
37. "Because it makes people get the wrong idea."
38. "You know, Kusanagi, there was a time when I too wished\nthat we could go on living together forever. That's the truth."
39. "Kei and I, and..."
40. Toritani swallowed the rest of her words without finishing.
41. "But that wish didn't come true. And that's fine, now.\nDo you know why? It's because Kei is happy as Natsume Kei."
42. "Watching him these past three years, I've come to see that clearly."
43. "So, for me, that's enough now."
44. "The rest is my own problem."
45. "There's a lingering attachment about Kei that I can't shake. About that person.\nThere are parts I haven't sorted out."
46. "But that's something I'll take my time and handle on my own."
47. "I know that if I keep saying I'm fine it starts to sound like the opposite, but\nlet me say it anyway. I'm fine. I can manage on my own."
48. "There's no scene here for a hero."
49. "So go, go to someone who needs you."
50. Having said that, Toritani suddenly shifted to a lighter tone.
51. "But it's funny -- for all how close you are with Kei, you don't know a thing about his past."
52. "Ah, well, I'm not one to pry into a friend's past.\nIf they want to talk about it, they can talk about it..."
53. Toritani gave a small laugh.
54. "You know, Kusanagi, do you realize that's a little unfair?"
55. "You may be right...... When it's truly necessary, I'll step in.\nEven if it makes the other person cry..."
56. "Saying they can talk about it if they want to talk is also a way of leaving it to them.\nBut that's fine between Kei and me."
57. Toritani and I looked at each other across the bed where Kei slept.\nThen, almost at the same moment, we both turned our eyes back to Kei.
58. "You may be right. Yes, perhaps......"
59. "Because you're so kind, Kusanagi, maybe I just wanted to try\ncalling you unfair once...... but,"
60. "Watching you, I sometimes wonder......\nwhat is it that lies between kindness and unfairness?"
61. "Because you're so very kind, and just a little bit unfair,\nI can't help thinking about it, always."
62. "About my unfairness?"
63. "That's right, but..."
64. "What's more important is that I don't let myself get the wrong idea."
65. In the closed Chimera, I sipped my coffee slowly.
66. After that, Kei kept sleeping until night fell.\nHe woke up past nine, and the moment he did, he headed home.
67. He said only a word of thanks for being looked after.\nHe may seem eccentric, but he's punctilious about things like that.
68. He was angry when he saw what he was wearing,\nbut Toritani explained.
69. Was it really all right to leave the uniform as it was......?
70. Toritani finished up the day's work and came back over.
71. While the owner is away,\nToritani takes on his duties as well.
72. She also handles the art club's accounting, and she really is a capable one --\nbut is it right to make a student mind the shop on top of everything?
73. Toritani would probably say that since she's renting the room here,\nthis much is only natural......
74. "Kei was angry, wasn't he."
75. "Yeah, I was thinking the same thing."
76. "Do you know why he was angry?"
77. "Well, waking up dressed like that..."
78. "That's not it. Not that.\nIt's about... before he fell down the stairs."
79. "The one Kei is most angry with is probably himself.\nThat's what I think."
80. "About the cause -- the reason -- you stopped painting."
81. "Even after he understood, Kei still gets angry.\nHe can't accept it. That's just how he is."
82. "But I think that's also Kei's purity...... though,\nI'm not him, so I shouldn't act like I can speak for him."
83. "Then what are you thinking about, Toritani? Right now?"
84. "Right now? Let's see......"
85. "Even like this, all sorts of things run through my head. You, Kei,\nthe art club members, the Moore Exhibition, plenty more besides......"
86. "But right now, more than anything...... the thing I can't get out of my head is......"
87. Toritani stood in front of me as I fell silent.\nShe lowered both knees to the floor, then quietly reached out and touched my right hand.
88. "Earlier, when Kei fell down the stairs...... when you shielded Kei and me\nand took the fall beneath us......"
89. "When I realized you were shielding me, I thought my heart would stop."
90. Softly...... as if a feather were brushing it,\nToritani's hand stroked my right hand, ever so gently.
91. Her face was so serious that I couldn't crack a joke,\ncouldn't brush the hand away.
92. "Please, Kusanagi. Even if it's worthless to you right now,\nthis hand is something very precious...... so, please,"
93. "You know, Kusanagi, my reason for trying to keep the art club going......\nthe reason I'm staking everything on the Moore Exhibition -- it's for two geniuses."
94. "Two geniuses."
95. "Yes, and it can't be done if either one is missing.\nWithout either, it would be incomplete."
96. "But you're incredible too, aren't you?"
97. Not just Toritani. Akashi, and others too......\nThe variety and depth of talent in the art club this year is almost abnormal.
98. "Of course. That goes without saying. If I'm doing this,\nI don't intend to lose either. But my winning isn't the whole point."
99. "You have to understand that clearly."
100. "In love, they say the one who falls first loses, don't they?"
101. "It's the same in all things...... w-wait, why are you reacting to that of all things!?"
102. "N-no! I know you were being very serious, and I was listening, seriously, as seriously as I could -- truly!!"
103. "But then you suddenly said 'the one who falls'!"
104. "I-idiot, that's not what I meant! They say in love the one who falls first loses.\nThe same is true of everything else, and -- aaargh, enough!"
105. Toritani let go of my hand and clapped her palms over her face.\nThrough the gaps between her fingers I could see her faintly reddened cheeks.
106. "Here I was trying to have a serious talk, and honestly......\nUgh, you put on this stoic front and then you react at the strangest things."
107. "I apologize for breaking the flow of your serious talk, in the end.\nBut you, of all people, should know I'm not a stoic, right?"
108. "That much was obvious from that whole business here at the Chimera."
109. "Ah...... panties."
110. "The one who misunderstood 'lift up your skirt'......\nI remember it well -- I mean, we talked about this the other day too, didn't we?"
111. "Come to think of it, we did."
112. "You really never tire of it...... in a way, I'm impressed.\nDo you love panties that much?"
113. "Yeah, I love them."
114. "Said that flat out, and I actually feel oddly refreshed by it......"
115. As if to collect herself, Toritani gave a light cough.
116. "Well...... fine. It's something I've been curious about anyway,\nso let's take this chance to clear a few things up."
117. "Whether or not you'll show me your panties, you mean?"
118. "No!! What I mean is...... um......\nDo panties really have that much value to men? Anyone's?"
119. Fidgeting, Toritani asked in a small voice.
120. "No, it's not that anyone's will do."
121. "Really? It's not just anything, as long as she's a cute girl?"
122. "You can't know without looking. Besides,\nyou don't exactly get chances to see panties all that often."
123. "There truly are things in this world you can't understand without experiencing.\nThe panties beneath the skirt are one of them."
124. "Thank you for answering so earnestly......\nBut it's such a stupid subject it's giving me a headache...... haah......"
125. Pressing a hand lightly to her forehead, Toritani let out a sigh.
126. "I feel drained......"
127. "Yeah...... sorry, when you were finally having a serious talk.\nI wasn't trying to make fun of it this time."
128. "It's just, a word came out that I couldn't let slide."
129. "......You always look like the sort who couldn't care less about who's fallen for whom.\nAnd really, you don't care, do you?"
130. "That's not true. There's no way I have no interest in dating or romance."
131. "Eh, y-you don't?"
132. "If it looks that way, well, it's what you'd call a pose."
133. "Oh, really...... huh...... that's kind of surprising."
134. "Surprising, huh...... I am a healthy young man,\nso I'm not going to claim I have no interest."
135. "Then, for now, I'll take that as the truth."
136. "Don't say 'for now.' I actually answered pretty sincerely."
137. "Ah -- yeah, it's not that I doubt you, exactly...... yeah......\nsomehow this is awkward."
138. "Well...... yeah."
139. "W-well, I'll be heading home."
140. "Ah, r-right, yes. Thanks for staying so late with me."
141. "No, I was studying anyway.\nYou and Rin both told me to study at the Chimera, right?"
142. "N-now that you mention it, we did. Feels like it's helping your efficiency at all?"
143. "Yeah, it does. There's nothing else to do here, so I have to study, the BGM selection isn't bad, and it's much better than doing it at home."
144. "I see. Then you should come back again."
145. "Yeah. If you ever want to continue today's conversation, tell me anytime."
146. "Eh, the panties one?"
147. "The serious one."

=== lines=19 stop_reason=end_turn in=2515 out=646 cache_read=3222 cache_create=0 last_entry_continued=0 ===
1. [Naoya] "Also, when it comes to panties, rather than talking about them I'd like to actually see them."
2. [Makoto] "I won't talk about them, and I won't show you! Now, goodnight!"
3. [Naoya] "For the record, breasts I'd like to grope."
4. [Makoto] "Just go home already!!!!"
5. [Naoya] "Phew......"
6. [NARRATION] I turned off the light and got into bed.
7. [NARRATION] So much happened today, and yet that panties bit at the end blew it all away.\nDating, falling for someone -- the conversation had turned into something strange......
8. [NARRATION] When I looked out the window, light was spilling from the direction of the atelier.
9. [NARRATION] Kei was still in the atelier, huh......
10. [NARRATION] I recalled what Toritani had said.
11. [Naoya] "For the sake of two geniuses, huh......"
12. [NARRATION] If that was really her reason for going to such desperate lengths to keep the art club alive,\nthen Toritani was making a pretty poor bet.
13. [NARRATION] Even so, Toritani wouldn't quit.
14. [NARRATION] Saying it was for the sake of two geniuses, while claiming that, in truth,\nit was for her own sake.
15. [NARRATION] And then, even if none of those hopes came true,\nshe'd face forward in her Toritani way, and laugh that she regretted nothing.
16. [NARRATION] Toritani......
17. [NARRATION] You live in a way that's far too beautiful.
18. [NARRATION] That's exactly why I want to be someone who answers your sincerity\nwith all the earnestness I can muster.
19. [NARRATION] Even if that answer stands utterly far\nfrom the outcome she's hoping for......

=== lines=134 stop_reason=end_turn in=5399 out=3688 cache_read=3222 cache_create=0 last_entry_continued=0 ===
1. "Hmmm..."
2. Whether it was because I'd been thinking about all sorts of things or what, I'd woken up strangely early.\nSurely a sign of some coming cataclysm.
3. Toritani would already be on her way to school by now.
4. ...I'll make breakfast, then head out myself.
5. After eating, I made my way to the climbing kiln,\nwhere Toritani was staring at her finished study piece with a difficult expression.
6. "You don't like it?"
7. "No, it's no good."
8. "Looks like it turned out pretty well to me."
9. "It doesn't come close. It's not even worth discussing."
10. "Aren't your standards a bit too high?"
11. "That's not it. That's not the issue."
12. "I'm staking everything on this Moore Exhibition. I intend to win a prize, no matter what."
13. "You do?"
14. "Hmph. Of course I do."
15. "But you know, Kusanagi, you understand, don't you?\nIn this world there exist differences in talent that simply cannot be overturned."
16. "And there's luck, too. That's why I have to choose the best possible path."
17. "The best possible path, huh..."
18. "Yes. My best path. Everything I can think of, with everything I've got.\nThat's why this isn't good enough. I haven't tried everything yet, haven't given it my all."
19. Toritani swung her arm up high and slammed the study piece down onto the floor.
20. It looked like her own way of parting with the work--\na declaration that she was heading toward even greater heights.
21. First period after lunch was self-study.
22. When I glanced up at the rooftop, I could see purple smoke drifting.
23. I slipped out of the classroom.
24. "You again."
25. "It was self-study."
26. Just like last time, she showed no sign of scolding\na student for skipping class.
27. This woman had driven Nakamura out of Yumihari Academy.\nA formidable woman, no doubt.
28. I thought of another person who had likewise stood their ground against the Nakamura family without giving an inch.
29. Natsume Kotoko and Kusanagi Ken'ichirou had fought the Nakamura family, local dignitaries.\nThey had fought them by every means imaginable.
30. What remained of that struggle was the Natsume estate as it stands today.
31. This woman, too, had fought the Nakamura family,\nand pulled this school out from under their influence.
32. Then she divorced and reverted to her maiden name, Toritani.
33. She took custody of her own daughter, and put Kei into the Natsume estate.
34. She hadn't left Kei with the Nakamuras.\nBut she hadn't accepted him as a child of the Toritani, either.
35. What this woman had been thinking, I couldn't say.
36. I recalled Toritani's words.
37. As a child, Toritani had wished to live together with Kei.
38. And not just that--perhaps she had wished for the three of them to live together,\nherself, Kei, and this headmaster.
39. But that wish had never been granted.\nNow, far from living together, the three of them lived entirely apart.
40. And Toritani had said she was fine with that.\nThe reason: because Natsume Kei was happy.
41. She didn't want to disturb the life Kei was living happily as a child of the Natsume family\njust for the sake of her own sentimentality.
42. Toritani had told me not to get close to the headmaster.\nShe was done with letting the art club be used any further.
43. I understood why Toritani was on her guard.
44. I had no wish to treat her earnestness dismissively.\nBut this headmaster didn't seem as dangerous to me as Toritani made her out to be.
45. ...And there was something else that had been bothering me a little.
46. "What is it? Zoning out like that."
47. "I was thinking about what you did the other day."
48. "Ah, that... that was..."
49. "That was a fake, wasn't it."
50. As we'd parted, the headmaster's lips, still holding the cigarette, had moved slightly.\nBut that had been nothing but a fake. Call it a bluff.
51. "That gesture in itself meant nothing. You simply moved your lips."
52. "...Do you have some training in lip-reading?"
53. "No, nothing like that. It just felt somehow like I was being tested.\nThe rest was intuition."
54. "Intuition, hm."
55. "Yes. And... I've had a bit of experience with games like that, so to speak...\nI just felt that reading too deeply into it would end up tripping me up."
56. A faint smile came to the headmaster's lips.
57. "Was I right?"
58. "Well, we can call that essentially correct."
59. This was what had been nagging at me a little.\nWhy had she thrown a fake like that at me?
60. "I'll grant that it was a suggestive thing to do.\nBut there wasn't much reason to it. I wasn't even testing you."
61. "To put it plainly, I just wanted to play with you a bit.\nAnd you entertained me more than I'd expected."
62. "It was a little nostalgic, too."
63. "Headmaster, you know Kusanagi Ken'ichirou, don't you."
64. "That's all?"
65. "My, how dispassionate. You don't want to hear about your own father?"
66. "Well, there's no point hearing about him now."
67. "That man probably didn't dislike you, Headmaster."
68. "...I wonder about that?"
69. "Well, probably."
70. Either way, the person who knew the truth had turned to ashes.
71. "By the way, did you see the news the other day?"
72. "It seems Kusanagi Ken'ichirou's fabled seven-part series has been unveiled."
73. "Ah, they were talking about that on the news a little while back."
74. "Apparently it was in the collection of that Nakamura Pharmaceutical.\nWord is Nakamura Pharmaceutical had deep ties with Kusanagi Ken'ichirou, or were close to him..."
75. "'Close to him,' indeed... don't make me laugh."
76. "In the sense of a long entanglement, 'deep ties' isn't wrong."
77. "They must have timed it to overshadow the mural here.\nHonestly, the timing is impeccable."
78. "Right..."
79. "That is, if it's genuine..."
80. "Well, I'd say it's genuine."
81. "I wonder... well, it has nothing to do with me."
82. The headmaster glanced at her wristwatch.
83. "Well then, that's enough for today. Even I'm quite busy. Plenty of troublesome matters."
84. "Yet you seem to have time to stare at the mural on the rooftop?"
85. "This is precious relaxation time. In life, one needs at least this much of a cigarette break."
86. "See you again, then."
87. The headmaster walked away.
88. The bell rang.
89. Class was over, huh...
90. "That man and the headmaster surely got along well enough..."
91. If that was the case, why had she fired Kusanagi Ken'ichirou?
92. "Ordinarily speaking, there's no way Kusanagi Ken'ichirou would just be fired for nothing..."
93. Either that had been advantageous for him, or it had been\nsome sort of deal...
94. "Either way, anyone who got along with Kusanagi Ken'ichirou can't be taken lightly."
95. Kusanagi Ken'ichirou was a world-renowned artist and, at the same time,\nsomething of a notorious swindler in that world.
96. The crowd around him was a gathering of hardened schemers.
97. I sensed the same scent from the headmaster.
98. Hearing the name Toritani hadn't rung any bells,\nbut either way, anyone tangled up with the Nakamura family required vigilance.
99. "And I really don't want to run into that old man again either..."
100. Friedman had said as much: in the world of art, especially--\nwhether you get stuck with a fake or you buy a genuine article, it's a test of your skill.\nHe must have understood at least that much about that world.
101. Though I don't really get it...
102. Homeroom ended, and it was after school.
103. Now what to do...\nEven if I went to the art room, Rin wouldn't be there.
104. Should I go check on Toritani?
105. I strolled along and stopped at Toritani's seat.
106. From here, you had a good view of the mural.\nAnd of the people standing in front of it.
107. So from this spot, it was easy enough to spot me\nskipping class to look at the mural.
108. The area before the mural was full of students.\nMeasures had been taken to keep outsiders from getting in.
109. Which meant anyone not in uniform stood out quite a lot.\n...That was the headmaster, I'd bet.
110. Through the window I watched the headmaster standing before the mural.\nI couldn't make out her features clearly, but she was speaking with someone.
111. The other person wasn't a student, nor did she seem to be a teacher.\nToo young to be one of the trustees, either.
112. The woman looked capable, too.\nI couldn't see her face well, but I could guess from her walk and bearing.
113. After talking for a while in front of the mural, the headmaster and the other woman\nbegan walking toward the parking lot.
114. So she really was busy, then.
115. "You are persistent, aren't you."
116. Without letting her cordial expression falter, she gave a slight bow of her head.\nOn the lapel of her suit, a lawyer's badge gleamed.
117. "This is my job, so I'm afraid I can't just meekly withdraw\nafter only one or two attempts at contact."
118. I studied her.
119. Indeed, if she was working under that woman,\nshe couldn't very well slink back empty-handed.
120. ...It was about time I made a move on my end, too.
121. "Ah, that reminds me."
122. I said it in the flattest tone, as though it were nothing at all.
123. "Yes? What is it?"
124. "It's not directly related to work, but there's somewhere I'd like you to come with me.\nNot today--tomorrow, or the day after, whenever I have the time."
125. "Somewhere... you'd like me to come?"
126. "Yes. I think you'll find it interesting, too."
127. At the climbing kiln, Toritani was at work.\nPreparing her next study piece, perhaps.
128. "Yo."
129. "'Yo' nothing! ...Honestly, skipping class right out in the open just because it was self-study."
130. "'Sorry' isn't going to cut it. Your grades are already shaky as it is."
131. "For the record, it was self-study in math, which happens to be one of my strong subjects, so I took a break..."
132. "That's not the point!"
133. "You... you were talking with the headmaster, weren't you?"
134. "Just small talk. About the news on TV and such."

=== lines=140 stop_reason=end_turn in=7472 out=5146 cache_read=3222 cache_create=0 last_entry_continued=0 ===
1. [Naoya] "I don't mean to take your advice lightly.\nIt's just, there were a few things bothering me..."
2. [Makoto] "I'm not angry that you ignored what I said, Kusanagi.\nI have no reason to be. You should act however you see fit."
3. [Makoto] "It's better that way. Most of what I do ends up spinning its wheels for nothing."
4. [Makoto] "I'm not being self-deprecating. Look at what I've been trying to do to get more members so the art club can survive. In fact, it hasn't been going well."
5. [Makoto] "...You said you were talking with the headmaster about the news on TV, didn't you."
6. [Makoto] "I saw the news too...\nThat Nakamura Pharmaceuticals had gotten their hands on six of the seven works from Kusanagi Ken'ichirou's seven-part series..."
7. [Makoto] "Somewhere out of my sight, there's always someone waging a battle.\nAll kinds of people's desires, and the feelings of those trying to protect someone..."
8. [Makoto] "Anyone with the eyes to see would understand. What that seven-part series really is..."
9. [Makoto] "I wonder if there really is nothing I can do..."
10. [Naoya] "That's not like you."
11. [Makoto] "That was a rhetorical question. 'Is there nothing I can do?'\nThe implied continuation is 'Of course there is.'"
12. [Makoto] "But you know, as I said this morning,\nthere are gaps in talent in this world that absolutely can't be overturned."
13. [Makoto] "You know it? Maugham's 'The Moon and Sixpence.'"
14. [Makoto] "It features an outrageous artist modeled after Paul Gauguin."
15. [Makoto] "He takes advantage of the goodwill of a third-rate artist who is enthralled by his talent,\nsleeps with the man's wife, and even drives her to suicide."
16. [Makoto] "But the artist shows not a trace of remorse,\nand the man who had fallen for his talent ends up forgiving that genius artist."
17. [Naoya] "Well, there are probably plenty of differences between the genius artist in Maugham's work and the real Gauguin..."
18. [Makoto] "But I think it's a marvelous work."
19. [Makoto] "Maugham, a literary genius standing in opposition to Gauguin,\nand the way he confronts him -- it's beautifully written."
20. [Makoto] "Maugham lived as a popular author. And he took pride in that.\nBut the genius artist modeled on Gauguin\nwas the last of the solitary artists who could afford to ignore the masses."
21. [Makoto] "In this age, an aloof artist like Gauguin couldn't possibly exist.\nAn artist can only be a clown."
22. [Makoto] "Art is nothing more than putting on an appropriate performance in a stage-set theater."
23. [Makoto] "But then, was Maugham really like that?"
24. [Makoto] "The mean-spiritedness between the last solitary artist and the popular artist is delightfully sharp,\nand I find it rather thrilling."
25. [Makoto] "Despite the gap between himself and Gauguin, Maugham could stand his ground.\nBut the man who was just a third-rate artist could only be tossed around."
26. [Naoya] "Hey now, don't tell me you're saying you're like him."
27. [Makoto] "You're right. But there is such a thing as the one who falls in love losing."
28. [Naoya] "You said that yesterday too."
29. [Makoto] "Back then, you gave such a weird reaction that\nit ended up getting all strange, but..."
30. [Makoto] "I saw Kusanagi Naoya's painting, and I gave up oils myself."
31. [Makoto] "But Kei saw that painting and aimed for something further beyond."
32. [Makoto] "Actually, the years right after your painting was unveiled\nwere the era when Kei won the most awards."
33. [Naoya] "Doesn't look like he's submitted much for awards since then."
34. [Makoto] "Would you like to come by Toritani's house sometime?"
35. [Naoya] "Right now?"
36. [Makoto] "Well... give me a moment. Let me contact my part-time job."
37. [Makoto] "I want you to see it as soon as possible, Kusanagi."
38. [NARRATION] The room at the Toritani home she brought me to was crowded end to end with paintings.
39. [Makoto] "This is nothing yet. There's more in the other room.\nEven after he became Natsume Kei, all of Kei's paintings are still managed by the Toritani household."
40. [Naoya] "All of these are unpublished?"
41. [Makoto] "Yes. And the same thing might happen with the Moore Exhibition this time."
42. [Makoto] "It's not that Kei paints without agonizing over it."
43. [Makoto] "The world he sees -- he's always cutting it out through his own eyes,\nas the world seen from himself."
44. [Makoto] "It's almost something like an impulse."
45. [Makoto] "For Kei, 'beautiful' means that the limits of the world and the limits of himself are the same.\nPainting is his act of seeing all the way to the very last edge of that."
46. [Makoto] "Look at these."
47. [NARRATION] Toritani disappeared into the next room, then returned with several paintings in her arms.
48. [Makoto] "These are from when he was still Nakamura Kei. You can tell the difference, can't you?"
49. [Naoya] "Yeah... They look the same, but there's something decisively different."
50. [Makoto] "That's right. After encountering your painting, Kei aimed even further beyond.\nYou can see that clearly, can't you?"
51. [Makoto] "That's why neither of the two geniuses can afford to be missing.\nBut it's not just for Kei's sake."
52. [NARRATION] I looked over the paintings that filled the room.\nFrom every corner of them, I could feel a raw, living breath.
53. [NARRATION] These weren't just paintings.\nIt was as if Kei's very life had been seared onto the canvases.
54. [Naoya] "Toritani, what about your paintings?"
55. [Makoto] "My paintings aren't here."
56. [Naoya] "Are they kept somewhere else?"
57. [Makoto] "Yes. My recent ones are in the art room.\nThough I've hardly painted anything these past few years."
58. [Makoto] "Shall we head back soon?\nIf I keep them waiting too long I'll feel bad for the kids at my part-time job."
59. [NARRATION] We walked down the sprawling corridor of the vast house.
60. [Naoya] "This really is a huge house, though."
61. [NARRATION] Perhaps because of all the additions built onto it over the years, the corridors twisted about,\nand it took forever to reach the front entrance.
