6. Well then...
7. At a desk in the art room, Toritani is staring intently at an open croquis book.
8. At the sound of the door opening, Toritani lifted her eyes and registered my presence,\nbut immediately dropped her gaze again.
9. "Working out an idea?"
10. "No, not really... something else, right now."
11. On the croquis book was a sketch of a vase.
12. The vase was decorated with a magpie.\nThe same as that Chimera oil painting...
13. "The vase the master made?"
14. "Yes, that's the one. I only drew it from memory,\nso the details are vague, and I'm sure there are inaccuracies too."
15. "It's identical to the Chimera oil painting, and the ukiyo-e that the principal supposedly made.\nAnd it's colored with the impressionist brushwork of the oil painting, not the ukiyo-e..."
16. "Yes..."
17. Toritani rested her chin in her hand, eyes fixed on the vase in her croquis book.
18. "The other day, when we went to my house together, I told you a strange story, didn't I?\nAbout how I once, just once, went into that man's -- the principal's -- room."
19. "Yeah, I remember. I didn't think it was a strange story, though."
20. "When I went into that room, I was still just a small child.\nBut even then, I could understand how valuable the things in that room were."
21. "There were so many wonderful things. I don't know their monetary value, but\ntheir artistic value was undeniable... that vase was one of them."
22. "At the time, I was desperate about something else,\nand I hardly noticed the state of the room."
23. "But thinking back on it now,\nI can grasp just how much was in that room."
24. "Come to think of it, the principal was apparently invited to become a curator at some museum when he graduated."
25. "That's right... he had a truly universal eye for beauty,\nand the ability to sense the currents of the times. I'm sure that's still true today."
26. "A white snowy landscape, a winter countryside, and a magpie..."
27. "The oil painting that hung in Chimera was transferred, just as it was, onto that vase.\nFor that, the master went all the way to Limoges..."
28. "Using local materials, and the techniques of the period..."
29. "Liquid clay -- slip -- mixed with metal oxides that develop color through firing, used like paint, applied over terracotta or faience pottery... a technique also called slip decoration."
30. "The craftsmen working in the ateliers of the time devised it in order to incorporate the new compositions and paint techniques of Impressionist painting. At the time it was described as 'just like a painter putting paint to canvas.'"
31. "An Impressionist painting rendered as is... is that even possible?"
32. "Yes, it's possible. It's tedious, and you need to be intimately familiar with firing adjustments and the properties of the colorants, but it's not a lost technique either. I've done something similar myself."
