34. "It was just a technique that was in vogue back then -- not specifically for pottery. What I did was with glass. Pate de verre, they call it."
35. Pate de verre, huh...
36. Pate de verre is French for 'paste of glass.'\nIn English, "paste of glass technique" -- easy to understand.
37. First you make a prototype in clay or the like, take a mold with refractory plaster to use as a casting form,\nthen pack glass powder inside and melt it with the heat of a kiln to shape it.
38. You can't remove the glass without breaking apart the plaster mold, and afterward it needs to be endlessly polished, so it isn't suited for mass production and takes tremendous effort.
39. It's said to have been invented in Mesopotamia, but it makes sense that when the technique was revived in the 19th century, it became a glass technique for artists.
40. However, since all the work up until putting it in the kiln can be done at room temperature, and it's safer than glassblowing, workshops are held fairly widely.
41. Making a prototype from clay is presumably second nature\nto Toritani, who does pottery.
42. "You know, while I was looking into all sorts of things, I felt like giving it a try."
43. "Don't 'give it a try' -- that's not something you can just casually do..."
44. "This school has the climbing kiln, of course, but also polishing equipment -- most of the facilities you'd need."
45. "Pate de verre is a relatively safe method compared to glassblowing,\nso after that it's just a matter of motivation."
46. "A matter of motivation, huh..."
47. "Well, I wanted to try it as part of my research into glazes.\nAlthough, admittedly, I may have strayed... a bit too far."
48. "No, way too far, I'd say."
49. "Not really. The feldspar used in glazes melts during firing and turns vitreous. That's what coats the pottery and makes it water-resistant."
50. "Well, that's true, but..."
51. "All right... I'll admit I went a little overboard.\nAnd I did end up making something that was, well, more of an accident than a failure..."
52. "An accident? A glass piece?"
53. "No. I did use the pate de verre technique to make aroma lamps and night lights too, though."
