61. "Sigh... that thing... it's a failed work, and it really was just a lark, so I never thought it would end up like this..."
62. "What on earth happened?"
63. "Well... this is from back when I'd just started with pottery..."
64. "Just like with painting, I figured imitation was the quickest way to learn a technique,\nso I was copying various famous pieces."
65. "I mentioned Lalique earlier, but I've been interested in Art Nouveau for a long time -- also Galle's pottery, I imitated a few of those..."
66. "Around that time, using materials the master had sent, I happened to come up with a rather unusual glaze, and I used it to make four Art Nouveau-style vases."
67. "Compared to the master's pottery, they're only Art Nouveau 'style,' but they turned out reasonably well. A jack-of-all-trades in full form, you could say."
68. "But partly because I used that unusual glaze, I can't reproduce them, no matter what.\nAnything I can't reproduce technically is a failure, in my book."
69. "Well, even so, that alone wouldn't have caused a problem or become the talk -- it was the 'lark' part I mentioned earlier..."
70. "I built a little trick into those vases."
71. "A trick?"
72. "Do you know about wind-flutes? When wind blows through a naturally occurring hole or gap,\nit makes a sound like a flute."
73. "Ah, same principle as when a strong wind passes through a gap in a window and makes a flute-like sound, right? Called the aeolian tone effect, I think."
74. "Yes, exactly. That wind-flute -- I built it into the vase."
75. "I made a hole that's hard to see from outside...\nMade the lower side wall double-layered, with a simple mechanism between them..."
76. "When the wind happens to pass through just right, it makes a sound like a woman sobbing..."
77. "Wh-what?"
78. "So you were the type to pull that kind of stupid prank... my opinion of you just went up a notch."
79. "Having your opinion of me go up over something like that doesn't make me happy at all... sigh..."
80. Toritani presses a hand lightly to her forehead and sighs.\nBut it's funny...
81. "That antiques dealer bastard took an interest in the vase and asked to borrow it 'just for a while,' so I lent it to him -- and he went right ahead and sold it."
82. "A pretty hefty sum was sent to me, but that's not the point. I tried to get it back,\nbut it was hopeless."
83. "Because of the wind-flute, it got a reputation, and before I knew it rumor built on rumor --\nthat the ghost of a sobbing woman was attached to it, that it would bring ruin to whoever owned it...\nit's been thoroughly treated as a cursed antique... it's obviously a modern piece, no matter how you look at it..."
84. "Even setting aside all that lore, I don't want a failed work of mine out in the world, so I'd like to do something about it..."
85. "But Toritani, you call it a failed work, but for the people who value it, it isn't -- so isn't that fine as it is?"
86. "I hate it."
87. "If it had at least gone under the name Toritani Makoto, that would have been one thing, but of all things, it got listed in that catalog under the name Shirasu Toko..."
88. "Even the addressee was written as Toko... they did that on purpose, absolutely on purpose."
89. "...I want to put out into the world only things I can reproduce, things that can be mass-produced.\nThat's what I want. That thing is the polar opposite..."
90. "It's slipped out of my hands and, against my wishes, become a work of art.\nThat's why it's no good."
91. "I want to deliver things that anyone can obtain if they wish. That's why that piece\nmust never, ever appear under the name Shirasu Toko of 'The Moon Rabbit.'"
92. After school --
93. "Come out so they don't spot you."
94. "It's really not that big a deal..."
95. "Idiot, take a look at how many students are gathering at the main gate and tell me that."
96. "The main gate? Ah, there really are people there... what's all that?"
97. "Like I said, they're here for you."
98. "That's absurd. There's no way that many people are waiting there for me.\nBesides, most of them are students from other schools."
99. "They're students from other schools, which is exactly why they come after class..."
100. "The impact of that TV show the other day is really something...\nI didn't think it would swell up like this again."
101. "Ah! There! There, isn't that Kusanagi Naoya-sama!?"
102. "Huh? Where? Where? Ah, it really is him! Kyaaaa!"
103. "Idiot, what did I tell you! We're running for it!"
104. Toritani takes off running, so I take off running too.
105. "Ah! He's getting away! There's some weird girl with him!"
106. "Did she just call me a weird girl!?"
107. "...Enough, just dash!"
108. "I am dashing! Full speed!"
109. "Oh, really?"
110. "Haa, haa, haa."
111. "Don't underestimate the track team!"
112. "They're catching up with terrifying momentum."
113. "Ugh! Kusanagi, leave me and get away. I'm not the one they're after."
114. "But they were shouting stuff at you -- won't they do something to you?"
115. "Are you kidding? Don't you know my debate skills?\nI'll talk my way out of anything with my mouth!"
116. "Actually... now that you mention it, I could see that..."
117. About how much does Toritani weigh?
118. She's taller than Ai, so surely she weighs more than Ai does.
119. Still, so long as it's not a long distance, it should be manageable, shouldn't it?
120. "What is it? You've been looking at me -- just go on already!"
121. "What?"
122. "I'm going to carry you!"
123. "C-c-c-carry me!?"
124. Toritani's feet come to a halt.
125. Just as I once did with Ai, I lift Toritani up into my arms.
126. "Don't talk, you'll bite your tongue."
127. "W-wait, this is impossible, isn't it?"
128. "No, it's fine. My arm strength is the one thing I have confidence in."
129. "Even so, you have almost no strength in your right arm, do you?"
130. Toritani claps a hand over her own mouth.
