25. "Excuse me? Why on earth should I go all the way upstairs\nto that filthy room of yours? It's beyond me."
26. Toritani murmurs a few brief words to the part-timer who's been watching from just behind her.
27. The part-timer scurries out, hangs up the "Closed" sign, and by the look of it takes her break right there.
28. "Well then... I've gone out of my way to come all this way.\nSurely you already know what it is you have to do?"
29. Toritani exhales lightly, then looks Reika square in the face.\nNot as a Chimera employee, but as Toritani Makoto, facing Reika down.
30. "Do I? I have no idea, and no wish to know."
31. "My my..."
32. Reika blinks in surprise.
33. "You really are... well, I knew it, but you really are a fool. Haaah... it can't be helped, then. Suetake, be polite and explain it so even this idiot can grasp it."
34. "Honma Reika-sama, here present, is requesting the return of the Magpie vase held by the Toritani family... the Vase of Magpies in a Snowy Landscape."
35. "Return...? That vase is part of the Toritani collection.\nI think there must be some misunderstanding."
36. "My, the nerve. Like parent, like child, isn't it... Ah, come to think of it, Shizuru was the same way."
37. Shizuru...? The master of Chimera, and Toritani's cousin -- why does that name come up here...?
38. No. There's no "why" about it. I see now...\nSo the seeds of resentment the master sowed in the past run very deep indeed.
39. "Let me enlighten you, since you know nothing. That Magpie vase --\nit was supposed to be handed over to me. Shizuru and I had an agreement,\nbut she got greedy and hid it away."
40. "Hid it... you mean, that's why you took such a risk... for that vase?"
41. "And one more thing. I've long since seen through your little scheme to collude with the police to obstruct me. So I've come prepared with countermeasures."
42. As if Toritani's question had never been posed at all, Reika breezes past it and jerks her chin at the lawyer.
43. The lawyer unties the knot on the wrapping cloth she'd brought in, and carefully lifts out an item nestled in a wooden box.
44. It was a vase. The design on it was exactly, precisely the same as the Magpie painting hanging on Chimera's wall.
45. "You see, I'll accept nothing but the real thing.\nAnd shouldn't works of art be owned by those with the eye to appreciate them? Otherwise, the poor things suffer."
46. "That Magpie vase can only show its true worth\nin the hands of one who knows real art -- that is to say, in mine."
47. "So listen carefully. Take this vase, and swap it with the one at the Toritani house. Do that, and I might just let go of Kei's painting."
48. "Understand? I'm saying I'll forgive Onda Kirino for injuring my child.\nBe grateful for my mercy."
49. "The one who badly injured Kirino-san's daughter was your child!"
50. "Huh? What are you on about? That was her own fault, obviously. And now you go blaming it on us... 'the thief cries loudest' indeed."
51. "I can hardly believe those words are coming from the woman who planted a bug in Kirino-san's clothes to eavesdrop on us... You're the worst."
52. "Ah, that's right. That device -- that woman broke it, didn't she. I've had her billed for it, so tell her to pay up properly."
53. "Billed...? Reika-san, do you understand what you're even saying?\nWhat you've done is a crime!"
54. "And what's that supposed to mean? Setting aside your own conduct, how dare you say such a thing. As expected of the daughter of that thieving cat who seduced my brother."
55. "You Toritanis have been nothing but trouble for me since forever, and frankly I'm sick of it. Snatching away Kei and Kei's paintings, which belong to the Nakamura family... does the Toritani line have thieves' blood in it or something?"
56. "Belong to... Kei is a human being. He isn't anyone's property."
57. "Ah... poor, poor brother. He never should have married that woman."
58. Reika lets out an exaggerated sigh and rises to her feet.\nThe conversation simply does not connect.
59. "Come, we're leaving. I'm a busy woman."
60. "Yes, Reika-sama."
61. "Well then, Toritani-sama. I will contact you again at a later date regarding the particulars, so I ask for your cooperation at that time."
62. The lawyer casts Toritani a brief, meaningful glance.
63. But it lasts only a moment, and she promptly returns to her businesslike expression.
64. "If you'll excuse us, then."
65. The lawyer bows politely and follows after Reika.
66. "Hey, Suetake. Can't that Magpie painting be mine as well?\nIt should be, don't you think? I'm merely asserting my rightful claim."
67. "Yes, it is as you say, Reika-sama."
68. "I'm paying you a considerable sum to put your incompetent, dawdling self to use, so hurry up and get it done. Don't trouble me further."
69. "...Understood."
70. Reika and the lawyer leave Chimera.
71. "Sorry... I dragged you into something strange."
72. "Ah, no... I don't mind at all... but,\nthat woman -- what she was saying was completely absurd, wasn't it?"
73. "Yes. Self-righteous, self-serving, riddled with fixed ideas...\nbending the facts to suit her own convenience."
74. "She projects her own actions onto others,\nthen denounces them for it without a moment's doubt that it's the truth."
75. "'Selfish' doesn't quite cover it...\nShe wasn't entirely without those tendencies in the past, but..."
76. "I sensed it from when we met again at Sunday school -- with the years, her underlying character has only grown more entrenched, and honestly, she's beyond dealing with now."
77. "Yeah. Still, to think that Reika woman had a hand even in the burglary you were telling me about at the Toritani house..."
78. "Reika herself didn't break in, of course...\nBut apparently the person who did admitted it was on Reika's orders."
79. "Huh, that headmaster caught the thief?"
80. "Who knows? She keeps bodyguards or something on retainer, so I imagine one of them caught him."
81. "As I recall, it was settled through discussion, and the police were never notified. I did think that was odd..."
82. "The police weren't notified? Then this 'collusion with the police' Reika's harping on about is nothing but her own delusion."
83. "Exactly. But... perhaps that was when that lawyer and the headmaster first crossed paths."
84. "That lawyer seemed to be having a rough time of it too..."
85. "You were talking about it the other day, weren't you? In front of that Magpie painting.\nAbout how someone was deceived by a piece modeled on that painting."
86. "Ah... yes, I did. You've already figured it out?"
87. "Yeah. You told me the master had been grumbling that she'd deceived too many people and the bill had come due."
88. "Right. This is a continuation of that bill coming due. Though I doubt even the master thought the fallout would come back around to us."
89. "Which is to say, that woman Reika's resentment ran that deep, and her fixations were that strong."
90. "Exactly. Over the long years, Reika-san has grown into a monster beyond anything the master ever anticipated... I suppose that's how to put it."
91. "Sorry, but I'm closing up for today.\nI'll contact the part-timer, then go get changed."
92. "You're going to see the headmaster? Then I'm coming too."
93. "You don't need to come along... is what I'd like to say, but after hearing all this, you're hardly the type who'd back off just because I told you to."
94. "That's right. And besides, I'm already in this thing up to my neck.\nSo just give up and take me with you. I might turn out to be of some use, you know?"
95. "I know that. Honestly... that's exactly what makes Kusanagi such a pain."
96. "Really? A pain?"
97. "Who knows?"
98. By the time we managed to pin down the ever-busy headmaster, it was already evening.
99. Having listened to the whole account in silence, the headmaster looks from me to Toritani and back.
100. "So, what do you two mean to do?"
101. "Are you telling me to hand that Magpie vase over to her?"
102. "No. In any case, women of her sort won't be satisfied until they've gotten everything."
103. "She intends to get her hands on the Magpie vase and Kei's painting both."
104. At the very least, that woman Reika hasn't the slightest doubt of it.\nShe likely believes, as always, that everything will go exactly as she wishes.
105. "That's right. And besides, the one who'll be humiliated by getting hold of it is her."
106. "There's an antique appreciation gathering coming up, I hear. The connoisseurs bring out their most prized pieces. In all likelihood, that's where she means to show it off."
107. "Is that so."
108. "...If it comes to it, I may need to request the Magpie vase.\nBut I'll handle my side of things on my own."
109. "Headmaster Toritani, I'm not asking you to do anything about it.\nBut keeping in communication is important."
110. "Indeed. Sharing information is of the utmost importance."
111. "Yes. Which is why I simply came to inform you that Reika came to me and demanded I swap the Magpie vase for a forgery."
112. "Or was this, too, something you already knew?\nYou do seem to be scheming various things with that lawyer."
113. "...Attorney Suetake is Honma Reika's retained counsel."
114. "So it seems. Though she does appear rather worn out by Reika's willfulness."
115. No, it's far past the level of "worn out by willfulness."\nToritani knows that too, and is deliberately choosing mild words.
116. That lawyer is utterly exhausted by Reika's unreasonable demands.\nThe headmaster isn't one to miss that.
117. "In any case, I'll settle my end of it my own way. So I'll leave the out-of-court settlement to you."
118. "You don't need to tell me that. But rest assured, it will be wrapped up in a matter of days.\nOtherwise I wouldn't have Onda and her daughter shut away like that."
119. "...Can you win?"
120. "Victory and defeat, you see, are for the most part decided before the battle is joined."
121. "You could've just gone straight home, you know. You must be tired."
122. Toritani, whom I'd insisted on accompanying all the way back to Chimera,\nturns an exasperated look on me. But her voice carries a note of concern.
123. "Well, you're going to sit down and think, aren't you? About how to handle Reika."
124. "Mm... yeah. If their settlement is coming up soon,\nwe'd better wrap ours up quickly too."
125. "Then why don't you hear my idea?"
126. "Kusanagi's? Don't tell me you've come up with something in this little time?"
127. "Yeah. When it comes to hatching schemes, leave it to me."
128. "Schemes... hm."
129. "Ah, no, it's not some fiendishly wicked plot, all right?"
130. "I know that. ...All right, let's hear it."
131. Toritani listens to my proposal and folds her arms with a serious expression.\nI can see from her face that she's weighing it carefully.
132. "Now, it does touch on one of your taboos, at least a bit,\nso if you don't want to, you don't have to--"
133. "I'll do it."
134. "That was fast."
135. "It's true that, by my way of life and by my policy as Shirasu Toko,\nI've refused to let that Four Stars vase leave my hands."
136. "I won't pretend those were empty words. But this, you see, is a question of priorities."
137. "I want to reach a resolution before the headmaster's settlement with Reika.\nIn which case, something we can carry out right away is best."

=== lines=138 stop_reason=end_turn in=9396 out=6915 cache_read=3222 cache_create=0 last_entry_continued=0 ===
1. [Makoto] "In the little time we have, no matter how hard I think,\nI don't believe I'll come up with anything better than what you've just proposed."
2. [Makoto] "Ever since I became a third-year, the harder I tried to think up something good, the more I spun my wheels, and the results were a mess... I'm well and truly aware of that foolishness in myself."
3. [Makoto] "Besides, it's true that I detest Reika, but I don't much care for the idea of riding along on the rails the headmaster has laid down and abetting her humiliation either."
4. [Naoya] "The headmaster mentioned there'd be an appreciation gathering."
5. [Makoto] "Yes. In all likelihood, the headmaster and that lawyer were the ones\nwho arranged for Reika to make contact with me at this particular moment."
6. [Makoto] "Important events are decided in places I know nothing about,\nand all I do is watch the results..."
7. [NARRATION] Toritani gazed off at something a little in the distance,\nthen shrugged her shoulders with a mock-flippant air.
8. [Makoto] "And if I just followed the tracks Headmaster Toritani has laid, everything would resolve neatly. Well... it's safe enough, but as far as I'm concerned it's damned uninteresting."
9. [Makoto] "If there were no other option, I'd have to go along with the headmaster's plan,\nbut you've presented me with another way."
10. [Naoya] "Oh?"
11. [Naoya] "So you've got a kid's rebellious streak in you too, huh."
12. [Makoto] "Well, of course? If anything, it's dyed-in-the-wool.\nWhat with the way I've been dragging things with that person around all this time."
13. [Makoto] "Well, that's how it is. So if I'm to go along with a plan, Kusanagi, yours is the best one."
14. [Naoya] "I see. Understood."
15. [Naoya] "That dyed-in-the-wool business of yours -- let me hear about it sometime."
16. [Makoto] "Wh-what, out of nowhere?"
17. [Naoya] "It's not out of nowhere. You went to the trouble of going all the way to city hall the other day\nand told me all sorts of things, didn't you?"
18. [NARRATION] Before that painting at the city hall she'd dragged me to that evening,\nI'd heard a small piece of what Toritani had been holding in her heart.
19. [Naoya] "If I'm good enough, I'll listen to as much as you want to say."
20. [Makoto] "...You remembered a thing like that, huh."
21. [Naoya] "Come on... after being told something that pointed, there's no way I'd forget.\nAnd besides, I'm curious about you. I want to know."
22. [NARRATION] Toritani's mouth opened and closed as if she meant to say something, and then she gave a small nod, lowering her head.\nHer ears were faintly red.
23. [NARRATION] That meekness must be close to Toritani's true self -- and it wasn't weakness, or anything of the sort, and once again I found her, in that state, terribly cute.
24. [Makoto] "All right. Once all of this is over... you can listen."
25. [Naoya] "Yeah. I'll be waiting."
26. [NARRATION] After school the next day--
27. [NARRATION] Toritani phoned the headmaster to inform her that the Magpie vase would be needed.\nShe'd gone all the way to the headmaster's office, but the headmaster had been out.
28. [NARRATION] Somehow, we managed to get through to Master, too.
29. [NARRATION] Getting through to Master -- who, despite carrying a phone, is almost never reachable --\nwas an extraordinary stroke of luck.
30. [NARRATION] Having heard the situation from Toritani, Master agreed to hand over the Magpie vase.
31. [NARRATION] It seemed he'd said something else over the phone,\nbut Toritani didn't tell me anything in particular about it.
32. [NARRATION] At the same time, he gave her the lawyer's phone number.
33. [Makoto] "Thanks for everything."
34. [NARRATION] The words of thanks to the headmaster passed from Toritani's lips in a calm, composed tone.
35. [NARRATION] We stepped into the storeroom at Toritani's family home.
36. [Naoya] "So your vase is in here?"
37. [Makoto] "No. This way."
38. [NARRATION] Making our way through the storeroom, we came to a door at the back.\nToritani drew out a key.
39. [NARRATION] The key -- which had been kept in a combination-locked drawer of the desk in the headmaster's office -- was something she'd obtained through the phone exchange.
40. [NARRATION] The space beyond the door wasn't much different from the storeroom itself.\nIf there was any difference, it was that there were not only paintings but various three-dimensional objects.
41. [NARRATION] I said three-dimensional objects, but a great many pieces on display were junk items you could hardly call arts or crafts.
42. [NARRATION] Though I called them junk, they weren't utter trash --\nmany had something drawing you in... a strange charm about them.
43. [Makoto] "This is the headmaster's private room. She did say it was practically a storage space, didn't she."
44. [Naoya] "The headmaster sleeps and lives in a place like this?"
45. [Makoto] "Hardly. Her bedroom is somewhere else."
46. [NARRATION] For some reason, that reassured me.\nThis didn't feel like a place where a person would live.
47. [NARRATION] There was no sense of daily life here.\nLike a treasure-house, like a warehouse, here time stagnated and settled.
48. [NARRATION] This wasn't a room made for people to live in.\nIt was a room prepared for the sake of 'things' carefully arranged on display.
49. [Makoto] "There are odd things on display too, aren't there. My guess is that this room is a collection of only the things that person took a liking to."
50. [Makoto] "Nothing to do with erudition, or asset value, or artistic value, or historical significance..."
51. [Makoto] "Only the things that that person judged to be 'good' have been gathered here."
52. [Naoya] "And your vase is in among them?"
53. [Makoto] "Who knows about that."
54. [NARRATION] Toritani gave a light shrug.
55. [Makoto] "As for my vase, it may simply have been easy to store. Or it may not. Either way, I don't know, and it isn't something worth pondering."
56. [Makoto] "And besides, I consider that piece a failure. If that person is appraising a failure so highly, then it's an outrageous piece of irony."
57. [Naoya] "But you're pleased with the vase itself as a work, aren't you?"
58. [Makoto] "Mn, well... yes, that part I am."
59. [NARRATION] Toritani's estimation of her 'Four Stars' vase wavered.\nYou could call it contradictory.
60. [NARRATION] Even having made it with her own hands, she seemed bewildered by, and unable quite to cope with, a masterpiece that had earned praise beyond what she'd expected.
61. [Makoto] "There it is... this."
62. [NARRATION] There, three of 'The Four Stars' vases stood in a row.
63. [NARRATION] A moist, milky-white waxen texture,\nand a soft form that put one in mind of drooping candy-work.
64. [NARRATION] The openwork silhouettes of flowers and women\nfloated faintly to the surface as the light passed through.
65. [NARRATION] It resembled phosphorescence, perhaps -- drinking in the faint light of the room,\nit gave off a pale, ephemeral glow like moonlight or starlight.
66. [Naoya] "This is that special glaze you were talking about?"
67. [Makoto] "That's right. I used the stones Master sent me as a glaze."
68. [Makoto] "It was in fine sand form from the outset, but with little of the gray-silver portion --\nthe overall whiteness was extremely high, like a white spectrolite."
69. [Makoto] "I didn't go so far as to analyze the chemical composition, so it's only a judgment by appearance.\nI kept a small sample, so I'll show you sometime."
70. [Naoya] "The stuff you packed into the openwork -- is that the same glaze?"
71. [Makoto] "That's right. That's why, compared to ordinary hotarude ware that uses a transparent glaze, the openwork sections come to the surface far less."
72. [Makoto] "...To tell the truth, I polished a bit and thinned the glaze in the openwork sections...\nwhich is also a bit cheating, and rather embarrassing..."
73. [Naoya] "No, being able to pull off this much when you're just starting out is no small feat.\nFrankly, I think it's incredibly beautiful."
74. [Naoya] "'The Moon' has been taken. Which means what's here is\n'The Evening Star,' 'The Morning Star,' and 'The Pole Star,' then."
75. [Makoto] "That's right... and then."
76. [NARRATION] Toritani laid a hand on a red-lacquered wicker chest set on the next shelf.\nA Japanese mirror and a Nihonga painting were mounted on the wall.
77. [NARRATION] The Nihonga was the Magpie painting the lawyer had shown us at Chimera the other day.\nOn the back of the mirror, a flock of magpies had been engraved into the pattern.
78. [Makoto] "Magpies were a motif that person liked to collect... but\nto think she'd painted one herself -- I never even imagined it."
79. [NARRATION] Toritani gazed up at the magpie mirror.
80. [Makoto] "Long ago... that person told me this magpie mirror was the moon.\nI was small at the time, and the meaning was completely beyond me."
81. [Makoto] "But there was nothing difficult about it --\nit was the sort of thing you could find in a dictionary."
82. [Naoya] "The magpie mirror, huh... what does it mean?"
83. [Makoto] "The magpie mirror is another name for the moon."
84. [Makoto] "And at that moment it clicked with me. Ah, so that's it."
85. [Makoto] "If that were so, then just as there is the shadow of a rabbit pounding rice-cakes on the near side of the moon, on the far side of the moon there must be the shadow of a magpie."
86. [Makoto] "I don't know why I thought so... but probably it was\na simple association from the magpie pattern on the back of the mirror."
87. [Makoto] "A pointless old story... come, let's get to the main matter."
88. [Naoya] "A pointless old story, huh... personally I'd rather listen to that kind of talk,\nbut this isn't the time for it."
89. [Makoto] "...! Y-you say that... yes, I was the one who went off on the tangent, but we can't keep sidetracking forever. Look at this!"
90. [NARRATION] I peered in over Toritani's hands as she lifted the lid of the wicker chest.
91. [NARRATION] Inside the chest, an old Japanese notebook and account book, along with what looked like clippings from magazine articles, had all been gathered together.
92. [NARRATION] The art clippings had faded completely with age,\nbut they seemed to introduce an exhibition with accompanying photographs.
93. [NARRATION] The notebook and account book looked quite old.\nIf someone told you they were from the Edo or Meiji period, you'd probably believe it.
94. [NARRATION] As I stared at the notebook, Toritani said nothing and handed me the magazine clippings.\nShe must have already read them.
95. [NARRATION] I began looking over the article.
96. [NARRATION] The Fever of Japonisme -- Japanese Beauty That Colored Life in Paris --
97. [NARRATION] This exhibition, currently being held at the Yumihari Municipal Museum, treats the fever of Japonisme in Paris, symbolized by the 1878 Paris World Exposition...
98. [NARRATION] Through the arts and crafts eagerly imported into France at the time, the paintings of the Impressionists, and ceramics made in France taking ukiyo-e and Impressionism as their subject, it presents the theme from multiple angles.
99. [NARRATION] The exhibition is arranged in four chapters: 'Chapter 1: The Drawing Room -- Family Portraits --,' 'Chapter 2: Salon Japonais -- The Collections of Women --,' 'Chapter 3: The Study -- The Collections of Men --,' and 'Chapter 4: The Dining Room -- Ceramics That Enchanted Paris --.'
100. [NARRATION] One of the highlights of this exhibition is a set of 'rooms' recreated using period arts and crafts, based on Impressionist and contemporaneous paintings.
101. [NARRATION] 'Chapter 1: The Drawing Room -- Family Portraits --' recreates the room from Renoir's representative portrait 'Madame Charpentier and Her Children,'
102. [NARRATION] 'Chapter 2: Salon Japonais -- The Collections of Women --' recreates the room from 'Young Ladies Looking at Japanese Objects' by James Tissot, who, though not an Impressionist, was among the first of his generation to incorporate Japonisme as a motif,
103. [NARRATION] and 'Chapter 3: The Study -- The Collections of Men --' recreates the room from Edouard Manet's 'Portrait of Emile Zola,' each based on its respective painting.
104. [NARRATION] After enjoying the individual crafts displayed in the rooms, it may also be worthwhile to compare the source paintings, displayed in a position that makes side-by-side viewing easy, with the recreated rooms.
105. [NARRATION] 'Chapter 4: The Dining Room -- Ceramics That Enchanted Paris --' differs from the other rooms in that it is based on a real 19th-century dining room.
106. [NARRATION] The tableware set out on the table is arranged chiefly around the 'Rousseau' series produced by the painter and etcher Felix Bracquemond.
107. [NARRATION] The great majority of the motifs in the 'Rousseau' series are drawn from the Hokusai Manga,\nand where identified, the source images are exhibited alongside the tableware.
108. [NARRATION] There is a well-known anecdote that Felix Bracquemond was the first among French artists to 'discover' ukiyo-e, and he was involved from the founding of the group of artists and critics who studied Japanese art.
109. [NARRATION] A friend of Manet and Baudelaire, he also exhibited at the First Impressionist Exhibition, and had close ties with the Impressionist painters and the critics who championed them.
110. [NARRATION] Bracquemond, himself an avid collector of Japanese art, met Charles Haviland, an ardent collector and the head of a ceramics company, and was appointed artistic director of the Auteuil studio, which became the center of design and painting for Haviland & Co.
111. [NARRATION] Bracquemond retired in 1881, but at the 1886 Impressionist Exhibition he met Gauguin. To Gauguin, who was struggling in poverty, Bracquemond introduced ceramic work,\nand Gauguin took up ceramics for the span of one winter.
112. [NARRATION] The Haviland studio thus employed many painters and artists,\nwho used the plain bodies of large plates and vases as canvases, painting one-of-a-kind pieces on them.
113. [NARRATION] Among the Impressionists, Van Gogh and Monet are especially famous as devoted collectors of ukiyo-e. Van Gogh frequented one of the two Japanese art shops in Paris -- Siegfried Bing's shop -- where he bought up ukiyo-e prints.
114. [NARRATION] Monet, too, obtained ukiyo-e by various means, and produced many paintings drawing inspiration from, keeping in mind, or being influenced by their compositions.
115. [NARRATION] Exhibited at the Second Impressionist Exhibition in 1876 and warmly received, 'La Japonaise' is a work incorporating an abundance of Japoniste elements, said to have been produced as a counterpart to 'The Woman in the Green Dress,' exhibited at the Salon ten years earlier.
116. [NARRATION] In 1876, Monet obtained a single ukiyo-e print from one Japanese individual through Siegfried Bing,\nand, influenced by it, produced a single painting.
117. [NARRATION] That painting is the jewel of this exhibition -- Monet's second 'The Magpie' --\nand there is also a vase in the Impressionist style using its design.
118. [NARRATION] The influence that ukiyo-e exerted on the second 'The Magpie' is thought to be of about the same degree as that of Hiroshige's 'Night View of Saruwaka-machi' on Van Gogh's 'Cafe Terrace at Night.'
119. [NARRATION] The first 'The Magpie,' produced at Etretat in the winter of 1868,\nand the second 'The Magpie,' produced at Argenteuil in 1874,\nwere not clearly painted as a series.
120. [NARRATION] However, given the similarity of overall impression, it cannot be ruled out that Monet had at some earlier point seen this ukiyo-e depicting a clear snowscape and a small magpie...
121. [NARRATION] and, keeping it in mind while painting the Etretat 'Magpie,' later sought out the ukiyo-e itself.
122. [NARRATION] Furthermore, the Magpie vase, which faithfully renders the Impressionist brushwork, is said to have been produced between 1876 and 1878, but its maker is unknown.
123. [NARRATION] Just as Gauguin took up ceramic work, perhaps Monet himself set brush to it. Detailed future research is awaited, but this is a vase that stirs a 'perhaps...' kind of expectation.
124. [NARRATION] The Magpie vase, displayed on the main shelf of the Dining Room, is set out together with Monet's second 'The Magpie' and the ukiyo-e that was its source of inspiration. This is an unmatched opportunity to see with your own eyes the three beauties resonating together.
125. [Naoya] "The headmaster produced this article too, huh..."
126. [Makoto] "Seems so. She went so far as to have an acquaintance's print shop make the whole magazine\nand then clipped out the relevant section -- there are limits to being a perfectionist."
127. [NARRATION] Toritani answered while reading the section of the old Japanese notebook where a bookmark had been slipped in.
128. [Naoya] "That's impressive."
129. [NARRATION] But if that headmaster meant seriously to deceive someone, she'd go that far.
130. [Makoto] "I've finished checking this one too."
131. [Naoya] "What does it say?"
132. [Makoto] "The Toritani family had been dealing with foreign clients as art merchants since the Meiji era, but from the Edo period they'd had deep ties with picture-book sellers, apparently."
133. [Naoya] "Picture-book sellers?"
134. [Makoto] "Shops that made and sold ukiyo-e and illustrated storybooks."
135. [Makoto] "Do you more or less get the picture?"
136. [Naoya] "Yeah. The Japanese person in that magazine article who arranged ukiyo-e for Monet --\nhe's been made out to be your ancestor, right?"
137. [Makoto] "Correct. And according to this notebook, the maker of the ukiyo-e was Kariyama Seiro."
138. [Makoto] "A style similar to Watanabe Seitei, who was active in the same generation, with a specialty in bird-and-flower painting.\nHis main work was in hand-painted Nihonga, but he also produced a small number of block-print designs for nishiki-e...\nthat's how the setup goes."

=== lines=148 stop_reason=end_turn in=10004 out=5127 cache_read=3222 cache_create=0 last_entry_continued=0 ===
1. "Aha."
2. I looked up at the ukiyo-e hanging on the wall.
3. "The contents of this Japanese notebook are written in the form of a diary\nby an art merchant named Toritani Tadamasa."
4. "Tadamasa had connections with the art merchant Samuel Bing, and in order to feel firsthand\nthe demand for Japanese art in Paris, he traveled to France at his own expense."
5. "There he met Monet, and was asked to search for the ukiyo-e Monet had once seen."
6. "Monet spent his childhood in the port town of Le Havre.\nApparently it was there that he saw the Japanese crafts and ukiyo-e that sailors brought back."
7. "The ukiyo-e of a magpie and a snowscape that he'd seen as a child --\nthat was what Monet asked the art merchant from Japan to find.\nTadamasa had an idea in mind, and had a Kariyama Seiro ukiyo-e sent over from Japan."
8. "But Monet at that time was still deep in financial hardship. Tadamasa, who was sympathetic\nto the Impressionists, offered him the Kariyama Seiro ukiyo-e in exchange for a Monet oil painting\nwith the same composition as the ukiyo-e -- so this notebook says."
9. "So that's why the first 'Magpie,' painted with the ukiyo-e in memory,\nand the second 'Magpie,' based on the ukiyo-e he got from Tadamasa, are similar."
10. "Exactly. Tadamasa was also interested in the Impressionist ceramics Charles Haviland was producing, so he commissioned an Impressionist-style vase using the second 'Magpie' Monet had given him as its design."
11. "That whole sequence is what's written in this notebook. The other volume is a period account book. This entry is the vase's record, and the record of the Kariyama Seiro ukiyo-e is... over here."
12. "Tadamasa had to return home before the vase was finished, due to work.\nAnd then there was a bit of trouble, so the completed Magpie vase and the oil painting\nended up being kept at Haviland & Co."
13. "I see. So it was those that got discovered later, when the headmaster was a student."
14. "That's right. Tadamasa later obtained a copy of the same ukiyo-e he had given to Monet. It slept in the Toritani family storehouse together with this diary and account book..."
15. "And the setup is that this is that ukiyo-e."
16. Toritani returned the article clippings and the two Japanese notebooks to the chest, and closed the lid.
17. "Come to think of it, the Magpie vase isn't here, huh."
18. "The Magpie vase was moved to the safe in the headmaster's office, apparently.\nShe said she'd have the security staff bring it over on the day we negotiate with Reika."
19. "That turned into a long talk. Shall we go get the Four Stars vases?"
20. Just before closing time, the chime of an arriving customer rang through Chimera.
21. The lawyer entered the shop at an unhurried pace and took a seat.\nI stood across from her.
22. "Good evening. Mind if I join you?"
23. "You're the one who called me out here. Please."
24. I nodded and took the seat. The part-timer brought over water.\nThe lawyer didn't even look at the menu -- she just said "Coffee."
25. "Did you get the phone number from Headmaster Toritani?"
26. "Yes. I'm the one who made the call, but the phone was Toritani's.\nI just used the number Toritani had gotten from the headmaster."
27. "I see. So then, what is this about?\nGoing out of your way to call only me out here first -- what do you plan to negotiate?"
28. "Negotiate, huh... no, it's nothing so grand.\nMore of a request, really."
29. "Heh. Is that so."
30. The coffee arrived right away.\nOnce the part-timer left, the lawyer calmly opened up.
31. "And so, what sort of request do you have?"
32. "The other day, you mentioned 'the master's catalog' to Toritani Makoto, didn't you."
33. The lawyer quietly nodded.
34. "At that time, you spoke very highly of Toritani Makoto's work.\nYou were asking her to make art -- not vessels for daily use."
35. "Yes, I won't deny that. She's magnificent. Which is why\nI want her to continue making pieces of the caliber listed in the master's catalog."
36. "And, if possible, you'd like them for your own collection?"
37. The lawyer's reaction froze for an instant.\nAfter a breath, she looked back at me carefully, as if sounding me out.
38. "...Did Headmaster Toritani tell you?"
39. "Ah, no..."
40. I gave my cheek a scratch.
41. "How do I put it -- I've seen a fair number of people with that kind of look in their eyes,\nso I thought maybe. To be blunt, it's a hunch."
42. "I see. So you've had a great many people with eyes like mine hovering around you."
43. "As one would expect from the son of the world-renowned artist Kusanagi Ken'ichirou --\nand someone who was chased by the media as a prodigy himself."
44. "It's not really anything so exaggerated."
45. "You're too modest."
46. "So, supposing I am an art collector, what of it?\nIs that where this request of yours comes in?"
47. "Suetake-san, the vase you want is one of a series."
48. The lawyer's -- no, Suetake's brow moved almost imperceptibly.
49. "It was made based on Mucha's series of panels, The Four Stars.\nThe one in the catalog is 'The Moon,' and the rest are..."
50. "'Evening Star,' 'Morning Star,' and 'Polaris'..."
51. "Right. And you're about to see the rest of those stars.\nWhat do you say?"
52. "What do I say...?"
53. "Would you not like to have them?"
54. "Heh..."
55. She pressed her lips together, then let a thin, unreadable smile drift across her face.
56. "With the vases as payment, you want me here and now to betray my current employer?"
57. "No."
58. At my instant denial, Suetake let her true expression show for a moment.\nAs if to say -- what on earth is this guy talking about.
59. Behind eyes that feigned composure, a glittering desire flickered in and out.\nWith that look, you didn't have to be me to see it right away.
60. A collector who has crossed a certain line always harbors a kind of madness.\nMore than money, more than life itself, they place their collection first.
61. Just as I thought.\nThis woman wants Toritani's vases so badly she can't stand it.
62. Otherwise she wouldn't have gone out of her way to praise Toritani like that while on the job.
63. She wouldn't have carelessly let something like 'With the vases as payment, you want me here and now to betray my current employer?' slip out of her own mouth.
64. "Then... what would you have me do?"
65. "The story is simple. We want to free the Onda mother and daughter from their current predicament.\nFor that, both Toritani and the Master say they'll part with the vases."
66. There is no artist who is content to release into the world a work they aren't satisfied with.
67. And yet, that very thing -- something they wouldn't ordinarily want to release --\nis what Toritani and the Master are offering up for the sake of the Onda mother and daughter.
68. Toritani, the vases she's insisted are failures.\nThe Master, the vase that is a forgery.
69. This isn't a matter of surrendering to Homma Reika.\nToritani is one thing, but with the headmaster and the Master, it's crueler than that.
70. In her phone call with Toritani, the Master reportedly said this.
71. She couldn't say -- not even with her mouth torn open -- that that person was likeable.\nEven so, she'd rather not see someone she knew be shamed if she could help it, but\nif that's what they were demanding, then so be it.
72. The headmaster, from the start, has no interest in Reika's obsession with the vase.\nHer stance is more like -- give it to her, if it keeps her from staging another theft.
73. "The most important thing is the settlement between Headmaster Toritani and Homma Reika a few days from now.\nAnd if that settlement wraps up safely, as we want it to..."
74. "If it wraps up...?"
75. "After that, I'd like you to negotiate with Toritani Makoto."
76. "...I see."
77. "And that wouldn't be a betrayal of my employer?"
78. "No. You said just now, 'With the vases as payment, you want me here and now to betray my current employer?' --\nso if you betray her here, it actually makes things difficult for us."
79. "Right, yes... heh."
80. "Tonight, will I be able to see\nthe vases of Toritani Makoto-sama that you mentioned earlier?"
81. "That... will be a pleasure."
82. She murmured it in a somehow enraptured tone, and Suetake stood up.
83. "I have to go pick her up, so if you'll excuse me."
84. "Then, until later."
85. Suetake left the shop.
86. "Thank you very much~"
87. "Thank you very much..."
88. I absently echoed the part-timer's send-off and looked up at the ceiling.
89. Th-that was tense.
90. I hadn't known how Suetake might let something slip, and\nthough I'd put on a cool front, on the inside I was breaking out in cold sweat.
91. I'm bad at negotiations like these, but for improvised push-and-pull,\nwell, I think it went pretty well.
92. But this was only the preliminary skirmish.\nThe real thing was still ahead.
93. Chimera closed for the night.
94. Once the cleaning, the register, and the rest of the closing work were done,\nToritani went upstairs to change.
95. In the meantime, I rearranged the tables and drew all the curtains in the shop.\nWith that, preparations were complete.
96. "Ah, thanks. That's a big help."
97. Toritani came back down changed into her uniform,\nnoticed the preparations were done, and thanked me.
98. "In your uniform, huh."
99. "Well, of course. While I'm a student, this is my formal dress. And besides..."
100. "For a girl, the uniform really is a set of battle gear, isn't it?\nIf you want to psych yourself up, it's the uniform. And I like the design, too."
101. "Is that how it works."
102. "That's how it works."
103. Toritani glanced at the clock in the shop.\nFive minutes till the appointed time.
104. "You know, when I called the Master the other day about the Magpie vase,\nshe said, 'I'm sorry to leave the cleanup to you.'"
105. "I told her not at all. In fact, I was grateful.\nBecause in this whole affair, she'd given me the one chance to actually do something."
106. "This whole affair, huh..."
107. That was the incident surrounding Onda Kirino and Homma Reika --\nand also the string of troubles between Homma Reika and the Toritani family.
108. "Reika, if it meant making us suffer, would do anything.\nWhatever she wanted, by whatever means, she would get."
109. "For Reika, Kei's paintings, the Magpie vase -- they were things that should, by rights, belong to her, and by extension to the Nakamura family, but which for some reason are in the Toritani family's hands."
110. "That's insane."
111. "Exactly. But that's the logic Reika lives by."
112. "...What I'm about to do to Reika -- I can't fully predict\nwhat meaning it will hold for her."
113. "But even so, I'm going to take the best course of action I can."
114. "Kusanagi, thank you."
115. "If you hadn't proposed using the Four Stars vases,\nI'd never even have thought of what I'm about to do."
116. "No, you're the one who decided to use vases you hated using that much.\nAll I did was float an idea."
117. "Even negotiating with Suetake-san for me?"
118. Toritani let out a little laugh.
119. "Well, fine. I'll let you have that."
120. The hands of the clock were about to point to the appointed time.
121. I had proposed to Toritani using the Four Stars vases to win the lawyer over.\nAnd having said it, I'd carried out the negotiation myself.
122. The result hadn't been bad. With that, what I'd set out to do was done.
123. What Toritani was about to do to Reika from here on was, she said,\nthe best course of action she'd thought up on her own.
124. "It won't have a bad effect on what you did for me, or on what Headmaster Toritani is about to do.\nSo you can just sit back and watch."
125. "You don't have to tell me. I know that much."
126. "...Thank you."
127. Good intentions sometimes bring about terribly cruel results.\nKnowing that, Toritani was going to see her own best through to the end.
128. A brief silence fell.\nThen, in a small voice, Toritani asked me:
129. "Does Kei... know?"
130. "No, nothing. He just keeps painting. That's all."
131. "I see... then that's fine."
132. "That child doesn't need to know a thing."
133. Reika turned a suspicious gaze on the several boxes lined up on the table.
134. "What are those? You're still trying to pull something at this point?"
135. Toritani silently shook her head, and together with me began opening the boxes.
136. When the Four Stars vases were taken out,\nI could tell that Suetake quietly held back an intake of breath.
137. Looking at Toritani's three vases and the Magpie vase lined up on the table,\nReika furrowed her brow further.
138. "The Magpie vase, we will hand over.\nBut before that, there's something we'd like you to hear."
139. And then Toritani began, quietly, to speak.
140. Whether Reika would listen to the end had been a gamble.\nBut, strangely quiet, Reika kept listening to Toritani's story.
141. Perhaps, before coming here,\nSuetake had said something to prepare her.
142. Toritani carefully explained the process by which the Magpie vase had been made.
143. The forgery of documents by the headmaster, the making of the vase by the Master...
144. Her explanation, illustrated with the magazine clippings and the two Japanese notebooks she'd brought, was almost like a single story, and even I, who already knew it, found it worth listening to.
145. Then Toritani moved the conversation to the Four Stars vases.
146. She showed the photograph of 'The Moon' vase listed in the master's catalog, laid out its market value,\nand calmly explained that its artistic worth was acknowledged.
147. There was even a moment when Suetake, in an offhand whisper, told Reika that Toritani's vases were extraordinarily precious pieces.
148. "As you can see... on the Magpie vase, the metal nanoparticle pigments that only appeared\nin the twenty-first century have been used on the magpie's feathers."

=== lines=52 stop_reason=end_turn in=4561 out=2194 cache_read=3222 cache_create=0 last_entry_continued=0 ===
1. [Makoto] "These are materials I prepared in advance in order to prove, beyond any doubt,\nthat the Magpie vase produced by Toritani Shizuru is unmistakably a forgery."
2. [Makoto] "...That concludes my explanation."
3. [Makoto] "The Snowscape Magpie vase, and the Four Stars -- the reason I prepared these two kinds of vases tonight is that I wanted you to choose."
4. [Makoto] "I have given every explanation I possibly can. On that basis,\nwhichever vase you choose, I will accept it."
5. [NARRATION] When she had finished, Toritani bowed politely.
6. [NARRATION] Toritani fell silent, and stillness descended over the Chimera.
7. [NARRATION] On Reika's face as she stared fixedly at the vases,\nthere hung a horribly eerie quiet.
8. [Reika] "...I'm surprised."
9. [NARRATION] After a long, long silence, Reika let the words slip out with what sounded like a sigh.
10. [Reika] "I'd like to tell you that you've done your homework very well, but..."
11. [Reika] "Ahahahahahahaha, too bad!!"
12. [NARRATION] At Reika's sudden burst of laughter, Toritani only faintly moved her brows.\nNo one's startled, huh... frankly, I was pretty rattled.
13. [NARRATION] Toritani was remarkably calm, seemingly aware in advance of the violence of Reika's mood swings. Reika must have been the sort to let her emotions explode like this from long ago.
14. [Reika] "After coming this far, you put on such a clumsy little show? And drag out those piece-of-junk vases? So it really is true that things can leave you dumbstruck."
15. [Reika] "You picked the wrong person to deceive, didn't you? I know your scheme.\nBy any means necessary, you don't want to let go of that Magpie vase!?\nBut me, I will absolutely not be fooled!!!!"
16. [Suetake] "L-Lady Reika...! The Four Stars serial vases are hardly what one could call ju--"
17. [Reika] "Silence!! Suetake, just who do you think you are!?"
18. [Suetake] "...M-my apologies."
19. [NARRATION] Reika shook her head as if utterly exasperated.
20. [Reika] "Honestly... the greed of the Toritani clan never fails to astonish me."
21. [Reika] "I have the eye to see the real thing!! No matter how you juggle words,\nyou can hardly match me, who discerns true beauty, can you!?? Fools, fools!!!"
22. [Reika] "The parents are fools, the child is a fool, the branch family's daughter is a fool, nothing but fools and fools and fools and fools and fools and fools of a clan!!"
23. [Reika] "Ah, my poor, poor Brother!! To think he passed the precious blood of the Nakamura on to such a good-for-nothing daughter!!"
24. [Reika] "Toritani Makoto! What a filthy child you are! I'd like to drain every drop of your blood and swap it with some stray dog's, and pull every last piece of Brother out of your genes -- but I'll be merciful about that."
25. [Reika] "Tonight, the Magpie vase is at last being returned to me, its rightful owner... and the day after tomorrow, Kei's paintings, which you Toritani clan stole, will be coming back."
26. [Reika] "It's such a relief to think I'll finally be rid of any tie to you people!!"
27. [Reika] "Now then, Suetake, hurry up and finish your business."
28. [Suetake] "Yes... Then, Toritani-sama. Might I ask you to sign here?"
29. [NARRATION] Toritani took the document from Suetake and quietly ran her eyes over it.\nThen, without a word, she passed the document to me.
30. [NARRATION] It was a document to the effect that the Magpie vase was acknowledged to be genuine,\nand that the Toritani family relinquished all rights to it.
31. [NARRATION] I said nothing and returned the document to Toritani.\nToritani quietly signed and handed it back to Suetake.
32. [NARRATION] Friedman's words came back to me again.
33. [NARRATION] Especially in the world of art:\nwhether you get sold a fake or buy the real thing, that's your own ability.
34. [NARRATION] Reika, in high spirits, cradled the Magpie vase and walked out.
35. [NARRATION] Nobody said a word as we saw her off.\nIt was the return of the naked empress.
36. [Makoto] "It's over..."
37. [Naoya] "Yeah, good work. You did well.\nAnd there was nothing in that document that puts us at a disadvantage, either."
38. [Naoya] "People believe only what they want to believe.\nReika is one extreme of it, but it's a trait everyone has."
39. [NARRATION] Toritani, who had been looking down as if in thought, suddenly raised her eyes.\nShe looked at me, and then gave a small smile.
40. [Makoto] "You know, my little spinning of wheels tonight -- it didn't cause anyone any trouble.\nSo don't you think it wasn't so bad, all things considered?"
41. [Naoya] "Not bad -- more than that..."
42. [NARRATION] Even toward a woman like that, Toritani had responded with sincerity.
43. [NARRATION] Toritani would surely say this:
44. [NARRATION] It wasn't because she didn't want to shame Reika.
45. [NARRATION] Preparing the two kinds of vases, giving every possible explanation --\nfrom start to finish, she had done it all for her own sake.
46. [Naoya] "You showed me the beauty of the shape called Toritani Makoto.\nSo for me, it was a great haul."
47. [Makoto] "Sigh... saying that with such a straight face..."
48. [NARRATION] After Toritani looked exasperated, she gave a light shrug.
49. [Makoto] "Well, I'll take it as a compliment."
50. [Naoya] "Yeah, please do. Otherwise I'll be embarrassed."
51. [Makoto] "You're right."
52. [Makoto] "Otherwise, I'd be embarrassed too."

=== lines=133 stop_reason=end_turn in=6360 out=4387 cache_read=3222 cache_create=0 last_entry_continued=0 ===
1. "Sing a song of sixpence~"
2. 　The part-timer decorates the Chimera while humming some offbeat, made-up melody.
3. 　Today, the Chimera closed up shop early since it had been rented out for a private party.
4. "W-Wow, that's some serious decorating."
5. "Ehh, but it's a party, right? A celebration for something that went well.\nThen you gotta go this hard with it~"
6. 　The part-timer decorates the inside of the Chimera as much as she possibly can with stars and moons and flowers and rings and nets, all made of origami and soft paper.
7. "But with all these decorations...\nit looks less like a party venue and more like a preschool play."
8. "Ah, you figured it out? I want to be a nursery teacher, you see~.\nSo I make stuff like this with my little cousins."
9. "I see... Toritani, are you okay with all this?"
10. 　Toritani gives a wry smile and nods as if to say there's nothing to be done.\nShe's putting on that air, but she looks like she's enjoying it too.
11. "I've left tonight's affairs in her hands."
12. "That's right~. Tonight's celebration party, from the food on down,\nus part-timerz are giving it everything we've got!"
13. 　Led by the part-timer, a few girls in maid outfits are working busily,\nsetting up the venue and cooking in the kitchen.
14. 　The tables are briskly rearranged as well.\nFrom the layout, it seems this is going to be a standing buffet.
15. "So Makoto-san, please just kick back and enjoy yourself as one of the guests."
16. "Is that song from earlier something you learn at preschool too?"
17. "Ah, that one, actually~, I learned it from Makoto-san."
18. "My name is Tsugumi, you see. And so Makoto-san taught me that poem. I love it, but I just can't seem to memorize it."
19. "Sing a song of sixpence~. And then, twenty-four blackbirds sing inside a baked pie, which is so clever, and the king is startled...?"
20. "Well, that's more or less right."
21. "Yes, that's more or less right."
22. "Sixpence is used in a charm for saving up money, right?\nIf you tuck one in your shoe at your wedding, you'll never want for money...?"
23. "Something Four, huh."
24. "Oh, so Kusanagi knows about it too. I would've thought that wasn't your area of interest.\nIt's the custom where a bride wears them at her wedding to bring her happiness."
25. "Something old, something new,\nsomething borrowed, something blue."
26. "And in her shoe, a silver sixpence."
27. "Ah, that's it, that's it."
28. "My big sister did that at her wedding~.\nAnd that's how I got it -- the happiness sixpence!"
29. "I keep it in my wallet so I won't lose it~.\nSomething about it just feels nice, like I'll save up money, and I'll be happy."
30. "Whoops, I've chatted too much. I've still got prep to do so\nI'll get going. You two, please take it easy~."
31. 　The part-timer bustles off toward the kitchen.
32. "Sixpence, huh..."
33. 　Toritani folds her arms, leans against the wall, and looks up at the moon through the window.
34. 　I sit in a chair and drink my coffee.\nIt had gone cold, but it was as good as ever.
35. "...I heard from Suetake-san how it went for Reika at the appraisal."
36. "Reika's vase was declared a forgery at the appraisal."
37. "But apparently, Reika didn't back down an inch."
38. "Yes. The other connoisseurs and authorities in the field politely explained\nto Reika that it was a forgery, citing the glaze and other details."
39. "But Reika hurled abuse at the other members, scorned them,\nand while cackling that her vase was the real thing, tried to destroy the other pieces..."
40. "She was restrained by everyone, thrown out of the venue along with her vase,\nand permanently expelled from the society."
41. "You don't seem too surprised. Kusanagi, does that mean you saw this coming to some extent?"
42. "Hardly. I just thought it was one possibility.\nPeople who go down with their own delusions aren't especially rare in that world."
43. "So it's really nothing but people who've clearly crossed some line...?"
44. 　Being handed a forgery and flying into a rage over being humiliated doesn't\nreally qualify as crossing a line.
45. "I'm different from you, Kusanagi, so I was a little surprised. And for the first time, I was impressed by Reika. I'd always seen her as a simpler, vain, worldly sort."
46. "The master not handing the magpie vase over to Reika was primarily an act of revenge,\nbut it was also pity, and mercy."
47. "But maybe... yes, just maybe, for Reika,\nthat kind of mercy meant nothing -- if anything, it was only a hindrance..."
48. "So in a way, this might have been the ending she wanted..."
49. "What's the matter? You're awfully sentimental."
50. "Oh my, didn't you know? I'm a sentimental person."
51. "...Especially on a night like this."
52. 　The party began with a simple toast.\nThere weren't even any words from the headmaster.
53. 　A week had passed since the incident between Toritani and Reika.\nThe settlement between the headmaster and Reika over Kei's painting had concluded without issue.
54. 　With attorney Suetake successfully brought over to his side,\nthe headmaster had no way to lose.
55. 　After the settlement there was no phone call, only an SMS from the headmaster to Toritani's cell that simply read, 'We won.'
56. 　There were six people at the party.\nThe headmaster, Toritani, me, the Onda mother and daughter, and attorney Suetake.
57. 　Not exactly the sort of crowd to propose throwing a celebration.
58. 　This strange party wouldn't even have been planned, let alone held,\nif the part-timer hadn't suggested it.
59. 　Kei wasn't there.
60. 　I asked Ai before I left, and apparently he's been holed up in his atelier painting.
61. 　Kirino-san has been forbidden from meeting with Kei.
62. 　And besides, Kei is already Natsume Kei now.\nNot Nakamura Kei, not Toritani Kei, not Onda Kei.
63. 　So this is how it should be, I suppose.
64. 　At the edge of my vision, I caught sight of Toritani and Suetake talking.
65. "...And so when I told him I was quitting as a lawyer,\nHeadmaster Toritani invited me to work at his firm. I'm still in my trial period."
66. "Now that the settlement's over, I can finally negotiate with you\nabout 'The Four Stars.'"
67. "But before that, there's one thing I intend to do."
68. "That 'Moon' vase you were swindled out of -- I intend to get it back.\nWhen the time comes, will you retain me?"
69. "...Let me think about it for a bit.\nWith the Open Exhibition coming up, I want to focus on that for now."
70. "Understood. I'll be waiting whenever you decide."
71. 　Suetake steps away from Toritani.
72. 　'I intend to get it back,' huh.\nHe already considers himself the owner of 'The Four Stars,' it seems.
73. 　Now, led by Kirino-san,\nNei approached Toritani, who was standing alone.
74. "Um... Makoto-san."
75. "Nei-chan, what's the matter?"
76. 　When she was offered a nearby chair, Nei timidly sat down.\nToritani knelt so she was at eye level with Nei.
77. 　Even though it was a standing buffet, low chairs -- probably for Nei's sake --\nhad been placed here and there around the room.
78. "Um, here. Thank you very much, for a long time."
79. 　Nei takes a picture book out of the paper bag Kirino-san was holding and holds it out to Toritani.
80. "Thank you. Did you like this picture book? If you want it, you can have it.\nI was going to donate it to the Sunday school's one-tsubo library anyway."
81. "...Are you sure? If this book is in the one-tsubo library, lots of people can read it, but if it becomes mine, only I can read it."
82. "It's fine. If you want it, Nei-chan, that's all that matters."
83. "...Understood. I would like to receive this picture book."
84. "All right. It's yours now, Nei-chan."
85. "Thank you, very much."
86. 　Nei tightly hugs the picture book and bows her head.\nKirino-san, standing beside Nei, quietly bows her head as well.
87. "Saki-sama... no, Headmaster Toritani... thank you truly for everything.\nI don't know how to properly express my gratitude..."
88. "No, I've been thanked plenty already. That's enough.\nBesides, the hard part starts from here."
89. 　The trouble with Reika had passed, but from now on Kirino-san and Nei\nhad to endure long days of patient rehabilitation.
90. 　That was probably what the headmaster meant by 'hard.'
91. "Yes. Just the two of us, mother and daughter, we'll do our best."
92. 　Nei bowed her head beside Kirino-san too, and tried to get off the chair.\nThe headmaster stopped her with a hand and gently stroked her head.
93. "It'll be difficult... but you can do it."
94. 　Looking up at the headmaster, Nei quietly nodded.
95. 　The headmaster gave Kirino-san a slight bow, then stepped out of the cafe to smoke.
96. 　My eyes met Nei's. I gave her a small wave.
97. 　Nei started to get off the chair, but I stopped her with a gesture as well\nand walked over to her.
98. "I received this picture book from Makoto-san."
99. "Ah, that's good."
100. "Yes. And so I... unrelated to my thanks from before, once again --\nI would like to read this book with you all the way to the end. Is that all right?"
101. 　There was no reason to refuse.
102. 　When I pulled up a nearby chair and sat down,\nNei opened the picture book and slowly began to read.
103. 　And so, from beginning to end, Nei read to me the story\nof the rabbit that tried to go to the moon.
104. 　Purple smoke rose at the eaves of the Chimera.\nThe headmaster glanced over as I stepped out of the shop.
105. "Hello."
106. 　With the silence that fell at once, the smoke wavered.
107. "Weren't you having Nei read the picture book to you?"
108. "It's finished. Everyone's having a meal now.\nThe food here is pretty decent, actually."
109. "There's no alcohol, is there?"
110. "Well, this is essentially a cafe..."
111. "I'll have a bit later."
112. "Do you not like picture books?"
113. 　It felt like there was some reason he hadn't come back inside\neven though he knew Nei had started reading the book.
114. "Ah... it's not that I dislike them."
115. "It's just that I don't much care for that particular story. You listened all the way to the end, didn't you?"
116. "She just finished reading it to me."
117. 　The headmaster takes a deep drag on his cigarette.\nThe white smoke he exhales faintly obscures the profile of his face.
118. "If that's what it wanted, it should have let the rabbit go to the moon.\nAnd yet they blindfold it and put it to sleep."
119. "You think so?"
120. 　Not just his gaze but his whole body turns to face me.
121. "Your view is different?"
122. "It's less a 'view'... I'm not thinking about anything that difficult..."
123. "Maybe what it really wanted was to sleep."
124. "The rabbit is just being lured by the madness of the moon, but maybe deep down\nit wants to be blindfolded and sleep in the arms of someone dear to it."
125. "You really do think interesting things."
126. "Do I? It's just something that came to me."
127. 　The headmaster put the shortened cigarette in his portable ashtray,\ncrushed it out with his finger, and lowered his eyes.
128. "A human being will never understand how a rabbit feels."
129. 　As the headmaster raised his face, I looked up at the night sky too.\nWhether from the angle or the hour, the moon was nowhere to be seen.
130. "...And so?"
131. "Did you come out here to listen to my rambling stories?"
132. "I heard various things from Toritani."
133. "Ah... I decided it was necessary for the negotiations with Reika this time.\nIt's a story from long ago."

=== lines=141 stop_reason=end_turn in=7812 out=4702 cache_read=3222 cache_create=0 last_entry_continued=0 ===
1. "That article -- you went to the trouble of printing it yourself?"
2. "That's right. In fact, I had an entire magazine made at a printer's shop I knew. It was before DTP, so everything was done by hand -- which meant it cost me a fair bit of money."
3. "You sound like you enjoyed it."
4. "Yes, it was fun. I entered university, joined a circle... started planning in my first year, and pulled it off in my third."
5. "Was there someone in the circle you wanted to fool?"
6. "That's right. Fooling him would surely be a delight, I thought --\nthat childish temptation was undeniably where it started."
7. "And you went so far as all that. The Toritani family really is versatile,\nor whatever you'd call it..."
8. "We're a jack-of-all-trades household.\nAt first glance we can do anything, but in truth we accomplish nothing."
9. "Just enough talent to surprise people.\nBut you know -- that was tremendous fun."
10. "Japanese-bound books, ledgers, magazine clippings, ukiyo-e and oil paintings --\nI've seen the materials you used to deceive them, but how did you actually use them?"
11. "Let's see... I didn't do anything grand, but the important part was the source.\nFirst of all, I -- the person concerned -- must not be the one to start it."
12. "Then as now, I wasn't seen as the sort of man who'd boast\nor spread word about anything. It would have been unnatural."
13. "I see."
14. "In most any group, I think, there's at least one -- if you have a chatty one, that's ideal.\nYou make her, or him, the source."
15. "The place to start, let's say, is somewhere like the school cafeteria.\nYou sit down next to that chatty one and have a conversation with a friend."
16. "You don't have to speak all that loudly. Once you can tell she's listening in on your conversation, you take out the article and begin the story."
17. "I set it up so that the exhibition had been held several more years\nbefore we were university students."
18. "It was at the end of the article, wasn't it?\nThat further research was awaited to determine whether the vase was truly by Monet's own hand."
19. "The results of that research had come out. Apparently it was genuine -- that's what I told my friend."
20. "The rumor spread better than I expected, and various people took interest.\nAt an art university, no less, it became a bit of a stir."
21. "At first I was reluctant, and only at the end did I finally show the clipping."
22. "The vase itself was still in France. But the Monet painting it was based on -- that was in the storehouse. Pressed until I couldn't help but talk, I gave that answer."
23. "And then I led them to the storehouse where the oil painting and the ukiyo-e were."
24. "In the dim light of a storehouse it becomes all the more difficult to judge authenticity.\nSo everyone was neatly taken in."
25. "Yes -- everyone except Kusanagi Ken'ichirou."
26. "I'd gone to such trouble to prepare it, and the one man I most wanted to fool didn't fall for it.\nSo even when people praised how well-made it was, to me it was a great failure."
27. "You see, he hadn't read the fake article I'd worked so hard to make, and he hadn't heard the rumors either. He just caught wind of it and came to see the paintings alone."
28. "He said to me:\n'I judged by the paintings alone, so I wasn't fooled -- but most people love the stories that surround a painting. Your eye for it isn't bad. Above all, this is a good painting.'"
29. "I wonder if he meant that as consolation. What a fool... but yes... it was fun. A failure, but delightful. That's how I can think of it now."
30. "...Headmaster -- what was Kusanagi Ken'ichirou to you?"
31. "What was he -- hm."
32. 　The headmaster narrowed his eyes as if in nostalgia.
33. "The same age, the same university, and we even took the same classes on occasion."
34. 　He repeated the same answer as when I'd asked before. But in his voice\nthere was a certain distinctive familiarity he did not try to hide.
35. "By now, that's all our relationship amounts to."
36. 　My eyes, adjusted to the night, blinked as they took in the shop.
37. 　The interior of the Chimera felt more dazzlingly bright than usual.\nSurely it was because of the handmade ornaments strung around the room.
38. "The Milky Way and the bridge of magpies."
39. 　Looking up at the headmaster's murmur,\nI saw the Milky Way, made of folded paper.
40. "The bridge of magpies -- that was the Tanabata legend, wasn't it?"
41. "That's right. Once a year, on the night of Tanabata,\nthe magpies spread their wings to make a bridge, so Orihime and Hikoboshi may meet."
42. 　The bridge of magpies, huh.\nI remembered having spoken with Toritani about something similar before.
43. "It resembles you rather well, doesn't it?"
44. "That someone young like you takes pleasure in becoming a bridge\nfor something, or someone -- I can't say I entirely approve."
45. "Well... it's something I do because I like it."
46. 　The Milky Way of white folded paper, the bridge of magpies,\nthe glittering gold and silver stars, flowers as if dancing, paper moons...
47. 　I noticed Nei gazing up at them.
48. 　I walked over and spoke to her.\nNei was staring at a golden paper moon that hung above her.
49. "You want that moon?"
50. 　Nei gave a small nod.
51. "All right. Wait here a moment."
52. 　As I was looking around the shop for the part-timer who'd hung it up\nto ask permission, the headmaster came over.
53. "Wait a moment."
54. 　The headmaster soon came back with Kirino-san.
55. 　From across the room the part-timer was making a big circle with her arms.\nMeaning it was OK.
56. "That's the one you want?"
57. 　The part-timer brought over a sturdy stepladder.
58. 　Kirino-san climbed onto it timidly,\ngently removed the paper moon, and handed it to Nei.
59. 　Nei took it, touched the paper moon softly as if it were precious, and smiled.
60. "Thank you, Mother."
61. 　The headmaster's eyes, watching the Onda mother and daughter, were gentle.
62. "That was thoughtful of you, Headmaster Toritani."
63. "Yes. Precisely because we're strangers, I can see clearly and act well."
64. Because they're strangers -- was that it?
65. 　-- Because as a mother, she's a failure.\nThose words the headmaster had spoken came back to me.
66. 　A little way off, Toritani was watching --\nwatching the headmaster who quietly watched the Onda mother and daughter playing with the paper moon.
67. 　The gaze Toritani directed at the headmaster held no lingering grievance;\nit was simply calm.
68. 　But the distance between the two of them stayed exactly as it was,\nright until this time came to an end -- and even after it ended,\nthey never once drew any closer.
69. "Hup --"
70. 　I put the last table back where it belonged and glanced around the Chimera.
71. "That everything?"
72. "Yes. Thanks for the help."
73. 　The party had broken up peacefully, and the cleanup was finished.
74. 　The part-timers had all gone home too.
75. "Ah -- sorry, could you take that upstairs?"
76. 　The two of us carried the various odds and ends we'd used for the party\nup to Toritani's room.
77. "Come to think of it -- did you speak with the headmaster?"
78. "I didn't. There's no reason to."
79. "Worried about me?"
80. "Thanks. But we're fine as we are.\nI think I've said this before, but the problem is only on my end."
81. "For a long time I hadn't sorted out my feelings toward Headmaster Toritani."
82. "But that's gone now too."
83. "Did something happen?"
84. "Hmm... you could say something did, and you could say it didn't...\nI'm not trying to dodge the question or anything, I'm not you, Kusanagi."
85. "It's just that if I actually put it into words, it's the kind of trivial thing\nthat would make you think -- 'That's all?'"
86. "I'd like to know what happened."
87. "Do you remember when I went to the headmaster's office with Kirino-san?\nWhat the headmaster said then..."
88. "Whether blood-tied or not..."
89. 　Those words, murmured almost like a soliloquy, I remembered well too.
90. 　Toritani had been watching the exchange between the headmaster and Kirino-san the whole time.
91. 　Determined not to miss a single word, forgetting to breathe, forgetting even to blink,\nfor a long, long time --
92. "It settled into my chest that he loves Kei.\nNot as reasoning -- I understood it in my heart."
93. "So it's fine now."
94. "Of course I can't suddenly call him Father, and I'm not about to move back home.\nI think of it as having gone independent a little earlier than most."
95. "Yeah. You look like a weight's finally come off your shoulders."
96. "Right. That's about it. Honestly, I feel refreshed."
97. "Things with Kirino-san and with Reika got resolved too."
98. "Right. All that's left is to press on toward the Moore Exhibition."
99. "Oh, by the way. Do you have some time after this?"
100. 　Toritani spoke to me with a casually feigned lightness.
101. "Oh, I've got as much as you want. I don't mind staying up all night, even."
102. "Wha -- what are you saying?! I didn't mean it like --!"
103. "What do you mean, 'like that'? You want to talk, don't you?\nToritani -- you said it before, remember? That before long, you wanted me to hear all of it."
104. "A-Ah... y-yes, that's right... exactly..."
105. "Your face is totally red."
106. "Q-quiet! Even I go red in the face when I'm embarrassed!"
107. "No, I don't mind at all. That kind of unguarded Toritani is more than welcome."
108. "Ugh... you know what, go home already! Party dismissed!"
109. "Aww, noooo."
110. "Don't 'noooo' me! I said I wasn't going to talk, so we're done for today!"
111. "Come on, isn't it fine? Everything's been sorted out, everyone's cleared their minds --\nit's a fine night for saying the things you want to say."
112. "Right. I suppose being stubborn here won't get me anywhere."
113. "Stubborn, huh..."
114. "N-NOTHING AT ALL."
115. 　More like sulking than stubbornness, it sounded to me -- but I'll leave that unsaid for now.\nThe conversation isn't going anywhere.
116. "I'm interested in what you're trying to tell me too.\nSeriously -- I want to know."
117. "...Shall I make us some coffee?"
118. "Because I think it'll take a little while."
119. 　When I trace back my earliest memories,\nwhat comes back to me are, without fail, glimpses of her back.
120. 　Neither I nor Kei have any memory of being raised by our mother.
121. 　Mother was always a very busy person.
122. 　Our father -- we saw even less of him than of Mother.\nHe was absorbed in his work and his social life, and had no interest in child-rearing.
123. 　We ended up being raised by our paternal grandmother\nand by housekeepers who served as our nursemaids.
124. 　Kei was placed with the Nakamura family soon after birth.
125. 　But that Kei and I were half-siblings by different mothers -- I knew that from about the time I first became aware of myself.
126. 　The Nakamura household saw many people coming and going.
127. 　Relatives, guests, housekeepers... from the thoughtless adults around us,\nKei's position in the world was made plain to us,\nindirectly and directly, again and again.
128. 　Kei was shunned, mocked, and coldly treated.
129. 　But I, too, was in a similar situation.
130. 　I was never treated as the eldest daughter of the Nakamura family; I was always treated as my mother's daughter.
131. 　That my mother had done something the Nakamura family found unforgivable --\neven as a small child, I already vaguely understood it.
132. 　It's all Mother's fault -- how many times, like a curse,\ndid I hear that from my grandmother?
133. 　The very fact that you were born of that woman is your sin --\nhow many times did I hear that from my father's sister?
134. 　And yet, more than the mother whose face we hardly ever saw,\nit was the people right in front of us spitting their curses that we could not trust.
135. 　More than the words of these people, that silent back of hers seemed to us --\nfor some reason, somehow -- far, far more pure.
136. 　An unlovable child, my grandmother and aunt would call me, and berate me.
137. 　We were always alone in that house.
138. 　...That my mother had been not merely busy but deliberately kept away from us --\nI didn't come to know that until much later.
139. 　It was not a childhood one could call happy, but because the Nakamura family managed Yumihari Academy, which over its long history had produced many cultural figures and artists, the house was hung with many works of art, and those became our consolation.
140. 　Small children as we were, we would explore the Nakamura estate -- far too vast for us --\nhand in hand, and touch its many artworks.
141. 　Surrounded by so many paintings, we naturally began to paint as well.

=== lines=149 stop_reason=end_turn in=7975 out=4511 cache_read=3222 cache_create=0 last_entry_continued=0 ===
1. 　Our father, who was almost wholly indifferent to us,\nfor some reason showed reluctance about one thing alone: our painting.
2. 　No matter how much he disliked it, once he realized we wouldn't stop painting,\nhe grew ever colder, ever more distant.
3. 　Mother, on the contrary, was cooperative, and arranged for us to attend a painting class.
4. 　The hours we spent painting at that class were, for us,\nfree hours when we could enjoy painting without thinking of anything.
5. 　I wanted to grow up quickly.
6. 　I wished to grow up, and to be free of that Nakamura house that seemed to lie in stagnant water,\nfree of all its entanglements.
7. 　But I ended up leaving the Nakamura house far, far sooner\nthan I ever grew up.
8. 　Mother divorced Father.
9. 　Mother won custody of me, but Kei was to go to the Natsume family as an adopted son,\nand the paintings Kei and I had made were to be kept at the Toritani house.
10. 　-- Because he's a mistress's child, that's why Kei goes to the Natsume house!
11. 　-- For your mother, Kei is unwanted!
12. 　-- The paintings she bought back, but Kei -- Kei she wouldn't buy!
13. 　...Reika's words that day pierced deep into the weakest part of me,\nand became a thorn I could never pull out.
14. 　Why. Mother, why.
15. 　I was still far too young,\nand to accept with reason that only Kei was going to the Natsume family was terribly hard.
16. 　After the divorce, Mother grew even busier.
17. 　Swamped with the tangled negotiations following the divorce, with her duties as principal, with the mediations among the board members,\nshe simply could not find time for me.
18. 　After Mother's divorce, I came to live at the Toritani house, her family home.
19. 　My Toritani grandparents did not shun me, but neither did they especially welcome me.
20. 　Torn away from Kei, who had been with me all along, alone in a house I could not get used to,\nI grew quietly, inwardly, more and more brooding.
21. 　The one who consoled me then was a cousin on my mother's side, much older than I was.\nHer name was Toritani Shizuru.
22. 　Until I came to the Toritani house I hadn't even known she existed,\nbut through the presence of this cousin who visited often, my heart little by little softened.
23. 　That I grew up relatively sound and cheerful, given my circumstances,\nI owe, I think, to Shizuru.
24. [Makoto] "Why is it that Mother wouldn't take Kei in, I wonder?"
25. 　One day, when I murmured this while looking at Kei's paintings in the storeroom,\nShizuru answered me like this.
26. 　"That person is very clever, and very kind,\nso surely there must be some reason."
27. 　I was grateful for Shizuru's kindness.
28. 　But by then I had already lost the ability to believe in the kindness of Mother\nthat Shizuru spoke of, or in her reasons.
29. 　After a while, Shizuru went abroad to train in the art of coffee, a hobby that had grown into a calling.
30. 　I was her greatest understander, her greatest supporter.\nSmiling, waving, I saw her off.
31. 　And once again, I was alone.
32. 　From Mother's manner, on the occasions when I met her at the Toritani house,\nI could tell that things were, little by little, settling down.
33. 　But the time we had spent apart had grown too long.
34. 　From the moment I was born, I could count on my fingers the times we had spoken,\nand neither of us any longer knew how to be, or should be, mother and daughter.
35. 　Even at our rare meals together, words were few.\nEven face to face, I felt as though her back were turned to me.
36. [Saki] "Is there anything you want to talk about?"
37. 　To Mother's words, I only shook my head.
38. 　It wasn't that I didn't want to talk.
39. 　There was something I had long, long wanted to ask.
40. 　-- Mother. Why.
41. 　-- Why couldn't Kei come to this house?
42. 　-- Was Kei a child you didn't want?
43. 　The question, cold and hardened in the back of my throat,\nhad, over the long years, grown far too large.
44. 　If I were told that Kei really had been sent to the Natsume family because he was a mistress's child,\nI might come to hate my mother.
45. 　That was what I feared.\nEven so, she was still my mother.
46. 　My grandmother died.
47. 　My grandmother had been neither kind nor cold to me,\nbut she had properly seen to the care of my daily life.
48. 　That was enough. I was grateful.
49. 　Mother did not arrive in time for her mother's deathbed.
50. 　When Mother appeared before her dead mother, she was terribly composed.
51. 　Facing her, she did not shed a single tear,\nand as soon as she stepped out of the room, she began to talk about arrangements for the wake and funeral.
52. 　Something inside me snapped.\nA violent surge of feeling came pouring out of my mouth.
53. 　I railed at Mother.\nWhat a cruel, cold person she was.
54. 　Mother said nothing, only listened in silence.
55. 　All the resentment I had bottled up inside toward her,\nI hurled at her in words.
56. 　But even then, I could not bring myself to ask about Kei.
57. 　When I had spat it all out, Mother told me quietly,
58. [Saki] "That's right. I am a cold person."
59. [Saki] "In fact, I put the school before my children.\nI left my niece to look after my daughter, and did nothing myself."
60. [Saki] "I am a mother who deserves to be called cold."
61. 　That is what she answered.
62. 　What, I wonder, should I have said in that moment?
63. 　...There is one memory from my childhood.
64. 　It flickers inside me like a small light,\nand has warmed my heart all this long time.
65. 　My mother appeared before us and handed Kei and me each a set of paints.\nThey were a matching pair.
66. 　Was it at the painting class? Or was it at home?
67. 　That, I cannot quite recall.
68. 　I was a very gloomy little girl in those days,\na child who did nothing but cry for the moon.
69. 　With those paints, I painted the moon.\nAnd I wept.
70. 　That no matter how I longed, this hand of mine would never reach the moon.
71. 　In place of words, Kei drew a long, long ladder up to my moon.
72. 　Kei and I gave the painting of the moon and ladder to Mother.\nMother took it.
73. 　Did Mother smile for us?\nWas it a happy time?
74. 　It is all too far away now,\nand that, too, I can no longer clearly recall.
75. 　The night my grandmother died.
76. 　I opened the room where my paintings and Kei's were kept,\nand carried every one of my own out into the back garden.
77. 　In the bustle of the wake, no one in the house noticed what I was doing.
78. 　Only the painting of the moon and the ladder I could not find.
79. 　I searched, and searched, and at last found it in my mother's room.
80. 　The painting of the moon and the ladder had been beautifully set into a frame.
81. 　The frame was lovely.\nPerhaps my mother had made it herself.
82. 　I had heard that she was very deft with her hands, that there was nothing she could not do.\nEven that I knew only from what Shizuru had told me,\nand only as words.
83. 　But surely it was so.
84. 　My mother was very deft, very strong-willed, and it was for that very reason that she had taken over the school,\nfreed herself from the Nakamura family, and separated Kei from me.
85. 　Facing the painting of the moon and the ladder, I wept again.
86. 　Why. Mother. Why.
87. 　If you could treasure it like this.
88. 　From the painting of the moon and the ladder, I cut out only the moon,\nand together with my own paintings I burned it all.
89. 　The orange flames covered my paintings, and a pillar of fire rose high into the air.
90. 　With a soot-blackened face, I swore.
91. 　That I would not cry anymore.
92. 　That I would never cry over my mother again.
93. 　That I would grow up quickly.
94. 　The fire in the back garden that burned my paintings threw the Toritani house into an uproar,\nbut Mother said nothing to me.
95. 　The next day.\nWhen the funeral was over, I went to the city hall.
96. 　I went to look at the family register.
97. 　By confirming the register --\nthat I had become one of the Toritani, no longer a Nakamura,\nthat Kei was no longer at my side --
98. 　and, in doing so, to resolve that one day I would remove my name from the Toritani register as well.
99. 　It was there that I met a Kusanagi painting.
100. 　The certificate of the family register slipped from my hand and fluttered to the floor.
101. 　It was a shock.
102. 　As though the brain in my skull had been struck directly,\nas though the heart in my chest had been gripped directly --\nsuch a shock.
103. [Makoto] "-- -- -- Heh."
104. 　My lips shaped themselves, just a little, into a smile.
105. [Makoto] "Heh, heh heh..."
106. 　The laughter spilled little by little from my lips, spilled more and more,
107. [Makoto] "Ha, haha... ahahaha... ahahahahaha! haha! ahahahaha!"
108. 　It wouldn't stop. The laughter wouldn't stop.\nI laughed and laughed, and even when the tears came, I went on laughing.
109. 　Passersby turned strange looks on me, but I did not care in the least.\nI laughed, laughed, and then I wept.
110. 　I had sworn never to cry again.
111. 　That childish vow of mine collapsed easily before this painting,\nand only sakura, sakura upon sakura, spread before my eyes.
112. 　Amazing. Amazing. To think there was such a talent in the world.
113. 　The self who had come to look at a family register\nfelt so small, so trivial.
114. 　My heart trembled.\nAnd I was excited. I think I was thrilled.
115. 　To see a painting. To meet a painting.
116. 　Only that.\nBy only that.
117. 　Could my world be so easily changed?\nCould it be so easily changed as this?
118. 　Or was it that this painting was so great?\nI didn't know.
119. 　But truly, my world had been changed by this painting.
120. 　I resolved to surpass this painting.\nThat became my goal.
121. 　I thought there was nothing beyond it.\nI tried to surpass it. But I could not.
122. 　And so I gave up on my own talent.
123. 　There was a clean, clear-skied feeling in it.
124. 　Once again, I went to city hall to see that painting.
125. 　These two years, I devoted everything to painting.
126. 　During these two years, I feel as though I was constantly competing with the one who painted that.\nStrangely, I felt him very close.
