130. Either way, something was off about it.\nRin-chan really was a strange girl.
131. The bell rang, and lunch break began.\nToday, unfortunately, there was no bento.
132. But if I headed to the art room, there might be some raw rice left over.
133. Rice being a perishable good, it isn't something you're supposed to leave lying around,\nbut it should still be edible.
134. As I was about to enter the art room, I sensed something inside.\nSomeone seemed to be groaning...
135. Quietly, without making a sound, I opened the door.
136. "Uuu..."
137. Toritani's voice, but no Toritani in sight.\nMust be up in the loft.

=== lines=78 stop_reason=end_turn in=4640 out=2064 cache_read=3222 cache_create=0 last_entry_continued=0 ===
1. "Uuuu~~~~......"
2. Crouched in the corner of the loft, Toritani had some sort of notebook open and was groaning over it.
3. She seemed so absorbed she hadn't even noticed me.\nMaybe she was worrying about the sculpture?
4. "No way, ah... no way... how could anyone just... n-no way...\nth-they even do this kind of thing..."
5. Wh-what the hell????
6. "I-I can't..."
7. Her eyes spinning, Toritani flopped face-down onto the floor.
8. "Hey, hey, hey, don't push yourself. You okay?"
9. Toritani sprang upright and hid the notebook behind her back.
10. "Y-y-y-y-y-you were watching?!"
11. "I was watching, but... sorry about that.\nThe agonies of creation aren't exactly something you want people to see."
12. "The agonies... of creation."
13. "Th-that's right. Yes, exactly. I've got all kinds of worries."
14. "I see. Whenever you feel like it, come talk to me anytime.\nSometimes just talking clears your head, or opens up a new perspective."
15. "Y-yeah... thanks."
16. Eyes still averted, Toritani mumbled her thanks.\nRed-faced and meek -- an unusual reaction for her.
17. Cute, though.
18. Well... it was the day after we did what we did.\nMaybe it couldn't be helped.
19. "Makoto-san, Makoto-san."
20. "What is it? ...Huh?\nBut... well, sure, but... but..."
21. She stopped the mechanical pencil racing across the notebook.\nSome kind of trouble, maybe?
22. "Whoa."
23. Toritani was closer than I'd expected.
24. "What's up? You've got a weird look on your face."
25. "Excuse me. This is just what my face looks like."
26. "So, what happened?"
27. "They told me to take a break. It's true I hadn't taken as much as I'm supposed to...\nWhen they cite the labor laws at you, you can't really refuse."
28. As she spoke, Toritani sat down across from me.
29. It was a seat by the wall, hidden behind a potted plant,\nso customers wouldn't be able to see her at all.
30. "It's because you're here that everyone's fussing over me... honestly."
31. Muttering complaints -- unusual for her -- she opened the book she'd brought.
32. "Reading, huh."
33. "For a change of pace. I'd rather be studying, really, but... I've finished\nthis week's English vocabulary quota, and there isn't enough time to preview ahead..."
34. "Ah, Kusanagi. If there's anything you don't understand, I can teach you?"
35. "The other part-timer went out of their way to tell you to take a break.\nIf you teach me, that's not a break anymore, is it?"
36. "Well... that's true, I guess."
37. "Guess I'll take a break too."
38. "Fine by me, but are you making proper progress?"
39. "Yeah, no problem. I got from here to here done."
40. "Hmm... quite a bit of progress. I'm impressed."
41. "By the way, what are you reading?"
42. "Hm? This."
43. Toritani held up the cover for me to see.
44. "Did you buy it yourself?"
45. "As if. The Master left a book upstairs, so\nI'm being allowed to read it."
46. Toritani stroked the slightly sun-faded hardcover.
47. "This one, we had it at home too."
48. "Your parents' place?"
49. "Yes. Toritani's parents', that is. The words on the obi left an impression on me -- I remember them well."
50. "What did they say?"
51. "'Love is never fulfilled; what is fulfilled is only what is not love.'"
52. "What is fulfilled is only what is not love, huh."
53. "Sometimes I find it terribly strange.\nThe way this thing called love is treated as so precious."
54. "'I'll take on the whole world to protect you' -- there are countless\nstories overflowing with that sort of thing, right? Which means turning the world into an enemy for love's sake."
55. "As one ideal, as something of matchless beauty, love is spoken of,\nand love is set on the scale against everything else."
56. "Are love and all other things really equal in value? Does the scale balance?"
57. Eyes lowered, Toritani stroked the cover again and again.\nAs if sinking into her own thoughts.
58. "Things other than a fulfilled love.\nIf they were tragedies no one wanted, that would be a serious problem, but..."
59. "If it could be gained by sacrificing love... there's something I want..."
60. "Hey now."
61. "That's quite a thing to say in front of the person you're dating."
62. Toritani lifted her eyes and looked at me.
63. "True... but you know."
64. "In reality and in fiction alike, there's a fair number of men who leave\ntheir beloved behind and walk away, isn't there?"
65. "For something they must achieve, for glory, for an ideal, for peace, for justice... something beautiful that isn't love..."
66. "Or else... they leave in order to give themselves to something ugly that isn't love."
67. "Love may be too vast to coexist with other important things...\nI'm not a man, but I think I can understand that."
68. "Something like that -- wouldn't you understand it better than I do?"
69. "No, not really."
70. At my instant reply, Toritani blinked.
71. "R-really?"
72. "Yeah. The men of the Kusanagi family... tend to live for love, more or less."
73. "I-I see... yeah, that's true."
74. "Love, huh..."
75. Toritani flipped through the pages. Not to read them -- just turning them, then snapping the book shut.
76. "But I have something I want. No matter what.\nI can't give it up, it's precious to me... I have to make it come true."
77. "For the sake of the genius, right?"
78. "I think I do understand, more or less."

=== lines=139 stop_reason=end_turn in=6562 out=4339 cache_read=3222 cache_create=0 last_entry_continued=0 ===
1. I pick up one of the glass jars lined up in a corner of the clubroom.\nInside is something that looks like a very fine powder.
2. "Powder? Flour? No, that's not it... ash?"
3. "Kusanagi? What are you doing in a place like... oh, that."
4. Toritani gestures at the sample in my hand.
5. "This is ash?"
6. "That's right. Raw material for glazes.\nI told you before, the master sends them over."
7. "Sometimes he sends over ten kilos at a time, and other times just\na few hundred grams."
8. "Either way, I keep them like that as samples, but..."
9. "The collection site, of course, but what kind of ash it is, what metal,\nwhat stone, what soil--none of it is ever clear..."
10. "All I can really do is note the date it arrived and some rough info.\nHonestly... makes me wonder if 'sample' even means anything."
11. "The date?"
12. "It's written on the bottom."
13. "Right, so it is."
14. When I flip the jar over, the date is written on the bottom.\nWhat little is confirmed is also noted--this one's birch ash.
15. "Ah, that's right. Perfect timing. Wait a sec."
16. Toritani pulls a box out from the shelf and lifts the lid.\nInside are miniature ceramics along with a small glass jar.
17. "Remember I said I'd show you a sample of the stone I used for 'The Four Stars'?\nWhat's in this glass jar is it."
18. "What are these miniature ceramics over here?"
19. "These are test pieces. Before applying glaze to the actual work, I try out the mix and various other things on them."
20. "I mean, I know about them, but I didn't know you made such cute ones."
21. "C-Cute? Really? Making them three-dimensional like this shows how the glaze\nruns and how unevenness appears, so it's useful, but..."
22. "They're well made. Couldn't you sell these as they are?"
23. "Hmm... some people make bean plates or sake cups in batches and sell them,\nbut I prefer to keep them together with the mix and firing data."
24. "I see, as records of the experiments."
25. "But these three were made with the same glaze, same temperature, and same kiln,\nso they're basically identical... I'll give you one."
26. Toritani plops the test piece into my hand.
27. "You had such an obvious 'I want it' face on.\nIt's rare to see you make a face like that."
28. Toritani says this with a smile, then closes the lid.
29. "Well... strictly speaking... though I'm sure you already know this,"
30. "depending on where in the kiln it's placed, the type of kiln, and various\nother factors, kiln transmutation can occur..."
31. "Kiln transmutation, huh."
32. What happens inside the kiln can only really be known once you open it.
33. Various conditions overlap and influence one another, and effects appear that you never predicted, never even imagined.
34. That is kiln transmutation.
35. "You don't seem too fond of it.\nOr should I say, Toko-san isn't?"
36. "That's right. For the vessels I--or rather, Hakusu Toko--am aiming for,\nI want to avoid uncontrollable kiln transmutation as much as possible..."
37. Suddenly, Toritani falls silent. The mood of her gaze shifts.\nAs if looking far away, or sinking deep into herself...
38. I recall something Toritani once said to me.
39. --Why? ...I just know. The paint smell soaked into you.\nThe atmosphere of your daily life... the atmosphere unique to when you're painting...
40. --The face you make while thinking about composition. The face when thinking about pigments.\nIt's distinct. The behavior of people who paint...
41. Yeah... that's true.\nCertainly, when a person is making a painting--making something--their behavior is distinct.
42. The chime rings, and morning classes end.\nStudents begin moving off to spend the lunch break however they please.
43. ......Hm?
44. The direction Toritani heads after leaving the classroom...
45. It's neither the climbing kiln nor the art room.\nWhere is she going?
46. I thought I'd catch up along the way, but Toritani moved at quite a clip.
47. The way she slipped smoothly through the students loitering in the hallway,\nwithout hesitation, was light on her feet.
48. Unwavering, unhurried. She walks on without looking back.
49. It doesn't even seem to cross her mind that I might be\nfollowing behind... good grief.
50. The library, huh...
51. I walk slowly between the shelves, looking for Toritani.
52. The Yumihari Academy library is spacious, and with Toritani moving about,\nshe's exceedingly hard to find.
53. I give up and decide to wait near the reading area.\nThe wide reading space is nearly empty.
54. Even for lunch break, it's the time when most people are still eating,\nso the whole library is deserted and very quiet.
55. "...Oh my."
56. I raise a hand lightly to Toritani, who's carrying several medium-sized books and one large one.
57. "How unusual, you being in the library."
58. Even though no one else is around, Toritani takes care to keep her voice low as she sits down.
59. "Aren't you going to sit?"
60. Once I sit down, Toritani stops paying me any mind\nand silently starts to read her book.
61. It seemed to be a specialist book on ceramics.
62. She's already accumulated plenty of knowledge and skill, and still she reads...\nvery like the diligent Toritani.
63. Yumihari Academy, being a school strong in the arts,\nhas an impressive collection of art and technical books.
64. For hunting down specialist books that tend to be expensive per volume,\nthe Yumihari Academy library is perfect.
65. Only the sound of pages turning, riffle... riffle... echoes.
66. My eyes drift to the large book Toritani brought, and I reach for it.
67. This one alone is a different sort from the others.\nMore of a catalogue than a large book...
68. "Botero, huh..."
69. Fernando Botero. An artist known for his distinctive plump forms.\nUnder his hand, both the Mona Lisa and Jesus Christ come out chubby.
70. The catalogue Toritani brought is Botero's--and not the paintings but the sculptures.
71. "Oh, that. It's the catalogue from an outdoor sculpture exhibition held in Tokyo a few years back.\nI wanted to look them all over together, so it was a big help."
72. "For reference? Feels pretty far from what you make... after all this time, are you changing direction?"
73. "As if I'd take a risky gamble like that now. It's just..."
74. "Kusanagi, what do you think is the biggest difference between viewing sculpture and viewing painting?"
75. "I think it's being able to touch it."
76. "That's why I really love Botero's bronzes--they're popular as public art\nand actually fun to touch. To me they're a kind of ideal form."
77. "That great, weighty mass... those round, plump forms...\nif something like that were in the streets, I'd have to laugh."
78. "A form that departs from the everyday landscape, but with humor built in, so it doesn't feel unpleasant."
79. "A simple, gentle form a child could innocently reach out and ask, 'What is that?' about."
80. "Well, that was me, after all."
81. "You've actually seen one?"
82. "Yes, I met a whiskerless cat in Alice's Garden."
83. "A whiskerless cat in Alice's Garden... it's not... some kind of metaphor, right?"
84. "It's exactly what I said. Since I was a child, I don't remember which museum it was...\nbut there's a sculpture garden called 'Alice's Garden.'"
85. "Back then I didn't know the name Fernando Botero, and I didn't even\nrealize right away that it was a cat."
86. "Something big and round. What is this...? I wondered.\nI just wanted to touch it, so I did.\nIt was smooth, and warm from the sunlight."
87. "I was a child, so I couldn't put it into clear words, but\nif I put it into words now, what filled my heart was awe."
88. "Some huge thing I couldn't identify.\nGenerous, bright, and to me... it looked very much like a secret."
89. "It was packed full of 'something.' That's why this thing I couldn't quite understand\nwas so big and so weighty, I thought."
90. "That 'something' felt so much like a secret to me... it's not logical,\nso I can't explain any further why it looked like a secret, but..."
91. "In any event, that overwhelming mass and texture, and the awe that came from it--\nI first came to know all of that in that garden."
92. "It was your primal scene, then."
93. "Ah... yes. That's true."
94. "Maybe that's why I wanted to reconfirm it."
95. "Even if the details look wildly different from what I'm trying to make,\nif I'm going to make an objet..."
96. "I want it to be something people can casually reach out and touch."
97. "If I really wanted to reconfirm it, I could just look up which museum\nit was and go touch it in person, but..."
98. "Then go."
99. "Huh?"
100. "Shall we go together this weekend?"
101. "Ah, ah, yeah... I suppose..."
102. "What's wrong? Unusual for you to be so hesitant."
103. "This weekend, well... there's something I've been undecided about."
104. "Ah, you mentioned that before. Still undecided?"
105. "Yeah... it feels a bit like a gamble, you know...\nand I wonder if I should spend a precious weekend on something so uncertain..."
106. "If possible, I want to stop spinning my wheels and do the best I can, for certain...\nbut it keeps drifting through my mind..."
107. "But I think I'll be able to decide soon."
108. "I see. Then I'll keep the weekend open for you, so just say the word. I'll help."
109. "You really say that so easily. What'll you do if it turns out to be something serious?"
110. "So what? I'd still just be going with you."
111. "You really... say that so casually..."
112. "Look, it's not like I go around casually saying that to just anyone.\nIt's because it's you, that's exactly why I'm saying it."
113. "I know that..."
114. Toritani buries her face in the book she's been reading.\nI decided to keep quiet about the redness of the ears and nape her hair couldn't quite hide.
115. As I was fiddling with my laptop, Toritani appeared.
116. "Told to take another break?"
117. "Exactly... honestly, those girls read way too much into things.\nAnd they look strangely pleased about it..."
118. "You really are making a weird face."
119. "'Weird' is rude... I'm just not convinced, that's all."
120. Grumbling, she sits down across from me, just like last time.\nHidden behind the houseplants, Toritani is basically invisible to the customers.
121. "Laptop today?"
122. "Yeah, there was something I wanted to look up.\nI asked the other part-timer about the outlet and got permission."
123. "I know. We generally allow customers to bring in laptops."
124. I turn the laptop so the screen faces Toritani.
125. "This is it, right? The whiskerless cat."
126. Toritani looks a little surprised, then nods.
127. "Yes, that's it. Good work finding it."
128. "It was actually pretty easy."
129. "Since it was at a height where kids might get hurt, they got permission\nfrom the artist and removed the whiskers."
130. "So there was a reason like that behind it...\nI suppose he chose to coexist with the viewers."
131. "I'd like to make works like that too, ones many people can touch.\nJust being carefully tucked away in storage would be lonely."
132. "Maybe because you have that feeling, you ended up making\ntableware that many people can pick up."
133. "Ah... yeah. I feel like that's part of it. At first it was just for money, but it kept getting more and more fun."
134. "It doesn't matter what your first motive was.\nYour tableware is made with careful thought toward being picked up by someone."
135. "That's true... but that might also be what keeps me from real artistry. Artists tend to be nothing but ego..."
136. "But maybe that's exactly the driving force behind those overwhelming works."
137. "Well, there are plenty like that, but there are others too.\nBotero was like that, wasn't he?\nHe chose not letting children get hurt over the perfection of his own work."
138. "Heh, that's true."
139. "This cat really does look nice to touch.\nThat weighty mass and... that texture..."

=== lines=39 stop_reason=end_turn in=3277 out=1131 cache_read=3222 cache_create=0 last_entry_continued=0 ===
1. "Wh-what is it, suddenly going all quiet on me..."
