39. "With these hands, I'll create it."

=== lines=140 stop_reason=end_turn in=6830 out=4850 cache_read=3222 cache_create=0 last_entry_continued=0 ===
1. Two days later--
2. "Fwaah..."
3. Toritani let out a small yawn in the deserted train car.\nA rare sight.
4. Right now, Toritani and I were changing trains, on the hunt for that shining white sand--the sand that the Master had discovered, the sand Toritani had used for "The Four Stars."
5. The precise location was still unknown. All we had to go on were the email the Master had sent Toritani two days ago, and a picture postcard from those days.
6. The scenery outside the window was almost entirely swallowed up in green.\nNothing but mountains and fields as far as the eye could see.
7. "Not getting enough sleep?"
8. "Mm, well... And I pulled a bit of an all-nighter yesterday, too..."
9. "Normally you'd be the one saying, 'Listen. All-nighters are just inefficient, you know?'\n...Or something like that."
10. "I'd still say it even now."
11. "...I had a bit of a talk with my mother."
12. An unexpected person to come up.
13. "With Principal Toritani?"
14. "Yes. I figured the more clues we had about where we're headed, the better."
15. "I asked whether she'd been contacted around the time the Master sent that sand,\nand... about the Master's mental habits, and things like that."
16. "Did you turn up anything?"
17. "Nothing promising. Only..."
18. Toritani murmured while gazing out the window.
19. "I'm glad I got the chance to talk with her--with my mother--about all kinds of things."
20. Silence fell, and only the sound of the train echoed in the car with no one in it but us.
21. "I heard about the talk you had with her, about 'The Ladder to the Moon.'"
22. That was the picture book Nei had received from Toritani.\nOn the night of that party, Nei had told me the rest of the story.
23. "I do vaguely remember that... The Principal has a good memory, though."
24. "In other words, to you it's the kind of thing you forget right away... just that trivial."
25. "Hmmm. I wasn't really giving it deep thought when I spoke."
26. "...I don't think I'll ever forget it. Even if it was nothing to you."
27. "But isn't the Moon Rabbit something closer to you?\nIt's your other name, and your brand as well."
28. "That was just something the Master came up with. I had planned to make the brand name pica-pica. Even my name was going to be something different."
29. "pica-pica means magpie.\nWhich is exactly why the Master told her not to give me a name like that on top of it."
30. "The subject of the paintings Principal Toritani used to deceive people was the magpie.\nAnd the character for heron is used in her artist's name, too."
31. "Based on those paintings, the Master made the magpie vase."
32. The magpie had far too deep a karma with the women of the Toritani family.\nAnd not a good karma, either.
33. At the outset, there hadn't yet been a problem.\nIt was Reika's involvement that tilted things toward the bad.
34. If I'd known the circumstances, I would have told her to leave it alone, too.
35. "The Principal told me yesterday: apparently, when the Master gave me the name 'rabbit,'\nshe had a little idea in mind."
36. "Inside the rabbit, the heron sleeps."
37. "I see--so that's why the reading is not 'Usako' but 'Usagiko.'"
38. "In the artist's name Aosagi, the Principal used a play on words between 'sagi' meaning fraud and 'sagi' meaning heron... and taking all of that in, the Master tried to seal the heron inside the rabbit."
39. "So the wish was to keep the magpie's karma from carrying on\ninto your generation... is that it?"
40. "Who knows. The Principal said something similar, but...\nwhether the Master really intended that much, I can't say."
41. "Oh, and this too--I only learned it yesterday, but apparently the Master\nhas a very strong personal image tied to the rabbit."
42. "Always drowsy, the very picture of peace--a white, harmless, adorable creature."
43. "Maybe she wanted to entrust me with that extremely modern, personal image of hers."
44. "Drowsy, the very picture of peace, harmless... true, that's an image about as far from you as it gets."
45. "Yes. And that image belongs solely to the Master.\nI don't hold any such gentle image of rabbits."
46. "Do you know the story of Taishakuten and the rabbit? Long ago, Taishakuten disguised himself as a decrepit old man and appeared before a fox, a monkey, and a rabbit who wished to serve him."
47. "The fox and the monkey were able to bring the old man fruit and fish.\nBut the rabbit could gather nothing, and had nothing to offer the old man."
48. "So the rabbit had the old man kindle a fire, said, 'Please partake of my flesh,' and threw itself into the flames."
49. "Moved by that rabbit, Taishakuten, to preserve the rabbit's deed for all eternity,\ncarved its shadow into the moon... its charred figure."
50. "...I can't be a rabbit like that. I can't sympathize with it, I don't admire it,\nI have no impulse or reason that would drive me to do such a thing."
51. "At best, I'm a rabbit on the ground, yearning for the moon, unable to find a ladder,\nhopping around in little leaps."
52. "Oh, I'm not putting myself down or anything, so don't get the wrong idea."
53. "Of course not. Putting yourself down--that's got to be the word furthest from you."
54. "Hmph. Naturally."
55. If Toritani wanted to behave with utter arrogance, then I would respond in kind.\nToritani knew that, and so we had exchanges like this.
56. Even feigned cheer is cheer, as they say.\nAct that way long enough, and sometimes it turns out to be real.
57. Sometimes arrogance itself becomes a kind of strength.
58. "Sounds like you talked with the Principal about a lot of things."
59. "Yes. It's been a while... maybe even the first time. Discussing so many topics\nwith my mother, for that long."
60. "That reminds me, I talked about the postman. Do you remember?"
61. "That one I do remember. Between you and the Principal, I'd act as the go-between."
62. "Right. And so the postman became the sole existence capable of putting an end\nto the eternal cycle of the white goat and the black goat."
63. "The Principal said it was just like the bridge of magpies."
64. "Ah, both the so-called bridge of magpies and the postman in this case\nfunction as intermediaries, after all."
65. "She said the magpie is interesting from a comparative-cultural perspective, too."
66. "East and West... it's a crude division but let's set that aside for now... The meanings assigned to it as an image or symbol are apparently quite different."
67. "We didn't go too deep into it, but in Japan, when you say magpie,\nthe story of the magpie from just now is the famous one."
68. "And in the West... you'd know more about this than I do, wouldn't you?"
69. "The general image? If so, it'd be caution... tattling, that sort of thing."
70. "Right. I suppose Pieter Brueghel's 'The Magpie on the Gallows' captures that image plainly."
71. "Monet's magpie seems to keep a distance from that traditional Western\niconographic meaning."
72. "Which is perhaps exactly why the Principal found it easy to adopt as a subject for ukiyo-e."
73. "Yes. But when she rendered the magpie painting as ukiyo-e, she said there was an image she was strongly conscious of, on the contrary."
74. "The bridge of magpies from earlier?"
75. "A waka based on that. It's famous, so you probably know it, don't you?\n'When I see the whiteness of the frost that lies on the bridge the magpies have built, I know the night is deep.'"
76. "A waka?"
77. "What, it's a poem from the Hyakunin Isshu. Don't tell me you don't know it."
78. "Weeell, I wonder?"
79. "Weren't you taught it in middle-school classics?"
80. "W-were we?"
81. "Well, whatever... fwaah."
82. Toritani let out another small yawn.
83. "For now, I feel like if I don't keep talking about something, anything, I'll really fall asleep,\nso stay with my chatter for a bit longer."
84. "About that waka--apparently there are several interpretations.\nThe one my mother went with likens the stars of the Milky Way to frost."
85. Every so often, the word "mother" slipped into Toritani's speech.
86. Some of it was surely the drowsiness loosening her guard, but in the Toritani who had so stubbornly kept saying "that person" or "the Principal," I could feel a softening in her attitude toward her mother.
87. The all-night mother-daughter talk had not, it seemed, been in vain.
88. "The magpie in the ukiyo-e is a single bird, of course, and it's daytime, not night...\nand it's not frost but a snowy scene, but..."
89. "The stars falling like frost on the bridge the magpies made by lining their wings\nso they could cross the Milky Way... that image of winter and white\nwas always in her head, she said."
90. "...When I heard that story from my mother, what came to my mind was 'The Bird-Catcher.'"
91. "The bank of the Milky Way where the magpies come falling like snow... that white glittering sand stretching out there... shimmering, I imagined, like white frost filling the sky..."
92. "What the bird-catcher in 'The Bird-Catcher' actually catches is... fwaah... wild geese... associations are pretty haphazard... aren't they..."
93. Toritani's voice grew more and more drawn out.\nHer head swayed gently side to side, her heavy eyelids sinking.
94. "The place Shizuru... the Master... found... surely there too...\nsand like white frost or snow... must stretch on and on..."
95. "That's why I... that image..."
96. "Why don't you sleep a bit? We've still got time before our stop."
97. "If I leave it to you... we won't get where we're going..."
98. "No, in this case it'd be the opposite."
99. "The Master and I are of the same tribe--the directionally challenged. I'm sure that if I'm the one reading her email and the postcard, we're more likely to reach where we're headed."
100. "That's ridiculous... but I guess you do have a point, maybe..."
101. Toritani, now nodding off, agreed to my point with far less resistance than usual.
102. "...Sorry, I'm going to sleep for a bit... But this is my journey, so... leaving it all up to you would... feel bad... so wake me up properly, okay...?"
103. "Mm, thanks..."
104. With a soft thunk, Toritani rested her head on my shoulder and her quiet sleeping breaths began.
105. For a Toritani so mindful of other people's eyes to fall asleep so easily in a train car, even with only me around... one or two all-nighters wouldn't do this.
106. Back then too, she'd said her sleep was shallow.\nShe must have been thinking about her Moore Exhibition submission the whole time.
107. I looked up at the overhead rack.\nOn it sat the camping gear I'd brought from the Natsume house.
108. I'd brought it in case we ended up wandering through the mountains.\nWe were probably going to need it.
109. To find the land Toritani was aiming for over this weekend,\nordinary methods probably wouldn't cut it.
110. I looked over the Master's email that Toritani had forwarded to me,\nand the wording on the picture postcard.
111. "Overslept, so came back and got off. The moon was out, so I turned right..."
112. Perhaps I could see paths that Toritani couldn't.
113. "...ey, hey..."
114. "...Nn, what...?"
115. To Toritani, who woke rubbing her eyes, I calmly announced:
116. "We missed our stop."
117. "Haaah?!?"
118. After a beat, Toritani grasped the situation, snapped her eyes open, and looked around.
119. "You told me you'd wake me up properly, didn't you?!\nWhat are you doing falling asleep right along with me?!"
120. "Now, calm down, Toritani."
121. "How am I supposed to be calm about this--?!!"
122. Her machine-gun tirade cut off just before it fully burst out.\nShe stared at me as if testing me.
123. "Was this too, Kusanagi... part of your plan?"
124. "No, I wasn't thinking that deeply about it, but..."
125. "For now, let's head back."
126. "Right... Let's get off at the next station and go back. Honestly... without meaning to,\nthings are unfolding just like the Master's email said..."
127. But by degrees, we were learning that we ought not underestimate rural local trains.
128. "Hey, we've been going and going and there's no sign of the next station...\nDon't tell me this is an express, even at this crawling pace? Did we pass a station?"
129. "No, the distance between stations must just be pretty wide.\nOne segment might be twenty minutes long."
130. "I mean, where even are we in the first place? What prefecture, what line? Wait...\nugh, no signal, either..."
131. "Trains up in the mountains sometimes can't even manage a signal...\nJapan is bigger than it looks. Oh, an announcement."
132. "Next stop is~... ri~~... ri~~"
133. "It's so muffled I can't make out a word...\nFor now let's just get off at the next stop."
134. "Hey, Toritani. The next train's in two hours."
135. "Seriously?! Are we really still in Japan?! What backwoods is this?!"
136. "If you called a place a backwoods just because only a handful of trains run per day,\nI think a lot of people would get angry."
137. "Ugh, I'll apologize for that... Still, I guess... we've got no choice but to wait."
138. "Right. Good things come to those who sleep and wait, they say."
139. "Sitting around with you, I feel like I'll grow moss..."
140. "Say... are we really on the right road...?"

