1. [Naoya] "Yeah. Trust me."
2. [Makoto] "I want to say I can't, but... I despair at the fact that I've got no choice\nbut to trust you, of all people, when it comes to being directionally challenged..."
3. [NARRATION] 　After waiting two hours for the train... having somehow made it onto the next one,\nI talked down Toritani, who insisted we should ride back as far as we could,\nand got off when I felt I just couldn't stand it anymore.
4. [NARRATION] 　What I couldn't stand wasn't a bathroom emergency.\nIt was at the station where I judged we no longer needed to go back.
5. [NARRATION] 　Which station, which land -- it didn't matter.\nSomehow I had a strange feeling things would work out.
6. [Naoya] "I'm aware I'm lost."
7. [Makoto] "Then why on earth are you pressing forward without a shred of hesitation...?"
8. [Naoya] "I've got camping gear."
9. [Makoto] "I just pray we get back before Monday comes..."
10. [Makoto] "Haah... haah..."
11. [Makoto] "I'm fine. More importantly, isn't it dangerous to keep pushing on down this mountain path?"
12. [Naoya] "No, let's go a bit further."
13. [Makoto] ".........Understood."
14. [Naoya] "The moon was out, so I turned right..."
15. [Naoya] "This way."
16. [Makoto] "Huh, we're turning here? And with the moon at our backs???"
17. [Naoya] "Yeah, I have a feeling it's this way."
18. [Makoto] "Well, at this point complaining would be uncouth.\n--Let's go."
19. [Makoto] "Where in the world are we, I wonder..."
20. [Naoya] "I've no idea, but..."
21. [Naoya] "Guess it's about time we camped out around here. I'm hungry, and you must be worn out."
22. [Makoto] "Right... let's do that..."
23. [Makoto] "...Wait, hold on."
24. [Makoto] "Isn't that the sound of a river?"
25. [NARRATION] 　We strained our ears.
26. [Naoya] "It's that way."
27. [Makoto] "Isn't it the other way?"
28. [NARRATION] 　The two of us pointed in complete opposite directions.
29. [Naoya] "Should we split up?"
30. [Makoto] "No. Let's go the way you said."
31. [NARRATION] 　Toritani was speechless.\nI too, standing at the riverside, felt something well beyond ordinary emotion.
32. [Makoto] "Is this... is this really real...? Am I not dreaming?"
33. [NARRATION] 　Toritani, wandering forward as if in a daze, stepped into the shallows still wearing her shoes,\nand plopped down right there.
34. [NARRATION] 　Not caring in the least about getting soaked, she stared blankly at the riverbed.
35. [NARRATION] 　The water reflected the moonlight, glowing faintly white.\nWhat glowed was the fine sand spread across the bottom of the river.
36. [Naoya] "...Whew."
37. [NARRATION] 　Somehow, it had worked out.
38. [NARRATION] 　The smoke trailing through the autumn sky slowly faded away.
39. [NARRATION] 　There was no more firewood to feed into the climbing kiln, but the inside of the kiln was frightfully hot.\nFrom here on, we would let it cool naturally.
40. [Naoya] "We finally made it this far."
41. [Makoto] "Yes... we did."
42. [NARRATION] 　After obtaining the sand she'd sought that weekend,\nwe gathered clay from the land where the sand had been collected, and visited a farmhouse at the foot of the mountain for straw as well.
43. [NARRATION] 　The one who helped haul the enormous load was Suetake.
44. [NARRATION] 　Suetake, a fan of Toritani not as a tableware artist but as an artist proper,\nresponded to the request with something closer to delight.
45. [NARRATION] 　Toritani had thrown herself into her pottery work with hardly any sleep or rest, and dragged the project to this point.\nAll the heavy lifting I took on as much as I could.
46. [NARRATION] 　The work of applying, over broad areas of the clay object she'd prepared for glazing, an extremely complex iron underglaze painting with oniban ore,\nthen pouring on white glazes of differing thickness while leaving parts of the raw body exposed -- that was all done by Toritani.
47. [NARRATION] 　I'd watched her set down the iron-painting patterns, but this was decoration meant to be completed only after firing with the white glaze on top,\nand with wax resist also involved,\nit was hard to imagine the finished form.
48. [NARRATION] 　She'd apparently agonized over whether to make the white glaze using the collected sand into a straw-ash glaze like oni-hagi, or\na single-composition glaze like Shino, and ultimately settled on something closer to the latter.
49. [NARRATION] 　It was the white glaze she'd chosen as the best for this ceramic object, drawing on the experience of the various glaze blends\nshe'd tested on trial pieces back when she'd made The Four Stars.
50. [NARRATION] 　What the ceramic object now firing in the climbing kiln would look like when it was done, even I didn't know.
51. [NARRATION] 　But it was certain that, in every sense, it would be the culmination of everything Toritani had done up to now.
52. [Makoto] "After this it's slow cooling and then opening the kiln, but... honestly,\nyou don't know until you crack the mouth open."
53. [Naoya] "You don't have confidence?"
54. [Makoto] "Hardly. But you know it too, don't you -- the difficulty of a climbing kiln."
55. [NARRATION] 　A climbing kiln has too many chance factors for you to get exactly the result you intended.
56. [NARRATION] 　It's swayed not only by the regulation of the fire but by the weather and the wind at the time, the state of the firewood, the humidity and temperature,\nthe falling ash, the flow of flame -- every single condition inside the kiln.
57. [NARRATION] 　Which is precisely why astonishing masterpieces can also be born.
58. [NARRATION] 　Pottery is an art form birthed through the little cosmos of a climbing kiln.
59. [NARRATION] 　That's what makes it difficult, and what makes it fascinating. No wonder so many people are captivated by it.
60. [Makoto] "This time I'm going for gray -- ash-gray, rather than crimson --\nso I'm keeping an eye on that side of things too..."
61. [Makoto] "And ideally, if the glaze crackles just as I want it to... into a fairly coarse kairagi pattern, that'd be best..."
62. [NARRATION] 　Her muttering was flat, but the expression on Toritani's face as she stood at the kiln was severe.
63. [Makoto] "I've done everything that's possible by calculation. What I'm aiming for isn't\nsimple enough to reach by chance alone."
64. [Makoto] "I've given it my all, done my best. I can't let my guard down during the cooling, either,\nand I'll stay focused until I open the kiln. But the result..."
65. [Makoto] "Only the god of the kiln knows that."
66. [NARRATION] 　One way or another, the result... would be known in a little while.
67. [NARRATION] 　I waited outside for Toritani, who had gone inside the kiln now that the cooling period was over.
68. [NARRATION] 　It was the work Toritani had staked everything up to now on.\nHad it turned out well or not? My gripped hand tightened of its own accord.
69. [NARRATION] 　I called out to Toritani when she appeared.\nHer face, as she stood silent at the mouth of the kiln, was drained of all color.
70. [NARRATION] 　Had it failed? No, but... it couldn't be!
71. [NARRATION] 　And yet, that "it couldn't be" was exactly what made pottery so terrifying.
72. [NARRATION] 　Inside the kiln there is a god, but there is also a devil.
73. [Naoya] "Tori... tani?"
74. [NARRATION] 　As if in answer to my call, Toritani let out a long, deep breath.
75. [Makoto] "What are you making that face for?"
76. [NARRATION] 　A smile rose to Toritani's lips.
77. [NARRATION] 　It was a smile that tried to play at fearlessness and failed -- but a beautiful, relieved smile\nin which Toritani's true self shone through vividly.
78. [NARRATION] 　Her voice trembled a little.
79. [Makoto] "Sorry, but could you help me carry it to the art room?"
80. [Naoya] "So it was a success."
81. [Makoto] "Heh, of course. Naturally."
82. [NARRATION] 　Toritani, laughing as she said it, was her usual self, full of confidence.
83. [Makoto] "And -- I know it's this early in the morning, but there's a place I'd like you to come with me to.\nIs that all right?"
84. [Makoto] "Yes. There's someone I want to show it to first."
85. [NARRATION] 　We stood in front of the atelier door.\nBeyond the door I could sense Kei moving about.
86. [NARRATION] 　Toritani stopped just as she was about to knock,\nand quietly rested her forehead and both hands against the door.
87. [Makoto] "There's something I want you to see."
88. [NARRATION] 　In contrast to her prayerful pose, the crisp voice that left her lips seemed to shoot through the door like an arrow\nand pierce the eardrums of the single person sitting in the atelier.
89. [Makoto] "Kei... I want you to be the first to see it."
90. [Makoto] "It's in the art club room, so... see you."
91. [NARRATION] 　That said, Toritani stepped away from the door.\nHer words were few, but a boundless flood of feelings had been packed into them.
92. [NARRATION] 　A step, then two, and then -- thunk -- Toritani's back met the storehouse wall.
93. [Naoya] "Hey, you okay?"
94. [NARRATION] 　There was no reply. Her body simply slid down along the wall\nand sank to the ground.
95. [NARRATION] 　The moment I touched the shoulder of the crouched Toritani,\nher body tilted and toppled toward me.
96. [Makoto] "Zzz..."
97. [NARRATION] 　Toritani slept deeply against my chest, all but fainted.\nAt the sound of her peaceful breathing, my face went slack.
98. [Naoya] "--Well, of course."
99. [NARRATION] 　At the end, she'd been running on almost nothing but willpower.\nRather, it was impressive she'd managed to make it as far as Kei.
100. [NARRATION] 　Having Kei see it, telling Kei...\nthat must have been Toritani's foremost objective.
101. [NARRATION] 　She'd wrung out her willpower to the last drop, come here, and conveyed her feelings.
102. [NARRATION] 　That she'd then fallen asleep like a battery going dead in a place like this\nmust be deeply unsatisfying for Toritani, but.
103. [Naoya] "Alley-oop."
104. [NARRATION] 　I scooped the sleeping Toritani up in my arms.\nThe princess-carry, the same one I'd done once before escaping the schoolgirls.
105. [NARRATION] 　Now there were no complaints -- she was simply, deeply asleep.
106. [NARRATION] 　For now, I'd carry Toritani back to my room.\nTo get Ai's consent, I'd have to wake her up first, after all.
107. [Makoto] "Why didn't you wake me up?!"
108. [NARRATION] 　Toritani, indignant, went charging up the slope at tremendous speed.
109. [NARRATION] 　The students heading home turned to look at what was going on,\nbut she was in no state to worry about the surrounding gazes.
110. [NARRATION] 　When she woke up it was already evening, so Toritani's anger wasn't entirely incomprehensible.\nI was surprised too.
111. [Naoya] "I tried to wake you plenty of times. But you didn't so much as twitch."
112. [Makoto] "Wait, really?"
113. [Naoya] "That's a lie. I was asleep next to you the whole time, wasn't I?"
114. [Makoto] "...Now that you mention it, yes. Ugh...\nto not catch a simple trick like that -- what a blunder."
115. [Naoya] "You're worn out from everything being over. Let it go this once."
116. [Makoto] "It's not over yet! Aah, I looked, but Kei wasn't in the atelier...\nwhich means he must still be at school, but..."
117. [Naoya] "He's probably in the art room, isn't he?"
118. [NARRATION] 　At my words, Toritani came to a dead stop.\nHer earlier fury flipped, and she went strangely quiet.
119. [Makoto] "You think?"
120. [Naoya] "Why are you so unsure? It's fine. He's in the art room."
121. [Makoto] "It's not that I'm unsure..."
122. [Makoto] "But you're right... let's go."
123. [NARRATION] 　Toritani, having lingered in front of the art room for a while,\nreached out hesitantly and slowly opened the door.
124. [NARRATION] 　Leaning lightly against the doorway, I watched Toritani's back\nas she made her way deeper into the art room.
125. [NARRATION] 　When Toritani opened the door to the preparation room, she stopped where she stood.
126. [NARRATION] 　I stepped closer to Toritani, who was quietly holding her breath, and peered into the room over her shoulder.
127. [NARRATION] 　Countless canvases were scattered about.
128. [NARRATION] 　At the very back center of the preparation room sat Toritani's ceramic object.
129. [NARRATION] 　The object, in the dim room lit only by faint moonlight,\nwas wrapped in quietude and a dim white glow.
130. [NARRATION] 　It was the moon, descended to earth.\nClad in a white light that resembled moonlight, it sat there in silence.
131. [NARRATION] 　The thickly applied white glaze represented the shining lunar surface, while the gray areas where the patterns rose up from beneath the thinly applied translucent glaze, and the black areas laid bare by the crackling known as kairagi, formed the shadows of the moon in exquisite gradations.
132. [NARRATION] 　The shadow of the heavenly moon rose up as a rabbit.
133. [NARRATION] 　The shadow of the earthly moon, in the form of countless magpies,\nlaid a bridge across the Milky Way that glowed smoothly across the surface of the sphere.
134. [NARRATION] 　The far side of the moon -- that was the name Toritani had given this ceramic object.
135. [NARRATION] 　The far side of the moon has already been unveiled by science.\nAnd yet, the far side of the moon can never be seen from the earth.
136. [NARRATION] 　Toritani had poured her imagination, her wish, and every ounce of her powers\ninto making the far side of the moon.
137. [NARRATION] 　One can never fly to the visible face of the moon.\nBut to the far side of the moon --
138. [NARRATION] 　From this earth, with the heart alone, one can reach.\nA landscape of fantasy.
139. [NARRATION] 　It made me want to ask, honestly, which of them was the more impressive.
140. [NARRATION] 　As if afraid of something, Toritani began to inch her way forward.
141. [NARRATION] 　Around the moon Toritani had brought forth, countless canvases lay scattered.\nThe pictures painted on those uncountable canvases.

