130. [Rina] "A cypress reaching skyward like a single streak of pitch-black flame, and a single road passing beside it."
131. [Rina] "A hollow star that seems to melt vaguely into the sky, in the left night sky; and against it, a moon thin and sharp as if cut out of the sky. Rising up in the center, dividing them, the pitch-black cypress."
132. [Rina] "A motif that symbolically hints at death......"
133. [Rina] "To Miyazawa Kenji, I think those cypresses -- ZYPRESSEN -- looked like black flames reaching the sky......"
134. [Rina] "A cluster of stories in which things become flame in the night sky and go on shining...... probably a black flame that reaches all the way to heaven."
135. [Rina] "The scorpion's crimson flame, the nighthawk's deep blue flame...... a black flame -- the cypress is that kind of thing."
136. [Yumi] "Um...... is that why you're drawing a cypress in a place like this at night, Hikawa-san?"
137. [Rina] "Ah, um, that's, well, that's part of it...... oh, right. It's time for my medicine."
138. [Yumi] "Time for your medicine?"
139. [Rina] "Ah, sorry."
140. As if hiding it, Hikawa-san took the medicine out of a blister pack.
141. But I didn't want to miss a single one of her movements, so I was peeking at her.
142. On the blister pack was written "UFT Sunfural S" -- I committed it to memory.
143. [Rina] "Will you meet me again tomorrow, too?"
144. [Yumi] "O-of course......"
145. The name of the medicine she'd swallowed wouldn't leave my head.

=== lines=144 stop_reason=end_turn in=6821 out=7488 cache_read=3222 cache_create=0 last_entry_continued=0 ===
1. Why was it -- as though I somehow already knew what that medicine was.
2. --Symbol and Death--
3. That night, I looked up that medicine.
4. And so, I came to know all of her secrets.
5. Why she avoided sunlight.
6. Why she hinted at death.
7. No -- why she loved only works that seemed to accept death.
8. Van Gogh. Cypress.
9. Miyazawa Kenji. Scorpion. The Nighthawk Star.
10. Each of them, though symbols of death, were works that seemed to embrace it willingly.
11. She chose such works,
12. and through the power of that world of fantasy,
13. was trying to flee from the terrifying things of reality.
14. She avoided the sun,
15. preferred to be beneath the moonlight,
16. and alone at night, played among cypresses and playground equipment.
17. That was --
18. that her beauty
19. lay side by side with death;
20. that she resembled the moon, resembled a pale blue burning flame,
21. was because her life could only be produced by the reflection of light from without,
22. her whole body sunk in the darkness that is death --
23. it was a string of such metaphors.
24. The medicine to hold back that which sought to take her life --
25. "UFT Sunfural S"
26. And, as its side effect, photosensitivity.
27. Her parasol, her white clothes,
28. playing only in the park at night,
29. her frail beauty, her manner --
30. none of it was coincidence; it was pure necessity.
31. The disease nesting within her.
32. And at the same time,
33. her spirit,
34. out of that terror,
35. required art that accepted death.
36. The next day too, at noon, she was laughing.
37. Smiling gently in the midst of a large circle of people.
38. To me now, her polite way of speaking, her manner, her cleanliness --
39. those things looked not like elegance,
40. but like a nervous quality she had instinctively taken on because of her illness.
41. The beauty she possessed stood upon that sort of precariousness.
42. Again our eyes met.
43. But this time, Hikawa-san didn't smile when she saw me.
44. She only made a slightly sad face and then looked away.
45. Someone beside me said, "What's with her? We just made eye contact."
46. I had no will even to argue back, and I simply left the spot.
47. I fell in love with her.
48. Now that I knew the reason for her beauty, I came to love her all the more.
49. A girl side by side with death.
50. And it was for that very reason that she was beautiful.
51. I am a fly.
52. Probably a single fly drawn in by the scent of her death.
53. To liken myself to a lion would be presumptuous.
54. I am not the one who takes, but a bystander who watches the one from whom things are taken.
55. So I plant my eggs in her beautiful body.
56. A fantasy of my other selves swarming from her surely beautiful corpse.
57. My spirit was truly like that of a maggot.
58. Without so much as encouraging her,
59. as she tried to accept death,
60. I had simply come to love her.
61. Truly, the spirit of a maggot.
62. --The Sakura Artist--
63. Here, I must speak of that infuriating sakura artist.
64. The young sakura artist's works, too, evoked death.
65. At first it was only a premonition, but afterward it turned to certainty.
66. Because after that, I saw that sakura painting at the community hall.
67. Carved into it was the sentiment of "mourning the death of a loved one."
68. That the painting was titled "Sakura Day Rhapsody" is suggestive.
69. It's obvious he painted it with Nakahara Chuuya's "Spring Day Rhapsody" in mind.
70. When one you love has died, you must kill yourself
71. When one you love has died, there is no other way
72. The poem that begins with this famous phrase
73. is said to have been composed by Chuuya to mourn the death of his own child.
74. Why the sakura artist had Nakahara Chuuya's poem in mind, I do not precisely know.
75. But from that painting, just as in Chuuya's poem, came the sorrow of losing someone dear.
76. That anguished heart had been smeared across the canvas along with the paints.
77. That must be why.
78. On his first look, he had bluntly named the death drifting from Hikawa-san's work.
79. "Your cypress won't do."
80. "Who are you?"
81. "I'm Kusanagi Naoya. This area is my graffiti (rakugaki) territory."
82. "The one painting here right now is me."
83. "Right now, yeah. But originally, it wasn't......"
84. "Eh? Then that means......"
85. Without so much as a greeting, this rude fellow had trespassed into our domain -- I stood in his way, ready to expel him at once!
86. "Territory or whatever, get out of here this instant, or else --"
87. I drew out my knife.
88. But he was unfazed, staring at my knife.
89. "I was here first. You two came later."
90. "Doesn't matter! Whether you were first or not isn't the issue!"
91. "N-no. Put that away, Yumi."
92. "Well now......\nA dangerous toy like that isn't something a brat should be holding.\nYou'll hurt yourself, kid."
93. "What did you say......"
94. I swung the knife down at the boy.
95. "Whoa, wha, ho-hold on, wait, wait, that'll stab, that'll stab!"
96. "Shut up, get out of here already!"
97. "Wait, timeout, timeout -- put the knife away first!\nDan-- dangerous! Wa! Wawa!"
98. What was up with this guy?
99. The man before me, all that earlier composure gone who knew where, was crying out in falsetto and scrambling away from my knife.
100. "Th-that trunk!\nThat trunk -- did you draw it?"
101. "Hikawa-san......"
102. "Why didn't you finish the painting?"
103. "Who knows. It wasn't as though I had any grand idea."
104. "But I was looking forward to seeing it completed."
105. "I do studies on walls all over the place.\nWhat I drew here was one of them."
106. "Why do you draw them all over?\nWhy didn't you finish the painting here?"
107. "They say a dog hides its precious treasures in all sorts of places......"
108. "In other words, you forgot the paintings you'd drawn yourself, like a dog that forgets where it buried its bones......"
109. "That was an extraordinarily warped, strange trunk."
110. "And to you, it looked like a cypress. A black cypress that seemed to burn."
111. "Wasn't it?"
112. "Who knows. But what I was trying to draw wasn't a motif that accepts death so meekly."
113. "To put it another way -- 'it wasn't the sort of thing an understanding girl would draw by imitating Van Gogh's death-conscious style so as to accept her own death.' How's that?"
114. Even I had grasped her condition from the medicine Hikawa-san was taking.
115. This man had met us today for the very first time.
116. And yet, just from looking at Hikawa-san's painting, he had named her condition.
117. "The painting I drew partway...... You wanted to see the continuation of that painting, and that's why you drew. Isn't that right?"
118. "So today, I drew that continuation."
119. "Eh? You mean, the continuation of the painting in the dome?"
120. "Yeah, this really is a cypress grove.\nI understand thinking of Van Gogh. Especially under the night sky, I understand wanting to let one's imagination wander toward his work."
121. "But -- you might not notice because of the season, but there's a somewhat different scenery here as well."
122. "A different scenery."
123. "I came to draw that. I haven't killed your cypress.\nYour painting is, honestly, too influenced by Van Gogh and lacks freshness,\nbut still, I felt something eerie and pressing in it."
124. "There's a vividness to it, as though your very life had cast its shadow on the dome wall."
125. "I felt as though I saw the shadow of your life in that."
126. "There's just one part I don't care for, though."
127. "What are you droning on about?! Just who are you?! Get lost!"
128. "Yeah, I'll be gone whether you say so or not."
129. "But just know this: your cypress has been repurposed as part of my study."
130. "Your painting is too childish to be called acceptance of death. Life is still burning in it.\nThat's why I decided to draw the painting I'd originally planned."
131. "If you don't like it, just erase it. It's only chalk. Not something that can't be erased."
132. The boy vanished.
133. After that man had gone, we entered the dome.
134. And the two of us caught our breath.
135. A blazing black cypress -- and that cypress was surrounded by many stars.
136. However, they were not stars that had been drawn.\nStars expressed by not being drawn.
137. In other words, stars-not-drawn, expressed by drawing the sky.
138. They looked almost like crimson sparks born of the black flame.
139. Hikawa-san, seeing this painting, kept silent for a long time.
140. Her swirling emotions -- "anger," or perhaps "sadness"; either way, I had thought they came from having her own work overwritten without her leave.\nBut her next words made me realize how mistaken I'd been.
141. Simple admiration.
142. And the impulse born from it.
143. I felt an indescribable unease at the unprecedented strength brimming in Hikawa-san's eyes.
144. Hikawa-san at once took up her chalk and drew the continuation.

=== lines=141 stop_reason=end_turn in=6026 out=2941 cache_read=3222 cache_create=0 last_entry_continued=0 ===
1. Hikawa-san, silent.
2. I could only stand there in a daze.
3. The stench of death fades.
4. Maggots cannot breed in a healthy body.
5. The flies will be at a loss.
6. --Modulation III--
7. --The Sakura Artist II--
8. That boy was not there.
9. And yet, when we went to the dome, the continuation of the painting had been drawn.
10. Seeing it, Hikawa-san took up her chalk again.
11. The next day too.
12. And the day after.
13. And the day after that.
14. The two of them never met face to face -- they only took turns before the same canvas.
15. Paintings finished by each, on their own selfish terms.
16. An uncooperative collaboration.
17. Without ever meeting, they simply kept updating the work.
18. --Update I--
19. --Update II--
20. --Update III--
21. The selfish version-ups went on.
22. And something in them began, little by little, to head in a single direction.
23. (The maggots inside me are stirring.)
24. Letters
25. (The flies can no longer come near.)
26. Letters exchanged
27. (The scent of death is being erased.)
28. The abominable Sakura Artist
29. (Maggots that have lost their seedbed do not hatch.)
30. Through the artist, the wall had become a letter in the form of painting.
31. There were no words there, only pictures.
32. Hikawa-san is not together with that man.
33. She is always here with me, in this place.
34. And yet the distance between us keeps widening.
35. The Sakura Artist is taking her from me.
36. In the proper way,
37. Her cold-looking blue skin began to flush a warm red.
38. With each brushstroke, her vitality grew.
39. She drew the symbols of death.
40. The Sakura Artist only drew the sky.
41. Yet each time she drew death's symbols, somehow the scent of death faded from her.
42. In its place, the smell of a living human being began to rise.
43. White chalk.
44. There is no color.
45. And yet within the layered whites they wove together, infinite colors lived.
46. In living human beings, maggots do not breed.
47. I simply stood there beside her, unable to move.
48. --A Midsummer Painting--
49. A midsummer night.
50. The clouds streamed by as if gone mad.
51. The moon was hidden by them,
52. appeared and vanished,
53. appeared again,
54. flickering giddily on and off.
55. From the radio, forecasts of the approach of a huge typhoon
56. sounded again and again.
57. The flies rejoiced at the midnight storm.
58. A typhoon, surely, would wash it all away.
59. The strong winds would tear that dome full of holes to shreds,
60. and easily wipe away the chalk paintings the two of them had drawn.
61. The maggots inside me squirmed.
62. The wind grew stronger.
63. Across the midsummer night, the clouds raced lightly on.
64. Unable to wait for that hour, I set off for the place.
65. The junk playground beyond the cypresses.
66. In front of the dome enshrined at its center, she was standing.
67. Hikawa-san, robbed of her voice, only standing there.
68. Not knowing what her silence meant, I simply made my way to where she stood.
69. The moment I stood in the same place as her,\nI saw, inside the dome, flowers blooming in full glory.
70. As if wrapped in the breath of spring.
71. The boy in the depths of the dome turned around, chalk in one hand.\nHe looked as though he had emerged from the whirlpool of flowers......
72. "On a mad, stormy night like this --
73. yes, why not --
74. -- I figured it was about time to let it flower."
75. So spoke the Sakura Artist.
76. "...Th-they weren't stars......?"
77. "No, they aren't stars. What's drawn here is cherry blossom petals, all of it."
78. "You can't tell in this season, but this place has many cherry trees blooming among the cypresses.\nWhen I first started on the work here, the cherry blossoms were in full bloom."
79. "That's not the only reason, though. Stars just don't suit you to begin with."
80. "A brat who looks like she wants to become a star for real -- stars don't suit that kind.\nSomeone like you needs flowers. And flowers that bloom in spring, at that."
81. "The cherry blossoms are beautiful because they make one feel death there......"
82. "There was a novelist who said something like that, too."
83. "But by sensing death in the cherry blossoms, he succeeded in accepting their beauty lightly, quite naturally."
84. "Cherry blossoms are too gaudy to lay upon the dead.\nThey suit living people best......"
85. "See, it turned into a painting overflowing with life, didn't it?\nA masterpiece in its own right."
86. Ignorant as I was, I could not grasp the finer points.
87. But the work of the man called Kusanagi Naoya carried a brighter light than Hikawa-san's, and by that light, the darkness of the night sky drawn in chalk grew deeper still.
88. Where Hikawa-san's work had clung to drawing shadows in an effort to express the abyss, that man, by putting in light, had expressed the abyss as something deeper still, and with the greatest of ease.
89. To put it plainly: the symbolic motif of "death" is thrown into relief by "life."
90. And this also meant that "life" is thrown into relief by the symbol of "death."
91. Sorcery of technique.
92. That technique alone could so change the way a thing appeared -- I marveled at it, and at the same moment,
93. I found myself shouting that man's name.
94. "What is it? Don't yell at me."
95. "Or what -- you want to add something?"
96. I shook my head.\nMy back teeth chattered a few times.
97. "Then it's your turn now. I'm done."
98. "No...... This painting is finished with this......"
99. "Why? There's still time before the typhoon makes landfall."
100. "It isn't a matter of time."
101. Hikawa-san stepped into the dome.
102. "This painting is as splendid as anything could ever be."
103. "So it is finished."
104. The Sakura Artist spoke as if a little relieved.
105. "So it's complete, huh......"
106. "Though in a few hours there'll be no trace of it left."
107. "No!"
108. "It won't disappear!"
109. Hikawa-san cried out.
110. And at that, the Sakura Artist bared his teeth in a grin.
111. "This art, right up until this moment. It was fun."
112. "Well then -- enjoy it and be done!"
113. The two of them laughed.
114. The sky rumbled.
115. The clouds raced.
116. The moon flickered on and off in dizzying flashes.
117. The summer night was gleefully mad,
118. and I, unable to keep up with the crazed tempo,
119. could not even laugh.
120. Amid the forest of the cherry-blossom painting, Hikawa-san laughed.
121. A smile I had never seen before.
122. That cherry blossom is a deadly poison.
123. A deadly poison the likes of "UFT Sunfural S" couldn't hope to match.
124. It had turned her fleeting, beautiful smile into this radiant one.
125. "Kusanagi-san! Would you teach me painting? Would you become my master?"
126. "Ow! Hey, quit attacking me over every damn thing!"
127. "Shut up! Die!"
128. "Now hold on -- I get why the brat here would be upset, but why are you the one taking swings at me?"
129. "Why would you refuse!"
130. "Give me a reason!"
131. "That's obvious, isn't it...... Taking a disciple at my age -- makes no sense."
132. "Age has nothing to do with something like that!"
133. "Uh, well then -- brat, are you serious about this?"
134. "I am not a brat. I am Hikawa Rina."
135. Eyes of strong will -- within Hikawa-san's gentleness, there was that dignified core.
136. "Hmm...... Oh, that's it! 'Disciple' -- deshi -- you write it with 'younger brother' and 'child,' right? You're a girl, so you can't be one! That's a boys-only thing."
137. "Then write it with 'younger sister' and 'child' and read it 'deshi' -- problem solved!"
138. "No, that's not the issue here."
139. "Then what IS the issue?!"
140. "Well, as for what the issue is......"
141. "Yes, what is the issue?"

=== lines=143 stop_reason=end_turn in=6249 out=3728 cache_read=3222 cache_create=0 last_entry_continued=0 ===
1. Under Hikawa-san's gaze, Kusanagi Naoya fell silent.
