32. "'Is that so? I'd like to go and see her too,' said the wolf. 'I'll take this road, and you take that one, and we'll see which of us gets there first.'"
33. "The wolf rushed off at full speed by the shortest way, while the girl went by the longer road, dawdling as she went -- gathering nuts, chasing butterflies, and picking little flowers to make bouquets."
34. "The wolf reached the grandmother's house without any trouble at all.\nHe knocked at the door.\nTap, tap."
35. "'Who's there?'"
36. "'It's me, Little Red Riding Hood. Mother has sent me with a galette and a pot of butter for you.'"
37. "The grandmother, who was in bed a bit unwell, called out loudly,"
38. "'Pull the latch, and the bolt will fall.'"
39. "The wolf pulled the latch and the door opened.\nHe pounced on the grandmother and gobbled her up in an instant."
40. "In truth, the wolf had had nothing to eat for over three days, and he was terribly hungry."
41. "Then the wolf shut the door, went and lay down in the grandmother's bed, and waited for Little Red Riding Hood."
42. "After a while, there came a knocking at the door."
43. "'Who's there?'"
44. "Little Red Riding Hood was frightened at first by the wolf's deep voice, but she thought her grandmother must have caught a cold, and so she answered,"
45. "'It's me, your granddaughter, Little Red Riding Hood. Mother has sent me with a galette and a pot of butter for you.'"
46. "The wolf softened his voice a little and said,"
47. "'Pull the latch, and the bolt will fall.'"
48. "Little Red Riding Hood pulled the latch, and the door opened. When he saw her come in, the wolf kept himself hidden under the covers of the bed."
49. "'Put the galette and the pot of butter on the bin, and come lie down here in bed with Grandma.'"
50. "Little Red Riding Hood took off her clothes and made ready to get into bed, but she was very startled to see how her grandmother looked in her nightdress."
51. "'Grandma, what big arms you have!'"
52. "'All the better to hold you tight, my dear.'"
53. "'Grandma, what big legs you have!'"
54. "'All the better to run with, my dear.'"
55. "'Grandma, what big ears you have!'"
56. "'All the better to hear with, my dear.'"
57. "'Grandma, what big eyes you have!'"
58. "'All the better to see with, my dear.'"
59. "'Grandma, what big teeth you have!'"
60. "'All the better to eat you with!'"
61. "The wolf pounced upon Little Red Riding Hood and ate her up."
62. "Now you can understand, little children --\nno, and especially you young girls."
63. "Pretty, well-mannered, kind-hearted girls\nwho lend their ears to just anyone -- they make a great mistake,\nand it is no wonder at all that in the end they are eaten by a wolf."
64. "For though we say 'wolf' with a single word,\nnot all wolves are alike."
65. "There are those who slip in cleverly,\nquiet, without a barb, never quick to anger,\nfriendly and pleasant and smooth of tongue,"
66. "who follow young ladies\ninto their very homes, right up to their bedsides."
67. "Ah, this indeed is a grave matter. Who does not know\nthat these gentle-seeming wolves\nare the most dangerous of all!"
68. "Little children --\nno, and especially you young girls --\ntake very great care."
69. Charles Perrault's collection of fairy tales may be called one of the oldest of all such collections dealing in folklore.
70. Its composition of course predates the Grimm tales.
71. In Perrault's Little Red Riding Hood, there is no huntsman coming to save the girl and her grandmother as there is in the Grimms' version.
72. In its place, the story is capped with a moral.
73. Its allegory is very easy to read.\nA wolf who invites you into bed is not found in any picture book.
74. The lesson this story would teach is not so difficult.
75. But then -- what if?
76. What if that girl were not Little Red Riding Hood at all?
77. What if, for instance, in place of a red hood she were a girl carrying a white parasol?
78. The girl with the white parasol was, like Little Red Riding Hood, adored by many people.
79. And so, most likely, the wolf would try to eat this white-parasoled girl just as he did Little Red Riding Hood.
80. But the white parasol is dangerous.
81. When something beautiful comes with a white parasol, it is best to be wary.
82. There are poisonous mushrooms so deadly they are called the Three Deaths.
83. Three poison mushrooms of world renown.
84. One is the Destroying Angel -- Amanita virosa.
85. In Europe and America it is called the "Destroying Angel."\nIn this country, too, it boasts the highest fatality rate of any.
86. As its likeness to an angel suggests, cap and stem alike are pure white all over.
87. Then there is the Fool's Mushroom -- Amanita verna.
88. As its name, crowned with "white," suggests, its form is white and beautiful.\nBut a single one, eaten, brings on death by its ferocious poison.
89. This one too -- cap and stem -- is pure white.
90. Last is the Death Cap -- Amanita phalloides.
91. Its cap a somewhat olive-tinged white.\nAnd its flesh pure white.
92. It grows wild throughout Europe, and is called "death cap."
93. These three mushrooms, the most poisonous of all by reputation, are all three of them white in form.
94. They wear white clothing, and they carry white parasols.
95. So what if Little Red Riding Hood wore no red hood at all -- what if she were a white girl, carrying a white parasol?
96. What would become of the wolf who, having eaten nothing for three days, at long last managed to devour the two of them?
97. The girl with the white parasol was called neither "Destroying Angel" nor "Death Cap," but like the other poison mushrooms, she too bore a poison peculiar to herself.
98. The girl lived on a diet of "UFT Sunfural S."\nBy taking poison as her staple food, she stored the poison up within her body.
99. Just as the pufferfish, feeding on poisons, stores tetrodotoxin within itself, so the girl stored up the poison of "UFT Sunfural S."
100. The symptoms of one who ate her would closely resemble those of tetrodotoxin.
101. A poison mushroom with a white parasol and a white stem.\nThe poison-mushroom girl beckons people in with a smile.
102. However capable a wolf may be, he will not notice the difference between her and Little Red Riding Hood.
103. "Hey, did you know...? Yumi."
104. "I was carrying poison too..."
105. It was in childhood that I saw the wolf.
106. She led a great pack, and I thought her beautiful, and I found her hateful too.
107. No matter how strong the sunlight, she was out there under the sun with her pack, hunting down every kind of prey.
108. She was just like the running wolf of the story -- always swift, swifter than anyone, seizing her prey.
109. One time, I heard that an older girl had had her hair cut to pieces.
110. Another time, I heard that an older girl had had her clothes torn off and been stripped naked.
111. I heard these stories from within the great flock of girls.
112. Many of them listened in shivering fear.
113. But the girl with the white parasol was different.
114. She only found the wolf hateful.
115. The girl could not run, nor could she hunt anyone.
116. Not only that -- she was not even permitted to stay outside for long.
117. So she found the girl who freely led her wolves beneath the sun so enviable that she came to hate her.
118. The poison-mushroom girl thought: let this wolf girl eat me.
119. Then the poison-mushroom girl's poison would course through her body and bring her to her death.
120. The wolf would then no longer be able to run the earth, nor to hunt any other.
121. So the poison-mushroom girl thought.
122. A certain day, in the classroom.
123. When her eyes met the wolf girl's, the poison-mushroom girl smiled -- pointedly, artfully.
124. She smiled at her with a kind, ever so beautiful smile.
125. The wolf girl sensed danger in that smile.
126. And so she kicked her desk aside and stormed out.
127. Her intuition was correct.
128. A wolf's instinct must have told her that this smile was dangerous.
129. But fate is cruel.
130. She stepped, of her own accord, into the park where the poison mushroom grew.
131. The park of ZYPRESSEN.
132. Van Gogh called them black obelisks.
133. Symbols of death.
134. And into such a place the wolf girl came, blithely, unwarily.
135. Seeing her up close, the poison-mushroom girl found the vital gleam of her skin hateful.
136. She wanted to dye her the same color as herself -- the color of death.
137. So the poison-mushroom girl called out to her, doing all she could to arouse the wolf girl's interest.
138. It's all right. The poison-mushroom girl cannot move by herself, but instead she reads the air and dyes herself in its color.
139. White is the color most easily dyed.
140. She was skilled at behaving in ways that pleased others.
141. And so the wolf girl easily fell in love with the poison-mushroom girl.
142. Seduced by the white poison-mushroom girl...
143. "I only found her hateful..."
144. "I was only envious..."

