46. Kawachino was drawing something too.
47. "Kawachino, what are you drawing?"
48. "It's a secret."
49. Once I'd drawn my wolf, Hikawa drew a little person on top of it.
50. Or so I thought, but --
51. "What's this? A cordyceps?"
52. "Cordyceps?"
53. "You don't know? It's a mushroom that grows out of the corpses of living creatures. In Chinese medicine, it's a top-tier luxury item."
54. "Huh, so there are mushrooms like that......"
55. "Well, the shape is different from that."
56. On top of the wolf I'd drawn, Hikawa had drawn a mushroom that looked like a tiny person.
57. It even looked like a girl holding up an umbrella.
58. It seemed Kawachino had finished her work too.
59. "What's this drawing? A matagi hunter?"
60. "Something like that. This is a hunter."
61. "A hunter, huh...... With a wolf and a hunter, you might as well throw in Little Red Riding Hood too."
62. "It's fine, though."
63. "Little Red Riding Hood isn't here."
64. "Because this mushroom takes her place."
65. "A mushroom instead of Red Riding Hood?"
66. Honestly, I didn't really understand what Hikawa was getting at.
67. Still, Kawachino seemed to understand.
68. "Yumi, so you drew a hunter."
69. "Why is that?"
70. "Because the hunter is strong......"
71. The three of us kept on drawing after that.
72. Hikawa had me draw all sorts of creatures, and she'd sprout mushrooms on every one of them.
73. The gathering of creatures with mushrooms growing from their backs felt strangely fantastical.
74. "We really had fun."
75. "Though it was pretty different from a date."
76. "Well, all's well that ends well, right?"
77. "What's wrong, Yumi?"
78. "Ah, no, it's nothing......"
79. "But a date for three...... I wonder if we'll ever get a chance like this again......"
80. "Who knows?"
81. "The memories from back then are still my favorites."
82. "In this park, with Yumi, and then Kusanagi-san shows up."
83. "Such a strange relationship."
84. "But back then, I really felt alive."
85. "Felt...... alive, huh."
86. "It's a strange way to put it.\nStill, that's how it felt."
87. Hikawa was looking up at the darkening sky.
88. The color of the setting sun is a wondrous thing.
89. Like glass with an unbelievable transparency -- thin, thinner than anything, a gradient of blue and red.
90. I know of no such transparency anywhere else in the world.
91. "The first star!"
92. "The evening star."
93. "When you find it in the morning it's the morning star, but by any name, it's the same star."
94. "Venus."
95. "The most brilliant star -- \nin the whole sky, there's no star that shines brighter than it......"
96. "No. There was a star that shone as brightly as Venus."
97. "What was it?"
98. "A star that appeared in Cassiopeia and shone ten times brighter than Sirius......\nIf I said Tycho's Star, would you know it?"
99. "Ah, I've heard of it -- I think it was said to be the model for the Nighthawk Star."
100. "Properly speaking, Tycho's Star wasn't a star's light, though."
101. "It was a supernova explosion.\nThe brightness of a star's final flame as it burned."
102. "So we can no longer see it......"
103. "Tycho's Star...... there was a star like that? How long ago?"
104. "It was discovered in medieval Europe, so quite a long time ago."
105. "And it only shone for two years."
106. "But for those two years, it shone in the sky with a light that not even Venus could rival......"
107. "It shone as if burning up."
108. "I can kind of understand why Miyazawa Kenji layered the image of it onto the Nighthawk Star."
109. "A fantasy of self-immolation...... I see."
110. "Miyazawa Kenji's self-immolation fantasy -- the northernmost extreme of the spirit of service......"
111. "A shining, sinful, servant star, huh......"
112. "Back then......\nWhen Rina told me the story of the nighthawk,\nif only I could have talked about things like this......"
113. "No, even now it feels really fresh. I didn't know any of it."
