115. "Unlike Rina, I'm dumb, so I couldn't talk about literature -- I thought if I could at least talk about the stars......"
116. "So that's why you joined the astronomy club too......"
117. "But I really do love the stars."
118. "Because the sky I looked up at with Rina in this park was beautiful......"
119. "Stars are wonderful. Down on earth, everything is so hectic and chaotic, and yet they turn beautifully, in perfect order."
120. "Back then I didn't really understand...... but now I can a little, how the nighthawk and the scorpion felt."
121. "The Nighthawk Star, huh...... it's too beautiful for me, it has nothing to do with me, and yet precisely because it doesn't, I'm drawn to that story now......"
122. "Ugliness, shame, guilt -- all of them turned the nighthawk into a star that burned beautifully in the night sky..."
123. "It's a story too beautiful for me."
124. "So there you have it, Rina.\nI've become able to talk about the sky this much."
125. "Yeah, it was fun, and it was such a beautiful story."
126. "If you talk about beautiful things, it becomes a beautiful story."
127. "Tycho's Star is beautiful in its very existence......"
128. "Beautiful things really are wonderful......"
129. "I love beautiful things."
130. "Since we mostly don't understand what's inside, I'm drawn to things that are at least beautiful on the outside."
131. "That's why the Nighthawk Star -- where an ugly inner self becomes beautiful through self-immolation -- is very much to my taste......"
132. "Well then, shall we head home? It's getting late."
133. We left the cypress park behind.
134. The park where we first met.
135. The flower language of the cypress is death, mourning, despair.
136. But paradoxically, it's also a symbol of immortality and rebirth.
137. "Well, I'll head off here."
138. "That's right, it's closer to your house if you go down the side of the shrine, Rina."

