69. It was paradoxical, and yet it was right.
70. Because along with death, the cypress also carries the meanings of rebirth, immortality, eternal life.
71. The cypress does not lose its leaves even in winter.
72. In every season it holds its deep, living green.
73. It is closer to the image of "immortality" than to the image of "death."
74. The legend that the cross on which Jesus Christ was crucified was made of cypress ties directly into Christ's immortality.
75. Rina completed a cypress that had swallowed whole the cherry blossom of life which Kusanagi Naoya had drawn.
76. When she finished, Rina said only one thing.
77. "Even if Kusanagi-san's right hand can no longer paint, I, his apprentice, will carry on his work."
78. Nagayama Kana had been dumbstruck all this time, but when she came back to herself, she let out a small laugh.
79. "It's true -- your brushwork calls Kusanagi Naoya's to mind..."
80. "But your picture carries more shadow than his ever did."
81. "It makes you feel death."
82. "And yet... it makes you feel life too."
83. "Tch... damn, that's amazing..."
84. Nagayama Kana let out a breath, then a sigh, and murmured,
85. "Ahh, what a pain."
86. "Sure, Kusanagi Naoya has turned into junk, but..."
87. "It seems an artist just as troublesome as Kusanagi Naoya -- maybe even more troublesome -- has been born."
88. "So in the end, it comes down to talent, huh..."
89. "I hate it, I hate it. Anyone with more talent than me can just drop dead."
90. For some reason, Nagayama Kana laughed.
91. "Well, whatever."
92. "I am Nagayama Kana. I'm the woman who's going to become a greater artist than you."
93. "From now on, as an artist, I'll crush you."
94. "As an artist?"
95. "What's your name?"
96. "Hikawa Rina."
97. "Hikawa Rina... with skill like this, and I've never seen you at any of the Open Exhibitions..."
98. "That sort of thing doesn't really interest me..."
99. "Doesn't interest you, huh...\nBut I know."
100. "You lot, the Yumihari art club, you're aiming for the Moore Exhibition, right?\nAnd of course Hikawa Rina too."
101. "But after Kusanagi Ken'ichirou's death, it's I who will lead the art world."
102. "So then -- I'll be looking forward to the Moore Exhibition.\nCan't wait to see what kind of work you bring!"
103. "See ya!"
104. And with that, Nagayama Kana turned on her heel.
105. "Ahaha... she seems to have left with the wrong idea, didn't she?"
106. "All I did was give shape to what Kusanagi-san was trying to draw..."
107. "I don't even intend to enter the Moore Exhibition in the first place."
108. "But you said you'd carry on my work, didn't you?"
109. "That was because my master was being mocked -- as his apprentice, that's the least I could say."
110. "Still, you drew something incredible..."
111. "If we leave graffiti like this on the wall, we'll get in trouble. Let's wipe it off properly."
112. "Wipe it off properly, huh...\nIs this really the kind of thing we should be erasing...?"
113. Kusanagi looked up at the picture Rina had drawn.
114. "Heh heh heh, even setting Nagayama aside, if someone this talented suddenly appeared out of nowhere, you'd hate it too."
115. "If you can take the composition I meant to draw and turn it into a picture that leaves the other side terrified, aren't you already a full-fledged artist?"
116. "I couldn't have done it alone.\nI could only paint it because your rough sketch was there, Kusanagi-san."
117. "Rough sketch? It was just a cherry blossom..."
118. "But, but, the essence is what matters."
119. "The essence, huh..."
120. "Then, what does that make us?"
121. "Together, the two of us make one full-fledged artist, is that it..."
122. Probably, after that, Kusanagi held Rina in his arms.
123. Without watching to the end, I left that place.
124. Out of character for me, I've come to the sea.
125. It wasn't as if I had a purpose.
126. I just, somehow, wanted to look at the sea.
127. Nothing in particular to do, just music playing at random through my earphones.
128. Anime songs, electronica, game music, shoegaze, idol pop. Truly haphazard selections.
129. "Huh? What are you doing in a place like this?"
130. A pickup, huh... annoying.
131. If a woman were the one calling out to me it wouldn't bother me in the slightest, but,

