44. [Mizuna] "It's all right. No matter how my body is defiled, I won't lose sight of who I am.\nKen'ichirou-san taught me that."
45. The old woman Natsume pressed her fingers to the bridge of her nose.
46. [Kotoko] "Hey! Ken'ichirou! What about you?"
47. [Kotoko] "You get it, don't you! What is it that you get?!"
48. [Ken'ichirou] "That this is as far as the old lady can go, right?"
49. She tapped her own shoulder a few times, thump, thump.\nJust looking at her posture, she was like an old person relaxing on the veranda.
50. [Kotoko] "Fuu, honestly, the human heart is not an easy thing."
51. [Kotoko] "It always drags the outcome off in some crazy direction."
52. [Kotoko] "Well, I suppose that's human nature too......"
53. [Kotoko] "Is this all right? Mizuna? This is your last chance to choose."
54. [Mizuna] "Thank you for everything until now. I'll be all right from here on."
55. [Kotoko] "I see. If that's your will, then it can't be helped."
56. [Kotoko] "In that case -- Ai and the mansion, as payment for the one billion yen!"
57. [Nakamura Shouichi] "Ah, that's fine!"
58. The hospital room had grown completely dark.
59. It was long past visiting hours for anyone but family.
60. But Wakata was still sitting before Ken'ichirou.
61. [Wakata] "Mizuna-san wanted to protect everyone, didn't she."
62. [Ken'ichirou] "Yeah, she must have really thought they were about to start killing each other there."
63. [Wakata] "There's no way she could understand high-level yakuza negotiation tactics."
64. [Ken'ichirou] "Still, in her own way, the old lady seemed satisfied with how it turned out."
65. [Wakata] "Satisfied?"
66. [Ken'ichirou] "I think she fell for Mizuna all over again."
67. [Ken'ichirou] "The old lady's old-school in her way -- she puts great weight on giri and duty."
68. [Wakata] "Even so, as a negotiation, it was a failure, wasn't it?"
69. [Ken'ichirou] "No, that turned out fine."
70. [Ken'ichirou] "A woman had steeled her resolve that far.\nThe old lady bore witness to it, and that's why she turned to me."
71. [Ken'ichirou] "'The rest is for a man to answer -- can you do it?' That's what she was asking."
72. [Ken'ichirou] "After that, I took Mizuna and eloped."
73. [Ken'ichirou] "From your side, that's how it must look......"
74. [Ken'ichirou] "But that was less an elopement and more an abduction.\nMizuna didn't want to go."
75. [Ken'ichirou] "She was terribly worried that if she wasn't there, Ai would be treated horribly."
76. [Wakata] "That'd be all right, though. After that, that mansion, the Kootowa mansion, became the Natsume mansion."
77. [Ken'ichirou] "Yeah, she probably never imagined the old lady would go that far for us."
78. [Ken'ichirou] "The old lady said she'd protect Ai and the mansion herself.\nSo the two of you -- run, and keep on running. That was her line."
79. [Ken'ichirou] "Well, in fact the house was more than big enough, the young guys could come and go, and Ai could be raised properly."
80. [Wakata] "Ai-san seems to have been very attached to Grandma Natsume."
81. [Ken'ichirou] "Yeah, that's probably why Ai still loves the Natsume family to this day......"
82. [Wakata] "But still -- it took nerve to think about running. Even if not on the Natsume family's level, the Nakamura house was pretty hardcore back then too, right?"
83. [Ken'ichirou] "Other way around, other way. If I hadn't spirited Mizuna off then, I'd probably have had my finger cut off by Natsume......"
84. [Wakata] "By finger, you mean...... no, come on, that couldn't happen. Ken'ichirou-san, technically you're Grandma Natsume's grandson, right?"
85. [Ken'ichirou] "That kind of thing doesn't fly with that old lady. Her principle is that if a woman shows that kind of guts, the man has to answer for it."
86. [Ken'ichirou] "I ran off with Mizuna. But that in its way was fun.\nWe went to a lot of places Mizuna had never been. Even America."
87. [Wakata] "Sounds carefree."
88. [Ken'ichirou] "Carefree, hell. Once I made off with Mizuna, they treated it as a loan. In short, a debt."
89. [Ken'ichirou] "Well, with the old lady Natsume keeping an eye on things, it wasn't some absurd toichi-rate (ten percent every ten days), but even so, it was interest right at the legal limit."
90. [Wakata] "But because it became a debt, Mizuna-san was able to return to the mansion, right? The Natsume mansion."
91. [Wakata] "I see -- that connects to what you were saying earlier."
92. [Ken'ichirou] "Earlier?"
93. [Wakata] "What you mentioned before you started reminiscing. The girl you brought back from New York this time."
94. [Ken'ichirou] "Ah, right. Well, more or less."
95. [Ken'ichirou] "Roughly the same as Mizuna. Only, she's carrying a somewhat more troublesome problem than Mizuna did."
96. [Wakata] "Whew. And you pushed something like that onto Naoya?"
97. [Ken'ichirou] "Yeah. He's my son, and he's Natsume Kotoko's great-grandson."
98. [Wakata] "You trust him, then.\nEven though you left him alone all this time."
99. [Ken'ichirou] "That's not it. He was the one who ran off from New York.\nAnd I was going to all this trouble to give him an elite education in painting......"
100. [Ken'ichirou] "Naoya, huh......"
101. [Ken'ichirou] "That kid takes after me, but he takes after Mizuna too......"
102. [Ken'ichirou] "I still remember the day he was born."
103. [Ken'ichirou] "That I could have a child of my own. It's a strange thing."
104. [Ken'ichirou] "A human being who hadn't existed in the world until then."
105. [Ken'ichirou] "Comes into this world as the child of me and Mizuna."
106. [Ken'ichirou] "It's a perfectly ordinary thing. For a living creature it must be ordinary......\nBut when it happens to you, it's mysterious beyond words."
107. [Ken'ichirou] "The most utterly ordinary thing, and yet a strangeness like a miracle."
108. [Ken'ichirou] "To be granted a child is truly mysterious."
109. [Ken'ichirou] "Not only that, but this creature grows steadily, and before you know it he's past my eye level and walking on ahead."
110. [Ken'ichirou] "Even if I stop here, he'll keep walking on ahead from here......"
111. [Ken'ichirou] "It's a strange feeling......"
112. [Ken'ichirou] "The mystery of blood."
113. [Ken'ichirou] "The mystery called family......"
114. [Ken'ichirou] "Come to think of it, Wakata, aren't you going to get married?"
115. [Wakata] "There's no one for me to marry."
116. [Ken'ichirou] "Marriage is a fine thing. If it's with someone you truly love, there's nothing better."
117. [Wakata] "That's sure to be true."
118. [Ken'ichirou] "That said, I was posted away alone."
119. [Ken'ichirou] "To pay back the money, I went off to New York."
120. [Ken'ichirou] "Friedman, whom I met in America, said we should make money with painting. Well, gradually my name got out there, but I was scraping by just paying the interest."
121. [Wakata] "Winning the Platine Epaulard came quite a while after that, didn't it......"
122. [Ken'ichirou] "Yeah, that was after Mizuna died."
123. [Ken'ichirou] "It was Mizuna's death that made me complete 'The Reclining Sakura.'"
124. [Ken'ichirou] "It's ironic. I'd spent all that time thinking only of making money to buy back Mizuna's freedom, and yet while she was alive I couldn't come up with the big money."
125. [Ken'ichirou] "After she died -- precisely because she died -- I completed that work, and at the world's foremost exhibition, the Moore Exhibition, I won the top prize, the Platine Epaulard."
126. [Wakata] "Why, when Mizuna-san passed, did you paint cherry blossoms?"
127. [Ken'ichirou] "It was cherry blossom season when I met her......\nNo, it isn't as simple a story as that."
128. [Ken'ichirou] "Illusory spring-hued sky across the whole vista\nBlind causal alternating-current lamps\nBrightly, brightly, brightly they light."
129. [Ken'ichirou] "Watermelon tourmaline\nThe crossing point of sound and language\nIn a world ever more saturated with color."
130. [Ken'ichirou] "There is no 'I' before the landscape\nThere is no landscape before 'I'."
131. [Ken'ichirou] "In a soft, downy cherry forest the world rang out\nWith a beautiful tone the world rang out."
132. [Ken'ichirou] "If that is emptiness, then emptiness itself is thus\nAnd to a certain extent it is common to us all."
133. [Ken'ichirou] "(For all things are the everyone within me\nAnd everything within each of everyone)"
134. [Wakata] "That's the poem written on the back of 'The Reclining Sakura's' canvas, isn't it."
135. [Wakata] "What does it mean?"
136. [Ken'ichirou] "It means exactly what it says.\nI wrote it that way to convey exactly that."
137. [Wakata] "Exactly what it says...... it's a rather difficult poem......"
138. [Ken'ichirou] "Difficult, huh...... is that so.\nNo, I suppose it might be......"
139. [Ken'ichirou] "Mind and body......\nMizuna suffered over that, right up to the end."
140. [Ken'ichirou] "It's easy to tell yourself in words to be kind to yourself, but actually doing it is enormously hard."
141. [Ken'ichirou] "Mind and body -- they're really just a difference in perspective......\nThe two are exactly the same thing......"
142. [Ken'ichirou] "And yet, people feel a mind there."

=== lines=49 stop_reason=end_turn in=3672 out=1222 cache_read=3222 cache_create=0 last_entry_continued=0 ===
1. "It's precisely because people feel it that the mind exists."
2. "And the mind, at times, torments people, and even eats away at their bodies......"
3. "But it's because people have a mind that they can also feel happiness."
4. "In my dream, an angel with white wings came to me, smiling."
5. "At her side stood an old man holding an enormous hourglass."
6. "'It's no use asking me,'\nthe angel said to me."
7. "'I know what you are thinking.'"
8. "'Before long, ask this old man to guide you into the infinite.'"
9. "'Then you will know what God intends to do with you, and you will understand the meaning of why you as you are now are so especially incomplete.'"
10. "'If the Creator's work were only for a single day, what would it be like?'"
11. "'God would never rest......'"
12. "What is this you're talking about?"
13. "It's the tale of a dream that a French savage, who crossed south a hundred years ago, saw before he died."
14. "A French savage?"
15. "No, it's nothing......"
16. "People have a mind, and so they feel happiness......"
17. "Ah, that's true. But happiness is a hard thing to catch, isn't it."
18. "Happiness is hard to catch?"
19. "Yeah, that's right. Just like this liquor......"
20. I pulled a bottle of whiskey out from between the futons.
21. "Y-You really had liquor ready?"
22. "Yeah, my treasured bottle. I bought it back when I was in America."
23. "But this is Japanese whiskey, isn't it."
24. "Yeah, that's right. Japanese liquor that was being sold on the far side of the earth -- that's why I bought it."
25. "At the time I told myself -- it was expensive, after all.\nI'll open this one at the finest moment, I said."
26. "But you never did open it."
27. "Of course not. The finest moment is something you always only recognize after the fact."
28. "The finest moment...... you only know it after...... is that so."
29. "That's how it is. The finest moment is a hard thing to catch."
30. "Looking back on it now, there were plenty of moments that felt like the finest."
31. "I've been a blessed man, after all......"
32. "Blessed, you say. Well, an ordinary person, looking at your life, Ken'ichirou-san, would find it far too turbulent to say something like that, I suppose."
33. "Maybe so."
34. "But you know. There was one. The finest moment of my life."
35. "So that's why -- now is the time to open it."
36. "Now?"
37. "Will you join me?"
38. Wakata gave a small nod.
39. Happiness is like that illusory spring-hued sky spread across the whole vista.
40. As if it had no substance at all -- at some slight moment, it flickers glittering into view, like clusters of color-saturated electric lamps.
41. The cherry blossoms I saw that day, and the one girl.
42. Did that scene go on past me, off to somewhere else?
43. Or did I leave it behind somewhere and forget it?
44. Seven sorrows, and seven happinesses.
45. Between them, we sway back and forth.
46. Just like a pendulum.
47. "Laugh, and drink a toast with me."
48. "To my life up to this point."
49. "And to the moments of happiness yet to come."

