=== lines=145 stop_reason=end_turn in=7879 out=4825 cache_read=3222 cache_create=0 last_entry_continued=0 ===
1. "Well, if you were saying you wanted to go to art school, I'd stop you, but as a hobby, no problem. You've got a good hand."
2. "So it really is difficult, isn't it, to advance down the path of art."
3. "Well, the universities vary, but if we're talking about making a living off of it..."
4. "Training from an early age matters, of course, but in the end, talent is what speaks. The skilled are skilled, and the unskilled are unskilled."
5. "The hardest of all, Tokyo University of the Arts, has a ratio over twenty to one. Oil painting only takes about fifty a year.\nSome departments take fewer than twenty."
6. "One, two, three years of ronin study is a given."
7. "That's what they say, but at the end of the day, the ones with talent get in.\nOr rather, a place that admits fewer than fifty a year can hardly be called an entrance exam anymore. It's practically a competition."
8. "Every single one of them was praised as a great artist from childhood -- the sort who has confidence in their painting, if nothing else."
9. "People like that grind sleeplessly at exam-prep painting at art cram schools, and every year thousands of them from all over the country gather to paint. Out of that, only a few dozen get in."
10. "No matter how hard the University of Tokyo is said to be, it doesn't have such a ridiculous quota, right?"
11. "Yeah, maybe. Even the quota for Rika III is more than double that."
12. I had no clear idea what Rika III meant, but\nso there are places with fewer than a hundred, huh...
13. "I wonder if you could do it, Nao-kun?"
14. "Impossible.\nOr rather, I wouldn't say that, but you can't tell without trying.\nThere are plenty of incredible people in the world. It's not something to be tackled halfway."
15. "You've entered a lot of open calls, right, Nao-kun? So you must remember the names of the amazing ones."
16. "Names, huh..."
17. Only one name came to me.\nHim, of course.
18. As for the rest, nothing in particular came to mind...
19. From childhood I'd been steeped in what you might call the true heart of the art world.
20. Precisely because of that, Japan's open calls felt to me somehow local.
21. In fact, my father had been trying to send me to a university overseas.\nHis own connections were overwhelmingly stronger abroad, and he had a rather low opinion of the Japanese art establishment.
22. In this country, no matter how tough an art university might be, the road from there to the world simply doesn't open up.
23. When it comes to art, this country is a frighteningly local place.
24. The low rate of Japanese laureates at the Moore Exhibition backs this up.
25. Before Kusanagi Ken'ichirou there were absolutely none, and since then only a handful, count them on one hand -- at the Moore Exhibition. Meanwhile, foreign artists take home laurels every year in droves, as you'd expect.
26. And the grand prize itself is awarded, without fail, every year.
27. "Well, I can't really think of any... True, I remember a few who had a lot of technical drawing skill, but..."
28. "Would Kei-kun be able to pass?"
29. "Kei, hm... His paintings just aren't really suited to art school in the first place."
30. "Really? But he's so skilled."
31. "His drawing ability is genius-level, but as for his personality... He's just too willful..."
32. "For anywhere else, no way, but at a peculiar school like Geidai, if a professor takes a liking to you, maybe you can get in?"
33. "But if the gate is that narrow, then that would make them elites, wouldn't it?"
34. "It's not so much elites as -- how do I put it, Geidai is...\nWell, apparently in the design departments they get snapped up on the job market, but in the fine arts departments..."
35. "Fine arts?"
36. "Yeah, fine art. Pure art. Painting, sculpture, that sort of thing."
37. "So pure artists take flight into the world, is the idea?"
38. "Well, I suppose that's the intent behind founding the university."
39. "As for the results, though..."
40. "Either way, it's got nothing to do with us."
41. "You're not going to sit for the art university exams, Nao-kun?"
42. "If I were going to sit for the art school exams, I wouldn't be spending my time this way."
43. "I see. So you shouldn't be teaching painting to someone like me, then."
44. "Right, enough of this silly chatter. It's about time I got home and studied."
45. "Yes, you're right. Thank you for everything up to today."
46. "Nah, I just taught, so it's easy. If it were for art school entrance exams, I would have had to teach you like my life depended on it."
47. "Compared to that, Rin, who's teaching me and steadily working to raise my deviation score, has a much harder job."
48. "It seems like your grades have been going up."
49. "Yeah, well, I'm studying as hard as I can. At this rate, I might just break out big."
50. "Maybe even the same university as me!"
51. "Now that's... not gonna happen..."
52. You, girl, are ranked in the top hundred of the national practice exams...
53. Where's the element of you and me going to the same university?
54. Lately I'd started actively taking practice exams and such, so I'd come to understand just how outrageous a scholar Misakura Rin was.
55. Even at the top prep school in the prefecture, you wouldn't easily find someone with her results.
56. "Anyway, the Moore Exhibition is coming up soon, huh..."
57. "Yes. The seasons have shifted completely -- it's turned wintry."
58. "I wonder what everyone's painting..."
59. "It seems Rina-chan isn't submitting anything."
60. "Is that so. Saying that, is Makoto going to allow it?"
61. "Yumi-chan is submitting, apparently."
62. "Well, just submitting, anyone can do that. Her painting will probably come back to her in no time, though."
63. "The Moore Exhibition apparently gathers an insane number of works, so the first round of judging finishes at a pretty ferocious pace."
64. "If you make it to something like the sixth round, apparently it takes quite a while for the work to come back to you..."
65. "The sixth round!? Th-there are that many?"
66. "Nope. Including the final round, it's eight. And beyond that, there's a nomination review for each of the prize ceremonies. Well, I don't think this country has any artists left standing at that level of judging."
67. "Not even at the national level..."
68. "Of course. It's the illustrious Moore Open Exhibition. Its worldwide prestige is not for show."
69. The Moore Open Exhibition has eight rounds of judging, from the first to the eighth.
70. The reason there are so many rounds -- more than one would normally imagine -- is because various foundations and corporations are involved.
71. Because there are so many rounds, the top prizes decided in the eighth round -- the "Platine Epaulard," the "Or Baleine," and the "Argent Dauphin" -- are announced after the new year.
72. Because of that, the award ceremonies are held on multiple occasions.
73. The names of the prizes vary by genre.\nIn particular, if we limit ourselves to the painting division, the prizes bear the names of animals of the Atlantic, the sea between Europe and America.
74. Elan Loutre\nThree slots worldwide.\nA Japanese artist took one of the three three years ago.
75. Elan Phoque\nTwo slots worldwide.\nNo Japanese winners.
76. Elan Otarie\nOne slot worldwide.\nA Japanese artist won it six years ago.
77. Argent Dauphin\nOne slot worldwide.\nA Japanese artist won it five years ago.
78. Or Baleine\nOne slot worldwide.\nNo Japanese winners.
79. Platine Epaulard\nOne slot worldwide.\nA Japanese artist won it six years ago.
80. The recipient of that top prize, the "Platine Epaulard," is Kusanagi Ken'ichirou.
81. Each prize is said to reflect differences in the backing universities, foundations, and corporations, and one cannot flatly say which is the top prize.
82. In particular, the "Platine Epaulard," the "Or Baleine," and the "Argent Dauphin" are regarded as roughly equivalent.
83. The differences between these prizes are said to be along the New York, London, and Paris lines.
84. Originally, the "Argent Dauphin," which falls under the London line, was said to be the especially strong prize.
85. The reason, of course, is the presence of the Royal College of Art -- the most influential in the world -- and the six national colleges of the University of the Arts London.
86. But from the 1990s onward, the New York-line "Platine Epaulard" grew in presence.
87. This strongly reflects its meaning as the center of the economy (as an aside, the reason London, rather than Paris, is strong is also said to be influenced by the presence of a world-class stock market).
88. It's because the Moore Foundation successfully constructed the concept of art as an investment.
89. America was already a country where the concept of art as investment had taken root, but with the involvement of major investment bank groups, its shape was greatly transformed.
90. Fused with financial engineering, this only accelerated further.
91. The Moore Exhibition may be a global open call, but it is not something that moves purely by artistic values.
92. Put the other way, precisely because it presupposes political and economic effects, it functions in this modern age as a kind of guarantor of "art's" presence.
93. Ken'ichirou's win was certainly a Japanese triumph,\nbut it was not a win that came out of nowhere.
94. In fact, it's said to have been a strategy to pull Tokyo -- a global city that as a city boasts the world's number-one GDP and population, and reigns as one of the world's three great cities -- into the art market.
95. But the fact that the mainstream of Japan's art establishment failed to ride that wave led, later, to the feat in Shanghai, China, of consecutive wins of the "Elan Phoque," the "Elan Otarie," and the "Platine Epaulard."
96. Some point to the lack of notable winners since as proof of the global isolation of Japan's art establishment.
97. These days, the world of art is nothing more than a single cog in the machinery of the economy.
98. That's why all kinds of calculations intersect there.
99. The Far Eastern economic power that fell most behind in those calculations... that is the general perception in the art world.
100. "Come to think of it, the Moore Open Exhibition only holds three ceremonies domestically -- the Elan Loutre, Elan Phoque, and Elan Otarie -- doesn't it?"
101. "Yeah, and they only hold them in years when this country had a winner. When it happened once three years ago, it was on TV, apparently."
102. "As for the remaining three prizes, the ceremonies are held in New York, London, and Paris respectively, right?"
103. "Well, of course. Each nation has its prestige to consider..."
104. "It's amazing. Every time I hear about it, I think, 'It really is a world-class art festival.'"
105. "Because it's the most prestigious art prize in the world."
106. "So Mako-chan is aiming for such a prestigious art prize."
107. Well, it's dead certain to be impossible, though...
108. "Come to think of it, Mako-chan is challenging with ceramics, apparently."
109. "Pottery, huh... Is she thinking of going to Geidai's crafts department or something?"
110. "No. When I asked, she said not an art school."
111. "Ho, so someone who isn't even aiming for art school has been fooling around with art at this critical time... Heh heh heh, that's one more academic power out of the running."
