100. The painter Gauguin has a famous work.
101. It's said to have been intended as his final work before suicide.
102. Gauguin's religious outlook was profoundly pantheistic--Buddhist, even.
103. He wrote a piece of intense Christian critique called "The Catholic Church and Modernity," in which he declared the Christian God to be "something that must be killed, so that it can never be born again."
104. And yet, on the other hand, he placed great importance on God as mystery.
105. Among his writings, there is a passage like this.
106. "The unfathomable mystery has always been, is now, and will forever remain, unfathomable."
107. "God does not belong to the scholars or the logicians, but to the poets, to dreams--he is the symbol of beauty, of beauty itself."
