119. He tried to find paradise in the wild.
120. As if to escape the framework of Western civilization, he headed south, ever south, seeking there the root of his art.
121. But at the end of that road there was nothing but the death at the far end of despair.
122. Even so, that despair and that death are bewitching, and sweet.
123. That is precisely why Gauguin's paintings are still loved today.
124. He indeed failed to find in the tropics the paradise he sought.
125. But it was precisely because he never found it, precisely because he never stopped seeking it, that he was able to produce such a body of work.
126. Gauguin sought in painting the musical melody born of color.
127. And the place this Gauguin finally arrived at was the small southern island of Hiva Oa.
128. He spent about two years in that place.
129. He died in that place.
130. But in his late works, reality and dream, meditation and memory, death and life crossed all the more.
131. Just before he died, he wrote a short piece titled "The Empire of Death."
132. In it: an angel and an old man holding an hourglass, infinity and God.\nAnd how he himself, an incomplete work, had his life fulfilled by infinity and by God.
133. After his death, Gauguin's paintings were sold off for a pittance.\nAnd yet, up to the very moment of his death, he was convinced of his own rightness.
