104. [Naoya] "Do you two know whether you've won?"
105. [Sakamoto] "More or less, yeah."
106. [Sakamoto] "Word is, Murata's the one taking it this time."
107. [Murata] "Don't blab about it before the announcement."
108. [Naoya] "Really? Which prize did you win?"
109. [Murata] "The story is that Elan Faux is the likely one. There's no Japanese recipient of that one yet, so if I take it, it should draw a fair amount of attention."
110. [Naoya] "That's true. It wouldn't be strange at all for someone like you, Murata-san, to win Elan Faux."
111. [Murata] "You think? Hearing it put that way makes it feel iffy."
112. [Murata] "I actually thought about declining Elan Faux."
113. [Naoya] "Declining?"
114. [Murata] "Well, there's the fact that I'd want a prize above that."
115. [Sakamoto] "The London-affiliated Argent Dauphin, and the New York-affiliated Platine Epaulard. Each of those has had one Japanese recipient."
116. [Sakamoto] "But the Paris-affiliated Or Baleine has never gone to anyone from Japan. So naturally there's a longing to be the first for that one."
117. [Murata] "The Paris-affiliated Or Baleine is also said to be the least politically-tinged, the purest of the prizes."
118. [Naoya] "Politically-tinged?"
119. [Sakamoto] "Just means there's less at play from the organizations behind it. Platine Epaulard and Argent Dauphin involve big money, so big agendas move around them. That's why they're called political."
120. [Murata] "In that sense, Kusanagi Ken'ichirou winning Platine Epaulard and Miyazaki Hakai winning Argent Dauphin does make a certain amount of sense..."
121. [Murata] "Kusanagi had strong ties with the Moore Foundation from the start. Miyazaki was born in Japan, but effectively spent his childhood in England and has been active in the London art world all along."
122. [Sakamoto] "You could look at each of them as being an in-house artist of their respective city."
123. [Murata] "That said, there's no doubt those two are geniuses on a completely different level."
124. [Sakamoto] "Murata, you've had your studio abroad for over ten years too, haven't you?"
125. [Murata] "Yeah, I like to think I've built up a fair career overseas, but to stop at Elan Faux..."
126. [Sakamoto] "Listen to you. Winning any prize at the Moore Open Exhibition is a feat. In sixty years of history, there've been only six recipients."
127. [Murata] "For 54 years after its founding, there were zero Japanese winners. Recently, recipients have finally started to emerge, but bluntly put, that's nothing more than proof of just how local the Japanese art world had been up to that point."
128. [NARRATION] Well, that really is exactly right...
129. [Murata] "By the way, you're Kusanagi Naoya-kun, aren't you?"
130. [Sakamoto] "Ah, I was wondering why some kid was here -- Kusanagi Ken'ichirou Junior, huh. You're pretty famous in your own right."
131. [Sakamoto] "I've seen 'Sakura Day Rhapsody,' painted at that young age. It was a fine painting. Didn't look like something a kid could paint."
132. [Sakamoto] "That said, I didn't think it was work worthy of being nominated at Moore..."
133. [Murata] "That was years ago -- meaning he must be painting something better than that now. He's been nominated alongside us, so there's no doubt the work is up to standard."
134. [Sakamoto] "Ah, I see. For us, it feels like just recently, but two or three years is a long time at that age."

