102. [MC] "How can I put it... this fantastical swarm of butterflies, and the whirlpool in the foreground... for someone like me, it evokes death."
103. [MC] "Not a single butterfly has been swallowed by the whirlpool, and yet... how should I say it... though none are being swallowed, it feels as though they might be..."
104. [NARRATION] It's painted with that in mind, calculated.
105. [NARRATION] Elegant butterflies filling the sky, and a rolling transparent sea with the whirlpool rendered in emphatic detail.
106. [NARRATION] They look almost as though each were occurring in a separate world, yet by expressing them within a single world, the painting gives the viewer a strong sense of dissonance.
107. [NARRATION] That dissonance evokes unease, but that unease is counterbalanced by the beautiful swarm of butterflies painted in the distance.
108. [NARRATION] The conscious mind feels beauty; the unconscious feels dread.\nIt's calculated to inflict that kind of confusion on the viewer.
109. [NARRATION] 'The Seven Aspects of the Sakura' expresses death by daring to paint the corpse.
110. [NARRATION] But 'Dreaming of Butterflies' expresses death by not painting death at all.
111. [NARRATION] Of course, I couldn't tell whether that had entirely succeeded.
112. [NARRATION] But this feeling in the room --\nthere was no doubt: my intention had gotten through.
113. [MC] "This sensation... I can hardly put it into words.\nIs this a nihonga -- a traditional Japanese-style painting?"
114. [Naoya] "Yes. I deliberately made it that kind of work."
