58. 　The sea is painted realistically.
59. 　Precisely because it is an intimation of death, I painted it so that it would rise up starkly.
60. 　So it is nothing more than a device meant to put the viewer in a certain mood.
61. 　Nothing more than a painting meant to draw one astray into such a world.
62. 　It was as though I myself had strayed into a painting I had painted.
63. 　The sensation of wandering the soft, floating world of my own making.
64. 　Smoke rises from the crematorium.
65. 　A scene I had already seen once this year.
66. 　But this was not like that time.
67. 　Kusanagi Ken'ichirou's death was a death to be understood.
68. 　For that understanding, my old man and I had traded words many times, and had conversations that were not words too.
69. 　That was the ritual of that understanding.
70. 　Natsume Kei left home on his usual motorcycle.
71. 　And, taking the usual national highway and the expressway, was to come into the city.\nOr so it should have been.
72. 　But he had not even made it out of Yumihari, let alone into Tokyo.
73. 　It was a road we knew well.
