62. "The news of Kei-kun's death, everyone around me grieving......\nand still, I just kept painting."
63. "I just kept on."
64. "I kept painting......"
65. "And that became the Moore Open Exhibition prize. And not just any prize -- the Platine Epaulard, the top honor. The first such feat since Kusanagi Ken'ichirou......"
66. "That's right...... but\nI don't really have much interest in that."
67. "Don't say things like that. If Toritani, who's been aiming for it all his life, heard you say that, he'd be furious."
68. "And Kei, probably too......"
69. "......Kei-kun?"
70. "Yeah, Kei would be angry too."
71. "Is that so? I don't think he would."
72. "The Swallow, you see...... he only wanted to stay by the Prince's side......"
73. "The Swallow?"
74. "Mm. But staying by that Prince's side is terribly hard......"
75. "You have to fly higher than anyone, higher than anything...... or you can't stay by the Prince......"
76. "That's exactly what I mean......"
77. "That's ridiculous...... what the hell is that......"
78. "You people always fly too high --
79. -- and I'm the one left behind."
80. "So you don't understand after all, Nao-kun......"
81. "Because Nao-kun doesn't understand,\nthat's why Kei-kun had to create a work like that."
82. "I understand his feelings very well......"
83. "How wonderful it would have been if what the Swallow loved had been a bronze statue......"
84. "Then they could have stayed together through the cold......\nEven if death waited at the end......"
85. "The cold might freeze the body, but if you can be together, that alone is warmth......"
86. "There's no reason for the Swallow to fly off to southern skies......"
87. "The Swallow does fly south."
88. "Kei too...... and you too......"
89. "But that's all right."
90. "The seasons come round again, and there's a land to return to."
91. "A land to return to?"
92. "No matter how high you fly, wings will one day lose their strength."
93. "That's why wings need a land to come home to."
94. "That would be me and Kei-kun?"
95. "Yeah, you and Kei."
96. "Kei has flown off to somewhere terribly far, but even so, the land he should return to is here."
97. "Same for you, Rin."
98. "The seasons will come round...... and you can alight again."
99. "As many times as I want?"
100. "Yeah, the seasons come round as many times as it takes."
101. "......Truly, you're an artist like the Sakura."
102. "Again and again, you make miraculous flowers bloom as though it were the most ordinary scenery."
103. "Because they bloom as though it were ordinary, people don't even realize it's a miracle......"
104. "Even when the blossoms fall......\neven then, the branches bloom again......"
105. "That vast trunk roots deep into the earth, and reaches up, endlessly, into the sky......"
106. "So with every scattering, the trunk grows greater, the branches climb higher, the roots pierce deeper into the earth."
107. "Only Kei-kun understood this......"
108. "That's why he painted the painting he did."
109. "Going beyond his limits......"
110. "If I am the cherry, then you're something far greater......"
111. "That's what Dad said."
112. "That a god dwells in me...... you mean?"
113. "I see, now I understand very well what Ken'ichirou-san meant......"
114. "Whether that can be attained -- and what price must be paid for it......"
115. "Wings that don't take root in the earth, however strong and noble and beautiful, are fleeting things......"
116. "Even if there is a god, without the earth those wings will be lost."
117. "Ah, I see......\nThought of that way, that painting expressed it well......"
118. "That painting is suggestive."
119. "Almost no one, probably, catches that intuition......"
120. "'Dreaming of Butterflies'......"
121. "Countless butterflies crossing the sea. Butterflies that seem infinite. As if to blot out the sky......"
122. "There is no earth there. There is only a beautiful, transparent abyss stretching on forever."
123. "But it's a dream. That landscape is a dream."
124. "And yet.\nIn just such a dreamlike instant, the world is swapped out."
125. "Earth becomes sea, sea becomes earth."
126. "The abyss is the very earth beneath our feet."
127. "The abyss is not a continuation of the dream."
128. "Beneath a sky filled with infinite butterflies lies the abyss.\nBut no -- that is the earth we stand upon."
129. "That is why, when we press our feet upon it, the earth sings the sound of a dream."
130. "The sound of a dream......"
131. "Is it a beautiful sound?"
132. "Mm, and it is a very cruel sound."
133. "If it weren't, it wouldn't be beautiful......"
134. "Nao-kun...... I......"
135. "I, I can't look back anymore......"
136. "I don't even have time for that......"
137. "Yeah, because you're leaving...... out into the world."
138. "No. That's not it. That isn't what I mean......"

=== lines=134 stop_reason=end_turn in=6736 out=4570 cache_read=3222 cache_create=0 last_entry_continued=0 ===
1. [Rin] "That's not it...... The truth is......"
2. [Rin] "Even so, I have to go......"
3. [NARRATION] 　Just like that, the two of us looked up at the sky.
4. [NARRATION] 　The spring wind lifted the cherry blossoms into the air, again and again.
5. [Rin] "Lying down like this to watch the cherry blossoms, isn't it beautiful?"
6. [Rin] "Looking up at the sky from the bottom of the earth, and the fallen petals soaring up again and again......"
7. [Naoya] "Once, I did this here with Kei......\nThe two of us gazed up at the sky from within the cherry forest."
8. [Rin] "From here?"
9. [Rin] "So this is the same scenery Kei-kun saw......"
10. [NARRATION] 　Beneath the fluttering cherry blossoms, Rin and I lay sprawled out on the ground.
11. [Naoya] "Wilde's theory of art, huh......"
12. [Rin] "The meaning of this painting is beauty itself.\nSimply to exist is this painting's reason for being."
13. [Naoya] "Yeah, I know. It's what the English poet Algernon Swinburne said in his review of A.J. Moore's painting 'Azaleas.'"
14. [Rin] "Yes, and those words later came to be regarded as the very essence of aestheticism."
15. [Naoya] "Aestheticism...... commonly called 'tanbishugi.' A late-nineteenth-century art movement that, together with its historical context, held that the reception and creation of beauty was the highest value."
16. [Naoya] "You once called me a 'Happy Prince' type -- well, the very author of that story, Oscar Wilde, is the symbolic figure of this movement."
17. [Naoya] "Dad told me. When you were little, he taught you art.\nBecause art is a fundamentally human thing, he had to teach it to you."
18. [Rin] "But things didn't turn out the way Ken'ichirou-san intended......"
19. [Naoya] "No, they didn't. When one says, 'The meaning of this painting is beauty itself. Simply to exist is\nthis painting's reason for being,' is there really any place for a human being to intervene?"
20. [Rin] "Who's to say. When Algernon Swinburne, or Oscar Wilde, spoke those words, we can't know what they were thinking."
21. [Naoya] "But with you, Rin, it's clear."
22. [Naoya] "Your aestheticism isn't the kind of thought that swept Europe at the end of the nineteenth century."
23. [Rin] "Still, I do love Oscar Wilde."
24. [Rin] "His every word feels like it's speaking on behalf of my own heart......"
25. [Rin] "You probably don't like him, Kusanagi-kun."
26. [Naoya] "No, that's not true. I think he pinpoints the essence of art."
27. [Naoya] "But I also love the original saying he was riffing on -- 'art imitates nature.'"
28. [Rin] "That's Aristotle."
29. [Rin] "A work of art is born from the imitation of nature. It's an extremely clear thought -- so solid it hardly needs explanation."
30. [Rin] "In fact, one could say the history of art is the history of how we interpret nature."
31. [Naoya] "But rephrase it this way, and it starts to look shaky."
32. [Naoya] "The history of art is the history of interpreting how nature appears to itself."
33. [Naoya] "When you put it that way, a crack runs through Aristotle's saying."
34. [Naoya] "And that crack, I think, is aestheticism itself."
35. [Naoya] "For a long time, human beings believed that what was projected on their retinas was the world they saw."
36. [Naoya] "They believed they saw nature through the retina."
37. [Naoya] "But the act of seeing is not the creation of a representation of the world."
38. [Rin] "That's true. The human eye has only about a million pixels' worth of nerve cells, really. And yet people crave cameras with far higher resolution than that."
39. [Rin] "If the world were only what was projected on the retina, our world would be far, far poorer than it is."
40. [Naoya] "So they say."
41. [Rin] "The act of seeing always brings meaning and value into it."
42. [Rin] "Wilde says:"
43. [Rin] "Until you have recognized the beauty of a thing, you have not truly seen it."
44. [Rin] "Only when we recognize the beauty of a thing does the 'thing' first come to exist."
45. [Rin] "William Wordsworth, the English poet, loved the Lake District dearly and wrote nature-praising poetry both artless and full of hidden passion."
46. [Rin] "He is said to have received poetic inspiration from nature and turned it into verse."
47. [Rin] "But to Wilde, such an act was --"
48. [Rin] "Wordsworth did indeed go to the lakeshore. But he merely read from within the stone -- that piece of nature -- the lesson he had already hidden there himself."
49. [Naoya] "Today, people see fog not because the fog is there."
50. [Naoya] "Fog exists because poets and painters and their kind taught countless people about the mysterious beauty called fog."
51. [Naoya] "There may have been fog in London for centuries. Nay, there certainly must have been."
52. [Naoya] "But no one saw the fog, no one even thought about the fog.\nUntil art discovered it, no such thing existed."
53. [Naoya] "Wilde writes things like that."
54. [Naoya] "It's an extraordinary way to put it. He's saying that until fog was discovered by artists, it didn't even exist......"
55. [Naoya] "But even so, one can say it is a correct observation."
56. [Rin] "Beauty is discovered. Because it was beautifully described, such a beautiful landscape unfolds before our eyes."
57. [Rin] "People think nature is the great mother that gave birth to us.\nBut the fact is the opposite. We are the ones who created nature."
58. [Naoya] "Just like something Wilde would say......"
59. [Naoya] "But that's right in one sense, and completely wrong in another."
60. [Rin] "In one sense?"
61. [Naoya] "Yeah. It's only half the truth."
62. [Naoya] "Aristotle really is great, after all.\nYou can't completely deny his words."
63. [Rin] "What do you mean?"
64. [Naoya] "Wilde speaks as if 'beauty' were something already sitting inside his own head from the start."
65. [Naoya] "But is that really true?"
66. [Naoya] "Is beauty really tidily housed inside our heads before we ever come into contact with nature?"
67. [Rin] "Who knows."
68. [Naoya] "There's a story like this."
69. [Naoya] "You raise a newborn kitten in a box that contains only vertical stripes."
70. [Naoya] "A world where only vertical stripes exist. No other concepts at all."
71. [Naoya] "What happens to that kitten,\nwhich has only ever seen vertical stripes, in a world like that?"
72. [Rin] "What does happen?"
73. [Naoya] "The experiment was actually done."
74. [Naoya] "According to the results,\nthe kitten couldn't avoid a rod placed horizontally -- it would always bump into it."
75. [Naoya] "In other words, this kitten couldn't see horizontal things."
76. [Naoya] "For this kitten, the very concept of 'horizontal' didn't exist. It was living in a world where only 'vertical' existed."
77. [Rin] "What happens to that kitten after that?"
78. [Naoya] "If you raise it properly in the outside world after that, it grows up normally.\nIn the outside world, there is 'horizontal,' and every other concept besides."
79. [Naoya] "Inside nature, one can discover every kind of concept."
80. [Naoya] "Well, 'concept' sounds too verbal for a cat......\nMore simply, an object of attention. The ability to distinguish an object from its surroundings, maybe?"
81. [Naoya] "What's the lesson of this story?"
82. [Naoya] "Not everything is in the kitten's head from the start.\nIn other words, living things learn from nature and discover every kind of reality."
83. [Naoya] "So we do, after all, discover something in nature......"
84. [Naoya] "Aristotle's words are utterly correct.\nHuman beings create everything by imitating nature."
85. [Naoya] "I'd like to say that -- but that too is only half right."
86. [Naoya] "Because in this experiment, one can put it the exact opposite way as well."
87. [Naoya] "Precisely because the kitten discovered the notion of 'horizontal' inside its head, 'horizontal' existed in nature."
88. [Naoya] "Yes -- Wilde said that until fog was discovered by artists, fog didn't exist. It's exactly the same."
89. [Naoya] "Until 'horizontal' was discovered by the kitten, there was no 'horizontal' in the world."
90. [Naoya] "One can say it either way."
91. [Naoya] "Which of the two is correct?"
92. [Naoya] "That kitten discovered the concept of 'horizontal' somewhere.\nAnd if so, then where did we discover beauty?"
93. [Naoya] "In the heart? Or in nature?"
94. [Naoya] "Or neither -- somewhere far away?"
95. [Naoya] "When I think about that sort of thing, it suddenly strikes me."
96. [Naoya] "The heart and nature."
97. [Naoya] "Are those two things really so different?"
98. [Naoya] "Are the heart and nature really two utterly different things?"
99. [Naoya] "Or subject and object, inner and outer -- put it however you like."
100. [Naoya] "But one thing I can say is: are they really two such foreign domains?"
101. [Naoya] "Take the story of the kitten. Whether the concept of 'horizontal' was born in nature or in the heart -- isn't it fundamentally unreasonable to try to judge which?"
102. [Naoya] "Isn't it unnatural in the first place to say it was born in one or the other?"
103. [Naoya] "After all, to the world the kitten sees, it is one and the same.\nIsn't the very act of thinking of outer and inner as if they were separated already a mistake?"
104. [Naoya] "The same holds for beauty."
105. [Naoya] "Nature imitates art.\nOr,\nart imitates nature."
106. [Naoya] "The two may seem opposed, but perhaps they are only saying the same thing."
107. [Naoya] "No -- they probably are the same thing."
108. [Naoya] "If you think of it that way, a lot of things add up."
109. [Naoya] "A certain genius poet said this."
110. [Naoya] "The Brain -- is wider than the Sky --\nOur heads are wider than the sky."
111. [Naoya] "For -- put them side by side --\nLook, place the two side by side."
112. [Naoya] "Our heads will easily contain the sky,\nand you along with it."
113. [Naoya] "The Brain is deeper than the sea --\nOur heads are deeper than the sea."
114. [Naoya] "For -- hold them -- Blue to Blue --\nLook, lay the two blues one over the other."
115. [Naoya] "Our heads will soak up the sea\nas a sponge sops up water from a bucket."
116. [Naoya] "Our heads weigh exactly the same as God.\nLook, weigh the two of them precisely."
117. [Naoya] "If they differ, it is only by\nthe difference between word and sound."
118. [Rin] "Dickinson?"
119. [Naoya] "It's probably just that -- the difference between word and sound."
120. [Naoya] "It may only be a difference of viewpoint, or of feeling."
121. [Naoya] "This thing I call 'I,' as this body, is only a part of the world."
122. [Naoya] "That statement isn't wrong."
123. [Naoya] "But in the same way, the world is only a part, filtered through the body."
124. [Naoya] "It's nothing more than attention split into two poles."
125. [Naoya] "The limits of my self are the limits of the world."
126. [Naoya] "Suppose you say that."
127. [Naoya] "That may well be a correct way of putting it, but I think just that alone isn't enough."
128. [Naoya] "Because it makes it sound as if either the world or the 'I' exists first. Or as if they exist simultaneously."
129. [Naoya] "The limits of my language are the limits of my world."
130. [Naoya] "It's exactly the same when you say that."
131. [Naoya] "The newborn kitten looks at nature and discovers all things within its heart.\nAnd because it discovers them, they exist within nature."
132. [Naoya] "It's the same. Our world is very fluid."
133. [Naoya] "Seeing, and discovering, and existing -- all of these are generated as if in a single circulation."
134. [Naoya] "At the limit of the world I circulate, and at my limit the world also circulates."

=== lines=145 stop_reason=end_turn in=6997 out=4090 cache_read=3222 cache_create=0 last_entry_continued=0 ===
1. "I and nature are always within a circle."
2. "The heart and nature always stand in a circular relation."
3. "So they are not two separate things."
4. "They are one and the same."
5. "It's obvious, really -- the familiar scenery we look at every day always contains past, present, and future within it."
6. "It's a gathering of every phenomenon that shifts in time... an aggregate of processes..."
7. "There's no way something like that could be static. The world is dynamic."
8. "A moment cut out of it isn't the world."
9. "That's why we can walk in the ordinary way."
10. "Why we can speak."
11. "Why we can bring ourselves to paint a picture."
12. "You stand before the canvas. You take up the brush, dissolve the paint, and then, from the dynamic world, you can cut out a moment."
13. "It's because, just like the kitten discovering 'horizontal,' we too discover all sorts of things. Within all of nature, we discover what it grants us, what it offers."
14. "And of course, not all of it is good. A stick lying sideways on the ground hurts if you bump into it."
15. "But that's how we keep discovering such qualities, in the world and in ourselves."
16. "And so the world and the self circulate."
17. "Nature and heart aren't separate. They are equal elements that constitute 'me.'"
18. "I see. So Kusanagi-kun, what you want to say is that beauty too, just like the limits of the world and the limits of the self, circulates and flows."
19. "Well, that's about it."
20. "Yes. Perhaps you are right, Kusanagi-kun."
21. "But I wonder -- does what I'm about to say fit into that too?"
22. "When humans became human, they invented a single tool."
23. "It was just a bone with notches cut into it.\nBut those notches had a certain meaning, and many of them were carved in."
24. "Many notches on a stick? Like a scale, or graduation marks? A ruler, say?"
25. "You're close. But a little different. That ruler wasn't for measuring things."
26. "It was for measuring time -- what we would now call a calendar."
27. "Using that calendar as a guide, they seem to have recorded when their prey moved through which places, and hunted accordingly."
28. "The act of carving that invisible thing, time, into graduations.\nThat means engraving time -- into the space of a bone -- with the concept of number."
29. "Of all the living things there had ever been, humankind was the first to give number to something as abstract as time."
30. "By your earlier logic, Kusanagi-kun, number is discovered from nature, and also discovered within the heart, and that's why it comes to exist in our world."
31. "But then, why is it that even facts number has not been discovered in within nature can still be proven mathematically?"
32. "Why is it that mathematics can explain nature?"
33. "Pythagoras, Kepler, Galileo, Descartes, Newton."
34. "They uncovered the laws of the world through mathematical truth."
35. "And every one of their answers was utterly unlike what experience had taught until then."
36. "Most people saw the sun rise. They saw the moon go round. They saw that the ground was flat. So they thought the celestial sphere turned above a flat earth."
37. "But mathematics laid bare that this way of seeing was in error."
38. "Mathematics proves, and discovers, facts that no human being can experience."
39. "In modern physics, mathematics foretells many phenomena that experiment has never observed."
40. "The mathematical world is discovering the very world we know."
41. "Going back to what you said just now, Kusanagi-kun...\nheart, or nature -- which of them is mathematical truth discovered in?"
42. "...That's..."
43. "Just what is number, I wonder..."
44. "...Number."
45. Mathematical truth -- it is certainly not something discovered by looking at nature.\nMany of the truths of physics are discovered mathematically before they are ever discovered in nature.
46. Then is it something the heart discovers?
47. Were mathematical truths installed in our brains from the start?
48. "I think pure mathematicians are mystics.\nAnd it's precisely because he was a mystic, they say, that a certain genius mathematician was able to construct the incompleteness theorems."
49. "A mathematician called one of the greatest intellects of the twentieth century carried out a 'proof of God' in the form of a mathematical formula."
50. "Art, originally, ought to have shared the very same thought as this mathematician."
51. "Western painting developed for the sake of proving the existence of God."
52. "Ah, that's true... At least, so far as art history goes, you're right, Rin..."
53. In Western art history, mathematics, astronomy, music, and art are tightly interwoven.
54. Each has an intimate relationship with mathematical beauty.
55. Music, of course, but art as well -- both agree in that they depict the beautiful laws of the world.
56. Michelangelo, Leonardo da Vinci, Raphael -- the artists of the Renaissance embedded many mathematical designs into their work.
57. The golden ratio, the silver ratio, the golden triangle, the pentagram, perspective -- one could go on without end.
58. And when you try to unravel these truths, you inevitably run into difficult mathematical proofs.
59. Pi, Desargues's theorem, and further, the Fibonacci sequence, the logarithmic spiral.
60. Hidden within the relation between nature and beauty are, in fact, a great many mathematical causalities.
61. "Even without humans, the truths of mathematics exist."
62. "Isn't that so?"
63. "Or would mathematical truths change in a place where there are no humans?"
64. "No, they wouldn't... just because humans vanished, it isn't as if mathematical truth would be lost with them..."
65. "Mathematical truth may well exist independently of the human heart..."
66. "'Why are numbers beautiful? It is like asking why Beethoven's Ninth Symphony is beautiful.'"
67. "'If you cannot see the answer, no one else can tell you.'"
68. "'I know that numbers are beautiful. If numbers aren't beautiful, then nothing is.'"
69. "The words of the Hungarian mathematician Paul Erdos."
70. "In mathematics, beauty is demanded even in a method of proof."
71. "Elegance too is demanded. Why do you think that is?"
72. "Because so many formulas are elegant... is that it?"
73. "Perhaps it is because they are beautiful that formulas can describe the world."
74. "Paul Erdos also said that God has an imaginary book in which the most beautiful mathematical proofs are written down."
75. "God, huh..."
76. "Yes. Behind beauty, the presence of God always flickers into view."
77. "This is what I think."
78. "Even without humans, the truth of beauty exists."
79. "Beauty exists independently, as beauty."
80. "That's why your beauty, Rin, needed no audience..."
81. "I see. If what you say is true, then in the value of beauty there is no reason for others to be involved."
82. "Beauty without human involvement, huh... a rather ascetic beauty..."
83. "There are artists like that, you know."
84. "That poem you recited earlier, Kusanagi-kun."
85. "Emily Dickinson, during her lifetime, published only seven poems."
86. "Even though she had written over seventeen hundred works."
87. "Why did she publish only seven poems?"
88. "Did her poems really require an audience?"
89. "There was a genius named Yevgeny Mravinsky, the permanent conductor of the Leningrad Philharmonic during the Soviet era."
90. "At one rehearsal, they achieved a performance so utterly perfect that even that genius could not believe it."
91. "At the climax of that performance, Mravinsky was struck by a sensation that seemed to be of another world."
92. "So he canceled the actual concert."
93. "His reason, he said, was that 'a performance like that dry run could never possibly be achieved again.'"
94. "I know that story. I do listen to classical music at least well enough to have picked it up."
95. "An artist as accomplished as you, Kusanagi-kun, must have had such moments too. Times when you felt: this doesn't need to go out into the world."
96. "I see. That's a sharp jab, isn't it..."
97. "You must understand this, Kusanagi-kun. The value of beauty, the value of ethics -- for such things, an absolute arbiter is required."
98. "The Last Judgment. It is then that every ugliness and beauty is judged."
99. "And that is why an artist can stake his life on art, on beauty."
100. "That is to say, God, then..."
101. "Yes. It is not the audience who bestows value. It is not the beholder who assigns worth."
102. "Beauty has value because it exists as beauty."
103. "And that is also God's reason for existing."
104. I find myself unable to offer a single word of rebuttal...
105. "Kusanagi-kun... may I ask you a question?"
106. "Do you not have a god, Kusanagi-kun?"
107. "That question is too meaningless to answer..."
108. "Because what you, Rin, call 'God' is the very ground behind the act of painting, the act of shaping beauty."
109. "You're not talking about some God sitting up in heaven, are you?"
110. "Right. The god I mean is that sort of thing."
111. "It's about what a person believes in when they create beauty."
112. "Then I too must have a god."
113. "Yes, I understand that.\nBut your god, Kusanagi-kun, does not judge, does not punish, before beauty.\nA god who does nothing on the day of the Last Judgment..."
114. "Does nothing on the day of the Last Judgment, huh...\nNo, I wonder about that..."
115. "He would?"
116. "Who's to say."
117. "Your god, Rin, is transcendent. Beauty for the sake of that absolute god -- that is what people give shape to."
118. "It is a character the West has had since ancient... no, that primitive religious art itself possessed from the beginning."
119. "In that case, to put it in Wildean terms, the beauty you speak of is this."
120. "Man imitates beauty, or imitates God."
121. "Because beauty exists long before man does."
122. "It is the same as the mathematician who believes that mathematical formulas existed in perfect form before man existed."
123. "Then the god, or the beauty, that I believe in may be something like this."
124. "God imitates man,\n         and\nbeauty imitates man."
125. "I see... put that way... beauty too is something that humans created..."
126. "If humans made it, then it isn't perfect. There is no perfection in beauty; it shifts hollowly according to people's feelings."
127. "There is no consistency in human beauty."
128. "A weak god, isn't he."
129. "A weak god, huh... you're right about that.\nA god whose value people alter as they please -- a god that might well be sold off for pocket change..."
130. "To someone like you, who can seemingly glimpse the Idea of Beauty itself, it may not seem worth anything at all..."
131. "It's a weak god."
132. "But when a person stands face to face with beauty --"
133. "or when they are moved --"
134. "or when they resolve upon something --"
135. "and when they love --"
136. "that weak god is there beside them."
137. "A god who dwells with people is a weak god,\nbut still, when a person believes, he is there beside them."
138. "A god created by man may certainly be weak."
139. "But it is precisely because he was created by man that he is together with man."
140. "That's how I think of beauty..."
141. "'If it be void, then that void itself is such as this,\n                                and to some degree common to us all.'"
142. "'(Just as everything is each one within me,\n                                so it is everything within each of them.)'"
143. "It's just like Spring and Asura, isn't it."
144. "Ah, it is... like Spring and Asura."
145. "The truth is... it's the same for me too, though..."

=== lines=127 stop_reason=end_turn in=5449 out=3370 cache_read=3222 cache_create=0 last_entry_continued=0 ===
1. Rin murmured something.
