1. "Understood. I shall report to the guidance counselor the fact that Kusanagi-sensei was smoking on the roof during lunch break."
2. 　I've been here, unchanged, ever since.
3. 　My university was in the city, but I didn't have the money to rent a place, so I commuted, about two hours each way.
4. 　Partly because my commute was just too long, I graduated without making any friends in college, and now I somehow scrape by right here in Yumihari, as a part-time instructor at my old school.
5. 　If you asked me whether I had no anxieties, I'd be lying.
6. 　But if you asked whether it was a carefree life, well, yes, it is carefree.
7. 　Naturally, I'm not married.
8. 　Not just that -- I've still never even dated a woman.
9. 　A ca-re-free bach-e-lor, that's me!
10. 　My savings are on the decent side.
11. 　That's because I couldn't hold up under the yearly property tax on the apartment, and I sold the place off.
12. 　It was a home I'd grown used to, but the property tax was no sum a part-time instructor could pay.
13. 　I put all that money straight into savings, so I have more put aside than most of my generation.
14. 　That said, the tax bill the year after I sold the apartment knocked me flat.
15. 　Apparently, even selling off the little I had -- my only real asset -- counts as income.
16. 　The tax system in this country is nothing short of insane.
17. 　I hear that if you'd asked a tax accountant, the bill wouldn't have been anywhere near that big... but I didn't have the wits to even think of that...
18. 　Still, living the life I do and having savings on top of it, I'm probably fortunate.
19. 　That day, I had nothing left to do.
20. 　As a part-timer, there's nothing much to the job beyond teaching the classes you're assigned.
21. 　Just as Sakizaki-kun had said, my life outside of those classes was to kill time on the roof, and once the last class was over I'd head home, even if it was still morning.
22. 　So I had time to spare, more or less.
23. 　If I'd taken on another part-time job, my income might have been steadier, but I was a born layabout, so I used my spare time on hobbies.
24. 　Hobbies, though, meant no more than building plastic models, reading books, going out on rides.
25. 　Sometimes I'd head out surfing at dawn.
26. 　A few years back I picked up a surfboard someone had thrown out with the bulk trash, so I gave it a try.
27. 　It was one of those huge ones they call a longboard, so I was able to get started pretty easily.
28. 　If I'd picked up a normal one -- what they call a shortboard -- I probably wouldn't have stuck with it.
29. 　Yumihari faces the sea, and I'd take my bike along the coastline and drop in at various spots.
30. 　"Surfing" sounds so hip and young and active, but in reality most of them are in their thirties. No -- there are plenty in their forties and fifties too.
31. 　It's a sport where middle-aged men and outright old-timers bob dopily on the water.
32. 　Surfing is a solo sport, so it suits someone like me who has no friends.
33. 　You just bob there, waiting for a wave.
34. 　What they call "sea bathing."
35. 　When there are no waves everyone just floats out there together.
36. 　Floating, staring at that offshore horizon that stretches on and on, you find yourself thinking about the most trivial things.
37. 　Whether at home or outside, I'm basically alone.
38. 　That's what I think about, bobbing out on the sea.
39. 　Of course, no reply ever comes back.
40. "What should I do about dinner..."
41. 　Normally I'd stop by the supermarket on the way and buy something, but with all that pointless thinking going on in my head, I ended up walking straight home.
42. 　Well, eating out now and then is fine too.
43. "Ah, it's me. Naoya. You free for drinks tonight?"
44. "Huh? Why should I be the one buying? Like hell I would. The usual place, then."
45. 　Short and sweet, and that's about the size of it.
46. "Alright, two draft beers to start--"
47. "Not my treat."
48. "Why? You're the one who invited me, Kusanagi."
49. "Whatever, lady. Not a draft -- a Hoppy for me."
50. "Split the bill, huh... geez."
51. "So? What's up? Something happen?"
52. "Do I need a reason to drink with you? I mean, I'm either drinking alone or drinking with you -- you're pretty much the only one I've got."
53. "Yeah, neither of us has any friends."
54. 　This guy is Katagai.
55. 　No one will remember him, but he's a guy who's popped up a few times in the past as basically a background character.
56. 　Funny thing is, I've completely lost touch with the Yumihari art club crowd, but with a nobody like this, the connection somehow lingers.
57. 　Well, more than "the connection lingers" -- it's just that the ones who happened to stay in Yumihari were me and this guy...
