1. Morning.\nAs always, I was eating breakfast alone up on the roof.
2. Then came the sound of someone dashing up the hard stairs.
3. "Oh, Sakizaki-kun. Morning. What's up?"
4. "Good morning. I don't have any particular reason for being here. I just thought I'd like to breathe the morning air from the rooftop."
5. "Hmm. What a refreshing person you are."
6. "Says you, sensei?"
7. "Why is it a problem when I say it?"
8. "Sensei, your breakfast is always French bread and coffee, isn't it?\nSomehow that's both refreshing and stylish."
9. "French bread and coffee?"
10. "Yes, it's always that, right?"
11. "Nope, it's French bread and barley tea."
12. "B-barley tea? Really? What kind of combination is that?"
13. "The cheapest bread is French bread. Same goes for barley tea. That kind of combination."
14. "I-is that so...? A-and here I thought you were eating something more sophisticated..."
15. "Why would you think that? What part of me could possibly be considered sophisticated?"
16. "The atmosphere, maybe?"
17. "Is that so? Where's this atmosphere of mine?"
18. "It's there. You're sophistication itself."
19. "The rumor is that you're the hidden love child of a legendary artist, and moreover you were the rival and lover of the world-renowned artist Misakura Rin.... Something happened in the past, and now you always sit alone gazing up at the sky from the rooftop."
20. "H-huh? What kind of rumor is that? Is that really how you kids see me?"
21. "That's why yesterday I asked you straight out whether the rumor was true, about your relationship with Kusanagi Ken'ichirou and Misakura Rin."
22. "Huh, I see..."
23. Well, it's true I don't openly declare that I'm that guy's son, but... why has it turned into a "hidden love child" plot?
24. And being lovers with Rin... that's subtly off from the truth too.
25. Well, that's just what rumors are like, I suppose....
26. "But when I asked yesterday, it didn't seem to be the case, so I thought maybe sensei was deliberately playing the part to make people think that way."
27. "Now, that's a nasty accusation."
28. "But, well, Kusanagi-sensei is fairly popular with the female students, you know. I thought maybe you knew it and were putting on airs on purpose."
29. "That's an accusation. Sensei isn't putting on airs -- sensei is just poor."
30. "Is that so. Well, it wasn't unexpected."
31. "So there's no surprise, huh."
32. "Yes. From the few times I've spoken with sensei up here on this rooftop, I thought that everyone's rumors lacked credibility."
33. "I never had any particular interest in sensei to begin with, so honestly, until recently I didn't even know such rumors existed."
34. "As I was warning sensei about various things, we started talking a few times, and around that point other students began asking me about you."
35. "Really? I don't particularly feel popular."
36. "Ah, that's because you're oblivious, sensei.\nJust from meeting you up here, all the others have been...\nAh, no, it's nothing."
37. "Just from meeting me, what?"
38. "You're annoying. More importantly, sensei, can you cook?"
39. "The topic sure changed suddenly.... Well, I've been living alone for a long time, so I can more or less do the basics."
40. "And yet, it's French bread and barley tea?"
41. "But you see, once I tried this, I got hooked."
42. I take a container of mayonnaise out of my pocket.
43. "Mayonnaise?"
44. "Yeah, you break the French bread apart like this until it's all crumbly..."
45. I spread mayonnaise on the French bread.
46. "Give it a try."
47. "Eh? Ah, this piece of bread?"
48. "It's the part I tore off with my hands. My mouth hasn't touched it, so no problem."
49. "I-I don't mind such things!"
50. "Really? Well, whatever, just try it."
51. "Okay, but this is just French bread with mayonnaise on it, isn't it...?"
52. "Yep, it's simply that kind of food."
53. "Nom... munch, munch..."
54. Sakizaki-kun slowly chews with her small mouth.
55. "Here, barley tea."
56. Timing it just right, I hold out the barley tea I've poured from the thermos, and she takes it.
57. "Gulp, gulp..."
58. "Mmm, this is really delicious."
59. "Right? It's easy too. Delicious, and above all, cheap."
60. "Huh... is that so. It's completely different from what the rumors say."
61. "People's rumors, you know. You can't rely on them.\nPeople say whatever they please. Because it's not their problem."
62. "Not their problem, huh... true enough."
63. "When it comes to themselves, humans desperately want to be understood correctly, but when it comes to other people, they feel like they've grasped everything based on rather sloppy assumptions."
64. "In that case, since I've spent this much time with you, shouldn't you tell me a little about yourself, sensei?"
65. "You don't need to know anything about me."
66. "I'm just part-time, and I don't know if I'll even be here next year."
67. "Isn't that attitude a problem, even for a nominal teacher?"
68. "Is it? Personally, a teacher who wants to talk about himself is someone I couldn't trust just on that alone."
69. "So conversely, a teacher who excessively hides himself is trustworthy?"
70. "My, you're persistent today. Well then, Sakizaki-kun, what exactly do you want to know about me?"
71. "W-when you put it that way, asking what I want to know... I'm at a loss..."
72. "Alone on the rooftop, under the sky, standing there like a shadow lingers over me."
73. "That's just because I don't get along well with the other teachers, so I avoid the staff room as much as possible."
74. "As for looking like a shadow hangs over me -- I have no money, can't eat anything good, and have extremely few friends. I'm just gloomy. Not a shadow -- gloomy is the correct answer."
75. "The person called Kusanagi Naoya is nothing more, and nothing less, than that.\nAnd you'd know that best of all, Sakizaki-kun."
76. "I would know best... yes, I suppose so."
77. "It's something I only noticed after I started coming up here."
78. "The mural on the church -- you can see it beautifully from here too, can't you?"
79. "The church mural?"
80. "Yes. Apparently it's a work by Kusanagi Ken'ichirou, and there are various anecdotes about how it was made...."
81. "I heard that even that Misakura Rin was involved in its creation.\nKusanagi-sensei, do you know anything about it?"
82. "Beats me. No idea."
83. Sakizaki-kun glares at me.
84. "Wh-what is it..."
85. "It's unusual to see you flustered, sensei."
86. "I'm not flustered or anything..."
87. "Why are you talking like a woman all of a sudden...?"
88. "That painting is mysterious, isn't it...."
89. "When I entered the school, that church mural was what surprised me the most."
90. "And then, when I went inside the church, I was surprised again -- to think the outside and inside could be so different, what an amazing work...."
91. "I bet. Well, it was designed by a world-class artist, after all."
92. "Yes, there's that too, but somehow it looks like fun. That work."
93. "Maybe it was calculated to look that way, but the creators really seem to have enjoyed themselves... that piece."
94. "The creators look like they were having fun... huh."
95. "Is that kind of thing something that can be conveyed through a work? Or is it something made through such calculation?"
96. "I see, you say interesting things."
97. "Why art doesn't use language..."
98. "Art doesn't use language?"
99. "No, of course, poetry and literature use words. It's just that calling that 'language' feels a little off, doesn't it?"
100. "Yes, I don't quite understand, but calling poetry or literature 'language' does feel wrong somehow. 'Words' fits better."
101. "I wonder why. Now that you mention it, 'language' feels like dead words somehow...."
102. "Eh? What do you mean? Was that just now an explanation?"
103. "Ah, yes. It was an explanation. I didn't speak the meaning -- I pointed to the meaning."
104. "I don't understand at all. What is that?"
105. "Try to imagine."
106. "You can hear the creators' cheerful voices. Is the meaning in the cheerful words themselves? It's not, is it?"
107. "More than the meaning of the words, isn't the value in the resonance of those cheerful voices?"
108. "What you were drawn to in that work -- wasn't it the tone of the words being cheerfully exchanged, rather than the meaning of the words themselves?"
109. "Ah! Amazing. I don't really understand, but somehow it feels like it just clicked."
110. "Really? That's good, then."
111. "As expected of Kusanagi-sensei. You really are an art teacher."
112. "Your explanation of how to face an artwork was truly exquisite."
113. "I see. Words sometimes hold their value in their tone rather than their meaning. It's kind of like a song, isn't it?"
114. "A song?"
115. "Yes, a song. A poem of music. It isn't just chasing the meaning of the words. Because it rides on a melody, it takes on a different meaning."
116. "Through melody, a poem transforms into a song."
117. "I see, a poem changes into a song, huh.... That itself is quite a poetic turn of phrase."
118. "What I felt as cheerful was the melodic quality -- that's what you're saying, right?"
119. "Well, something like that."
120. "Heh, is that so, is that so, hehehh~"
121. She gazes at me as if impressed.
122. "It's just, you know. Kusanagi-sensei is such a blockhead of a person, I never imagined you'd say something so poetic and cool."
123. "Idiot, art is supposed to be cool. It's art because it's cool."
124. "Is that so? Rather than cool, art feels more like something weightier to me."
125. "That's because it's become 'language' rather than 'words.' Like you said, that's nothing but a corpse."
126. "You can go to the library -- lots of dead words are pressed between the pages of books.\nIf you have time, you could go to a museum too. Plenty of dead paintings hang there as well."
127. "You mean literature and artworks of old are dead?"
128. "Yes, that's right. If you feel it's something 'weighty,' then it's already dead."
129. "That's the same as language."
130. "But art can be revived any number of times."
131. "When you stand before a beautiful butterfly that was nothing but a specimen, if you catch the illusion that it's alive, and dream of its taking flight, then it's no longer a corpse."
132. "The specimen has been granted the right to 'fly' again within a person. That's why it captivates people. Art is the same."
133. "An eternity that traps a moment.\nWell, saying 'eternity' isn't quite right since works have a lifespan too..."
134. "Even so, as long as that work exists, that moment is sealed within eternity."
135. "Sometimes just one, sometimes many more feelings to convey.... Well, that depends on the work."
136. "More isn't necessarily better, nor is a single one necessarily wonderful for being just one."

