=== lines=84 stop_reason=end_turn in=4755 out=2512 cache_read=3222 cache_create=0 last_entry_continued=0 ===
1. "Have you kept in contact with her since then?"
2. "With who?"
3. "Misakura Rin. That genius artist."
4. "What connection could there possibly be between a world-class artist and a part-time lecturer at Yumihari?"
5. "You two used to be so close, didn't you."
6. "In the past, yeah. But no one can stay the way they were.\nWe can't stay kids forever, can we."
7. "A fair argument. Exactly right.\nAs we age, differences emerge in each person's standing.\nThe environments people belong to become entirely different, too."
8. "Sometimes fools, without noticing that, will casually strike up conversation on the grounds of being old acquaintances -- but of course the conversation won't line up."
9. "A world-renowned artist and a lecturer at a rural private school......\nBetween Kusanagi-kun and Misakura-san, it's only natural that there'd be no connection now......"
10. "But I'm different."
11. "We're going to surpass Misakura Rin someday."
12. "Surpass Misakura Rin, huh......\nAre you still saying that sort of thing......"
13. "Of course. That's exactly what I've been working so hard for, after all."
14. "Your 'hard work' just looks like provoking the public, though?"
15. "And how is that any different from Kusanagi Ken'ichirou? How is it any different from that Misakura Rin?"
16. "Misakura Rin, too -- she was just young and beautiful, so she attracted attention, and she won her award through some trivial mistake. That's all it was."
17. "A trivial mistake? Idiot...... that was no mistake at all."
18. "I know."
19. "But do you think the fools of the world see it that way?"
20. "The value of a genius, you see -- the masses can't understand it. Those people, no matter how far they go, are just swept along by the evaluations around them, deciding their own opinions on a whim."
21. "The value of a genius can only be understood by the talented."
22. "By half-hearted talent like mine -- unable to become a genius, but not quite ordinary either -- only that kind of person can understand."
23. "You used to call yourself ordinary all the time, but you're not ordinary anymore?"
24. "I've put in a fair amount of effort, you know. I am talented."
25. "So? Why do you fixate on Misakura Rin so much?\nAre you in love with her?"
26. "I love her. But my hatred is greater still."
27. "The talented love the genius, and hate them."
28. "I see. That love-hate is just like Mozart and Antonio Salieri in 'Amadeus'......"
29. "Ah, that movie -- I saw it too when I was young. The story of the genius Mozart and the talented Salieri."
30. "Mozart's rare musical genius was praised by the masses, while his innocent, vulgar, ill-mannered character was scorned by other composers."
31. "But Salieri alone came to understand that 'Mozart's talent was the one supreme thing beloved by God,' and was forced to realize that he himself had only enough talent to recognize Mozart's true worth -- that he was merely a talented man."
32. "And Salieri, tormented by his fierce jealousy toward Mozart, is driven into anguish that gives birth to a great tragedy."
33. "Genius and talent. The most unbridgeable, and the most love-hate-filled, of relationships."
34. "You'll be swallowed up by that same kind of tragedy, you know."
35. "No. History doesn't belong to geniuses alone."
36. "There are times when the talented surpass the genius."
37. "No -- a genius isn't merely something we the talented tremble at; you could also call one a talented person who rode the currents of their time."
38. "I'll reach where she stands, without fail."
39. "...... Well, that's impressive."
40. "But you're being ignored, aren't you?"
41. "Earlier, you said 'I don't see eye to eye with him either' -- you meant Misakura Rin, didn't you? I'm guessing you've been trying to approach her from your end, but you're being completely ignored."
42. "Isn't that about the size of it?"
43. "But the history of art doesn't belong to geniuses alone."
44. "I'll say it again -- geniuses aren't the only ones who make history."
45. "Very soon, we're going to surpass Kusanagi Ken'ichirou."
46. "Surpass Kusanagi Ken'ichirou?"
47. "That's right. The media will make a huge fuss."
48. "What do you mean by that......"
49. "Who knows. I think even saying this much to you, Kusanagi-kun, was nonsense to begin with."
50. "Nonsense?"
51. "I think, probably, I just wanted to convince myself, for my own sake."
52. "How stupid. A ghost?\nYes. I think you were like a ghost."
53. "But now that I've stood before an empty husk of a person like you, it's all clear."
54. "There's no need to spit tough words at a person like you."
55. "To me, you're a ghost of the past.\nAlready, you're nothing more than an illusion."
56. "An illusion, huh...... quite a way to talk about someone standing right in front of you."
57. "Standing in front of me? So what?\nWalk through town and you'll run into crowds of people everywhere."
58. "But what difference is there between the people you just pass by and an illusion?"
59. "Most people are like illusions. There are only a few important people, a few important things. So I only put my faith in what matters."
60. "I see. So to you, I'm an illusion......"
61. As if she hadn't heard a word I said, Nagayama simply continued talking.
62. "I'll surpass Kusanagi Ken'ichirou, and then climb my way up to where Misakura Rin stands.\nThat's all there is to it."
63. "Yes. Mm, it's all clear now."
64. "Bye-bye, Kusanagi Naoya."
65. "You know...... I was crazy about you. I really, really loved you."
66. "But that too seems to have been a huge misunderstanding.\nIn a way, I'm relieved."
67. "I'm going far, far ahead.\nA place people like you -- people content in the circumstances you were handed -- can never reach."
68. "I'm going."
69. Nagayama started walking, just like that.
70. Her gaze was no longer on me at all.
