=== lines=139 stop_reason=end_turn in=6184 out=4146 cache_read=3222 cache_create=0 last_entry_continued=0 ===
1. "Fwaah... having class from first period today is such a drag."
2. With a lack of motivation that would put any student to shame, I finished climbing Yume-no-Ukisaka, and then I heard a loud voice calling out to me.
3. "Sensei!"
4. From the school building, I could see Sakizaki-kun rushing toward me with a fierce look on her face.
5. What now? Had I done something to make Sakizaki-kun angry again? \nOr so I thought,
6. but as she got closer, I quickly realized it wasn't that kind of expression.
7. She was running toward me with a face on the verge of tears.
8. "Um... what's the matter, Sakizaki-kun?"
9. Sakizaki-kun, having reached me, grabbed my hand and cried out loud.
10. "This isn't a 'what's the matter' situation! Something terrible has happened!"
11. "Something terrible?"
12. "Please come to the chapel!"
13. "The chapel?"
14. "The mural in the chapel. Something terrible has happened to 'The Footprints of the Sakura'!"
15. Why now? The Footprints of the Sakura? It was a piece from quite a while back, and by now no one should even give it a second glance...
16. In any case, I ran to the chapel, pulled along by Sakizaki-kun.
17. And then I saw the sight with my own eyes.
18. "Wh-what is this?"
19. Paint had been splattered all over The Footprints of the Sakura, and moreover, it had been dragged out with something like a board, leaving streaked marks.
20. "Look at it. It's been defaced like this!"
21. "Defaced..."
22. I looked carefully at the drastically altered 'Footprints of the Sakura.'
23. "This isn't graffiti..."
24. "Huh? But it's obviously graffiti."
25. It certainly looked like nothing but graffiti. \nThat's how amateurish it was.
26. But this wasn't mere graffiti.
27. This had been altered with a certain context in mind.
28. Even the trunk of the cherry tree Akashi had painted had been tampered with.
29. "Dragging paint out is Gerhard Richter's technique..."
30. And the cherry tree had been turned into a cyborg, with additions like something out of a manga.
31. "Sakizaki-kun, this... you've got it wrong."
32. "What's wrong about it?"
33. There is a kind of artistic activity performed in this context.
34. "Contemporary art..."
35. "Ah! Kusanagi-sensei! What is the meaning of this? Is this another one of your pranks?!"
36. This time, several teachers came running out of the staff room, shouting.
37. "Don't give me that 'ah, no'! \nYou're the only one who would do something like this!"
38. I was surrounded by several teachers. It felt like a repeat of that time.
39. "There's no way Kusanagi-sensei would do something like this! \nThis is a serious crime. It's vandalism!"
40. "Vandalism? This?"
41. "Can't you see it? It's been graffitied over!"
42. "Graffiti, huh..."
43. Even in response to Sakizaki-kun's impassioned words, the teachers who'd rushed over reacted lukewarmly.
44. "Well, well. To someone like me with no background in art, the original picture already looked like graffiti."
45. "Wh-what are you saying?!"
46. "No, they really look the same to me. \nBy the way, what was this mural called again?"
47. "Right, right. Back when you art club members made it, and now -- the situation isn't any different, is it? Come to school in the morning and the wall's smeared with paint... It's exactly like back then."
48. "Kusanagi-sensei, this really isn't your doing this time either?"
49. "What are you saying? Why would you say that?"
50. "You wouldn't know, Sakizaki-kun, but when that mural was first painted in this chapel, it was quite the ordeal. It was enough of a commotion that we called the police for vandalism..."
51. "But I heard this is Kusanagi Ken'ichirou's legacy."
52. "That's only in hindsight. It just happened to turn out that way. \nAs far as we teachers were concerned, it was just a huge piece of graffiti. It was a serious problem at the time."
53. "What do you mean 'in hindsight'? I heard it was an officially recognized work by the school."
54. "No, on that point the teacher is right."
55. "And besides, Kusanagi Ken'ichirou, huh... \nYou don't hear his name at all anymore. Even calling it his legacy..."
56. "Wh-what? Th-that's not true. Kusanagi Ken'ichirou is still a famous painter!"
57. "You think so? I don't really know, but this mural's popularity has completely dried up too."
58. "Lately, there have even been murmurs asking for it to be returned to its original plain wall."
59. "Where are those murmurs coming from?!"
60. "Well, I don't really know, but I hear rumors. \nProbably from customers, since this chapel gets used for weddings on holidays and all."
61. "I've never heard anything like that!"
62. "Well, it's not the sort of story a single student like you would hear, is it?"
63. "You're wrong. It's listed as a tourist spot in local pamphlets and such. It should be properly recognized."
64. "Those pamphlets are just reprints of stuff printed long ago. \nBack then it really was popular. Kusanagi-sensei was in the spotlight too, being the son of the famous painter Kusanagi Ken'ichirou."
65. "But now, a work completed by the son of Kusanagi Ken'ichirou is no selling point at all."
66. "Wh-what?!"
67. "I remember, you know. Back then, your art club had that Misakura Rin as a member, didn't it."
68. I silently nodded.
69. "Even the principal, whatever he said, had no eye for art."
70. "If Misakura Rin, and not Kusanagi-sensei, had led the completion of this work, the outcome would have been entirely different..."
71. "Honestly, I don't know about being the son of a world-class artist or whatever, but a piece completed by a part-time lecturer like you is no selling point for the school at all."
72. "Wh-what are you saying?! There are things you can and can't say--"
73. I stopped Sakizaki-kun.
74. Sakizaki-kun watched carefully for the words that would come out of my mouth.
75. What she was hoping for, probably, was my anger.
76. "Well, the teacher is right. If it had been credited to Misakura Rin, things would've been quite different."
77. "That's right. Yes. You've grown up too, and come to understand that much."
78. "Thanks to your guidance, sirs."
79. "Now, now."
80. Seeing my expression, Sakizaki-kun's face froze for an instant.
81. "That's enough, sensei! This is vandalism!"
82. "Well, that may be, but we need to investigate."
83. "Then call the police--!"
84. Once again I stopped Sakizaki-kun and shook my head.
85. "An organization like a school doesn't let outside forces in that easily."
86. "Especially since there's a chance the culprit is a student here."
87. "But--!"
88. "Either way, on this matter we'll have to consult the principal and various other higher-ups first."
89. "This isn't something we can just decide on the spot..."
90. "Where is the principal?"
91. "He's away on a business trip. Not sure if he'll be back today or not."
92. Once again, I stared at the graffitied mural.
93. The splattered paint made it look as though something ominous were rising up from the cherry tree.
94. And the cherry trunk, modified like a cyborg.
95. By that alteration, the cherry trunk that was spreading something ominous was transformed from a trunk into something artificial.
96. The cherry blossoms, originally rendered from the footprints of children, were now scattering some ominous substance.
97. A cyborg-ified cherry tree. And a cherry tree spewing poison.
98. In the background, faintly, was drawn that all-too-familiar structure from after the earthquake.
99. "Art as provocation, huh..."
100. A work in which children's footprints scattered into the petals of the sakura had been transformed into one where the sakura scattered harmful things.
101. Mediocre provocation.
102. A method of expression that could only be called infantile.
103. But this infantile, aggressive method of expression is what becomes "correct" in a certain genre.
104. Art history is not the property of artists alone. \nWithin it, centered on the artist, all sorts of people are writhing about.
105. Buyers, curators, critics.
106. Even if, hypothetically, art in the modern era is a dead thing, it cannot be openly treated as such.
107. The authorities of the art industry.
108. Contemporary art, even when it has already died out, is an industry that cannot subsist without clustering atop the corpses of its expressions.
109. No, this isn't limited to art. It's the same for literature, and the same for composition.
110. In the history of Western painting, especially after the Impressionists, works that were provocative toward the authority -- the salon -- have been numerous.
111. In fact, in France itself, Impressionism was long unrecognized by the authorities, and many of its paintings ended up crossing the Atlantic to America.
112. That is why American museums like the Metropolitan Museum of Art and the Art Institute of Chicago possess Impressionist paintings that don't fall the slightest bit short of what remains in France.
113. Art, since Impressionism, has continued to be provocative toward authority, and continued to be sensational.
114. But that avant-garde form of expression has kept narrowing and narrowing.
115. On the land the geniuses have exhausted, no fruit remains.
116. Everyone knows it.
117. There is no such thing as revolutionary new art.
118. This is easiest to understand in the world of classical music.
119. It is said that when the twentieth-century composer Schoenberg was asked by a friend, "Why do you write such hard-to-listen-to music, full of nothing but dissonance?", he replied like this.
120. "I too, if I could, would like to write tonal music. But history has forbidden me to write triads."
121. At the limit-point of Western music history, this genius tried to resist by means of the twelve-tone technique -- a method that altered music theory itself.
122. When he established this theory, he said: \n"This guarantees the hegemony of German music for another hundred years."
123. Of course, this technique never became a universal method of composition in the subsequent history.
124. Naturally, the hegemony of German music was in no way guaranteed; on the contrary, jazz -- rooted in Black music -- and rock -- centered on Anglo-Saxons -- came to sweep the world.
125. For us creators, "novelty" is a stranger. Almost everything is nothing but the corpse of expression, smudged with fingerprints.
126. But an industry cannot survive in such a state.
127. If it were some trivial, low-brow industry, it would be no problem; but this is art, a high-minded, cultural industry.
128. Even if it is hollow, it must fulfill its cultural mission and survive.
129. The Moore Exhibition, too, is broadly run by many wealthy patrons on this kind of thinking.
130. Cutting-edge, avant-garde, and provocative.
131. That which looks new, that which has a new style -- these are what the art world pursues.
132. Within this, art has, at some point, come to stand in an inverted world.
133. If you do something no one else does, that alone qualifies as "art."
134. Not new, but provocative works of the kind no one had done before.
135. Within this spiral, infantility and immaturity function as positive material.
136. Put kindly, the "child" is a symbol of destruction, and a symbol of new imagination.
137. But the majority of these "children" are nothing more than uneducated adults. \nMere indecent, senseless behavior becomes art.
138. The cutting edge of art. In contemporary art, more often than not, that kind of thing is affirmed.
139. Merely sensational. Whims, current events. \nBuyers pushing those things, and a market, for speculative purposes, with no convictions of its own.

