=== lines=133 stop_reason=end_turn in=5483 out=3566 cache_read=3222 cache_create=0 last_entry_continued=0 ===
1. I sit alone in my room, listening to music.
2. A song I used to listen to often, long ago.
3. It's been a while since I last heard it.
4. The song's title is "Sunflower."
5. Long ago, a great rock singer appeared in England.
6. His rock band was the sort that came to define an era, but to the regret of many, they disbanded a few years later.
7. Afterward, as if searching for what he himself was,\nhe tried his hand at every musical genre imaginable.
8. The genius absorbed every genre, and lost his way.
9. A wandering born of forward motion.\nIt is not only sloth that means retreat.
10. Sometimes -- no, rather often -- it is the very force of pushing forward that sends a person tumbling backward.
11. His name gradually vanished from the mainstream,\nand the great rock singer became someone no one so much as glanced at.
12. Again and again, he tried to throw music away.
13. And yet, always, music was there at his side.
14. So he chose to remain with it.
15. He played live in small halls and clubhouses, venues that would have been unthinkable for him before.
16. After steady, painstaking work, he finally completed his first solo album.
17. But back home in England he was treated as a wholly minor artist.\nUnable to sign a deal in his own country, he wound up signing with a Japanese record label.
18. A man forgotten in England.
19. And yet, on the far side of the earth, in the Far East, his music was still loved.
20. But that gradually began to change.
21. The song I'm listening to now.\nThe shift began with this album.
22. Artists of the younger generation, one after another, professed their respect for him.
23. Musicians who came to define their era\nbegan calling him the "Modfather."
24. It was around that time that I too came to know this song.
25. The time when that rock star made his comeback.
26. The one who told me about him was, of course, Kei.
27. Just as Kei said, it was cool, and it was music the likes of which I had never heard before.
28. "Sunflower."
29. A return to origins -- probably an old-fashioned song, really -- but to me at the time, it felt utterly new, utterly fresh.
30. That hasn't changed, even now.
31. When I first met this song, I was boundlessly young, and this rock singer was boundlessly grown-up.
32. That's why this sound strikes at my heart.
33. The music ends.
34. The nostalgic sound comes to a close.
35. If I press play again, the nostalgic sound will surely come back.
36. But time will not.
37. I straddle his beloved bike, listening to the music he loved.
38. Is this sentimentality?
39. Or something other than that?
40. "There are times when the force of moving forward sends you far backward..."
41. "There are times when evolution breeds wandering..."
42. "But..."
43. "There are also times when wandering itself becomes the nourishment that lets you go forward."
44. "Isn't that right... Kei."
45. "About time for the shop to open..."
46. After checking the clock, I decided to head out.
47. "It just can't be done."
48. "'I'd expect not' -- this really isn't the time to be so casual about it, is it? Besides, you have to get it done within three days, don't you?"
49. "No -- it might be less than three days.\nWorst case, it could be by tomorrow."
50. "That's beyond the bounds of sanity."
51. "Yes, I'm well aware."
52. "I asked every craftsman I know, just to be sure, but it's unheard of. Three enormous stained-glass panels in three days."
53. "Yes, I understand."
54. "But why stained glass?\nThe one that got graffitied was 'The Footprints of the Sakura,' right?"
55. "Making even more stained glass...? Don't tell me you mean to swap them in for Kusanagi Ken'ichirou's stained glass? If you do that, it's no longer a Kusanagi Ken'ichirou piece at all."
56. "As it stands now, it isn't a Kusanagi Ken'ichirou piece either."
57. "Even so, stained glass is what I need."
58. "Hmmm... well, knowing you, Naoya-kun, I'm sure there's some deep thinking behind it..."
59. "You give me too much credit.\nThere's nothing deep about my thinking."
60. "For my part, I'll do everything I can.\nBut that particular request, I can't grant."
61. "No, I just wanted to ask, that's all.\nSorry -- thank you."
62. I left the Sakuragahara art-supply shop behind.
63. "So it really is impossible..."
64. "After the art-supply shop, where are you headed next?"
65. "That voice--!"
66. "Tori... tani...?"
67. "What's with the surprised face? Who else would it be, hm?"
68. "Well, no, of course I'd be surprised -- it's been a while...\nWhen did you come back to Yumihari?"
69. "Come back? What are you talking about?\nHere."
70. Toritani thrust something up under my chin.
71. "This? A business card?"
72. "Being you, I bet you don't know what I do for a living, right?"
73. Toritani's card said "Editor."\nI recognized the name of the magazine... or rather, I'd read it in bookstores more times than I could count.
74. "I'd heard the rumor you'd become an editor at an art magazine, but...\nEditor at the monthly Art School, huh."
75. "That's right.\nI'm in Yumihari on business today."
76. "Business? Why is an art-magazine editor in Yumihari on business?"
77. "Don't play dumb! For the 'Footprints of the Sakura' matter, obviously."
78. "That's already been announced to the press?"
79. "No, not yet."
80. "Then why are you--"
81. "Everyone has their own special networks. And when it comes to anything Yumihari Academy-related, no matter how broad the field of art editors, no one brings back a scoop faster than I do."
82. "I see. Certainly, if that thing gets recognized as a work of art, it'd be your art magazines' cue to step in..."
83. "Right. I tried asking Yumihari Academy directly, and they said they're still deliberating whether to report it to the police. Don't report it, and it's art. Report it, and it's a crime -- that's about the size of it."
84. "Asking Yumihari Academy directly -- straight down the middle, as always..."
85. "But it's been a full day since the graffiti was discovered, and the police are still 'deliberating'? That's strange.\nWhich means -- exactly what you'd think."
86. "Which means what, exactly?"
87. "Yumihari Academy does not regard it as graffiti. In other words, they see it as a work of art. What's more, they're timing when to make it public."
88. "On a separate front, I've heard that the Moore Foundation is moving behind the scenes as well..."
89. "If it's art-related and there's money in it, that lot will stick their nose in.\nAnd the Moore Foundation manages Kusanagi Ken'ichirou's estate, after all."
90. "But that piece belongs to Yumihari Academy, doesn't it?"
91. "Yumihari Academy isn't monolithic either. Especially when it came to driving out the Nakamura family, they took investment from all sorts of places -- otherwise no one would be leaking anything to me."
92. "I see. That much is probably true.\nStill, even you can't have figured out who's behind that graffiti, right?"
93. "As if. There's only one who'd pull something like that -- Bourbagi."
94. "As... as expected. To think you'd have worked out even that."
95. "I'm not doing this job for show, you know...\nOh! Never mind that! More importantly, why were you coming out of an art-supply shop?!"
96. "Ah, right -- you wouldn't know, Toritani. I'm..."
97. "Yumihari Academy's part-time art instructor -- a stopgap until a proper art teacher arrives... right?"
98. "Well-informed as ever... then you understand, don't you? I am the art teacher."
99. "Hmm. Art teachers these days make stained glass, do they? And within three days, no less."
100. "Y-you were listening?"
101. "With a voice that loud, hard not to."
102. The only one with a loud voice was the shopkeeper...
103. "What are you planning?"
104. "It'll be publicly announced as Bourbagi's work within a few days. There's barely any time. In fact, thinking about it normally, nothing can be done."
105. "But Kusanagi is moving."
106. "You heard the shopkeeper, didn't you? Starting now, it's impossible."
107. "Hmm. Right. I said this to you a long time ago, didn't I?"
108. "That people who make art move in very distinctive ways."
109. "Ah -- staring into midair, tracing fingers as if they were brushes..."
110. "What, were you tailing me and observing? "
111. "Well, it's my job."
112. As she said it, for some reason, Toritani began to circle slowly around me.
113. Like an animal working its way around behind its prey...
114. "Then you've got the wrong job, haven't you? You came to Yumihari to cover Bourbagi's work. Which means I'm not part of it."
115. "True. Normally, that's how one would think."
116. "I checked with the principal, though..."
117. "The principal? You did?"
118. "Well, it is my job -- I do speak with the principal of Yumihari Academy, the party involved. Over the phone, though..."
119. "You didn't meet in person?"
120. "That isn't the job."
121. Same as ever, the Toritani parent and child...
122. "Whether Bourbagi's work will be recognized is still up in the air, so for now they're using paint that can be washed off."
123. "Figures. That coloration isn't ordinary paint."
124. "Meaning, right now, that work can still be erased."
125. "If so, isn't the first thing you should be looking for a solvent?"
126. "You don't accept that thing, do you?"
127. "Accept or not, I have no authority over it."
128. "Kusanagi Naoya is no longer the reckless boy he used to be.\nHe has a job now, and moreover a position tied to education."
129. "Exactly."
130. "So I won't lay a finger on this mural business."
131. "Well, if you went and washed off a Bourbagi painting, you'd risk becoming a criminal, after all."
132. "You get it, don't you? That's exactly it."
133. "That's exactly it... hmm."

