132. [Nonomi] "It's probably all very complicated."

=== lines=140 stop_reason=end_turn in=6199 out=3181 cache_read=3222 cache_create=0 last_entry_continued=0 ===
1. "Complicated?"
2. "Working at a nighttime place like this, I sort of think the adult world seems super complicated."
3. "It's not really. Even when you're young, the world is plenty complicated."
4. "Is that so?"
5. "Yeah, I think so.\nWorking a job like teaching, you end up watching young folks a lot."
6. "The society of school is complicated in its own way."
7. "So I always think, the young ones have it tough too."
8. "It's true, students have it tough in their own way."
9. "But for us, what's right, what's strong, and what's smart -- all of it is way, way easier to grasp than for adults."
10. "The popular guy in class. The brainy guy who's good at studies. The leader-type guy who's good at sports. Compared to the adult world, it's easy to understand."
11. "Even about the future, it's just, get the highest deviation score you can, get into your school of choice, yeah!"
12. "The rightness, strength, and cleverness given to us are extremely clear."
13. "So we can sparkle. Or rather, I think there are times when just being young makes you sparkle."
14. "That's exactly why I think it's wonderful when adults sparkle."
15. "Being able to shine in such a complicated world is somehow amazing.\nAt the very least, I think men like that are cool."
16. "A sparkling adult, huh... sounds more like glittering and greasy to me. I don't want that."
17. "See, you always brush things off like that..."
18. "But I suppose that is very like you, Kusanagi-san..."
19. "Working in a place like this, I see all kinds of adults."
20. "But there aren't many people like you, Kusanagi-san."
21. "You mean I'm a character? That's not much of a compliment."
22. "I meant it as one! Being able to live that willfully is cool."
23. "Willfully? Me?"
24. "Yes. You're always deflecting, living like you're not making waves,\nbut I think you're really willful, sensei, and overwhelmingly so."
25. "I really love the new Kusanagi Naoya version of 'The Footprints of the Sakura.' The Bourbagi goes without saying, but it's waaay better than the Kusanagi Ken'ichirou version!"
26. "What are you saying. Like the news reports, that one's a Kusanagi Ken'ichirou too."
27. "If your style, sensei, were a bit more subdued, then, well, maybe I could believe reports like that..."
28. "Well, artists are willful creatures.\nEven when they don't mean to, their ego seeps through."
29. "That famous philosopher, Ludwig Wittgenstein, said this:"
30. "In a shallow genius, one can see through to their dexterity."
31. "In that case, what is it that shows through a real genius?"
32. "How should I know."
33. "Come to think of it, he also left these words:"
34. "A genius is a talent with courage!"
35. "You know, people are watching you more than they seem to, sensei."
36. "Oops, Katagai-san's coming back. All right, I'm getting back to work!"
37. "Ah, work hard."
38. 　Nonomi-kun went back to work as Katagai returned, passing each other.
39. "You were talking with Nono-chan while I was gone, weren't you?"
40. "Yeah, she was there."
41. "Damn it, you're the real lolicon, aren't you?"
42. "Getting called a lolicon just for talking is a bit much."
43. "Young girls really are great, aren't they."
44. "Another criminal story?"
45. "No, that's not it. When you're young, everything is simple, so all kinds of things are wonderful."
46. "Nonomi-kun was just saying something like that."
47. "That's why they can put in effort in easy-to-understand ways."
48. "Don't talk like you understand... the result of us not putting in effort when we were young is what we are now, isn't it?"
49. "Maybe so. Well, I did get into a decent university. Even went to grad school!"
50. "You dropped out, didn't you."
51. "Yeah. To find myself."
52. "You're an idiot."
53. "You can't stay clever your whole life..."
54. "No, live as cleverly as you can."
55. "Sound argument.\nBut what fun is there in making sound arguments?"
56. "Maybe so... But 'finding yourself' isn't about being clever or otherwise, it's nothing but stupid."
57. 　A clever way of living, huh...\nCertainly, that's a way of life I've had no part in either...
58. "But hey, you're working hard, aren't you."
59. "How about you, Katagai?"
60. "I'm working hard. Because if I don't, I can't even make a living."
61. "Adults are working hard even when they don't shine."
62. "For the sake of living."
63. "Just barely making it by..."
64. "Another shochu!"
65. "Me too!"
66. "Again? Are you sure you're okay?"
67. "It's fine. Today's that kind of day!"
68. "Honestly, saying stuff like that, I'm not responsible if you're hungover tomorrow."
69. "When you're young, everything is simple, huh..."
70. "It's true that when you become an adult, all kinds of things get bothersome..."
71. "But there are lots of fun things too. You can drink, smoke, go to brothels, buy candy in bulk, buy toys and games in bulk."
72. "Adults can do anything."
73. "I don't go to brothels though..."
74. "Don't say that. Want to go together tonight?"
75. "Right now the booze is enough for me."
76. "Fine, fine. Just drink. Wash down the good and the bad with the alcohol. Revel in the sensation of it soaking through your gut."
77. "It's fine to have days like that, right?"
78. "You're paying!"
79. "Yeah, I'll pay!"
80. 　When you're young, everything is simple...
81. 　Strength, rightness, all sorts of things were easy to understand.
82. 　And then it gradually gets harder and harder.
83. 　Is that how it is?
84. 　But, indeed --
85. 　Did I have fun today?
86. 　Was I sad?
87. 　Was I giddy?
88. 　Was I irritated?
89. 　My feelings are too complicated to sort out.
90. "Urk..."
91. 　A sensation surging back up from my chest.
92. "Bleeergh..."
93. 　Over and over, waves of nausea hit me.
94. 　Why is it that just moments ago I felt so good, and now I'm in this much agony?
95. 　It's inside out.
96. 　Pleasure comes clinging with pain plastered to its underside.
97. 　I always think this when I'm throwing up from drinking.
98. 　At the end of the pleasure called drinking lies the pain called vomiting.
99. 　Misjudge the stairway of pleasure, and pain will come.
100. 　I puke.
101. 　From the puking, tears spill out.
102. 　I puke and puke.
103. 　I felt like someone was rubbing my back.
104. "Why are you crying..."
105. "I'm crying because I threw up..."
106. "It doesn't look that way to me, though?"
107. "Why is Ai here?\nYou got transferred to another school, didn't you... You said you wouldn't be coming back for a while..."
108. "But I said I'd come back someday."
109. "Yeah, and that's why I've kept this house."
110. "That's true. Even selling off the apartment full of memories with Mizuna-neesama..."
111. "This is my house too.\nI'm protecting this Natsume household, myself..."
112. "What's wrong, Naoya? You look miserable. Did something bad happen?"
113. "Nothing bad happened. Only good things."
114. "I see. So even though only good things have happened, you're still miserable."
115. "Miserable even though only good things happened?\nYeah, maybe so."
116. "Why? If only good things have happened, people shouldn't be miserable, right?\nAnd yet you are, Naoya?"
117. "Human beings, you see. People, you know."
118. "When things are going well, when you're really living properly --"
119. "That's when you're most full of yourself."
120. "When you're doing best, when you're most decent -- that's when you're the biggest piece of shit."
121. "You're at your worst when you're doing your best? That's strange.\nWhy would you be at your worst when things are going best?"
122. "Because you don't notice things are going well."
123. "You end up thinking this is normal."
124. "But it isn't normal."
125. "A life that shines --"
126. "A sparkling life being normal --"
127. "It isn't decent."
128. "But you don't notice it isn't decent."
129. "Because everything is glowing."
130. "It's too dazzling, so you can't see."
131. "Because you can't see, you don't notice."
132. "We don't notice."
133. "But is that really because of the brightness?"
134. "Well?"
135. "You don't know, huh..."
136. "Is that why you look so miserable, Naoya?"
137. "Because things are going well now. Because you're shining. Is that why you're miserable, Naoya?"
138. "No, that's not it..."
139. "I'm not shining right now. Things aren't going well.\nIt's the worst."
140. "Are you throwing this fit because you think now is the worst, Naoya?"

=== lines=137 stop_reason=end_turn in=5589 out=3024 cache_read=3222 cache_create=0 last_entry_continued=0 ===
1. "That's right... But people, you know. We, you know."
2. "When things aren't going well, when everything's a mess --"
3. "you get depressed, you drink, you throw up. You cough up blood."
4. "When you're doing worst, when you're the biggest piece of shit, that's when you're most alive."
5. "Things aren't going well, you're at your worst, and that's when you're most alive?"
6. "No, that's a bad way to put it. That would mean..."
7. "It isn't that things aren't going well."
8. "It isn't that you're worthless."
9. "If you were worthless, you wouldn't suffer."
10. "It's because we aren't worthless at all that we suffer."
11. "You suffer because you're doing great."
12. "You suffer because you're doing great, huh... That's paradoxical."
13. "Yeah, paradoxical. Totally upside down.\nThe best is the worst, and the worst is the best."
14. "The worst clings, thick and sticky, to every best."
15. "And of course the reverse holds too."
16. "The best is the worst, and the worst is the best..."
17. "The best is the worst, and the worst is the best, huh... Somehow I really get that, Naoya."
18. "Yeah. When you're living a life that looks like shit to other people,"
19. "when it's shitty and doesn't matter at all,"
20. "that's probably when we're most alive."
21. "That's when we're enjoying ourselves."
22. "That's what I think."
23. "So if that goes away, it's over."
24. "Frustration matters."
25. "The shitty things in this world matter."
26. "As long as you can still feel them,"
27. "that's the best way to live."
28. "If you can feel suffering,"
29. "that alone is enough to keep going."
30. "It's because you're trying to keep living that it hurts."
31. "It's because your body wants to live that it hurts."
32. "Come to think of it, my old man said something like that before he died."
33. "Suffering is right."
34. "Because you're resisting."
35. "Because you're resisting annihilation."
36. "That's why it hurts."
37. "Accept the pain of your body."
38. "Now I understand what he meant."
39. "The moments you shine aren't the only moments you're alive."
40. "The times when things are going well aren't the only times you're alive."
41. "Are you suffering right now, Naoya?"
42. "Yeah, I'm suffering."
43. "Is it hard for you right now, Naoya?"
44. "I drank too much... threw up, threw up, threw up over and over."
45. "Threw up so much my esophagus tore and there was blood mixed in,"
46. "and still I kept throwing up."
47. "Throwing up, throwing up, throwing up."
48. "It hurts."
49. "But that's the obvious part, you know."
50. "I drank too much, so of course it hurts."
51. "Hehe, that's right. If you drank too much, of course it does."
52. "But it's the same thing."
53. "The same? What's the same?"
54. "Living, that's what."
55. "Anything, if you get the dosage wrong, will make you sick enough to vomit."
56. "Even the most sparkling, most joyful thing -- if you get the dosage wrong, you'll want to spit it out even if it draws blood."
57. "Even the moments that shine the brightest, taken past a certain point, are nothing but agony."
58. "Anything in excess becomes the same."
59. "That's true. In excess, pleasure becomes pain. That goes for anything.\nBut you drank enough to drown in."
60. "Why did you drink like that, knowing it would make you sick?"
61. "Well, it's the same reason people wish for happiness."
62. "Happiness, like alcohol, will make you sick if you get too much.\nAnd yet this shitty thing is what happiness is... and yet --"
63. "people wish for happiness."
64. "Any amount of happiness beyond what a person can hold is nothing but nausea, and yet they still try to get it. They try to swallow it down."
65. "But people can't endure excessive happiness."
66. "Unable to bear the happiness, they get sick and flush it all down the toilet."
67. "You can see that kind of scene on weekends in any run-down back-alley bar."
68. "It's a common sight."
69. "Excessive happiness, to a person, is nothing but pain."
70. "And pain itself is back-to-back with happiness."
71. "The pain called misfortune is only ever back-to-back with happiness."
72. "Misfortune, too, is only happiness in a different guise."
73. "Are you happy, Naoya?"
74. "That's why I'm suffering, isn't it?"
75. "A truly miserable person doesn't wear a face like this."
76. "After Kei died..."
77. "I met Misakura Rin once."
78. "Misakura, huh. It sounds like she's working hard."
79. "Yeah, hard enough that it's too much..."
80. "Come to think of it, the last time I saw Rin, we talked about God."
81. "Misakura and you, Naoya? That's interesting..."
82. "I don't have Rin's level of learning. All I know is theology as seen through the history of art."
83. "But I know that beauty and God are equally important..."
84. "She takes beauty as something absolute.\nHer idea is that beauty can exist as beauty only because there is an absolute arbiter, like God..."
85. "People call that aestheticism..."
86. "From Rin's point of view, the beauty a person like me believes in must be a weak thing."
87. "She said... my god is a weak god."
88. "And she's right. My god truly is a weak one."
89. "Because my god is one that exists together with people."
90. "A god who wouldn't even exist without people..."
91. "But yesterday, I had a thought."
92. "What did you think, Naoya?"
93. "That I was right, in my own way... that's what."
94. "My god can only exist together with people."
95. "He isn't an absolute being, and he arbitrates nothing."
96. "He doesn't judge, doesn't punish, doesn't forgive either."
97. "But it's precisely because of that that the weak god is with people."
98. "I said it. Yesterday."
99. "Art has to be born again through the one who sees it."
100. "It's because it's born through the one who sees that art has meaning."
101. "That's what I said in front of the reporters."
102. "But you know, once I finished that piece, I think I became certain..."
103. "Beauty is rediscovered by the one who sees it."
104. "Beauty is reborn through the one who sees it."
105. "In that moment, God is there."
106. "Yeah. It's just as Rin says.\nA weak god."
107. "'A work of art is an object seen under the aspect of eternity.\nAnd the good life is the world seen under the aspect of eternity.\nHerein lies the connection between art and ethics.'"
108. "You don't need an absolute god to guarantee the aspect of eternity."
109. "It's enough for people to feel a god that belongs to people,"
110. "and to be moved..."
111. "The weak god dwells within the beauty of frail people."
112. "That's why that frail god has meaning."
113. "That's why I say it."
114. "Here, take it."
115. "The god that resides in this painting is the aspect of eternity."
116. "This emotion is a single instant, but it is eternal."
117. "And there, happiness is..."
118. "You can only feel happiness together with a frail god."
119. "Because happiness that's too strong is no different from liquor that's too strong,"
120. "and it gives people the worst kind of hangover..."
121. "You two are alike..."
122. "Thank you, Naoya."
123. "For being here with me..."
124. "Idiot... it's not something to thank me for..."
125. "I'm the one who has to say thanks..."
126. "You gave me a reason to be here, a reason to stay in the Natsume house."
127. "That's why I can be here."
128. "If anyone has to say thanks, it's me."
129. "Is that so? Really...\nThen say it for me."
130. "Naoya, say thank you to me."
131. "Yeah, Ai."
132. "Thank you for staying by my side, when everything else had left me behind..."
133. "For being a person of this house..."
134. "What are you saying. That's my line."
135. "Thank you for staying in this house... for staying in this town."
136. "Thank you for keeping my place here, while I was away..."
137. "What are you talking about...\nWe're family, aren't we?"

=== lines=8 stop_reason=end_turn in=1530 out=152 cache_read=3222 cache_create=0 last_entry_continued=0 ===
1. "No blood ties. Every one of us scattered, nothing about it holding together."
2. "No father, no mother, no one at all..."
3. "And yet, this is the place I ought to come back to..."
4. "The place I come back to..."
5. "Nothing about us holds together... but even so, we're family."
6. "That's right, isn't it, Naoya..."
7. "Yeah. That's right..."
8. Ai's warmth came down over me.

=== lines=133 stop_reason=end_turn in=5186 out=3483 cache_read=3222 cache_create=0 last_entry_continued=0 ===
1. "!? Ai!"
2. "Ai? Ai, are you there?"
3. The usual room, the usual scene.
4. Naturally, no one answers my call.
5. "Ha... what am I even doing..."
6. I crouch down.
7. Dreaming that Ai had come back...
8. Just how weak have I gotten...
9. Even to me, one embarrassing line after another.
10. Thank god that was a dream.
11. I feel reasonably good.
12. Probably because I threw up so much booze yesterday that not even enough remained in me to give me a hangover.
13. "Today's classes start from third period, so..."
14. "No problem in taking my time, right?"
15. Just as I'm about to change, my cell phone rings.
16. "What? This number..."
17. An unfamiliar number.
18. I hardly ever get calls on my cell.
19. Much less from a number I don't recognize.
20. "Wrong number?"
21. "Hello......"
22. "A-a personnel notice?"
23. "I guess it really was a bad move......"
24. My feet feel heavy climbing Yume-no-Ukisaka.
25. "Trustee Murayama already hated me to begin with, and I probably made him lose face on top of that......"
26. "Ahh, out of work in this crappy economy... rough."
27. Honestly, I hadn't ruled out the possibility of being fired.\nBut since I'd been acting on Trustee Inoue's behalf, I figured the safest bet was that I'd get off without a scolding and just continue as before.
28. "I don't wanna......"
29. The front gate draws closer and closer.
30. "It's Kusanagi Naoya."
31. "Ah, come in......"
32. "Good work the other day."
33. "Ah, um, how do I put this... it's true I didn't get your permission, Headmaster......"
34. "Why would you need my permission? You got permission from Trustee Inoue, didn't you? Naturally, word came to me as well."
35. "More than anything, once Mr. Friedman got involved, I never assumed nothing would come of it.\nI've heard his nose for these things is quite sharp."
36. "Oh, then......"
37. "That said, Trustee Murayama is furious."
38. "Everything he'd planned got snatched away by Trustee Inoue, after all."
39. "Apparently he was up in arms, demanding that part-time brat's neck."
40. "Um... so today, being called here, is that......"
41. "Yes, as I said on the phone -- a personnel notice."
42. "Which means......"
43. "By rights, this should have been said before March began, but that graffiti business happened in the middle of it, and the confusion made it impossible."
44. "Before March? Then, just like the rumors......"
45. "Rumors? What rumors?"
46. "Ah, no, it's nothing."
47. "Well, it's a promise. It was actually decided quite a while ago......\nStarting next academic year, we'd like to move Kusanagi Naoya from part-time to full teacher -- in other words, regular employment."
48. Words I hadn't anticipated at all.
49. From part-time to teacher.
50. Meaning regular employment?
51. "Eh? A teacher?"
52. "During your part-time period, you took the private school aptitude exam and passed it."
53. "You have your own will in the matter, Naoya-kun, but I do think it would mean a much more stable life than what you have now. It's not a bad offer, is it?"
54. "Bad? It's a wonderful offer!\nB-but, why now?"
55. "I haven't produced any real results to speak of......"
56. "Even setting your record aside, leaving the art teacher post vacant indefinitely is a problem. Even reduced to a shadow of itself, Yumihari is a school that's produced many artists."
57. "The fact that we've been without an art teacher up until now is itself a problem, if you ask me."
58. "That said, it wasn't easy, believe me.\nTrustee Murayama holds quite a grudge against you. When this came up, he opposed it with tremendous fury......"
59. "Even so, this is a promise.\nIt's not something to be decided just by reading a trustee's expression."
60. "Um... what is this promise about?"
61. "That's as much as I can explain from my end.\nFor now, I'd like your answer as soon as possible."
62. "Well, OK. Please let me do it."
63. "Very well, then would you fill out these documents?\nBy rights the paperwork ought to have been completed long ago."
64. I sign the documents at the headmaster's desk.
65. "There. With this, from next year you're an art teacher.\nJust like a regular teacher, various things become permissible."
66. "How about it? Won't you revive Yumihari Academy's art club again?"
67. "No, isn't that something the students are supposed to do?\nNot something a teacher sets up."
68. "Perhaps so. Well, fine, at any rate I look forward to working with you going forward."
69. "Yes, thank you for having me."
70. I bow my head.
71. I descend Yume-no-Ukisaka, where the cherry buds have begun to appear.
72. "Looks like the blossoms will open early this year."
73. "That's right. What will this year's cherry blossoms be like?"
74. "Yes, it's Ai. Ai-sensei."
75. "You're heading home now, aren't you, Naoya?\nMe too. Come on, let's go home."
76. "Home, you say......"
77. "Our home, of course."
78. "You mean the Natsume estate?\nB-but weren't you posted to a distant sister school......"
79. "Ha, that headmaster is as bad about skipping explanations as ever.\nYou haven't been told anything, Naoya?"
80. "'Not told anything' -- well, all I got was the notice about regular employment......"
81. "Good grief, only telling you the conclusion. That Headmaster Toritani has a mean streak!"
82. "What is going on?"
83. "First, I wasn't posted to a sister school. I was shipped off there."
84. "Shipped off?"
85. "Yes. There was a bit of a problem over there, and I got roped in as the personnel who could solve it."
86. "Why did it have to be you, Ai?"
87. "It was a problem at a girls' school, so they needed a young female teacher like me. Well, it was troublesome in all sorts of ways."
88. "Since I'd had all this trouble pushed on me, I made a promise with Headmaster Toritani beforehand.\nIf I could resolve this problem, she'd move you from part-time to full teacher."
89. "That's why I had you take the private school aptitude exam."
90. "It's true, I was made to take that exam even though I wasn't a full hire."
91. "Which means -- it was you, Ai? That was a promise with you?"
92. "That's right. Leaving you as part-time indefinitely was just too cruel. Ever since Yumihari went to that multi-owner system, there's been a real trend of cutting personnel costs. Wakata-san had a hard time with that too."
93. "I haven't done anything for you up until now, Naoya.\nQuite the opposite -- you've done a great deal for me......"
94. "The matter with Shizuku... it should be fine to say this now, but you saved her from the Nakamura family, didn't you?"
95. "By letting go of a good deal of the inheritance......"
96. "No, that was just how things worked out......"
97. "Kei was mortified. By all rights we, her family, should have been the ones to save Shizuku, and yet again it was a Kusanagi who did......"
98. "For Kei, the Kusanagi are like heroes."
99. "You're his rival. And Ken'ichirou-san was someone he admired."
100. "Kei was? Had Kei even met Ken'ichirou?"
101. "No, he hadn't, but his beloved bike, like my Mach III, was one Ken'ichirou-san used to ride."
102. "Eh? Really?"
103. "The Mach III was the bike he rode in Japan, the Vespa was the one he rode in New York."
104. "When Ken'ichirou-san came back to Japan, I took them into my keeping... but Kei insisted he wanted to ride it himself."
105. "The Mach III is a handful of a bike, but that one is quite a handful too.\nIt's dangerous, so I was against him riding it......"
106. "But even for Kei, I suppose Ken'ichirou-san was someone he admired.\nHe never showed it on the surface, though......"
107. "I knew about the Mach III, but I never knew the Vespa was Ken'ichirou's too......"
108. "It seems you're riding it now."
109. "Yeah. Old bike, so maintenance is a pain."
110. "Right, Kei used to grumble about that too."
111. "He loved the Kusanagi family......"
112. "No, not just Kei. Shizuku too, and me......"
113. "I love Kusanagi Naoya and Kusanagi Ken'ichirou."
114. "It's not enough to repay everything you've done, but this time I made the headmaster promise absolutely."
115. "I-is that how it was......"
116. "Yes. I ended up spending a whole year out there, but it was worth it."
117. "To think there was such a reason......"
118. "That's the reason I was away from this place."
119. "And now I've returned.\nI'm home, Naoya."
120. "Welcome home, Ai."
121. "Fufufu... something about this is embarrassing."
122. "Well then, shall we head home? We've both got things to catch up on."
123. "The Natsume house, huh... it was only a year, but somehow it feels terribly nostalgic."
124. "Same here -- it feels like we've been apart forever."
125. "Now that's something nice to hear. Were you a little lonely?"
126. "You saw me last night... unless that was a dream..."
127. "Yes, you were crying."
128. "T-that was because I was puking!"
129. "What's wrong with it? Probably I'm the only one who gets to see you like that, Naoya."
130. "O-of course you are! Or rather, it's rare for me to end up in a state like that at all!"
131. "You endure too much, always. Naoya."
132. "You should cry more. And you should lean on me more."
133. "T-t-that's......"

