79. [Yumi] "Ha!? I see!"
80. [NARRATION] Kawachino reaches for her bag.
81. [NARRATION] I react instantly.
82. [Naoya] "Hmph... I can see through your plan."
83. [Naoya] "Knowing you, pervert that you are, you were going to use checking the tag as a pretext to strip Hikawa completely naked. And to do that, I standing before you had to be dealt with."
84. [Naoya] "I knew you'd definitely come to blind me. By the time you shoved your hand into that bag, my battle stance was more than ready!"
85. [Yumi] "No, no, there's no need to strip her completely naked. You don't have to take off the underwear..."
86. [Naoya] "Ah, right... now that you mention it..."
87. [Yumi] "Now, I've blinded Kusanagi. Now's the time! Now is when you strip off the uniform! Come on, fully naked!"
88. [Rina] "H-hey, why fully naked? All I need to do is lift it, right! Wait, w-wawawa, no, don't, kyah!"
89. [Yumi] "Strip, strip, full nudity is the highest -- guhoh!? Wh-what is this? A chokehold!?"
90. [Rin] "Honestly... you can't do that, Kawachino-san. Handling her roughly like that will tear the uniform."
91. [Rin] "If I just tighten a little more like this, I can even put you out, Kawachino-san."
92. [Yumi] "Y-yes... I sort of understand. If the huge breasts pressed against my back were pushed against me any harder... I might climax."
93. [Rin] "Um... isn't the meaning kind of different?"
94. [Yumi] "Come, press those huge breasts against my back! Send me to heaven! Come!"
95. [NARRATION] My vision is still blurry, but apparently, while I'd been blinded, Kawachino had tried to strip Hikawa's uniform off and was stopped by Rin...
96. [NARRATION] Via her breasts...
97. [Rina] "I've never heard of Olympia as a clothing maker either."
98. [Yumi] "Ha!? Maybe an underwear maker!?"
99. [Rina] "--don't reach for my skirt while you say that!"
100. [Rin] "It seems it isn't the maker that produces this school's uniforms."
101. [Sui] "In the end, what does it mean? Please explain in a way Sui can understand."
102. [Naoya] "To explain it in terms you'll understand: 'still no leads.'"
103. [Sui] "Is that so? Hmm. The mystery only deepens."
104. [NARRATION] The mystery only deepens...
105. [NARRATION] No, to begin with, does Sui even realize how strange all this is?
106. [NARRATION] When we first met.
107. [NARRATION] For just an instant, she mistook her own name for a clothing maker.
108. [NARRATION] And on top of that, this lost thing she doesn't even know what she's looking for.
109. [NARRATION] But even so, there are things we've figured out.
110. [NARRATION] Sui's uniform is not one designated by this school.
111. [NARRATION] Which means it is not an official uniform of this school.
112. [NARRATION] If that's the case, there's a strong chance her uniform was made in imitation of the real one.
113. [NARRATION] Why would something modeled after Yumihari Academy's uniform exist?
114. [NARRATION] Cosplay?
115. [NARRATION] In that case, you'd be better off just buying a real uniform.
116. [NARRATION] Which means there was some reason Sui's uniform had to be made in imitation of Yumihari's.
117. [NARRATION] Even this alone is hint enough for me.
118. [Naoya] "...Olympia, huh."
119. [NARRATION] I recall... Shizuku said something when she heard that name...
120. [NARRATION] That aside from Manet's painting, there was apparently something else that bore the same name...
121. [Rin] "Sui-chan, isn't there any other clue?"
122. [Sui] "Hmm... let's see. First off, I feel like this school is important."
123. [Rin] "This Yumihari Academy?"
124. [Sui] "Yes, I can't help feeling that there's some connection between this place and what I'm looking for..."
125. [Naoya] "This place... you just said that, Sui."
126. [Sui] "Yes, that's what I said, but is something the matter?"
127. [Naoya] "In other words, could that mean not just Yumihari Academy, but the Yumihari Hill itself?"
128. [Sui] "That's true. Now that you mention it, it's true that rather than this building, I feel it's this whole grounds that matters."
129. [Naoya] "I see..."
130. [NARRATION] It began at that construction site... and next was Yumihari Hill...
131. [Naoya] "Is there anywhere else that stands out? Somewhere that feels vaguely familiar to you."
132. [Sui] "A place that feels familiar... um, there's a river flowing. And beside it there's a park..."
133. [Naoya] "A park by a flowing river. Around here, that would definitely be Sakuragahara Park."
134. [Rin] "Ah, that's right. A park next to a flowing river around here would definitely be Sakuragahara Park."
135. [Naoya] "For now, let's head over and see."
136. [Rin] "It's been a while. Sakuragahara Park, that is."
137. [Naoya] "You used to come here all the time, didn't you?"
138. [Rin] "Yeah, we used to come here a lot. But I wonder why... it should be pretty far from where I live..."
139. [Sui] "It's true... it's this place."
140. [Naoya] "Did you figure something out?"
141. [Sui] "I can't say for certain. But I do feel something, vaguely... Can we walk along the edge of the park?"

=== lines=137 stop_reason=end_turn in=7218 out=4447 cache_read=3222 cache_create=0 last_entry_continued=0 ===
1. The headmaster crushed the shortened cigarette into her portable ashtray.
2. "I see... so that child is still standing still at a place like that."
3. "That room is my private study. Though calling it a private room is generous -- it's more like a storeroom.\nJust junk, nothing of any value."
4. "There is, however, a ladder."
5. [Naoya] "A ladder? Is that some kind of metaphor?"
6. "It couldn't be more literal. A ladder is a ladder."
7. "Now that I mention it, that painting of yours displayed at city hall... it's a splendid piece."
8. [Naoya] "O-oh... uh, thank you?"
9. "When I look at your paintings and your father's, there's a phrase that comes to mind.\n...'What is hidden is the flower.' It's famous enough that even you must have heard it."
10. [Naoya] "Yes, I've heard it at least."
11. "'The flower, the interesting, and the rare -- these three are of one mind.'\nBoth Ken'ichirou and you are the type who produce that same kind of flower."
12. "It's quite tricky, but that boy... Akashi, was it?\nHe has something of the same in him."
13. "It's a quality Kei and that girl lack."
14. "Kei's paintings are the very gaze that beholds the world."
15. "And what that girl paints... is steady and sincere. For now, the peculiar urgency of adolescence just barely makes her paintings 'watchable.'"
16. "What that girl produces isn't suited to the arts."
17. [Naoya] "You see her clearly."
18. "I did graduate from an art school, believe it or not. I was even asked once to work as a museum curator."
19. "I had to decline, since I was going to marry as soon as I graduated."
20. [Naoya] "So that's why Kei's paintings are stored and managed at the Toritani home?"
21. "Yes, those. I bought them all up in one lot. I decided, at the time of my divorce,\nthat it was best to keep Kei's paintings somewhere within my sight."
22. "His paintings are complicated to handle, you see. It's not a simple matter."
23. Which was another way of saying that Kei himself is difficult to handle.
24. [Naoya] "Is your poor relationship with Toritani connected to that?"
25. "Perhaps it is."
26. "But then again, I'm a failure as a mother. I don't really know what the right answer is."
27. [Naoya] "You speak of it as if it were someone else's affair."
28. "Hardly. It's because it's my own affair that I can say so."
29. [Naoya] "To take over Yumihari Academy, you threw everything away.\nEven your relationship with your daughter?"
30. "Well, that's about the shape of it."
31. [Naoya] "For the Nakamura family to have entered into a marriage tie, the Toritani family must have had some value worth using. But there's no such value in the Toritani family today."
32. [Naoya] "It makes sense that the name Toritani didn't ring any bells for me."
33. "When I married, Toritani was still a fairly well-known trading company. A long-established one going back to the Meiji era."
34. "Even now my elder brother is barely keeping a small shop going."
35. "The collapse of the bubble economy played its part, but more than anything, my divorce brought us under the Nakamura family's glare."
36. "Even if their old influence had waned, in this town you can't operate under the Nakamura family's glare.\nThe Toritani house fell as easily as if it were rolling down a slope."
37. [Naoya] "...You crushed your own birth family, ruined your relationship with your daughter, and still that mural had to be completed. For your own long-cherished wish, and your atonement."
38. [Naoya] "For atonement for having used and thrown him away, the sacrifice is far too great.\nWhy go that far, for that man?"
39. "Yours is a difficult question."
40. [Naoya] "Is it?"
41. "Yes. Human beings understand themselves least of all."
42. "It's just how it is. Well, I could break it down for you a little more, if you'd like?"
43. [Naoya] "I'm not particularly interested, but would you break it down for me?"
44. "After being told that, I hardly feel like breaking it down for you..."
45. [Naoya] "Then never mind."
46. "Not understanding what isn't precious to you isn't much of a problem.\nIt's because you don't understand what is precious to you that it becomes a problem."
47. [Naoya] "That's your broken-down answer?"
48. "Did it sound that way?"
49. [Naoya] "Perhaps."
50. "Hehehe."
51. The headmaster let slip a knowing smile, then glanced briefly behind her.
52. "Well then... I suppose it's about time I went..."
53. Having started walking, the headmaster stopped and turned back.
54. "He and I were the same age. Different majors, but the same university -- we even sat in on some classes together."
55. "Well then."
56. I watched the headmaster's back as she left the rooftop.\nThere was no trace, in that back, of the powerless girl she once was.
57. 'Human beings understand themselves least of all,' huh...
58. It's precisely because things concerning oneself matter most that you can't understand them.
59. No -- because they matter, the not-understanding is what's most troubling.
60. Not understanding what's precious to you being a problem -- that's what she meant.
61. Say there's some obscure research being carried out somewhere in the world.\nOf course you wouldn't understand what it means.
62. What's too far past understanding is too far past understanding to matter.
63. How many dung balls dung beetles are rolling right now on the continents on the far side of the earth.\nIt's beyond understanding, but it's so beyond understanding it doesn't matter.
64. A failure as a mother, huh.
65. I'd heard similar words yesterday.
66. It was, 'I'm a hopeless mother, aren't I...,' wasn't it.\nIt sounds like the headmaster's line just now, but it's completely different.
67. And that, in itself, felt like the decisive difference in how those two women live.
68. Hands stuffed in my pockets,\nI ambled toward the rooftop entrance.
69. Toritani stepped out from behind a pillar.\nHer face said she had something to ask, but she also seemed to be hesitating.
70. [Naoya] "What."
71. And then Toritani fell silent again. It didn't look like she was hesitating over whether to speak\nso much as considering where to begin.
72. [Makoto] "Are you tangling with that woman because you want to know about Kusanagi Ken'ichirou?"
73. Toritani broached it with a solemn look.
74. It made me want to tell her not to overthink it.\nShe didn't have to treat it like some tender wound.
75. [Naoya] "No, not really.\nIt just came up as one of the reasons in the flow of the conversation."
76. [Naoya] "If I need to, I'll think about it, and look into it.\nThe reason I haven't looked into it up till now is that I wasn't interested."
77. [Naoya] "Well, if anything, it's closer to a service."
78. [Makoto] "A-a service?"
79. [Naoya] "Like I said, the headmaster wants to talk, so I'm just listening.\nBut that's strictly incidental. The real reason is something else."
80. [Makoto] "You said there were two reasons... but you didn't reveal the other."
81. [Makoto] "Why not? What's the other reason?"
82. [Naoya] "Obviously because I'm concerned about you."
83. Toritani tried and failed to hide her obvious agitation, which threw me a little too.
84. [Naoya] "I mean, you and Kei, the Natsume family and all that... okay?"
85. [Makoto] "Ah, u-uh, yeah... if that's what you meant... I get it."
86. Even after nodding as though satisfied,\nToritani's face stayed faintly flushed.
87. When she started to speak again, Toritani was oddly hesitant.
88. [Makoto] "I've said it a lot of times, but you don't have to worry about me.\nReally, it's not a big deal."
89. [Naoya] "It is a big deal. You even ran away from home."
90. [Makoto] "T-that's -- like I said, that's my problem..."
91. [Naoya] "How long have you been living alone?"
92. [Makoto] "Why bring it up out of nowhere... Around when I entered Yumihari Academy, why?\nThe Chimera's closer to school."
93. [Naoya] "You're trying to cover not just living expenses but even your tuition yourself, aren't you?\nYou've got all sorts of things weighing on you, don't you?"
94. [Naoya] "Didn't you say you wanted to sort them out and be done with it?"
95. [Makoto] "That's true, but! But!!"
96. [Naoya] "Since the mural was finished and the headmaster showed up, you've been strangely apologetic and wobbly...\nyou've even gone so far as showing this oddly meek face."
97. [Naoya] "I'd rather you got it out of your system quickly and went back to your usual overbearing style. Otherwise it throws me off my stride."
98. [Makoto] "'Style,' like I'm some kind of topping..."
99. [Naoya] "It's basically a topping. Your true essence is elsewhere."
100. [Naoya] "Well, I did say I want you back to your usual overbearing-style self quickly, but honestly the wobbly you is pretty cute, so from my point of view it's not a bad situation."
101. [Makoto] "W-what's that supposed to mean?"
102. [Naoya] "A girl who acts overbearing but occasionally lets her meek side slip out -- that alone is moe, isn't it?"
103. [Naoya] "There, there, don't get angry, don't get angry. If you get angry there it'll only look like I hit the mark."
104. [Makoto] "Grrrrrr----"
105. Toritani's fists were trembling; her whole body was shaking in tiny fits.
106. Toritani let out a long sigh.
107. Rather rational of her.\nI'd expected her to fly into a rage and land a high kick on me. Disappointing.
108. Well -- Toritani is an extremely rational sort.
109. [Makoto] "Turn around."
110. [Naoya] "Turn around?"
111. [Makoto] "Just do it!"
112. I turned around. For a while, nothing happened.\nI stood there without looking back.
113. Thump.
114. Something warm touched my back.\nIt seemed Toritani had rested her forehead against my back.
115. [Makoto] "I'm worried."
116. [Makoto] "That headmaster -- a former Nakamura, a person tied to this school --\nI can't sit by and watch her cause trouble for someone of the Kusanagi family."
117. [Naoya] "Between the art club and this, your knack for looking after people is really something."
118. Even that precious art club, I'd been told the other day, existed for the sake of two geniuses.\nDo all of Toritani's actions come back to that?
119. Two geniuses...
120. That's the truth for Toritani, but it differs from the fact of the world.\nKei is a genuine genius, no question.
121. But for Toritani, Kei and I -- more precisely, the paintings Kei and I produce -- have become an absolute value worth staking her entire school life on.
122. [Naoya] "Worrying about me and Kei more than yourself -- how much are you planning to carry on your own?"
123. [Makoto] "That's not it. I'm just moving on my own selfishness."
124. [Naoya] "Your selfishness doesn't even qualify as selfishness. If anything happens, just tell me. And use me however you please, like always."
125. [Makoto] "Honestly... Kusanagi, you're always like this..."
126. [Makoto] "...Am I usually strong enough, do you think?"
127. [Naoya] "Usually strong? What's that?"
128. [Makoto] "Ah, no, since you said just now something about the weakness I show now and then."
129. [Naoya] "Not weakness -- meekness, wasn't it?"
130. [Makoto] "Wobbling around and being meek -- that's nothing but weakness."
131. [Naoya] "It's not weakness. What you occasionally show is your unguarded sincerity.\nYou live with your guard up all the time, but I know you're not that strong."
132. [Naoya] "But you're always living strong. As hard as you can.\nNo one thinks you're weak."
133. [Makoto] "Is that so... then that's good..."
134. [Naoya] "We've known each other three years now. Now and then, in front of us,\nyou don't have to keep your guard up, you know."
135. [Makoto] "I don't really mean to keep my guard up..."
136. [Naoya] "But you're forcing yourself to live strong, aren't you?"
137. [Naoya] "I don't know why you do it,\nbut either way, the weakness you show now and then -- I think it's cute."

=== lines=31 stop_reason=end_turn in=4015 out=989 cache_read=3222 cache_create=0 last_entry_continued=0 ===
1. I strolled aimlessly through town.
2. Every so often, cherry petals fluttered down here and there.
3. My destination was Chimera.
4. Today I'd been ordered to serve as taste-tester for the chiffon cake Makoto had improved.\nThis is already decided, so shut up and eat -- or something to that effect.
5. Makoto still put on that same overbearing air as always,\nbut it looked like she was enjoying the "act" itself.
6. Under the pretext of "prototypes," she'd feed me exceptionally good things,\nand though she still said her coffee was hit-or-miss,\nto me, every cup was delicious -- coffee brewed by Makoto.
7. Makoto's part-time job at Chimera would soon be over.
8. [Makoto] "Welcome! ...oh, what, it's just you, Naoya."
9. I remembered being greeted like that about a year ago.
10. And yet, though it seemed the same, little things were subtly different.\nMakoto's soft expression, and my given name instead of my family name.
11. The same days that repeat every day change little by little without anyone noticing.\nBy the time you realize they've changed, they've already become "the norm."
12. That, I suppose, is what daily life is.
13. [Makoto] "Well, whatever. Go sit down somewhere."
14. With that, Makoto headed off toward the kitchen.\nI sat at my usual seat and opened a book.
15. Makoto came back about ten minutes later.
16. [Makoto] "Here you go."
17. She set the chiffon cake on the table and sat down in the seat across from me.
18. [Naoya] "On break?"
19. [Makoto] "That's right. There aren't any customers right now, either."
20. Right -- from this seat, hidden behind the potted plants, Makoto couldn't be seen.\nAll this time, the two of us had sat facing each other in this seat.
21. The chiffon cake was the color of cherry blossoms.
22. [Naoya] "So this is the improved version. Looks the same to me..."
23. [Makoto] "There's a subtle difference. Come on, eat up."
24. [Naoya] "All right, all right."
25. Spring had come around again,\nand I put into my mouth a chiffon cake that seemed the same as last year's, yet was just a little different.
26. [Naoya] "It's fluffy."
27. --Wrapped up soft and fluffy, the world fades away.
28. The verse written on the back of "The Reclining Sakura" drifted into my mind.
29. Lately, Makoto had been making a lot of cherry-colored sweets at Chimera.\nMost were fluffy things, soft against the tongue.
30. I wondered if Makoto had that poem in mind.\nMaybe I'd ask her sometime.
31. It could be today, or tomorrow, or even ten years from now.

=== lines=133 stop_reason=end_turn in=5203 out=3357 cache_read=3222 cache_create=0 last_entry_continued=0 ===
1. One day, several weeks after the Moore Open Exhibition submissions had closed.
2. Two sealed envelopes arrived from Friedman.
3. Neither one had a name written on it.
4. I called Friedman up to ask him about it.
5. "With those tickets, you'll be admitted to the award ceremony."
6. "Why are there two of them?"
7. "Who knows. Maybe a clerical error... or maybe the judges are still torn on their decision..."
8. "You talk like it's someone else's problem. You're one of the judges yourself, aren't you?"
9. "That was just a joke.\nWhat you have there is a proper nomination invitation."
10. "Which means... the two of us have both been nominated?"
11. "That's right. Both of you."
12. "T-That's incredible... There are years when no Japanese are nominated at all..."
13. "No, not the past few years. Every year several people get nominated."
14. "Even so, having two of these show up in one house is quite an achievement."
15. "For two mere students to be nominated -- the Moore Open Exhibition really has fallen, hasn't it."
16. "You just can't resist the sarcasm, can you."
17. "That said, those are formal nomination documents.\nAs a matter of fact, the two of you passed the sixth round of judging."
18. "By all means, come to the venue together with Natsume Kei."
19. "We'll handle attire and everything else. Just make sure you arrive on time."
20. "I see. Understood."
21. "If anything comes up you don't understand, feel free to contact me directly. I'll take care of it."
22. "Yeah... I don't understand any of this, so I'll probably be calling."
23. "Then I look forward to seeing you at the venue on the day..."
24. Passed the sixth round of judging... two rounds shy of the final.
25. Us... we really made it that far?
26. "Was that a phone call?\nWho were you talking to?"
27. Once the call with Friedman ended, Kei came out from the back.
28. "Yeah, an unpleasant guy... More importantly, two envelopes came from the Moore Open Exhibition."
29. "For real!?"
30. "Yeah, look, right here."
31. I showed Kei the two envelopes.
32. "H-Hey, this means... could it be we've both won?"
33. "No, at this stage it's a nomination.\nBut a nomination means both of us made it past the sixth round of judging, apparently."
34. "Seriously, isn't that amazing?"
35. "Yeah, just getting past the sixth round is an achievement. It's practically unheard of."
36. "Yeah, we both worked hard for it!"
37. "Well, I did what I could within my limits..."
38. "If you're going to say that, then same here."
39. I haven't seen Kei's painting.
40. Kei hasn't seen mine either.
41. But looking at his face, I can tell.
42. He must have produced a work he was satisfied with.
43. "Man, both of us famous... this town's going to be in an uproar, huh."
44. "Idiot, you're getting ahead of yourself. We haven't actually won anything yet."
45. "But a nomination's still a big deal, right? Think we'll be on TV and stuff?"
46. "On the day, sure. Well, at our age it's exceptional, so we'll probably get TV coverage."
47. "TV! That's amazing. Hey!"
48. "Is TV really that great? I hate the stuff."
49. "That's you. You've been famous since forever so you can say that, but TV is a big deal."
50. "I guess. Well, if the coverage is favorable, maybe it's an honor."
51. "That said, it's still just a nomination. Not a prize."
52. "But listen, I bet we're the ones who'll end up winning."
53. "It won't go that smoothly."
54. "No, no, it's you and me, Naoya. Two geniuses."
55. My own work turned out excellently.\nNo doubt Kei felt the same about his.
56. But in this country there are still plenty of artists active on the world stage.
57. Among them are more than a few who, through sheer bad luck, have missed out on winning at the Moore Exhibition despite having the skill.
58. Their names alone would take more than two hands to count.
59. "What if we ended up winning the Platine Epaulard Prize?"
60. "You don't need to worry about that.\nThese are nominations for one of the three bronze prizes. Nominations for the other prizes are separate."
61. "Sure, apparently you can be nominated for both... but don't get your hopes up."
62. "More to the point, what were you doing back there? Your hands are covered in oil."
63. "Oh, this. I'd been leaving my bike alone for so long it stopped running altogether."
64. "It's an old bike, so what do you expect? Why not switch to a new one?"
65. "A new one? No way, I love this bike."
66. "Hmm... you're pretty attached to it, huh."
67. "Well, yeah. I'm attached. And it looks cool, right?"
68. "Nope, to me it just looks like a moped."
69. "I told you, it's not a moped. Man, Naoya, you really don't know anything about bikes..."
70. "Anyway, keep it well maintained. It's an antique that loses its brakes the moment you hit a puddle, right?"
71. "Yeah, well. If I'm not careful, there's no telling what'll happen."
72. "And you rode that bike to school after pulling all-nighters. How reckless can you get?"
73. "Nah, I was awake the whole time, I was fine. Just a little sleep-deprived."
74. "Says the guy who collapsed in the art room..."
75. "Yeah, that one. Surprised me too."
76. "Don't say it surprised you! Think about how I felt!"
77. "Come on, it's fine. I was okay in the end."
78. "Just be careful from now on."
79. "Yeah. But I can't wait for the big day."
80. "Right. By the way, the venue's in Tokyo. You're taking the train, obviously."
81. "Hmm, I wonder what to do. It's kind of far on the bike."
82. "Yeah, that distance on a moped would be brutal."
83. "I keep telling you it's not a moped."
84. "But... well, I'll think about it.\nFor now I'm planning on going by bike."
85. "You're still riding the bike...?"
86. Since then, the season had turned fully to winter.
87. The winter sky is high.
88. The clouds are so far away.
89. I return to my room.
90. Back to studying for now.
91. These past few weeks, I've done nothing but paint. My studies haven't progressed at all.
92. "Well then! Guess I'll tinker with the engine a little more."
93. Kei heads back to the far end of the yard. Probably to work on the bike.
94. He really has it easy.
95. Honestly -- there was a time when he looked like he might fade away at any moment, but now he's cheerful, like it never happened.
96. Well, I prefer him this way.
97. I look up at the distant sky.
98. The Moore Open Exhibition, huh...
99. The prize that Kusanagi Ken'ichirou challenged again and again, only to be rejected again and again.
100. The top prize that he finally won only after losing what mattered most to him.
101. "It won't be easy..."
102. "How far, I wonder, can we possibly reach as we are now..."
103. And the day arrived.
104. The venue was packed with press and industry people.
105. "That's a huge crowd...\nThe general public isn't allowed in, right? So this is just the industry folks?"
106. "Yeah, that's right. This year in particular is drawing attention for the number of Japanese nominees."
107. "Elan Rootle won three years ago, so if there's another win the press will be in a frenzy again."
108. "Hmm. I see. Amazing."
109. "Excuse me! I'm Matsushita, from ASO Television... You're Kusanagi Naoya-kun, aren't you? The son of the famous Ken'ichirou-san!"
110. "Sanei Newspaper here, do you have a moment?"
111. "Ah, I'm Arimoto from Art Kodan. Before, we --"
112. "Uh, sorry, I've got something to take care of."
